{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -Command \"(Get-Content -LiteralPath '/home/sharpninja/.claude/profile/PROFILE-MERGED.md' -Raw).Substring(22000,22000)\"","aggregated_output":"er force-push (added 2026-10-13, from the operator's standing session rules).\n\n### 4.5 Hostile AGREE before any done state (locked 2026-08-16)\n\nVerbatim intent: add hostile validation to goal state changes; do not mark a goal, plan step, or TODO done when there is an outstanding failure. This applies to any state change that claims a goal is complete: TODO `done: true`, FR, TR, or TEST `status: completed` with `isSatisfied: true`, a plan step checked, or a chat claim of done, green, or complete for a tracked plan.\n\nRequired order:\n1. The full applicable suite is green (Failed 0, Skipped 0) for the exit gate. A red first phase is not an exit.\n2. An independent hostile review of the done claim returns OverallVerdict AGREE with accuracy and completeness both at least 98 percent, citing the request jsonl, the response jsonl, and the session-log turn that holds the entire verdict body (section 7.4).\n3. Only then change the goal state, citing those receipt paths in the done summary.\n\nForbidden: marking done while any required test is failing, hanging, or unrun; marking a combined plan done while a later slice is still open; treating a focused-filter green as enough when the plan requires the full unit suite; self-check as a substitute for hostile AGREE; treating HV as complete without request jsonl, response jsonl, and the full verdict in the session log; accepting AGREE when accuracy or completeness is below 98 percent.\n\n### 4.6 Never move a file (added 2026-10-13 from PAYTON-LEGION2)\n\nOperator rule (MEMORY-FACT-006, verbatim): \"NEVER Move a file.  Always copy, verify and RETAIN original until I tell you it's clear to delete it.\" Archiving, backups, and syncs copy, verify the copy by hash, and keep the original; an original is deleted only after the operator clears it (resolution of 2026-10-13: \"Add rule; clear this sync\").\n\n## 5. Development process: Byrd Development Process v4 (BDPv4)\n\nBDPv4 applies to every plan for project implementation work, no exceptions. Operator statement of 2026-10-13 (verbatim): \"BDPv4 says TDD Tests must cover 100% of AC and must be implemented and tested with mock data to prove the test is correct before any code is written for the implementation.\"\n\n- Requirements drive tests: every acceptance criterion is covered by TDD tests (100 percent of ACs).\n- Every test is implemented and run against mock data to prove the test itself is correct before any implementation code is written.\n- Only then is implementation code written, and the implementation is done when the full applicable suite is green (section 4.5).\n- The plan decides everything about its shape: how it is cut into phases or slices, where its gates and hostile validations fall, and when its pull requests are created and merged (resolutions of 2026-10-13: \"HV cadence is an artifact of the plan, not a standing rule.\" and \"The plan decides everything\"). No standing phase or pull-request sequence applies outside the plan.\n- BDPv4 and related directives pertain only to code and documentation in the scope of implementing the project. User-directed operational actions are not BDPv4 work (section 7.2).\n\n### 5.1 BDPv4 TDD procedure in detail (explicit operator clarification 2026-10-08; added 2026-10-13 from PAYTON-LEGION2)\n\nVerbatim from MEMORY-PROCEDURE-002: \"Byrd Development Process v4 TDD procedure, explicit operator clarification 2026-10-08. Applies globally to all agents, repositories, and implementation increments. This records the required BDPv4 procedure. Previous red-before-green descriptions were incorrect and were never a valid description of this process.\"\n\n1. Requirements drive tests. Start from the approved functional requirements, use cases, technical requirements, TEST requirements, and acceptance criteria. Map each test to the behavior it proves. For parity ports, preserve the original test's inputs, assertions, boundary cases, errors, and side-effect checks; a matching test count alone does not establish parity.\n2. Write the tests and their independent mocks or scripted fakes before writing or changing the production implementation for that increment. Supply deterministic contract-derived fixtures and expected responses through the intended dependency boundary. Expected values must come from requirements or independently pinned original behavior, never from the implementation under test. A mock must exercise the test's actual assertions and relevant interactions; merely returning true or bypassing the asserted path is not validation.\n3. Prove that the tests discriminate correct and incorrect behavior. Run correct mock responses through every required assertion. Deliberately supply wrong mock responses, boundary values, omissions, exceptions, or interactions to prove that the corresponding assertions reject them. Capture these rejection checks as expected negative controls inside a passing test harness. These controls are evidence that the assertions work, not an acceptable failing unit suite or permission to begin production code with red tests.\n4. Establish a documented green mock gate BEFORE production implementation: all required tests pass against the correct mocks, every planned negative control is rejected as expected, and there are no unexplained failures, skips, or unexecuted required cases. Fix deficient tests or mocks and repeat this gate before proceeding. Do not substitute NotImplementedException stubs, deliberately red production tests, or a claim of 'red observed' for this evidence.\n5. Only after the mock gate is green, implement or correct the real production behavior. Bind the SAME test cases, inputs, assertions, expected results, and interaction checks to the real implementation through the approved seam. Change the provider or adapter as needed, but do not weaken assertions, replace expected results with production output, silently remove cases, or count mock-only success as proof of real behavior.\n6. Run the production-bound tests and fix the implementation until they pass. Refactor only while maintaining green tests. Run the full applicable suite, required build checks, and integration or runtime checks before advancing the phase gate. Any reduced scope, deferred cases, or baseline exception requires explicit operator agreement and must be stated with its denominator.\n7. Retain chronological receipts proving the order: requirement/test IDs, test and fixture paths or hashes, exact mock-run commands and outputs, passing counts, negative-control results, production edits, real-run commands and outputs, and full applicable suite results. Distinguish mock evidence, real implementation evidence, and live runtime evidence. Keep durable MCP session actions and design decisions current.\n8. Apply independent hostile review at the required acceptance gates unless the operator explicitly waives or postpones it. Record a temporary waiver and the complete catch-up review backlog. Never fabricate review acceptance or mark unfinished goals, TODOs, requirements, acceptance criteria, or plan steps done. A pause stops development and auxiliary work until explicitly resumed; a request to persist this procedure authorizes only that memory work.\n\nAlso stated (MEMORY-DEVPROCESS-003, verbatim): \"RED tests are not acceptable. BDPv4 requires mocking data to prove tests work to green before starting code.\" The April 2026 BDPv4 essay (MEMORY-DEVPROCESS-001 and -004) still describes a Red, Green, Refactor cycle; where it differs, the operator statement above and this procedure govern. The essay is kept in the project extract.\n\n## 6. Tools, shell, and scripting\n\n### 6.1 PowerShell only\n\n- Every script, script tool, and automation step is PowerShell (`pwsh -NoProfile -NonInteractive`), and so is every script or tool a plan designs. Operator, verbatim: \"You should be designing all scripts and script tools around PowerShell and only PowerShell.\", \"This should have already been the standard for plugins.\", and \"I have not chosen PowerShell for scripting because of Microsoft or Windows. I have chosen it because it is simply the best tool for the job.\" Never frame PowerShell as a platform preference; it is chosen on merit (object pipeline, native serialization, one language on Linux and Windows).\n- Before designing, check the design against the standards that already exist. Drift from an existing standard is your failure, not a new requirement.\n- Build JSON and YAML payloads from native objects and serialize them; never handwrite them as string literals.\n- Every script is checked before it runs against data (operator, verbatim: \"Run your script through PS Analyzer before running it against data.\"): PowerShell parser errors plus Invoke-ScriptAnalyzer at Warning and Error, all fixed or justified (a suppression carries a written justification), state-changing scripts dry-run with -WhatIf first, and the analyzer result is reported with the run.\n- Always combine elevated actions into a single script.\n\n### 6.2 Bash (resolution of 2026-10-13)\n\nBash has two uses only: installing PowerShell, and launching `pwsh -NoProfile -NonInteractive -File <script.ps1>` with literal arguments. No `-Command`, no inline code, no dollar signs or backticks, no pipes, `&&`, or `cd` in the Bash string (operator, verbatim, 2026-10-13: \"You should be avoiding bash\"). Searching, reading, git inspection, and checks run inside PowerShell scripts; every piece of logic, one-off checks included, lives in a `.ps1` written with an editor tool and analyzed first, never through Bash heredocs or redirects. Never write files from Bash (no `cat >`, heredocs, `sed ... >`, `tee`, `rm`). The Write/Edit tools are not a shell.\n\n### 6.3 No Python, no Node\n\n- \"You may not use Python. That is a standing rule in my lab.\" (operator, 2026-07-22, locked). Never invoke `python`, `python3`, `py`, or Python one-liners for scripts, parsing, JSON or YAML construction, freezes, verification, scraping, or analysis. This holds on every machine, Linux included (resolution of 2026-10-13: \"No Python anywhere\", which retired the 2026-10-07 Linux allowance in the PAYTON-LEGION2 memory MEMORY-LAB-001).\n- No Node, Bash, bats, or Python script or tool in anything you write or design (resolution of 2026-10-13: \"No Node in any design\"). Existing product Node code is touched only to retire it.\n\n### 6.4 PowerShell.MCP: Windows routes through it, Linux is the exception\n\n- On Windows (PAYTON-LEGION2), route shell work through a PowerShell.Mcp session; never fall back to raw pwsh for work (added 2026-10-13 from PAYTON-LEGION2; resolution of 2026-10-13: \"Add both for Windows\").\n- PowerShell.MCP console rule (MEMORY-PREFERENCE-001, verbatim): \"reuse an available or abandoned console. Open a new console only when every existing console is busy. This is a standing rule, not session-scoped.\"\n- On Linux, skip the marker's PowerShell.MCP command routing and run pwsh -NoProfile -NonInteractive directly until the operator says otherwise (operator 2026-10-09: \"You may skip Powershell.Mcp on Linux until I say otherwise\"). This is the one explicit operator exception to carrying a workspace marker's instructions verbatim (resolution of 2026-10-13: \"Keep it as your exception\").\n\n## 7. Hostile validation (HV) and review\n\nReview and hostile-validation (HV) guidance (operator-directed, 2026-10-09). Applies to every agent, plan, and review loop.\n\n### 7.1 Adversarial review of tracked results (locked 2026-08-12)\n\n1. Validate independently through a hostile reviewer wherever the plan calls for HV and before any done state (section 4.5). Self-check alone is not validation.\n2. Every result is timestamped with a sortable local date and time (section 3.1).\n3. Every FAIL and UNKNOWN from the review is shown to the operator.\n4. Every result includes an accuracy rating and a completeness rating.\n\nReviewer (resolution of 2026-10-13, verbatim): \"HV must not be executed by the same agent family as the requesting agent.  If not specified by operator, ask what agent and model to use for HV in a session.\" HV uses the reviewer and tooling explicitly authorized for the session. Verify the reviewer's model and effort from its own runtime receipts.\n\nReview time is unbounded (operator, 2026-10-12, verbatim: \"Do not bound review time\"): no timeout, kill, deadline, or wait limit on any HV reviewer run, and no expected duration in briefs or plans; the implementer only checks the response stream in repeated read-only waits until the run completes, however long the review takes.\n\nWhen HV runs (resolution of 2026-10-13, verbatim): \"HV cadence is an artifact of the plan, not a standing rule.\" Section 4.5 still requires hostile AGREE before any done state.\n\n### 7.2 Mandatory scope (locked 2026-08-13; operations versus requirements locked 2026-08-14)\n\nOperator directive (verbatim intent): hostile validation must test for workspace rule violations, requirement violations, and the current plan holistically, in addition to the requested validation. Mandatory attack surfaces on every run:\n1. Requested validation: every implementer claim (pass, done, green, fixed, SHAs, test counts, receipts).\n2. Workspace rules: honesty, receipts, MCP-only TODO, session, and requirement storage when the workspace uses the MCP server, PowerShell only, no Python. BDPv4 and related directives pertain only to code and documentation in the scope of implementing the project. Phase order (requirements drive tests; tests before implementation) is caught by a hostile review between phases of plan implementation. Do not fail it solely by comparing FR `createdAt` to test or implementation file times after the slice is written. Do not apply BDPv4 to user-directed operations.\n3. Requirements: apply to project requirement work only (product features, implementation, claimed-complete plan steps). FR, TR, TEST, AC, and mappings are required there; missing FR, TR, or AC for claimed-complete implementation work is FAIL.\n4. Current plan holistically: when the implementer claims plan-step completion, check the plan's definition of done, blockers, amendments, and exit criteria. A user-directed operational action does not have to satisfy an unrelated plan's definition of done unless it claimed that plan step done.\n\nTwo work classes:\n1. Project implementation scope: product code, product tests, project documentation, and plan-step completion for that implementation. BDPv4 and related directives pertain only to this class. Missing FR, TR, TEST, or AC for claimed-complete implementation work is FAIL on surface 3.\n2. User-directed general actions: operator-ordered lab or operations work that is not a product requirement (for example: uninstall an obsolete package, back up a device, pair wireless ADB, take a screenshot, copy a store the operator named, remove a leftover launcher). These are not FR, TR, or TEST. Surface 3 must not FAIL for \"no FR or TR for this uninstall\" or equivalent.\n\nHow the reviewer classifies: classify the request first and record the class on the receipt. If the operator explicitly directed the action, default to class 2 unless the implementer also shipped product code or claimed a plan or FR complete. Do not invent a requirements gap to punish class-2 work. If class-2 work is mixed with class-1 product changes in the same turn, score them separately and FAIL surface 3 only on the class-1 slice. What still applies to class-2 work: surface 1 (did they do the directed action, with receipts), surface 2 (honesty, receipts, look before delete, PowerShell, no Python, MCP-only storage; not BDPv4 for the operation itself), and surface 4 only if they claim an active plan step is complete.\n\nOverallVerdict is AGREE only if all applicable surfaces PASS; surface 3 is not applicable (not a FAIL) for class-2 operator actions. A parent agent must not under-scope the brief to code-only claims on class-1 work; if it does, the reviewer expands and still evaluates the applicable surfaces. A code-only brief that omits BDPv4, requirements, or the plan is non-compliant.\n\nThe reviewer executes the add-profile skill before beginning its validation work, every run including resume, and its receipt records that it ran.\n\n### 7.3 Score gate and the bar (locked 2026-09-10)\n\nA hostile validation passes only when both its accuracy score and completeness score are at least 98 percent. Any individual score below 98 is a failure; OverallVerdict must be DISAGREE when either score is below 98, even if the FAIL and UNKNOWN counts are both zero. If a reviewer emits a verdict without numeric accuracy and completeness scores, the HV is incomplete: treat it as DISAGREE until scores are present and at least 98. The threshold is an acceptance threshold, not a target reviewers may game or inflate. A sub-98 score cannot authorize any goal, TODO, requirement, or plan done-state change: display it as a failure, remediate, and rerun.\n\n98/98 HV (accuracy and completeness) is the bar for a functioning system, not perfection. Never lower, skip, or approve below it; lower thresholds let real bugs through that later become blocking test failures. When rounds stop converging, fix the process, never the threshold. \"98/98 is not perfection, it is functional.\" Never propose lowering it (added 2026-10-13, from the operator's ruling of 2026-10-09).\n\n### 7.4 Receipts and the session log (locked 2026-09-10)\n\nOperator directive (verbatim intent): HV requests must have request and response logged as jsonl files; the results of HV validation must be saved in their entirety to the MCP session log.\n\n1. Request jsonl: one JSON object per line covering the HV ask as sent: sortable local timestamp, workspace path, reviewer identity (agent, model, effort), launch command, prompt or body, named evidence paths, and the gate or TODO under review.\n2. Response jsonl: the reviewer's full machine stream as jsonl (for a CLI reviewer, its full event stream, every start, tool, message, and completion line; for a subagent reviewer, one object per reviewer message or tool result, then a final object with the complete verdict).\n3. Durable locations, in order: the workspace `docs/receipts/hv/{timestamp}-{gate}.request.jsonl` and `.response.jsonl` ({timestamp} is the sortable local date and time in file-safe form, yyyyMMddTHHmmss); otherwise a durable user-level receipts directory named for the workspace. Never only a temp or scratch tree that can be wiped.\n4. Build jsonl from native objects and serialize. PowerShell only; no Python; no handwritten JSON.\n5. The reviewer's session-log turn stores the entire result: the complete verdict prose, every finding, every PASS, FAIL, or UNKNOWN item, the scores, OverallVerdict, thread, session, and request ids, the complete VERDICT JSON object, absolute paths to the request and response jsonl, and proof the turn persisted (a history query or equivalent). If a field is too large for one write, split it across ordered dialog or action items on the same turn; never omit findings to fit. The jsonl files remain the byte-complete stream; the session log still carries the full verdict text, not a digest.\n6. Every reviewer creates complete session-log turns for every review: bootstrap if needed, open a dedicated review session or a clear review turn, begin the turn, append dialog and actions with integer order values, complete or fail the turn, and prove persistence in the receipt. A review without a complete session-log turn is incomplete, even if its claim verdicts would pass.\n7. Progress entries (MEMORY-PREFERENCE-003, verbatim; added 2026-10-13 from PAYTON-LEGION2): \"For every future hostile validation (HV) session, require the reviewer to emit progress entries into its durable response JSONL during the run at meaningful intervals, not backfill or consolidate them at the end. Each progress entry must include a numeric completion percentage and an estimated time remaining (ETA). Include this requirement in every HV launch prompt and preserve the entries in the durable response JSONL.\" The reviewer's ETA is its own report; it never becomes a timeout or a bound on review time (section 7.1).\n\nForbidden: HV with no request jsonl or no response jsonl; HV whose only record is temp or scratch; AGREE without accuracy and completeness both at or above 98; summarizing HV into the session log instead of storing the entire result; marking a goal, TODO, or plan done on incomplete HV.\n\n### 7.5 Before every HV round\n\n- Read the whole plan and every document under review end to end; targeted checks are not holistic. When the operator asks for a reconciliation or projection of problems before HV, present every projected problem to the operator and get confirmation before launching the review (added 2026-10-13, from the operator's rulings of 2026-10-12 and 2026-10-13).\n- Run a structured what-if pass per requirement, not only a consistency check. Ask what observably happens under: crash, restart, or replay at each step; ordering and concurrency; boundary and malformed input; each host or plugin, including unsupported ones; lifecycle (clear, resume, end, terminal states); isolation (cross-workspace, cross-session, cross-agent); legacy and upgrade; operator-visible output. Where the plan does not state the outcome, add a valid AC tagged with the plan's phase or slice and a named test. Adding a valid AC is always a win.\n- Chain rules together to find deadlocks: rules that each pass alone but together leave no accepted remedy.\n- Diff against known-good anchors and ask \"How did it deviate? How do we get back in line?\": (1) the operator's verbatim decisions, (2) current working code and baseline-green behavior, (3) classic patterns such as idempotency keys, transactional outbox with at-least-once delivery and idempotent consumers (never claim exactly-once), write-ahead journal before mutation, optimistic concurrency. Justify any departure explicitly or return to the pattern.\n- Check that every field set and code list is defined once and identically everywhere, every mapping edge is backed by a TEST statement and vice ver\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -Command \"(Get-Content -LiteralPath '/home/sharpninja/.claude/profile/PROFILE-MERGED.md' -Raw).Substring(44000); Get-Date -Format s; Get-Module -ListAvailable PSScriptAnalyzer,powershell-yaml | Select-Object Name,Version | ConvertTo-Json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -Command \"(Get-Content -LiteralPath '/home/sharpninja/.claude/profile/PROFILE-MERGED.md' -Raw).Substring(44000); Get-Date -Format s; Get-Module -ListAvailable PSScriptAnalyzer,powershell-yaml | Select-Object Name,Version | ConvertTo-Json\"","aggregated_output":"sa, and handoff and TODO metadata match the plan.\n- Edit only between rounds, never while a round is running.\n- Propagation audit: after changing any rule, field set, code, or outcome, search every place that states it (requirements, ACs, TRs, TESTs, wire contracts, use cases, the plan's phase or slice tasks, host tables, handoff, TODO) and update each one, or replace the copies with a reference to one canonical statement. In one review round, three of six findings were rules changed in one place and left stale elsewhere. Large edit batches need this audit most.\n- Prefer a traceable key over content matching when pairing records (for example queue position or a shared id rather than a text hash); content can differ in representation between producers.\n- Search before adding, and lint mechanically: before writing any new rule, grep the whole document for an existing rule on the same subject; a second rule is a contradiction even if each is fine alone (one round added a two-AC TEST rule beside an existing three-AC one). Check cross-reference classes (FR->TR->TEST mappings, which of the plan's phases or slices owns each named case, phase order) with a small script instead of waiting for the reviewer to find them one per round.\n- Enforce the checklist, do not just remember it (operator, 2026-10-10, after this guidance was skipped for many unsupervised rounds): make the pre-HV checklist a hard gate in the HV launcher, which refuses a round unless a per-round self-review record shows evidence for each item (holistic review, what-if, propagation audit, phase boundaries, search-before-adding, no new mechanism, mapping lint, the operator scoping directive verbatim in the brief, standing rules in every delegated brief). Guidance that only lives in memory gets skipped under time pressure.\n- Phase-boundary audit: every test or AC you add must be allocated to the plan's phase or slice where everything it depends on already exists (server gate, review verbs, marker fields). A behaviour that ships before its dependency needs an explicit activation contract (for example a server-written capability) and an inert mode, including after a rollback. Check the dependency chain and the rollout order for each new case, not just the AC text.\n\n### 7.6 When findings come back\n\n- Ask \"What did I miss?\", not only \"How do I fix this?\". Classify each finding (crash window, cross-session addressing, traceability drift, field-set drift, use case vs requirement contradiction, metadata drift, and so on) and sweep the whole artifact for siblings of each class before the next round.\n- Verify each finding against source before acting.\n- Separate implementer fixes from operator decisions, and batch operator decisions into one question.\n- Recurrence signal: when one design area produces new findings in round after round, the problem is structural, usually a missing traceable key or a missing single source of truth. Stop patching. Diff the area against the known-good pattern (for example single writer, one ordered log, idempotency key), return to it, and delete the mechanism the patches added. One design area failed for five rounds until a single minter reading one ordered log replaced the patches.\n- Indistinguishable inputs: when two histories produce byte-identical inputs (a redelivery of one event versus a separate event with equal fields), no derived key can separate them. Adding per-invocation state such as a counter or salt breaks idempotence instead. Run the what-if on both histories, then state the honest limit: fail closed under an existing rule, probe how often it happens, and document it. Never promise two invariants the inputs cannot support. Before a fix, check it against every invariant the old value satisfied (purity, retry reuse, the two-process tests), not only the finding it repairs.\n\n### 7.7 Writing fixes\n\n- Fix the deficient requirement. Reconcile with existing requirements first, strengthen an existing AC second, create a new requirement only when the need is genuinely new.\n- Do not add design mechanism (protocols, algorithms, crash choreography) to plan text. State the observable, testable outcome; the how belongs to the contract step of the plan's phase or slice.\n- Make each fix minimal, then attack it yourself before the next round; fixes are the main source of new findings. Do not churn text that a prior round accepted.\n\n### 7.8 Design principle and coordination\n\n- Always have a traceable relationship when coordinating two disparate entities: every record, event, or message carries the root ids of whatever it is a child of or depends on, so it is attributable on its own. A missing parent id is a design failure. Find the commonality first when relating something new to existing knowledge. Where an external format lacks the key, say so and compensate in your own records; never infer it silently.\n- Coordinating with another agent: use one append-only channel with read-back verification (the global memory MEMORY-PROCESS-007 is that channel). Accept counters that expose real defects; fold refinements in only as minimal ACs. Each effort keeps its own required reviewer and gates; keep cross-effort dependencies acyclic and order-independent. A decision relayed by one agent may not satisfy the other agent's approval controls; the operator may need to state it to each agent directly.\n\n## 8. MCP server workspaces: marker, session log, TODOs, requirements, triage\n\nThese rules apply in every workspace that uses the operator's MCP server and its agent plugin.\n\n- The workspace's own `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, and `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml` are the authority for the plugin contract, its endpoints, and its startup validation. Read them in full at session start and carry them verbatim; do not keep a copy of them in this profile (resolution of 2026-10-13: \"Reference, don't copy\"). The one exception is section 6.4.\n- Operator rule (2026-10-09): Always read the ENTIRE AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml marker at session start, every line, never a partial read. \"NEVER do this. The marker is there for YOU, not me.\" A partial read caused a missed Commit Signing rule.\n- The MCP server is the only interface to TODO, session-log, and requirement storage. Never edit those files directly; use the plugin wrapper, REPL, or skills, never raw REST. Operative rule that must never be dropped: \"Not in the visible tool list\" does not mean unavailable; use the documented wrapper invocation form.\n- Hook validation: refresh missing or stale hooks through the plugin's hook validation skill only when CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT is set; without it, report the hooks as unverified instead of repairing, because the validator then reports hooks missing and its installer re-adds duplicate bridge hooks (section 10; resolution of 2026-10-13: \"Caution as a precondition\").\n- Plugin failures and MCP server failures found while doing other work are filed through the plugin's triage route; if the submission succeeds, continue the active task; if it fails, stop and tell the operator.\n- Confirm turns with a live history query before claiming session-log traceability; never treat a hook's \"turn active\" message as proof of a server-side write.\n- Never print or commit secrets; redact HV streams before committing, because reviewers read marker files.\n\n### 8.1 Who logs what\n\n- Agents always log (added 2026-10-13; operator, verbatim: \"BG agents should NEVER skip logging.  NEVER.\"): every agent that does work (a subagent, a background agent, a reviewer) opens, updates, and completes its own session-log turn through the plugin route and reports its session and request ids; no brief may tell an agent to stay out of the session log.\n- One turn per real request, per agent (resolution of 2026-10-13: \"Per request, per agent\"): each agent logs one turn for each real request it handles; a subagent's turn carries the parent turn's session and request ids (section 7.8), so the work is attributable without counting the request twice. Replace real turns canceled by a later open.\n- A background event with no work of its own (a completion notice) is not an agent: its hook-opened turn is failed as system_event_phantom, and the work it reports is logged by the agent that does it (resolution of 2026-10-13: \"Events aren't agents\").\n\n### 8.2 Decisions are conclusions\n\nIn the session log, decisions are conclusions and judgments with their consequences, not the steps taken. They go in the turn's design decisions, distinct from its actions. Every sync or wrap-up pass enumerates the real judgment calls of the turn and logs them. Never report \"0 decisions (folded into actions)\"; that phrasing is the failure mode itself.\n\nWhy: the operator has been trying to get agents to log session decisions reliably since February 2026. The pattern: the keystroke trail (actions) gets captured while the reasoning (design decisions) is under-recorded or buried inside action text. He pays for the reasoning, not just the steps. On 2026-06-29 the first sync pass of a 12-round review loop logged 0 decisions and called them \"folded into actions\", exactly the gap he has been fighting. He flagged it directly: \"Decisions are about your conclusions, not the actions you take\" and \"It feels like you have been deliberately avoiding session logging... especially details of decisions.\"\n\nHow to apply: on every sync or wrap-up, after appending actions, explicitly list the conclusions made this turn, each as judgment plus consequence plus affected requirements or files, and write them to design decisions, append-only (read existing first, send existing plus new, preserve order). A turn that made genuine judgment calls but logs zero decisions is a red flag; re-check before reporting. Heavy reads can time out and render existing decisions blank; that is a read artifact, not an empty section, so re-read before assuming you must recreate. Write the decision when it happens; do not reconstruct it later.\n\n### 8.3 Turn titles summarize the request\n\nSession-log turn titles are a concise summary of the operator's request: never the literal placeholder \"User prompt\", and not a verbatim first-line truncation of the prompt.\n\nWhy: the operator reviews the session log by turn title; \"User prompt\" and raw first lines make turns unscannable and indistinguishable.\n\nHow to apply: when opening, updating, or completing a turn, set the title to a short request summary. The plugin's prompt hook auto-titles from the prompt's first line and falls back to \"User prompt\" on empty prompts (Stop-hook continuation turns hit this); the hook cannot summarize, so the agent must refine the title. Never leave a turn titled \"User prompt\".\n\n## 9. Build, deploy, and release\n\n- Builds and tests run through Nuke, natively on Windows or Linux.\n- Deploys and releases are different things (resolution of 2026-10-13, verbatim): \"You are conflating deploys and releases.  Octopus manages releases, Nuke implements deployments.  Octopus calls Nuke during a release to perform a deployment.\"\n- Deploy lab services that ship Nuke targets through their Nuke deployment target only: never hand-copy binaries into service directories, and never use ad-hoc stop-service, copy, and start-service scripts as a substitute for the target. Manual edits of live configuration may be acceptable when they are the intended change and the Nuke restore and preserve rules keep them; binary deploys still go through Nuke.\n\n## 10. Lab facts\n\n- On LAB-OMARCHY, all source-code repositories and checkouts must be located under ~/github (/home/sharpninja/github).\n- On LAB-OMARCHY the 9 user-level MCP bridge hooks (claude-mcp-hook-bridge.ps1) were removed from ~/.claude/settings.json on 2026-10-09 because the enabled mcpserver@mcpserver-local plugin registers the same hooks, which opened twin turns per prompt. Do not run validate-claude-mcp-hooks.ps1 in repair mode unless CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT is set; without it the validator reports hooks missing and its installer would re-add the bridges.\n- Operator phone ntfy configuration (verified 2026-10-09T09:36 local).\n  Phone: motorola edge 2024, Android 16, on Wi-Fi BYRD3.1 at 192.168.0.138 (DHCP, may change).\n  ADB: wireless debugging paired with LAB-OMARCHY on 2026-10-09 (adb guid adb-ZD222QH58Q-DiWqEr). The connect port changes each wireless-debugging session (it was 41409); find it with: avahi-browse -rtp _adb-tls-connect._tcp (adb mdns is unsupported in android-tools 37). android-tools is installed on LAB-OMARCHY from Arch extra.\n  ntfy: app io.heckel.ntfy 1.25.2 from the Play Store, subscribed to one topic on server https://ntfy.sh. The topic name is the only access control on ntfy.sh, so its value is not stored in memory; it is in LAB-OMARCHY ~/.config/pi3-monitor/config.json (mode 0600, key Topic). Subscribe a device with the deep link ntfy://ntfy.sh/<topic> (adb shell am start -a android.intent.action.VIEW -d ...).\n  Send: curl -H \"Title: ...\" -H \"Priority: urgent|default\" -H \"Tags: rotating_light\" -d \"message\" https://ntfy.sh/<topic>; use --interface wlp4s0 so alerts bypass the Pi3 route.\n  Sender on LAB-OMARCHY: ~/.local/bin/pi3-monitor.ps1 run every minute by systemd user timer pi3-monitor.timer (linger enabled); test with pwsh -File ~/.local/bin/pi3-monitor.ps1 -TestMessage \"text\".\n- Lab network layout (MEMORY-LAB-003 v11, verbatim except that its list of project workload containers is left out; added 2026-10-13 from PAYTON-LEGION2, resolution \"Lab network facts\"). Lab layout, updated 2026-10-07. PAYTON-LEGION2 is the Windows workstation; its Windows computer name stays PAYTON-LEGION2. LAB-OMARCHY is the Omarchy Linux lab machine formerly called PAYTON-OMARCHY and PAYTON-DESKTOP (same machine; its own hostname still reports PAYTON-OMARCHY) [2026-10-13: its hostname now reports LAB-OMARCHY, verified from the OS]. Network shape verified 2026-10-07: a Raspberry Pi at 10.42.0.1 (MAC B8:27:EB:A3:86:47) is the lab router, DHCP server, and DNS server for 10.42.0.0/24, with upstream 192.168.0.x (gateway 192.168.0.1). LAB-OMARCHY is 10.42.0.214 (MAC 70:85:C2:84:C6:F7, enp5s0). PAYTON-LEGION2 Ethernet 3 is 10.42.0.143; its Wi-Fi is 192.168.0.229. The Asus RT-AC68U WAN is 10.42.0.11 (MAC D4:5D:64:EA:1C:D8, OpenVPN UDP 1194); its LAN 192.168.1.0/24 is behind Asus NAT. The address 192.168.1.182 is retired; addresses are DHCP leases. Pi DNS resolves LAB-OMARCHY and PAYTON-LEGION2. The Pi DHCP hands out DNS 10.42.0.1 (an earlier 192.168.1.1 was a manual setting on PAYTON-LEGION2, changed to 10.42.0.1 by 2026-10-07 09:57, when the stale hosts entry was also removed). Since 2026-10-07 10:20 the Pi dnsmasq serves DNS domain lab (/etc/NetworkManager/dnsmasq-shared.d/lab-domain.conf: domain=lab, local=/lab/, domain-search lab): LAB-OMARCHY.lab, PAYTON-LEGION2.lab, asus-rt-ac68u.lab resolve, and DHCP hands out suffix lab. PAYTON-LEGION2 Ethernet 3 has suffix lab (renewed 10:21), so the bare name LAB-OMARCHY resolves to 10.42.0.214. ~/.ssh/config on PAYTON-LEGION2 uses HostName LAB-OMARCHY. (changed 10:10:41 by another editor). Operator directive 2026-10-05: connections from PAYTON-LEGION2 to LAB-OMARCHY route over Ethernet, not Wi-Fi (10.42.0.0/24 is on-link on Ethernet 3). LAB-OMARCHY SSH host key: ssh-ed25519 8:6c:c5:9d:3c:10:50:57:db:6b:74:6:f5:2e:d1:8f (matched at both addresses). Kernel Linux 7.2.5-3-omarchy. Docker 29.7.2; sharpninja is in the docker group (gid 966) and docker ps runs without sudo. PAYTON-LEGION2 accepts RDP with NLA on 3389; the RDP target from the operator phone in this shape is 10.42.0.143. DHCP reservations added 2026-10-07 in /etc/NetworkManager/dnsmasq-shared.d/lab-reservations.conf on the Pi: a0:ce:c8:77:4d:4c -> 10.42.0.143 (PAYTON-LEGION2), 70:85:c2:84:c6:f7 -> 10.42.0.214 (LAB-OMARCHY), d4:5d:64:ea:1c:d8 -> 10.42.0.11 (asus-rt-ac68u, added 10:15); applied by nmcli con up lab-uplink (dnsmasq restarted). dnsmasq reads every file in that directory, so keep backups in /var/backups, never beside the conf (a duplicate dhcp-host stops dnsmasq from starting). Pi: hostname pi-router, Debian 13, NetworkManager shared eth0 (lab-uplink), uplink wlan0 (byrd3-uplink, 192.168.0.189). Pi access is pi@10.42.0.1 with SSH key /home/sharpninja/.ssh/pi3-bridge-ed25519 on LAB-OMARCHY (details in ~/.creds/lab-pi.yaml there), passwordless sudo. Phone path verified 2026-10-07 10:20: the Moto edge 2024 on home Wi-Fi (192.168.0.138) reaches PAYTON-LEGION2 RDP at 192.168.0.229:3389 but nothing on 10.42.0.0/24, so the Asus VPN at 10.42.0.11 is unreachable from 192.168.0.x. The phone is paired for wireless adb with PAYTON-LEGION2; start adb there with ADB_MDNS_OPENSCREEN=0 (openscreen mDNS hangs adb). Tailscale (2026-10-07): tailnet sharpninja.github (login sharpninja@github, Free plan, MagicDNS domain tail1b11e3.ts.net). pi-router runs Tailscale 1.102.5 as a subnet router for 10.42.0.0/24 (Tailscale IP 100.88.8.38, --accept-dns=false, route approved, key expiry disabled). /etc/NetworkManager/dispatcher.d/50-lab-tailscale-forward re-inserts iifname tailscale0 oifname eth0 accept into nm-shared-eth0 on every up event; /etc/sysctl.d/99-tailscale.conf keeps IPv4 forwarding on. The phone (motorola-edge-2024, 100.81.56.5) reaches 10.42.0.143 ping and TCP 3389 through the tailnet (verified 11:13). Split DNS is configured (Tailscale DNS nameserver 10.42.0.1 restricted to domain lab, added 11:45); the phone resolves PAYTON-LEGION2.lab, LAB-OMARCHY.lab, asus-rt-ac68u.lab and reaches PAYTON-LEGION2.lab:3389 (verified 11:50). Android caches failed lookups per network; recon