{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_37","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -Command \"pwsh.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -File './docs/receipts/hv/hv-r1-inspect.ps1' -Task Read -Path './docs/architecture/SessionTurn-Review-AgentContext-Architecture-Amendment-1-Durable-HV.md' -Count 230\"","aggregated_output":"SOURCE ./docs/architecture/SessionTurn-Review-AgentContext-Architecture-Amendment-1-Durable-HV.md totalLines=2785\n1: <!-- SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 -->\n2: # Architecture Amendment 1: Durable Multi-Agent Hostile Validation\n3: \n4: Status: proposed amendment, documentation only. It is not approved, not hostile-validated, and not an authorization to implement. It amends the foundation architecture `docs/architecture/SessionTurn-Review-AgentContext-Architecture.md` (the \"foundation\"; at develop `36783b79` it is byte-identical to the copy in the `session-20261009` worktree, SHA-256 `6E5D4F418E78CF9A76AB366F64D4F24002C09E3C3F024502ED89D77C6E77295E`). The plan derived from this amendment is the TODO `PLAN-HVPIPELINE-001`; the problems found while writing it are the TODO `REMED-HVPIPELINE-001`, which that plan pulls in (operator, 2026-10-16, verbatim: \"keep track of problems to fix as a single todo item that will be pulled into the plan.\").\n5: \n6: Operator directive for this document (verbatim): \"Create an amendment to the architecture document with the same level of rigor for diagrams, interfaces, AC, UC, and descriptive documentation.\"\n7: \n8: How to read this amendment:\n9: \n10: - It follows the foundation's conventions (foundation section 3) unless a clause below amends one. Its diagrams use the foundation's notation (foundation, \"How to read the diagrams\"): activity diagrams are Mermaid flowcharts read top to bottom, a double-bordered node (`[[\"...\"]]`) is a call to a shared component, policy, or process drawn once where it is defined, every error exit ends in a terminal that names its code, and sequence diagrams list every response a caller can receive in `alt` blocks.\n11: - A unit of work is exactly one CQRS command or query with its handler (operator, 2026-10-11: \"I want to be clear, unit of work maps to one CQRS handler\"). A policy is a rule a handler applies. A process is orchestration that calls handlers (the worker host, the reviewer run, plugin verbs, Director commands).\n12: - Section numbers of this amendment are its own (1 to 22, then Addendum B). \"Foundation section X\" always names the foundation document.\n13: - Every statement is marked by its origin: a settled operator requirement is quoted verbatim with its source (section 3); a design resolution is labelled R-n and states its rationale and the alternatives it rejects (section 5); an amended foundation clause is labelled A-n (section 6). Anything else is derived from those three and cites them.\n14: - Requirement, test, and use-case identifiers in this document are proposed. The 63 requirement records of section 19 (FR, TR, and TEST `-MCP-HVPIPE-001` to `-021`) and their 21 mappings were created in the canonical store on 2026-10-16 between 16:32:30 and 16:37:30 local, status `pending`, so the requirements are captured as they emerge; the use cases are created in H0 (section 19).\n15: \n16: ## 1. Scope, relationship to the foundation, and precedence\n17: \n18: - Scope: the durable, multi-agent hostile validation (HV) pipeline the foundation deferred (foundation section 3.1, \"Model grade\": \"We are going to add an HV pipeline in the future, but not in this effort.\"). The pipeline admits HV requests over session-log turns, schedules them across a pool of five reviewer agents, runs each review as a durable, leased, live-streamed execution, keeps complete compressed evidence, and finalizes per-turn and holistic results in one transaction.\n19: - Relationship: the foundation stays the authority for turn logging (Phase A), turn reviews (Phase B), and stateless agent context (Phase C). This amendment adds one bounded context, HostileValidation, and amends the foundation clauses listed in section 6, which are exactly the clauses that assume a manual HV with one configured grader.\n20: - Precedence: where section 6 names a foundation clause as amended, the amendment's text governs; every other foundation clause stands unchanged. Because the foundation's plan (PLAN-SESSIONTURNREVIEW-001) was approved on 2026-10-10, the amended clauses are a change to that plan, and the foundation's own rule applies to them: \"a change to this document after the plan is approved is a plan change that needs a new hostile validation\" (foundation, introduction).\n21: - Implementation status of the foundation (verified 2026-10-16 by a search of `src` at `36783b79`): of the foundation's new types only `IDispatcher` exists (`src/QBrainAi.Cqrs/IDispatcher.cs:9`); `SerializableWrite`, `IdempotentCreate`, `ReadableKeyAllocator`, `TurnLocator`, `ScoreLexeme`, `ScoreVerdict`, `ClassifiedErrorException`, `WorkspaceResolutionBehavior`, `RecordSessionTurnReviewCommand`, `CreateAgentContextHvRequestCommand`, and `RecordModelGradeCommand` are architecture only. Every amendment of section 6 is therefore a document change; no migration of an existing table or route is involved.\n22: - Authorization: the operator authorized documentation and plan persistence for this work, not implementation. The standing code freeze applies (no new code until the operator approves the plans).\n23: \n24: ## 2. Goals and non-goals\n25: \n26: Goals:\n27: \n28: 1. A durable HV queue whose admission, scheduling, ownership, and finalization survive server restarts, worker crashes, and concurrent workers without losing or duplicating a review result.\n29: 2. A reviewer pool of five agents (Cline, Copilot, Claude, Codex, Grok), selected round-robin among the ready reviewers, never the submitter, the agent that wrote the reviewed turns, or an alias of either.\n30: 3. Complete evidence: the complete accepted request and the complete response log of every review, compressed at rest with GZIP, verified lossless, retained until explicitly removed, and streamed live while the review runs.\n31: 4. Per-turn assessments and a separate holistic evaluation for every request, committed atomically with the turns' hostile reviews; reviewed turns and final results are immutable.\n32: 5. Independence: an agent's self-review never affects HV admission, execution, or verdict, and an HV verdict never completes a TODO.\n33: \n34: Non-goals (each settled by the operator, section 3): access roles; a force re-review flag; automatic TODO state changes; automatic retention expiry; execution time limits; a second HV verdict store beside this one for turn reviews.\n35: \n36: ## 3. Settled requirements (verbatim sources)\n37: \n38: Every requirement below was stated by the operator. Sources: the Cline session `/home/sharpninja/.cline/data/sessions/1792165626722_56f98/1792165626722_56f98.messages.json` (message index in brackets, every operator message read in full on 2026-10-16), the operator's messages in the Claude Code session that wrote this amendment, and the operator profile's standing HV rules. The handoff `docs/handoffs/hv-architecture-amendment-handoff-20261016T182551Z.md` (main checkout, untracked) summarized 22 of them; this register carries all of them.\n39: \n40: Operator decisions of the HV planning conversation (2026-10-16):\n41: \n42: - S-01 [24] \"We need to plan a Hostile Validation queue and execution mechanism.\"\n43: - S-02 [51] \"HV queue needs configuration for available HV agents (cline, copilot, claude, codex, grok) and either the agent's default (highest effort) model, or a model/effort specified in configuration.  The HV agent configuration must have endpoints and REPL coverage so Director and other tools can manage in real time.\"\n44: - S-03 [68] \"When a HV request is received, it must submit a range of session log turns to include in the review.  The reviewer must grade each turn individualy and apply their review score and update the turn record.  The result will include the score for each turn, and an overall score for the whole HV request.  HV request/results will be store in the database.\"\n45: - S-04 [76] \"worktree session-20261009 should have updates to the session log schema for storing both agent self-review and hv review outcome on the turn.\" (Satisfied by foundation Phase B: `agentReview` and `hostileReview` on the turn.)\n46: - S-05 [96] \"That document is the base we are building on in this plan.\" (The foundation.)\n47: - S-06 [104] \"Review of a turn should be considered final as any subsequent changes should be reflected in a later turn.\"\n48: - S-07 [112] \"Session log may not allow updates to a turn with an HV review.\"\n49: - S-08 [114] \"A turn must have an agent review before it can be included in a HV.\" Superseded by S-28.\n50: - S-09 [118] \"Turns submitted, but marked Unnecessary, should still be included in the overall HV grade for the request.\"\n51: - S-10 [120] \"No, overall HV is a holistic review of the whole set of turns submitted.\" (Answer to a proposed mean-plus-floor overall score.)\n52: - S-11 [122] \"If the request recieves a session id (not a session turn id) in the set of turns, then the totality of session turns in that session are included by default.\"\n53: - S-12 [124] \"plugin core should present all missing agent reviews as a list for the agent to perform review on.\" (Scoped by S-28; resolution R-21.)\n54: - S-13 [128] \"HV reviews must use a standard json payload for responses that must include the complete request, the complete jsonl response log, and delineated list of specific failures with adequest description to remediate.\"\n55: - S-14 [130] \"HV request must supply a free text field for agent to add specific context in markdown or yaml format.  The request should be parsed for MCP Server FR/TR/AC/UC in the workspace and provided to the reviewing agent in a delineated list.\"\n56: - S-15 [132] \"execution failures are to be triaged (prevent duplicates) and mark the HV agent as offline until resolved.\"\n57: - S-16 [134] \"If there are no HV agents available to perform a review, the endpoint must refuse the request and the plugin core add the necessary context.\"\n58: - S-17 [136] \"HV agent resolution should round-robin HV review agents, excluding the agent submitting the review.\"\n59: - S-18 [138] \"need endpoints for getting running hvs, their last reported output, and eta.  Need endpoints for cancelling any hv.  need endpoint to attach to sse strem of the output of any running hv\"\n60: - S-19 [140] \"need an HV notification stream endpoint to receive notifications of hv completion in the plugins\"\n61: - S-20 [144] (Force re-review flag?) \"NO\"\n62: - S-21 [146] \"Per turn should be a list of the requested turns to review with their score.\"\n63: - S-22 [148] \"Output should be captured live and streamed.\" Confirmed by the operator on 2026-10-16T14:14 local after the finding that develop has no true live streaming (problem P8, section 21): \"Explains a lot.  Gotta be fixed in the plan.\"\n64: - S-23 [150] \"Single trx at end.\"\n65: - S-24 [152] (Auto-start runners on submit or a manual trigger?) \"Auto-start\"\n66: - S-25 [154] (AGREE auto-moves the TODO, or verdict only?) \"Verdict only\"\n67: - S-26 [156] (Incident store?) \"File through endpoint.\"\n68: - S-27 [158] (Offline to online?) \"NONCE the agent.\" and [229] \"This is just a check that the agent is available and can execute an HV.\"\n69: - S-28 [214] \"Self-review of a turn is just a grade on the turn.  It is not relevant to the HV.\"\n70: - S-29 [239] \"Must participate in the audit log of the database.\" and [467] \"Retain until told to remove.  There are no access roles.  Audit-failure is a hard stop.\"\n71: - S-30 [474] \"GZIP HV result logs to save space.\"\n72: - S-31 [0] \"create worktree from develop\" and [463] \"I've already specified both.\" (The implementation worktree is cut from `develop`; the foundation is the base.)\n73: - S-32 [469] \"That's your job.\" (Verifying task and requirement links and persisting the plan are agent work.) and [478] \"write plan to high todo\"\n74: - S-33 [162] \"apply byrd dev proces v4\"\n75: \n76: Standing operator rules that govern every HV (operator profile, sections 7.1 to 7.4):\n77: \n78: - P-01 \"HV must not be executed by the same agent family as the requesting agent.  If not specified by operator, ask what agent and model to use for HV in a session.\" (2026-10-13)\n79: - P-02 \"Do not bound review time\" (2026-10-12): no timeout, kill, deadline, or wait limit on a reviewer run.\n80: - P-03 Every HV response log carries progress entries with a numeric completion percentage and an estimated time remaining, emitted during the run (MEMORY-PREFERENCE-003); the estimate is the reviewer's own report and never a bound on review time.\n81: - P-04 Verify the reviewer's model and effort from its own runtime receipts.\n82: - P-05 98/98: a review passes only when accuracy (correctness) and completeness are both at least 0.98; the bar is never lowered.\n83: - P-06 Every reviewer creates a complete session-log turn for its review and stores its entire result there.\n84: - P-07 \"Validate independently through a hostile reviewer wherever the plan calls for HV and before any done state. Self-check alone is not validation.\" (locked 2026-08-12)\n85: \n86: Statements read and not carried as requirements: S-08 (superseded by S-28, the later answer to the same question) and the drafting agent's own proposals in its plan versions (messages 103, 161, 165, 213, 462), which are inputs to the resolutions of section 5, never settled requirements. Where a proposal conflicts with an operator statement, the operator statement governs: draft 161 allowed a later HV of an already reviewed turn (\"new grade rows supersede\"), which S-06, S-07, and S-20 forbid.\n87: \n88: ## 4. System context and component ownership\n89: \n90: ```mermaid\n91: flowchart LR\n92:     subgraph Callers[\"Callers\"]\n93:         PL[\"Plugins: PowerShell core verbs and the notification watcher\"]\n94:         RP[\"qbrain-ai-repl: workflow.hv verbs\"]\n95:         DR[\"Director: HV commands\"]\n96:         MC[\"MCP clients: hv tools\"]\n97:     end\n98:     subgraph Server[\"QBrainAi.Support.Mcp\"]\n99:         API[\"HostileValidationController and hv MCP tools\"]\n100:         PIPE[\"IDispatcher pipeline: WorkspaceResolutionBehavior\"]\n101:         HV[\"HostileValidation handlers: reviewers, admission, queries, cancel, evidence\"]\n102:         WK[\"HvWorkerHost: claim, run, heartbeat, output, close, fail, finalize, sweep\"]\n103:         AD[\"Reviewer adapters: Cline, Copilot, Claude, Codex, Grok\"]\n104:         FIN[\"FinalizeHvRequestCommand\"]\n105:         TR[\"Foundation units reused: TurnWritePipeline, HvVerdictMapping, RecordModelGradeCommand, DecideAgentResponseTrustCommand\"]\n106:         TRG[\"ITriageService: POST triage reports\"]\n107:         BUS[\"IChangeEventBus: wake-ups only\"]\n108:     end\n109:     REV[\"Reviewer CLI process, with its own plugin and session-log turn\"]\n110:     DB[(\"Relational store: HV tables, GZIP evidence, notification outbox, DataAuditLogs\")]\n111:     PL --> RP\n112:     RP --> API\n113:     DR --> API\n114:     MC --> API\n115:     API --> PIPE\n116:     PIPE --> HV\n117:     PIPE --> FIN\n118:     HV --> DB\n119:     WK --> PIPE\n120:     WK --> AD\n121:     AD --> REV\n122:     REV -. \"live output lines\" .-> AD\n123:     FIN --> TR\n124:     TR --> DB\n125:     FIN --> DB\n126:     WK --> TRG\n127:     HV -. \"wake\" .-> BUS\n128:     BUS -. \"wake\" .-> WK\n129: ```\n130: \n131: Ownership: the HostileValidation bounded context owns every unit, policy, entity, and process of this amendment. It calls the foundation's SessionTurnLogging (TurnLocator, TurnWritePipeline), TurnReviews (ScoreLexeme, ScoreVerdict, policy HvVerdictMapping), ResponsesAndTrust (RecordModelGradeCommand, DecideAgentResponseTrustCommand), and SharedComponents (SerializableWrite, IdempotentCreate, ReadableKeyAllocator, PagedQuery, ClassifiedErrorException, ContentHasher, SecretRedactor) through their Phase 2 seams, and the existing `ITriageService` (POST `/qbrainai/triage/reports`, `src/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp/Controllers/TriageController.cs:13-14,33`). Namespaces follow the foundation rule (foundation section 3.5): commands in `src/QBrainAi.Services/HostileValidation/Commands/` (namespace `QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.HostileValidation.Commands`), queries in `.../Queries/`, policies in `.../Policies/`, the worker in `.../Execution/`, adapters in `.../Reviewers/`, entities in `src/QBrainAi.Storage/Entities/HostileValidation/` (namespace `QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.Storage.Entities.HostileValidation`), DTOs in `src/QBrainAi.Client/Models/HostileValidation/` (namespace `QBrainAi.Client.Models.HostileValidation`), one type per file.\n132: \n133: Trust boundaries:\n134: \n135: - Callers authenticate with the workspace API key, as every route does; there are no access roles (S-29). Agent identity is therefore not authenticated by the server: the submitter's identity is the agent of the submitting turn the server finds (R-08), and the limit of that model is stated in section 8.\n136: - The reviewer CLI is untrusted input to the server. Its output is evidence to verify, never an instruction; the server parses only the records the reviewer contract defines (section 13.3) and verifies them before anything is finalized (policy HvFinalVerification, section 15).\n137: - Turn text sent to a reviewer passes through `SecretRedactor` (foundation section 3.5.2) before it leaves the server; the stored request evidence is the redacted brief exactly as sent.\n138: \n139: ## 5. Design decisions and resolutions\n140: \n141: Each resolution names the settled requirements it implements, its rationale, and the alternatives it rejects. Resolutions are proposals for the operator's approval; the settled requirements of section 3 are not reopened.\n142: \n143: - R-01 One bounded context. HostileValidation owns the pipeline and is not gated by `Mcp:AgentContext:Mode`: HV of turns works in every Mode, because the operator's HV requirements (S-01 to S-30) name turns, not agent-context responses. Rejected: extending the foundation's `AgentContextHvRequest` in ResponsesAndTrust (draft 103), because that record is mode-gated (foundation section 3.5.1) and lists responses, not turns, so a turn with no response would silently drop out of review (draft 213, \"turn coverage versus response coverage\").\n144: - R-02 The HV request replaces the foundation's agent-context HV request. `HvRequest` keeps the foundation's key format `hvr-<sessionId>-<yyyyMMddTHHmmssZ>` (with `-<nn>` from the second key of one second, ReadableKeyAllocator) and lists turns (`HvRequestTurn`); the agent responses of those turns are linked as `HvRequestResponse` items when Phase C has recorded any. CreateAgentContextHvRequestCommand, GetAgentContextHvRequestQuery, QueryAgentContextHvRequestsQuery, and their entities are superseded (amendment A-04). Rejected: keeping two HV request records (one per turn, one per response), which would need a cross-record consistency rule the foundation never needed.\n145: - R-03 Model grades come from finalization only. FinalizeHvRequestCommand dispatches the foundation's RecordModelGradeCommand for each linked response inside its own transaction; the public model-grade route, the two-step `codex exec resume` protocol, and `ModelGrader:*` are removed, and the eight receipt checks become the verification steps V-1 to V-8 of policy HvFinalVerification against evidence the server itself captured (amendment A-05). Rationale: the pipeline launches the reviewer and records its complete output, so the server no longer has to trust receipts written by a runner it did not control. Rejected: keeping the reviewer-submitted grade route beside the pipeline, which would let a grade bypass reviewer exclusion and atomic finalization (S-17, S-23).\n146: - R-04 Freeze at admission, finality at finalization (S-06, S-07). Admission sets each member turn's HV state to `Pending` in the same transaction that creates the request; finalization sets it to `Final`. Every write to a `Pending` or `Final` turn or its children, from every surface and origin, is refused with 409 `turn_immutable` and `details.reason` `hv_pending` or `hv_final` (policy HvTurnFreeze), except the one write of finalization itself (origin `HvFinalization`). A request that ends `Canceled` or `Failed` releases its turns back to `None`. Rationale: the reviewed content equals the finalized content without a content comparison race, and the operator's \"may not allow updates\" is one guard in the existing write path. Rejected: letting writes continue and refusing finalization on a changed snapshot hash, which would waste completed reviews and still leave a window between the last check and the commit.\n147: - R-05 Only terminal turns are admitted. A member turn that is not terminal (`TurnStatus.IsTerminal`, foundation section 3.5.3) refuses the whole request with 409 `hv_turns_not_terminal`, naming each turn. The submitting turn is never a member: a session scope excludes it and reports the exclusion in the result, and a turn or range scope that names it is refused with 400 `hv_scope_includes_submitting_turn` (the foundation's rule that \"the request's own turn is never a candidate, a dependency, or cited evidence\", foundation section 3.1). A Code or Docs turn that needs HV before it can complete is completed first with `hostileReview` `Other` (for example \"HV requested hvr-...\"), which the foundation already allows and never counts as an AGREE (foundation C8); finalization then replaces that `Other` with the HV result. Rationale: no turn changes status after it is frozen, so there is no close-inside-finalization path. Rejected: admitting in-progress turns with a declared close intent (draft 213, section 3.B), which adds a mechanism and still freezes a turn the model is working in.\n148: - R-06 Hostile reviews are written only by the pipeline. On agent surfaces a `hostileReview` write may carry only `status` `Other` (with its required `otherNotes`); a write naming `Pass`, `Fail`, or `Running`, or carrying `hvVerdict`, is refused with 403 `review_hostile_pipeline_only` (amendment A-06). Process ConvertHvReceiptToReview is retired. Rationale: a manual Pass or Fail would bypass reviewer exclusion, complete evidence, and atomic finalization, and no surface may bypass them (draft 213, H5, adopted). Rejected: keeping the manual route for emergencies; a reviewer can be enabled and checked instead (S-27).\n149: - R-07 Assignment at admission, durable round-robin. The submit transaction picks the next eligible reviewer after the workspace's round-robin cursor and advances the cursor in the same SerializableWrite (policy RoundRobinAssignment), so assignment survives restarts and two concurrent submissions never pick from the same cursor value. When the assigned reviewer is no longer eligible at claim time (offline, disabled), the claim reassigns through the same policy and records the reassignment. Rejected: assignment at claim only, which hides the assignee from the submitter and lets claim order, not round-robin order, pick reviewers.\n150: - R-08 One agent identity, server-attributed. `AgentIdentity` (namespace `QBrainAi.Client.Agents`, shared by the server, the REPL, and, through the golden contract, the PowerShell core) maps every known alias to one of the five reviewer ids: Claude (`claude`, `claudecode`, `claude-code`, `claudecowork`, `claude-cowork`, `cowork`), Codex (`codex`), Copilot (`copilot`), Grok (`grok`, `grokcode`, `grok-code`, `grokbot`, `grok-bot`), Cline (`cline`, `cline-v2`, `clinev2`), case-insensitively and ignoring separators. The submitter is the agent of the submitting turn, located by its full turn key (TurnLocator); a free-text name never overrides it. The author is the agent of the reviewed session (one request names turns of one MCP session, R-26), stored normalized as `AuthorReviewerId`. A reviewer whose id equals the submitter's or the author's is never eligible (S-17, S-28, P-01, P-07). Develop holds three alias maps with different canonical forms (`src/QBrainAi.Repl.Host/MarkerFileClientOptionsResolver.cs:64-81`, `src/QBrainAi.Repl.Core/TodoSelectionStore.cs:166-171`, the plugin's `Get-ReplCanonicalAgentName`); they are context under the DRY ruling and are not changed by this amendment. Exclusion is by agent family, not by model vendor (D-01, settled): S-17 names \"the agent submitting the review\", P-01 constrains the agent family and asks separately for the agent and the model, and the operator runs Cline on `gpt-6-astra` (provider `openai-codex`, VI-01 receipt) while keeping Cline and Codex as two reviewers (S-02); a model a reviewer shares with the submitter stays visible in the run's provenance, and the operator can disable that reviewer (UpdateHvReviewerCommand). Exclusion covers the author as well as the submitter (D-05, settled): a review by the agent that wrote the turns is a self-review, which S-28 rules out of HV and P-07 rules out of validation.\n151: - R-09 Operator submissions. A caller with no session-log turn (Director, an operator at the REPL) submits with `submitter.kind` `Operator` and a name; no reviewer is excluded for the submitter, and the author of the reviewed session is still excluded (R-08). Agent surfaces (plugin verbs, MCP tools, the REPL workflow verb) always send the submitting turn's key, which the plugin binds automatically (foundation process BindVerbToTurn). Limit (section 8): every caller holds the same workspace key, so the server cannot prove that an `Operator` submission did not come from an agent.\n152: - R-10 Readiness through NONCE (S-15, S-27). A reviewer is `Unverified`, `Online`, or `Offline`, and separately enabled or disabled. \"NONCE the agent\" is a probe run of the reviewer through its adapter with a fresh random nonce: it passes when the reviewer answers with the probe record that echoes the nonce and the adapter's runtime provenance shows the reviewer's resolved model and effort (P-04). Only a passing NONCE moves a reviewer to `Online`. An execution failure moves it to `Offline` until a later NONCE passes. The worker host NONCEs every enabled `Unverified` reviewer at startup and after a configuration change; an `Offline` reviewer is NONCEd only when a caller asks (S-15: \"until resolved\"). Rejected: a liveness ping or the server health nonce, which S-27 rules out.\n153: - R-11 No execution time limit (P-02). A run has no timeout and no deadline. Liveness is the worker's lease, renewed by heartbeats; a stalled run stays visible (`lastOutputUtc`, the last progress record) and anyone can cancel it (S-18). The only expiry is the lease of a dead worker (R-13). This replaces the 30-minute `ExecutionTimeout` of the separate HostileReview options (`src/QBrainAi.Services/Options/HostileReviewWorkerOptions.cs:45-46`) for HV runs only. The process exit code is never the outcome of a run: the reviewer's records decide it (V-8 for a review, policy ReadinessProbeVerdict for a probe), because a CLI can end a refused or empty run with exit code 0 (VI-03: a headless Grok run whose write call was refused ended `cancelled` with exit 0, and two Copilot permission variants exited 0 with no output).\n154: - R-12 True live output (S-22, P8). Each reviewer adapter delivers every output line as the process writes it, through a line callback, never after the process ends; the worker appends lines in batches (at most `OutputFlushInterval`, default 250 ms, or `OutputFlushBytes`, default 64 KiB, whichever comes first) through AppendHvRunOutputCommand, which stores each batch as one GZIP chunk and wakes the streams. The output stream replays stored chunks and then follows new ones (shared component OrderedEventReplay). Develop's one-shot strategy yields its output only after completion (`src/QBrainAi.Services/Services/OneShotCliAgentExecutionStrategy.cs:57-75`), so the adapters need a real line path (section 21, P8). Acceptance criterion FR-MCP-HVPIPE-008-AC003 makes the difference testable: a line is observable on the stream while the reviewer process is still running. The reviewer's records are read from each agent message's complete text, assembled from the CLI's native events (Grok's `streaming-json` sends message text one token per event, and Copilot sends deltas and then the whole message, as the probe runs of 2026-10-16 show), by one extraction function that the worker runs as lines arrive and finalization runs over the stored log (section 16.3), so a record split across events is read exactly once and both read the same records (FR-MCP-HVPIPE-010-AC006).\n155: - R-13 Leases and fences (handoff requirement 10). A run is owned through a lease (owner, expiry) and a fence token that increases on every claim and takeover, the pattern develop already uses for handoff ingestion (owner and expiry with a `StateVersion` takeover, `src/QBrainAi.Services/Services/HandoffIngestionService.cs:724-735`, and a fenced terminal write that matches owner and version, `:782-807`). Every worker write carries the fence; a write with a stale fence changes nothing and returns `stale_fence`, and the worker that receives it stops its process. Expired leases are swept (ExpireHvRunLeasesCommand): the attempt becomes `Abandoned` and the request is queued again until `MaxAttemptsPerRequest` (default 3) is spent, then `Failed`, or `Canceled` when a cancel was requested; policy HvRetryDecision writes that outcome, with its turn release and its notification, for the sweep exactly as for FailHvRunCommand. Rejected: the unused lease options of the HostileReview subsystem (`HostileReviewWorkerOptions.cs:30-46`), which no code reads (section 21, P1).\n156: - R-14 Evidence in the relational store, GZIP at rest (S-13, S-29, S-30). The accepted request is one GZIP blob written at admission; the response log is the run's ordered GZIP chunks; the result document is one GZIP blob written at finalization. No uncompressed copy of an evidence item is stored in the evidence tables, and each evidence item is stored there once (the structured rows of section 12, such as the request's `Context` and the findings, are records the units derive, not copies of the evidence blobs). The audit ledger keeps its own history of every evidence write, as it does for every audited entity: develop's ledger snapshots every property of an audited entity, binary columns included (`src/QBrainAi.Storage/McpDbContext.cs:1540-1589`), compressed with GZIP (`src/QBrainAi.Storage/Entities/DataAuditLogEntity.cs:119-147`), and a removal never changes it (R-20, I-13). Retrieval returns `application/gzip` (download name `<hvRequestId>-<kind>.jsonl.gz` or `.json.gz`) or, when asked, the decompressed bytes. The pattern is develop's audit ledger, which stores GZIP payloads in the database (`src/QBrainAi.Storage/Entities/DataAuditLogEntity.cs:66-147`). Rationale: S-23 (\"Single trx at end\") and S-29 (the database audit log) both hold only when the evidence is in the same transactional store as the result, and no crash window between a file system and the database remains. Rejected: `.jsonl.gz` files beside the database, which would need a two-store protocol, keep evidence outside the audit ledger, and split backups.\n157: - R-15 Atomic finalization (S-23). FinalizeHvRequestCommand verifies the evidence and the reviewer output before it opens a transaction, then commits in one SerializableWrite: the result document, the per-turn assessments, the holistic evaluation, the failures, each turn's `hostileReview` through policy HvVerdictMapping (origin `HvFinalization`), each turn's `Final` HV state, the linked responses' model grades and trust decisions (when Mode is not `Off`), the request's `Completed` state, its notification, and the audit rows. The transaction re-checks everything the earlier reads relied on (the run's fence, the chunk manifest, the request and turn states). Crash windows are in section 9.\n158: - R-16 Transactional notification outbox (S-19). Every state change that callers observe writes an `HvNotification` row in its own transaction, numbered by a per-workspace sequence that the same transaction advances, so the sequence has no gaps and no row is visible before its change commits. The notification stream replays rows after the caller's `Last-Event-ID` and then follows new ones (OrderedEventReplay). Delivery is at least once; consumers deduplicate by sequence. `IChangeEventBus` (develop's in-memory bus, `src/QBrainAi.Services/Notifications/IChangeEventBus.cs:4`) is used only to wake waiting streams and the worker, never as the record.\n159: - R-17 Incidents through the triage endpoint (S-15, S-26). An execution failure writes an `HvIncident` row in the failure's transaction; FileHvIncidentCommand then submits it through `ITriageService` with its `dedupeKey` (`hv-reviewer-<reviewerId>-<failureCode>` for a failure attributable to the reviewer, `hv-server-<failureCode>` otherwise, section 12), which triage groups as `dedupe:<key>` (`src/QBrainAi.Services/Services/TriageService.cs:1475-1476`), and records the returned report and group ids. A filing that fails stays `Pending` and is retried by the worker host's sweep. Rejected: new incident tables with their own deduplication (S-26: \"File through endpoint\").\n160: - R-18 Honest ETA (S-18, P-03). The ETA a caller sees is the reviewer's own latest progress record (`percentComplete`, `etaSeconds`), with `etaStatus` `Reported` and the record's time; with no progress record it is `etaStatus` `Unknown` and no number. The server never estimates.\n161: - R-19 Audit participation and hard stop (S-29). Every HV entity carries `IsDeleted`, so `McpDbContext` writes its ledger rows for every HV mutation in the same `SaveChanges` (develop audits only entities with that property, `src/QBrainAi.Storage/McpDbContext.cs:1620-1627`). The ledger is append-only for HV: no HV unit or process updates or deletes a ledger row (I-13). Because develop skips the ledger when the audit table is unavailable (`McpDbContext.cs:1507-1538`), every HV unit first calls the shared component AuditLedgerGuard, which refuses with 503 `audit_unavailable` and writes nothing when the ledger cannot be written. Evidence reads (GetHvEvidenceQuery, GetHvResultQuery, and the output stream) write an explicit `read` ledger row before returning bytes, and a read whose row cannot be written is refused (the \"protected-log access\" of draft 213 participates in the audit log, S-29).\n162: - R-20 Retention until removal (S-29). No unit deletes, expires, or compacts HV evidence except RemoveHvEvidenceCommand, an explicit call that removes the evidence bytes of one request, keeps its result rows, hashes, and sizes, records who removed it and why, and is audited. It clears the evidence tables only and never changes or deletes an audit ledger row: the ledger keeps the history of the evidence's writes and gains the record of the removal, because an audit log is never purged (I-13). There are no roles: any caller with the workspace key can call it, as S-29 requires.\n163: - R-21 Self-review independence (S-28). No HV unit, policy, or process reads `agentReview`. The turn snapshot sent to a reviewer omits both review records (shared component TurnSnapshotBuilder), so a reviewer never sees a self-review grade. S-12 (a plugin list of missing agent reviews) is met by the foundation's Stop-gate remedy, which already lists each open turn's missing `agentReview` (foundation section 3.4, Stop gate, and `Get-CloseRemedy`, section 3.5.6), and is not an HV input. Reconciliation with the foundation's completion gate: HV admits only terminal turns (R-05), and the foundation's gate needs an `agentReview` status of `Pass`, `Fail`, or `Other` before a turn can close `completed` (foundation section 5.3, CompletionPolicy); that is a rule of closing a turn, not of HV, it is met by `Other` with a note as well as by a grade, and a turn closed `failed` or `canceled` needs no agent review at all; HV admission, execution, and verdict read none of it, and FR-MCP-HVPIPE-013 proves that every terminal turn is admitted and judged identically whatever its self-review.\n164: - R-22 Verdict only (S-25). No HV unit writes TODO state. A result names the reviewed turns' `todoId` values for information only.\n165: - R-23 Requirement references (S-14). The submitted context is stored as written (Markdown, YAML, or text, declared by `contextFormat`) and parsed for requirement references: FR, TR, and TEST ids of the form `<KIND>-<AREA>-<NNN>` with optional further dash-separated area segments, acceptance criteria written as `<FR id>-AC<NNN>` or `<FR id> ac-<n>`, and use cases by their store id or exact title. Each reference is resolved against the workspace's effective requirements and use cases and handed to the reviewer as a delineated list with its resolution status; an unresolved reference is listed as unresolved, never refused (shared component RequirementReferenceExtractor).\n166: - R-24 The reviewer's own turn (P-06). The brief tells the reviewer to log its review as its own session-log turn, classified `Chore` so that its turn never needs an HV of its own (foundation completion policy: Chore needs no hostile review), and to store its entire result there. Finalization requires that turn to exist, to be persisted (not a failsafe or queued record), to have been created after the run started, and to contain the run's `hv_verdict` record byte for byte in its stored normal form (V-6). A reviewer whose host cannot log turns fails its NONCE with `reviewer_turn_logging_unsupported`. The reviewer's plugin runs as a host session of its own: a review or probe run never reads or changes the plugin state or the session-log session of a live session of the same agent in the workspace (FR-MCP-HVPIPE-008-AC008), which matters because a reviewer starts in the workspace (section 16.2) and Grok's headless hooks write their host state under the working directory's `.mcpServer/grok/` (VI-04).\n167: - R-25 The HostileReview subsystem stays separate. FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW-001 to -006 (its queue, artifact links, findings, and query surface) are not changed by this amendment, as the foundation already states (foundation section 5.4); the problems found in it (section 21, P1 to P3) are tracked in REMED-HVPIPELINE-001. It stays separate (D-02, settled): it reviews artifacts (TODO, FR/TR/TEST, plans, files, commits; FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW-002) while this pipeline reviews session turns (S-03), the approved foundation keeps it separate, and consolidating it is scope the operator has not named. Its missing worker (P1) is built on this amendment's execution components (process HvWorkerHost and the reviewer adapters), so reviewer execution exists once (the DRY ruling).\n168: - R-26 Scope limits. One request names turns of one MCP session. `MaxTurnsPerRequest` (default 200, the foundation's `HvRequest:MaxTurns` default) bounds every scope; a session scope above it is refused with 400 `hv_scope_too_large` naming the count, never truncated.\n169: - R-27 One open review per turn. A turn is `Pending` in at most one open request (a unique filtered index on the turn key where the item is `Pending`); admission over a pending turn is refused with 409 `hv_turns_pending` naming the turns and their requests, and over a final turn with 409 `hv_turns_already_reviewed` (S-06, S-20).\n170: - R-28 Cancellation by anyone (S-18). `Queued` becomes `Canceled` at once; `Running` becomes `CancelRequested`, and the worker stops the process at its next heartbeat and closes the run `Canceled`; `Finalizing`, `Completed`, `Failed`, and `Canceled` refuse with 409 `hv_request_not_cancelable`. A cancel and a finalization of the same request are serialized on the request row, so exactly one of them commits.\n171: - R-29 Worker units are internal. ClaimHvRunCommand, RecordHvRunHeartbeatCommand, AppendHvRunOutputCommand, CloseHvRunOutputCommand, FailHvRunCommand, ExpireHvRunLeasesCommand, FinalizeHvRequestCommand, and FileHvIncidentCommand have no REST route, MCP tool, or client method; only the worker host dispatches them, in-process, through `IDispatcher`. The public mutations are submit, cancel, reviewer update, NONCE, and evidence removal.\n172: - R-30 Plugins consume notifications through the REPL. Plugins never call REST (foundation section 3); the REPL gains a streaming verb that follows the notification stream, and the PowerShell core runs it in the background per host session (process WatchHvNotifications), keeping the last sequence in the host session's state and handing completions of requests that host session submitted to the next prompt as context.\n173: - R-31 The 98/98 bar for the overall verdict (P-05). The overall verdict is `AGREE` only when the holistic correctness and completeness both meet `max(configured hostile threshold, 0.98)` (ScoreVerdict, foundation section 3.5.4) and no failure is FAIL or UNKNOWN; per-turn hostile reviews keep the foundation's configured hostile thresholds through HvVerdictMapping.\n174: \n175: ## 6. Amended foundation clauses\n176: \n177: Each amendment names the foundation clause it replaces and states the replacement; nothing else in the named section changes.\n178: \n179: - A-01 Foundation section 3.1, \"Trust\" and \"Model grade\", and section 6.1, fourth bullet: \"model grades come only from the configured grader (default Codex, `gpt-6-astra`, `max`, the plan's HV reviewer)\" and \"this effort builds no grading worker or HV pipeline\" are replaced by: model grades come only from FinalizeHvRequestCommand, for the reviewer the pipeline assigned to the request (R-03); the HV pipeline is this amendment.\n180: - A-02 Foundation section 3 (\"Process\") and section 8.1: \"a Codex gpt-6-astra max hostile validation\" for every gate is replaced by: a hostile validation by the reviewer the operator names for the session, never of the requesting agent's family (P-01). The HV of a plan's own work does not use the pipeline that plan is building until that pipeline has passed its own end-to-end verification (section 20.3).\n181: - A-03 Foundation section 6.2: `ModelGrader:AgentName`, `ModelGrader:RequiredModel`, `ModelGrader:RequiredEffort`, `ModelGrader:ReceiptRoot`, and `HvRequest:MaxTurns` are removed; the reviewer pool and `MaxTurnsPerRequest` of `HostileValidationOptions` (section 10) replace them. The startup validation of an empty grader identity and receipt root is removed with them.\n182: - A-04 Foundation sections 6.3 (`AgentContextHvRequestEntity`, `AgentContextHvRequestItemEntity`), 6.4 (`CreateAgentContextHvRequestRequest`, `AgentContextHvRequestResult`, `AgentContextHvRequestQuery`), 6.5 (CreateAgentContextHvRequestCommand, GetAgentContextHvRequestQuery, QueryAgentContextHvRequestsQuery with their nested activities), 6.7 (their sequences), and Addendum A.9 are superseded by `HvRequest`, `HvRequestTurn`, `HvRequestResponse` (section 12), SubmitHvRequestCommand, GetHvRequestQuery, QueryHvRequestsQuery (section 14), and Addendum B. Every foundation reference to an HV request's `HvRequestId` (the grade's root id and foreign key, the finding's root id) now names `HvRequest`. ResponseStatusTransition's side effect (\"the response's `Pending` items in `Open` HV requests become `Closed` with that reason, and each request left with no `Pending` item becomes `Completed`\") becomes: the response's `Pending` `HvRequestResponse` items become `Closed` with that reason; the HV request's own state is decided by its turns and is never completed by a response transition.\n183: - A-05 Foundation section 6.5, RecordModelGradeCommand: its caller becomes FinalizeHvRequestCommand only (internal dispatch inside the finalization transaction, origin `HvFinalization`); the REST route `POST /qbrainai/agent-context/responses/{responseId}/grades/model`, the MCP tool `agent_context_record_model_grade`, the client and REPL methods, and `New-McpAgentContextRecordModelGradeParams` are removed; 403 `agent_context_grader_not_allowed` becomes \"grader is not the reviewer assigned to the HV request\"; the eight receipt and reviewer checks of foundation section 6.3 are replaced by the verification steps V-1 to V-8 of policy HvFinalVerification (section 15), which finalization runs before it dispatches the grade; the receipt path, rollout, and thread columns of `AgentResponseGradeEntity` become the run reference `RunId` and the evidence hashes (section 12).\n184: - A-06 Foundation section 3.1, \"Review record\" (\"One record per kind per turn; latest write wins\") and section 5.2, RecordSessionTurnReviewCommand: on agent surfaces a `hostileReview` write may carry only `status` `Other`; `Pass`, `Fail`, `Running`, and `hvVerdict` are refused with 403 `review_hostile_pipeline_only` (R-06); `Unnecessary` and `Unstarted` stay server-managed as before. Policy HvVerdictMapping is applied only by FinalizeHvRequestCommand. Section 5.4, process ConvertHvReceiptToReview, and its plugin function `ConvertFrom-McpHvReceipt` are retired, with the `hvReceiptPath` parameter of the plugin verbs.\n185: - A-07 Foundation section 3.1, \"Reviews remain writable on terminal turns\", section 3.4 (section PUT and DELETE), and section 3.5.3, TurnWritePipeline: every turn write, review write, review reset, section replace or clear, dialog append, title change, and session-level replace or delete that would change a turn whose HV state is `Pending` or `Final`, or any of that turn's children, is refused with 409 `turn_immutable` (`details.reason` `hv_pending` or `hv_final`, `details.hvRequestId`), from every origin except `HvFinalization` (policy HvTurnFreeze, R-04). TurnWritePipeline runs that policy after TurnLocator and before every other policy.\n186: - A-08 Foundation section 3.5.3, `WriteOrigin`: a fifth origin `HvFinalization` is added; it is exempt from the completion gate (it never moves a turn's status) and is the only origin that may write a turn whose HV state is `Pending`.\n187: - A-09 Foundation section 3.1, check 7 (`reviewer_independent`, \"not a different agent name, so an independently launched Codex reviewer grades a Codex-authored response\"): replaced by V-5, which requires both a separate session and a reviewer id different from the submitter's and the author's (R-08, S-17, P-01, P-07).\n188: - A-10 Foundation section 7.8 (UC-7, \"Record a hostile validation review with receipts\") is replaced by the use cases of section 18 (UC-HV-03 to UC-HV-05); foundation section 7.12 (UC \"Submit and grade an agent response\") keeps its flow, and its model grade now comes from UC-HV-05.\n189: - A-11 Foundation section 5.1, `SessionLogTurnEntity`: two columns are added, `HvState` (16: `None`, `Pending`, `Final`; database default `None`) and `HvRequestId` (128, null while `None`), written only by the migration that adds them, which sets `HvState` `Final` (with `HvRequestId` null) on every turn whose hostile review is already `Pass` or `Fail`, because S-07 forbids updates to a turn with an HV review, and then by SubmitHvRequestCommand, CancelHvRequestCommand, FailHvRunCommand and ExpireHvRunLeasesCommand (both through policy HvRetryDecision), and FinalizeHvRequestCommand, through policy HvTurnFreeze's state changes (Addendum B.8).\n190: - A-12 Foundation section 6.1, \"Existing records and stores\", the FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW bullet: unchanged in substance (R-25); its phrase \"because this effort builds no grading worker\" is replaced by \"because turn HV runs through the HostileValidation pipeline of Amendment 1\".\n191: \n192: ## 7. Lifecycles\n193: \n194: ### 7.1 HV request\n195: \n196: ```mermaid\n197: stateDiagram-v2\n198:     [*] --> Queued: SubmitHvRequestCommand, turns frozen Pending\n199:     Queued --> Running: ClaimHvRunCommand, run attempt claimed\n200:     Queued --> Canceled: CancelHvRequestCommand\n201:     Running --> Queued: attempt Failed or Abandoned, attempts left\n202:     Running --> CancelRequested: CancelHvRequestCommand\n203:     CancelRequested --> Canceled: worker stops the process, or the lease expires\n204:     Running --> Finalizing: CloseHvRunOutputCommand, output closed\n205:     Finalizing --> Completed: FinalizeHvRequestCommand commits, turns Final\n206:     Finalizing --> Queued: verification failed, attempts left\n207:     Running --> Failed: attempts spent\n208:     Finalizing --> Failed: verification failed, attempts spent\n209:     Canceled --> [*]: turns released to None\n210:     Failed --> [*]: turns released to None\n211:     Completed --> [*]\n212: ```\n213: \n214: - `Queued` and `Running` hold the turns `Pending`. `Completed`, `Failed`, and `Canceled` are terminal and never change again (S-06, S-20). A request whose attempt fails returns to `Queued` with its assignment re-evaluated (R-07); with no eligible reviewer it stays `Queued`, its ETA `Unknown`, until a reviewer passes a NONCE or the request is canceled, so no work is lost to an outage.\n215: - `CancelRequested` is not terminal: the request is canceled when the worker has stopped the process or when the lease of a dead worker expires.\n216: \n217: ### 7.2 Run attempt\n218: \n219: ```mermaid\n220: stateDiagram-v2\n221:     [*] --> Claimed: ClaimHvRunCommand, lease and fence\n222:     Claimed --> Running: worker started the reviewer process\n223:     Running --> Running: heartbeat renews the lease, output appended\n224:     Running --> OutputClosed: CloseHvRunOutputCommand\n225:     Running --> Failed: FailHvRunCommand, execution failure\n226:     Running --> Canceled: cancel observed at a heartbeat\n227:     Claimed --> Abandoned: lease expired, ExpireHvRunLeasesCommand\n228:     Running --> Abandoned: lease expired, ExpireHvRunLeasesCommand\n229:     OutputClosed --> Succeeded: FinalizeHvRequestCommand committed, or probe passed\n230:     OutputClosed --> Failed: verification failed, or probe failed\nEXIT_CODE=0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -Command \"pwsh.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -File './docs/receipts/hv/hv-r1-inspect.ps1' -Task Read -Path './AGENTS.md' -Count 300\"","aggregated_output":"SOURCE ./AGENTS.md totalLines=155\n1: # Agent Instructions\n2: \n3: ## Session Start\n4: \n5: 1. Read `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml` in the repo root for the current API key and endpoints.\n6: 2. For specific operational steps (session bootstrap, session log turn workflow, and helper command sequence), follow `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml`.\n7: \n8: On every subsequent user message:\n9: \n10: 1. Follow `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml` for specific operational instructions.\n11: 2. Complete the user's request.\n12: \n13: ## Rules\n14: \n15: 1. `templates/prompt-templates.yaml` (`default-marker-prompt`) is the source of truth for specific agent instructions. `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml` is the rendered runtime instruction set.\n16: 2. Keep this file focused on durable workspace policy and conventions; avoid duplicating marker-file operational procedures.\n17: 3. Use helper modules for session log and TODO operations. Do not make raw API calls.\n18: 4. Persist session log updates immediately after each meaningful change (turn creation, action append, decision, requirement, blocker, file/context update). Do not defer saves.\n19: 5. Capture rich turn detail: interpretation, response, status, actions (type/status/filePath), contextList, filesModified, designDecisions, requirementsDiscovered, blockers, and relevant processing dialog.\n20: 6. Follow workspace conventions in `.github/copilot-instructions.md` for build, test, and architecture guidance.\n21: 7. Use **only `pwsh.exe`** for shell commands and script execution; do not use `powershell.exe`.\n22: 8. When you need API schemas, module examples, or compliance rules, load them from `docs/context/` or use `context_search`.\n23: 9. Do not fabricate information. If you made a mistake, acknowledge it. Distinguish facts from speculation.\n24: 10. Prioritize correctness over speed. Do not ship code you have not verified compiles and is logically sound.\n25: 11. When writing session logs or other audit records, agents must identify themselves accurately using their real agent identity in Pascal-Case. Do not use placeholder, legacy, or misleading sourceType values.\n26: 12. Never edit YAML by appending, replacing, or removing text lines. Deserialize the complete document into an object, mutate the object, serialize it, and save it. Use `plugins/core/lib-ps/yaml-object-mutation.ps1` for PowerShell work.\n27: \n28: ## Byrd Test Gate\n29: \n30: The canonical process is `docs/Development-Process-draft-v4.md`. To leave a Byrd implementation slice, the entire unit test suite for the current iteration and previous iterations must be completely passing. In this workspace, skipped tests are not passing tests: a validation gate requires zero failures and zero skips in the executed scope. Tests should directly track progress; deferred work belongs in MCP TODO/requirements state until that slice starts, not in skipped test placeholders.\n31: \n32: ## Where Things Live\n33: \n34: - `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml` — connection details, API key, workspace config (regenerated on server start)\n35: - `.github/copilot-instructions.md` — build/test commands, architecture overview, coding conventions\n36: - `docs/context/` — on-demand reference docs (schemas, module docs, compliance rules, action types)\n37: - `docs/Project/` — requirements docs, TODO.yaml, mapping matrices\n38: - `templates/` — prompt templates (loaded on demand)\n39: - `tools/powershell/McpContext.psm1` — PowerShell module for context ingestion/query workflows\n40: - `tools/powershell/McpContext.USER.md` — user-level guide for the McpContext module\n41: - `tools/powershell/McpContext.AGENT.md` — agent workflow instructions for the McpContext module\n42: - `plugins/core/lib-ps/yaml-object-mutation.ps1` — object-first YAML mutation helper for agents and plugin maintenance\n43: \n44: ## MCP Interaction via REPL Tools\n45: \n46: Agents running inside `McpAgent` must use the 27 built-in tools instead of raw HTTP calls. See `docs/REPL-MIGRATION-GUIDE.md` for the full tool inventory and migration patterns.\n47: \n48: Key rules:\n49: - Use `mcp_session_*` tools for session log lifecycle (bootstrap, turns, history).\n50: - Use `mcp_todo_*` tools for TODO CRUD (query, get, create, update, delete, plan, status, implementation).\n51: - Use `mcp_requirements_*` tools for FR/TR/TEST queries.\n52: - Use `mcp_client_invoke` for any sub-client method not covered by a dedicated tool (context search, GitHub, workspace, etc.).\n53: - Do not make raw HTTP calls to `/qbrainai/*` endpoints when a tool is available.\n54: \n55: ## Context Loading by Task Type\n56: \n57: - Agent Help: see marker `## Agent Help (QBrain.AI issues)` in `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml`; API `/qbrainai/agent-help`\n58: - Session logging → `docs/context/session-log-schema.md` + `docs/context/module-bootstrap.md`\n59: - TODO management → `docs/context/todo-schema.md` + `docs/context/module-bootstrap.md`\n60: - API integration → `docs/context/api-capabilities.md` (or `GET /swagger/v1/swagger.json`)\n61: - Adding dependencies → `docs/context/compliance-rules.md`\n62: - Logging actions → `docs/context/action-types.md`\n63: - New to workspace → this file + `docs/context/api-capabilities.md`\n64: - Migrating from raw API → `docs/REPL-MIGRATION-GUIDE.md`\n65: \n66: ## Agent Conduct\n67: \n68: You represent the workspace owner. Your work directly reflects the owner's professional reputation.\n69: \n70: ### Honesty\n71: \n72: - Do not fabricate information, capabilities, or results.\n73: - Distinguish between facts, informed opinions, and speculation.\n74: - Acknowledge mistakes immediately and correct them.\n75: \n76: ### Correctness\n77: \n78: - Prioritize correctness over speed.\n79: - When uncertain, state your uncertainty and suggest verification steps.\n80: - Prefer proven patterns over clever approaches unless directed otherwise.\n81: - All code must have XMLDocs. All public APIs must be documented.\n82: - Follow DRY, SOLID, and existing project conventions.\n83: \n84: ### Decision Documentation\n85: \n86: - Log every decision to the session log, including trivial ones.\n87: - For each decision, document: what was decided, why, what alternatives were considered, what was rejected.\n88: - Log design decisions as dialog entries with category \"decision\" and as session log actions with type \"design_decision\".\n89: \n90: ### Professional Representation\n91: \n92: - Every interaction is audited via the session log.\n93: - Every commit must be correct, clean, well-described, and complete.\n94: - Log all commits as actions with type \"commit\" (SHA, branch, message, files).\n95: - Log all PR/issue comments as actions with type \"pr_comment\" or \"issue_comment\".\n96: - Always sign PR comments using the same signature convention as commits, identifying the actual authoring agent.\n97: \n98: ### Source Attribution\n99: \n100: - Document all web sources in the session log as actions with type \"web_reference\" (URL, title, usage).\n101: - Add source URLs to the turn's contextList array.\n102: - Attribute external code in both the session log and code comments.\n103: \n104: ## Requirements Tracking\n105: \n106: When you discover or agree on new requirements during a session:\n107: \n108: 1. Update the files in `docs/Project/`:\n109:    - `Functional-Requirements.md` — append FR-MCP-* entries\n110:    - `Technical-Requirements.md` — append TR-MCP-* entries\n111:    - `TR-per-FR-Mapping.md` — append mapping rows\n112:    - `Requirements-Matrix.md` — append status rows\n113:    - `Testing-Requirements.md` — append TEST-MCP-* entries\n114: 2. Include the requirement ID in your session log turn's tags.\n115: 3. Capture requirements as they emerge. Do not defer to later.\n116: \n117: ## Design Decision Logging\n118: \n119: When a design decision is made:\n120: \n121: 1. Log it as a session log dialog item with category \"decision\".\n122: 2. Include: the decision, alternatives considered, rationale, and affected requirements.\n123: 3. Add a session log action with type \"design_decision\".\n124: 4. If the decision affects existing code or requirements, note what needs updating.\n125: \n126: ## Session Continuity\n127: \n128: At the start of every session:\n129: \n130: 1. Follow the session-start checklist in `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml`.\n131: 2. Read `docs/Project/Requirements-Matrix.md` to understand project state.\n132: 3. If resuming interrupted work, review the last session's pending decisions.\n133: \n134: At regular intervals during long sessions (~10 interactions):\n135: \n136: 1. Follow marker-file update cadence and session logging requirements from `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml`.\n137: 2. Ensure all design decisions are captured.\n138: 3. Verify requirements docs are up to date.\n139: \n140: ## Glossary\n141: \n142: - **MCP** — Model Context Protocol, an open standard for tool-calling between AI agents and context servers.\n143: - **Workspace** — a project directory registered with the MCP server. All workspaces share a single port; use the `X-Workspace-Path` header to target a specific one.\n144: - **Marker File** — the `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml` file at each workspace root. Contains connection details, auth token, and agent prompt.\n145: - **API Key** — a per-workspace cryptographic token that rotates on each server restart. Required for all `/qbrainai/*` REST endpoints.\n146: - **Streamable HTTP** — the MCP wire protocol transport at `/mcp-transport`. Carries JSON-RPC tool calls over HTTP POST with streaming responses.\n147: - **Session Log** — an audit record of every agent interaction, stored per-session with full request/response history.\n148: - **Context Pack** — an ordered set of document chunks retrieved by semantic + full-text hybrid search, scoped to the workspace.\n149: - **Tool Bucket** — a GitHub repository containing tool manifest files, similar to a Scoop package bucket.\n150: \n151: ## Response Formatting\n152: \n153: - Do not use table-style output in responses.\n154: - Use concise bullets or short paragraphs instead.\n155: \nEXIT_CODE=0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_39","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -Command \"pwsh.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -File './docs/receipts/hv/hv-r1-inspect.ps1' -Task Read -Path './CLAUDE.md' -Count 350\"","aggregated_output":"SOURCE ./CLAUDE.md totalLines=255\n1: # CLAUDE.md\n2: \n3: ## Run `/add-profile` at session start and after any model or effort change\n4: \n5: Execute the `add-profile` skill (`/add-profile`) as the first action of every new session, and again immediately after any model change or effort-level change. It loads the operator profile (identity and standing instructions). Do not skip it.\n6: \n7: **DO NOT COMPACT, SUMMARIZE, PARAPHRASE, OR OMIT** any instruction in this file, `AGENTS.md`, or `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml`, ever, anywhere. Carry them verbatim.\n8: \n9: This file provides guidance to Claude Code (claude.ai/code) when working with code in this repository.\n10: \n11: ## Build & Test Commands\n12: \n13: ```bash\n14: # Build (Nuke orchestrator)\n15: ./build.ps1 Compile\n16: # or: dotnet build src/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp -c Debug\n17: \n18: # Run all unit tests (excludes integration tests)\n19: ./build.ps1 Test\n20: \n21: # Run a specific test project\n22: dotnet test tests/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.Tests -c Debug\n23: \n24: # Run a single test by name\n25: dotnet test tests/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.Tests -c Debug --filter \"FullyQualifiedName~TodoServiceTests.QueryAsync_NoFilters_ReturnsAllItems\"\n26: \n27: # Run all tests in a class\n28: dotnet test tests/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.Tests -c Debug --filter \"FullyQualifiedName~TodoServiceTests\"\n29: \n30: # Integration tests (CustomWebApplicationFactory, in-memory EF)\n31: dotnet test tests/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.IntegrationTests -c Debug\n32: \n33: # Validate appsettings configuration\n34: ./build.ps1 ValidateConfig\n35: \n36: # Validate requirements traceability (FR/TR/TEST mapping)\n37: ./build.ps1 ValidateTraceability\n38: \n39: # Start server\n40: ./build.ps1 StartServer --instance default\n41: # or: dotnet run --project src/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.csproj -c Staging -- --instance default\n42: ```\n43: \n44: Swagger UI: `http://localhost:7147/swagger`\n45: \n46: ## Architecture\n47: \n48: **QBrainAi** is an ASP.NET Core 9 server (.NET 9.0) providing workspace-scoped context retrieval, TODO management, session logging, repository operations, GraphRAG, and GitHub automation for AI agents.\n49: \n50: ### Transports\n51: \n52: - **HTTP REST** — Controllers at `/qbrainai/*` in `src/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp/Controllers/`\n53: - **MCP Streamable HTTP** — JSON-RPC wire protocol at `/mcp-transport`\n54: - **MCP STDIO** — `--transport stdio` flag; tools in `McpStdio/FwhMcpTools.cs`\n55: \n56: ### Workspace Model\n57: \n58: A single host process manages multiple workspaces. Each workspace gets its own in-process Kestrel `WebApplication` via `WorkspaceAppFactory` (not child processes). The primary workspace is served directly by the host. Workspace targeting uses the `X-Workspace-Path` header. Config lives in `appsettings.yaml` under `Mcp:Workspaces`. Per-instance overrides: `Mcp:Instances:{name}:*` resolved by `McpInstanceResolver`.\n59: \n60: ### Search Pipeline\n61: \n62: Hybrid search combining SQLite FTS5 full-text with HNSW vector similarity (384-dim all-MiniLM-L6-v2 ONNX embeddings). `HybridSearchService` fuses results with BM25 scoring.\n63: \n64: ### Storage\n65: \n66: EF Core with SQLite (`McpDbContext`), with migration projects for SQLite, PostgreSQL, and SQL Server. TODO items live in the configured database as the sole source of truth; `docs/Project/TODO.yaml` is a read-only projection. `Mcp:TodoStorage:Provider` accepts `database` (`sqlite` is a deprecated alias; the removed `yaml` value fails fast per TR-MCP-CFG-007).\n67: \n68: ### Key Projects\n69: \n70: - `QBrainAi.Support.Mcp` — main server application\n71: - `QBrainAi.Services` — ingestion, context search, models\n72: - `QBrainAi.Storage` — EF Core with SQLite FTS5 + HNSW vector search\n73: - `QBrainAi.Cqrs` — CQRS command/query handlers\n74: - `QBrainAi.McpAgent` — agent hosting & tool execution framework\n75: - `QBrainAi.Client` — typed REST client (published as NuGet `QBrainAI.Client`)\n76: - `QBrainAi.GraphRag` — GraphRAG indexing & query (workspace-scoped, disabled by default)\n77: - `QBrainAi.Repl.Core` / `QBrainAi.Repl.Host` — REPL command interpreter and dotnet tool\n78: \n79: ### Test Projects\n80: \n81: - Unit tests: `QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.Tests`, `QBrainAi.Client.Tests`, `QBrainAi.Cqrs.Tests`, `QBrainAi.Launcher.Tests`, `QBrainAi.McpAgent.Tests`, `QBrainAi.Repl.Core.Tests`\n82: - Infrastructure (Nuke build, accountable through TR only; not unit tests, not part of `./build.ps1 Test`): `Build.Tests`\n83: - Integration: `QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.IntegrationTests`, `QBrainAi.Repl.IntegrationTests`\n84: - BDD/Validation (Reqnroll): `QBrainAi.Context.Validation`, `QBrainAi.GitHub.Validation`, `QBrainAi.Repo.Validation`, `QBrainAi.SessionLog.Validation`, `QBrainAi.Todo.Validation`, `QBrainAi.ToolRegistry.Validation`, `QBrainAi.Workspace.Validation`\n85: \n86: ## Coding Conventions\n87: \n88: ### XML Documentation Required\n89: \n90: `TreatWarningsAsErrors` and `GenerateDocumentationFile` are enabled globally in `Directory.Build.props`. **All public types and members must have XML doc comments** or the build fails (CS1591). Use `/// <inheritdoc />` for interface implementations. Test classes and methods also require XML docs stating what is tested, what data/fixtures are used, and which requirement IDs are validated.\n91: \n92: ### Requirement Traceability\n93: \n94: All source files reference FR/TR requirement IDs in doc comments (e.g., `/// <summary>TR-PLANNED-CORE-013: Constructor.</summary>`). When adding new functionality, reference the relevant ID from `docs/Project/Functional-Requirements.md` and `docs/Project/Technical-Requirements.md`. Requirements traceability is validated in CI via `./build.ps1 ValidateTraceability`.\n95: \n96: ### Central Package Management\n97: \n98: NuGet package versions are centralized in `Directory.Packages.props`. Use `<PackageReference Include=\"...\" />` without `Version` in `.csproj` files.\n99: \n100: ### Configuration\n101: \n102: Configuration uses YAML format (`appsettings.yaml`) via `NetEscapades.Configuration.Yaml`. Primary config section is `Mcp`.\n103: \n104: ### Shell\n105: \n106: Use `pwsh.exe` (PowerShell 7+) for all scripts. Do not use `powershell.exe`.\n107: \n108: ## Key Documentation\n109: \n110: - `docs/MCP-SERVER.md` — operational guide, configuration details\n111: - `docs/USER-GUIDE.md` — user documentation\n112: - `docs/CLIENT-INTEGRATION.md` — client library usage\n113: - `docs/Development-Process.md` — development workflow\n114: - `docs/Project/Functional-Requirements.md` — FR-MCP-* requirements\n115: - `docs/Project/Technical-Requirements.md` — TR-MCP-* requirements\n116: - `docs/Project/TODO.yaml` — canonical task list\n117: - `AGENTS.md` — agent workspace policy and session logging conventions\n118: \n119: ## MCP Session Logging — Mandatory Precondition\n120: \n121: **Speed is never more important than following workspace procedures.**\n122: \n123: ### Session Start (Run Once Per Session)\n124: \n125: 1. **Read `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml`** in the repo root for the current API key, endpoints, and base URL\n126: 2. **Bootstrap the required plugin interface** before any state-changing MCP call. For Claude Code, use the `mcpserver-claude-code-plugin` wrapper and its `workflow.*` / `client.*` methods. \"Not in the visible tool list\" does not mean unavailable; use the documented wrapper invocation form.\n127: 3. **Verify marker signature and health** through the required plugin status/bootstrap path. Use direct REST only for read-only diagnosis after the documented plugin path fails.\n128: 4. **Review recent session history and current TODOs** through the required plugin only after verification succeeds\n129: 5. **POST an initial session log turn** through the required plugin\n130: 6. **THEN** begin working on the user's request\n131: \n132: If signature verification, `/health`, or nonce verification fails: log `MCP_UNTRUSTED`, continue without the MCP server, and do not probe additional MCP endpoints.\n133: \n134: ### Per User Message\n135: \n136: 1. POST a new session log turn BEFORE starting work\n137: 2. Complete the user's request\n138: 3. Update the turn with results, actions taken, and files modified when done\n139: \n140: ### Re-run Full Session Start Only If\n141: \n142: - The user explicitly says \"Start Session\"\n143: - Signature verification fails\n144: - `/health` fails or nonce verification fails\n145: - Any `/qbrainai/*` call returns 401\n146: - The marker endpoint/key changes after a server restart\n147: \n148: ### Authentication\n149: \n150: All `/qbrainai/*` endpoints require a per-workspace auth token (from `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml`). These details are for plugin internals, typed client integration, and read-only diagnosis after plugin failure; they are not permission to bypass the required plugin route for session log, TODO, requirements, import/export, or traceability operations:\n151: - Header: `X-Api-Key: <token>`\n152: - Or query param: `?api_key=<token>`\n153: - If you receive a 401, re-read the marker file — the token rotates on each server restart\n154: \n155: ### Session Log Rules\n156: \n157: - Use rich turn detail: interpretation, response, status, actions (type/status/filePath), contextList, filesModified, designDecisions, requirementsDiscovered, blockers, and key processingDialog\n158: - Persist session log updates immediately after each meaningful change — do not defer saves\n159: - Before any compaction step, persist the current session log state; after compaction, update again to record the outcome\n160: - Agents must identify themselves accurately using their real agent identity in Pascal-Case (e.g., `ClaudeCode`). Do not use placeholder or misleading sourceType values\n161: \n162: ### Naming Conventions\n163: \n164: - **TODO IDs**: uppercase canonical form `<SDLC-PHASE>-<AREA>-###` (e.g., `PLAN-NAMINGCONVENTIONS-001`) or `ISSUE-{number}`. Never write to `TODO.yaml` directly\n165: - **Session IDs**: `<Agent>-<yyyyMMddTHHmmssZ>-<suffix>` with Pascal-Case agent prefix\n166: - **Request IDs**: `req-<yyyyMMddTHHmmssZ>-<slugOrOrdinal>`, unique within a session\n167: \n168: ## Agent Conduct\n169: \n170: - Do not fabricate information. Acknowledge mistakes. Distinguish facts from speculation\n171: - Prioritize correctness over speed. Do not ship code you have not verified compiles\n172: - Log every decision to the session log, including: what was decided, why, alternatives considered, what was rejected\n173: - Document all web sources as actions with type `web_reference`\n174: - Do not use table-style output in responses — use concise bullets or short paragraphs\n175: \n176: ## Requirements Tracking\n177: \n178: When you discover or agree on new requirements during a session, update the files in `docs/Project/`:\n179: - `Functional-Requirements.md` — append FR-MCP-* entries\n180: - `Technical-Requirements.md` — append TR-MCP-* entries\n181: - `TR-per-FR-Mapping.md` — append mapping rows\n182: - `Requirements-Matrix.md` — append status rows\n183: - `Testing-Requirements.md` — append TEST-MCP-* entries\n184: \n185: Include the requirement ID in your session log turn's tags. Capture requirements as they emerge — do not defer.\n186: \n187: ## Context Loading by Task Type\n188: \n189: - Agent Help: see marker `## Agent Help (QBrain.AI issues)` in `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml`; API `/qbrainai/agent-help`\n190: - Session logging: `docs/context/session-log-schema.md` + `docs/context/module-bootstrap.md`\n191: - TODO management: `docs/context/todo-schema.md` + `docs/context/module-bootstrap.md`\n192: - API integration: `docs/context/api-capabilities.md` (or `GET /swagger/v1/swagger.json`)\n193: - Adding dependencies: `docs/context/compliance-rules.md`\n194: - Logging actions: `docs/context/action-types.md`\n195: - New to workspace: this file + `docs/context/api-capabilities.md`\n196: \n197: ## Where Things Live\n198: \n199: - `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml` — connection details, API key, workspace config (regenerated on server start)\n200: - `AGENTS.md` — agent conduct, requirements tracking, session continuity, glossary\n201: - `docs/context/` — on-demand reference docs (schemas, module docs, compliance rules, action types)\n202: - `docs/Project/` — requirements docs, TODO.yaml, mapping matrices\n203: - `templates/` — prompt templates (loaded on demand)\n204: - `tools/powershell/` — PowerShell modules for MCP context operations\n205: \n206: ## CI/CD\n207: \n208: GitHub Actions (`.github/workflows/build.yml`) on Windows-latest with .NET 9.0. Triggers on push/PR to main/develop. Jobs: build-test, validate, package, msix, publish. The Nuke `Test` target excludes `*.IntegrationTests` projects.\n209: \n210: ## QBrain.AI Claude Plugin and Hook Contract\n211: \n212: Claude must treat QBrain.AI plugin and hook setup as a live runtime contract, not background documentation. On every new Claude session in this workspace, and whenever QBrain.AI behavior looks stale, incomplete, or inconsistent, perform this validation before relying on QBrain.AI workflows.\n213: \n214: ### Required Startup Validation\n215: \n216: 1. Read `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml` from the active workspace root and use it as the current source of truth for QBrain.AI endpoint, API key, workspace path, plugin instructions, and failure-reporting rules.\n217: 2. Identify the active QBrain.AI Claude plugin cache path and version from the marker file, plugin manifest, or active wrapper path. Do not assume a previously cached path is current.\n218: 3. Inspect `%USERPROFILE%\\.claude\\plugins\\cache` for stale QBrain.AI plugin entries. Delete stale `mcpserver`, `mcpserver-*`, or `mcpserver-cowork` cache entries that are not the active plugin cache path/version for this session.\n219: 4. Do not delete the active plugin cache path for the current session. If unsure which path is active, stop deleting and report the ambiguity.\n220: 5. Reload or rebootstrap the active QBrain.AI Claude plugin after stale cache cleanup.\n221: 6. Inspect Claude active settings, including user-level and workspace/session-level settings, and verify QBrain.AI hooks are actually installed in the active settings layer. Do not accept hook files existing in the plugin package as proof that hooks are active.\n222: 7. If hooks are missing or stale, install or refresh them through the supported QBrain.AI Claude plugin hook validation skill or wrapper path.\n223: 8. Verify the plugin wrapper/status command works and record the exact command or tool path used.\n224: 9. Verify subprocesses launched by the plugin use the workspace root as process current working directory, not the inherited user profile directory.\n225: \n226: ### Required Usage Rules\n227: \n228: 1. Use supported QBrain.AI plugin wrappers, hooks, skills, or MCP tools for session logging, TODOs, requirements, triage, and status checks. Do not use raw REST when a supported wrapper, hook, skill, or MCP tool exists.\n229: 2. Session logging must use the supported session wrapper or MCP tools and must create, update, and complete turns through the plugin flow.\n230: 3. TODO operations must use the supported TODO wrapper, workflow, or MCP tools. Do not edit TODO storage directly.\n231: 4. Requirements operations must use the supported requirements wrapper, workflow, or MCP tools. Do not edit requirements storage directly.\n232: 5. Triage operations must use the supported triage wrapper, workflow, or MCP tools.\n233: 6. QBrain.AI failures and plugin failures discovered while doing unrelated work must always be written as a normal failsafe YAML report through the plugin failsafe flow, then submitted through triage.\n234: 7. If triage submission succeeds, Claude must continue the user active task without waiting for triage research or TODO creation. If triage submission fails, stop work and notify the user. Do not invent a raw REST fallback or alternate reporting channel.\n235: 8. Normal plugin execution must use PowerShell only. Bash is allowed only for installing PowerShell. Node must not be used for JSON or YAML construction.\n236: 9. JSON and YAML payloads must be built from native objects and serialized. Do not handwrite YAML or JSON as fragile string literals.\n237: 10. If any validation check fails, report the exact failed check, the path or command involved, and the blocked capability. Do not claim QBrain.AI compliance until the check is fixed or explicitly marked unavailable.\n238: \n239: ### Minimum Validation Report\n240: \n241: When asked to validate plugin or hook usage, Claude must return a concise report containing:\n242: \n243: - Active workspace path.\n244: - Marker file path and timestamp.\n245: - Active plugin cache path and version.\n246: - Stale plugin cache paths deleted.\n247: - Hook settings file paths inspected.\n248: - Hooks found, installed, or refreshed.\n249: - Wrapper/status command used and result.\n250: - Session logging validation result.\n251: - TODO validation result.\n252: - Requirements validation result.\n253: - Triage validation result.\n254: - Process current working directory validation result.\n255: - Any remaining mismatch, unavailable surface, or failure.\nEXIT_CODE=0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
