{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_41","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -Command \"pwsh.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -File './docs/receipts/hv/hv-r1-inspect.ps1' -Task Read -Path '/home/sharpninja/.codex/plugins/cache/mcpserver-codex-plugin/mcpserver/1.120.0/skills/requirements/SKILL.md' -Count 400\"","aggregated_output":"SOURCE /home/sharpninja/.codex/plugins/cache/mcpserver-codex-plugin/mcpserver/1.120.0/skills/requirements/SKILL.md totalLines=548\n1: ---\n2: name: Requirements Management\n3: description: Use when the user asks to \"list requirements\", \"add requirement\", \"create FR\", \"create TR\", \"create test requirement\", \"generate requirements document\", or \"ingest requirements\".\n4: version: 0.1.0\n5: ---\n6: \n7: # Requirements Management\n8: \n9: ## Overview\n10: \n11: To manage functional requirements (FR), technical requirements (TR), test requirements (TEST), and their traceability mappings, use this plugin's declared hook/wrapper (or the REPL transport) for the `workflow.requirements.*` namespace. Do not substitute raw REST calls, generic `PowerShell.MCP wrapper`, helper modules, or another agent's plugin for normal requirements work.\n12: \n13: The plugin wrapper validates documented params and emits single-line JSON to REPL stdio. Any direct REPL diagnostic call must use one single-line JSON request envelope, not formatted YAML.\n14: \n15: The database is the source of truth for requirements. Markdown files are import/export projections only. Every operation is scoped to the workspace resolved from the signed marker, and generated workspace output must contain only the requested workspace's FR, TR, TEST, and traceability links.\n16: \n17: ## Initialization\n18: \n19: Call `workflow.sessionlog.bootstrap` through this plugin's declared hook/wrapper to initialize the session log subsystem before issuing any workflow commands. This call is idempotent and should be made once per conversation context.\n20: \n21: ```yaml\n22: type: request\n23: payload:\n24:   requestId: req-20260409T120000Z-bootstrap-001\n25:   method: workflow.sessionlog.bootstrap\n26:   params: {}\n27: ```\n28: \n29: ## Requirement Scope Layers\n30: \n31: Plugin version 1.13.0 and newer exposes native layer operations. Treat this section as the layer API reference when plugin status reports these methods; do not ask the operator or inspect wrapper source just to learn the parameter shapes.\n32: \n33: - `workflow.requirements.listLayers` / `req_list_layers` / `client.Requirements.ListRequirementLayersAsync`: omit params.\n34: - `workflow.requirements.createLayer` / `req_create_layer` / `client.Requirements.CreateRequirementLayerAsync`: required params are `key`, `order`, and `name`; optional params are `description` and `scopeEndLayerKey`.\n35: - `workflow.requirements.updateLayer` / `req_update_layer` / `client.Requirements.UpdateRequirementLayerAsync`: required param is `key`; optional params are `name`, `description`, and `scopeEndLayerKey`. Layer `order` is immutable; create a new layer rather than attempting to update `order`.\n36: - `workflow.requirements.effective` / `req_effective` / `client.Requirements.GetEffectiveRequirementsAsync`: optional param is `layerKey`; omit `layerKey` to use the active workspace layer.\n37: \n38: Use camelCase exactly in YAML:\n39: \n40: ```yaml\n41: type: request\n42: payload:\n43:   requestId: req-20260409T115900Z-listlayers-001\n44:   method: workflow.requirements.listLayers\n45:   params: {}\n46: ```\n47: \n48: ```yaml\n49: type: request\n50: payload:\n51:   requestId: req-20260409T115901Z-createlayer-001\n52:   method: workflow.requirements.createLayer\n53:   params:\n54:     key: future\n55:     order: 20\n56:     name: Future Layer\n57:     description: Requirements that are documented but not currently enforceable\n58: ```\n59: \n60: ```yaml\n61: type: request\n62: payload:\n63:   requestId: req-20260409T115902Z-updatelayer-001\n64:   method: workflow.requirements.updateLayer\n65:   params:\n66:     key: future\n67:     name: Future Layer\n68:     scopeEndLayerKey: release\n69: ```\n70: \n71: ```yaml\n72: type: request\n73: payload:\n74:   requestId: req-20260409T115903Z-effective-001\n75:   method: workflow.requirements.effective\n76:   params:\n77:     layerKey: future\n78: ```\n79: \n80: FR, TR, TEST, and batch create/update commands also accept requirement scope fields:\n81: \n82: - `scopeStartLayerKey`: first layer where the requirement is effective.\n83: - `scopeEndLayerKey`: last layer where the requirement is effective; omit for open-ended scope.\n84: \n85: Omit both fields for requirements that are effective in the default/current layer.\n86: \n87: ## Requirement ID Conventions\n88: \n89: Three ID spaces are in use:\n90: \n91: - **FR** - Functional Requirements: `^FR-[A-Z]+-\\d{3}$`, e.g. `FR-MCP-001`, `FR-AUTH-042`\n92: - **TR** - Technical Requirements: `^TR-[A-Z]+-[A-Z]+-\\d{3}$`, e.g. `TR-MCP-ARCH-001`, `TR-AUTH-SEC-002`\n93: - **TEST** - Test Requirements: `^TEST-[A-Z]+-\\d{3}$`, e.g. `TEST-MCP-001`, `TEST-AUTH-003`\n94: \n95: All IDs must be uppercase. TR IDs require both an area and a subarea segment; FR and TEST IDs require only an area segment.\n96: \n97: ## Functional Requirements (FR)\n98: \n99: ### Listing FRs\n100: \n101: To retrieve a filtered list of functional requirements:\n102: \n103: ```yaml\n104: type: request\n105: payload:\n106:   requestId: req-20260409T120000Z-listfr-001\n107:   method: workflow.requirements.listFr\n108:   params:\n109:     area: MCP\n110:     status: in_progress\n111: ```\n112: \n113: All params are optional; omit to return all FRs. The result contains an `items` array:\n114: \n115: ```yaml\n116: type: result\n117: payload:\n118:   requestId: req-20260409T120000Z-listfr-001\n119:   result:\n120:     items:\n121:       - id: FR-MCP-001\n122:         title: Agent authentication\n123:         description: System must authenticate AI agents via API key\n124:         status: completed\n125:         priority: critical\n126:         area: MCP\n127:         createdAt: 2026-03-01T10:00:00Z\n128:         updatedAt: 2026-04-09T12:00:00Z\n129:     totalCount: 1\n130: ```\n131: \n132: Valid `status` values: `pending`, `in_progress`, `completed`, `deferred`.\n133: \n134: ### Getting a Single FR\n135: \n136: To fetch one functional requirement by ID:\n137: \n138: ```yaml\n139: type: request\n140: payload:\n141:   requestId: req-20260409T120001Z-getfr-001\n142:   method: workflow.requirements.getFr\n143:   params:\n144:     id: FR-MCP-001\n145: ```\n146: \n147: ### Creating an FR\n148: \n149: To record a new functional requirement:\n150: \n151: ```yaml\n152: type: request\n153: payload:\n154:   requestId: req-20260409T120002Z-createfr-001\n155:   method: workflow.requirements.createFr\n156:   params:\n157:     id: FR-MCP-003\n158:     title: Context search\n159:     description: System must support semantic search across workspace documents\n160:     priority: high\n161:     area: MCP\n162:     notes: Use hybrid search combining BM25 and vector embeddings\n163: ```\n164: \n165: Required fields: `id`, `title`, `description`, `priority`, `area`. The `notes` field is optional.\n166: \n167: ### Acceptance Criteria\n168: \n169: FR, TR, and TEST create/update commands accept structured `acceptanceCriteria` entries with the same shape used by TODO execution criteria:\n170: \n171: ```yaml\n172: method: workflow.requirements.createFr\n173: params:\n174:   id: FR-MCP-XXX-001\n175:   title: Acceptance criteria example\n176:   description: Requirement records preserve structured pass/fail criteria.\n177:   priority: high\n178:   area: MCP\n179:   acceptanceCriteria:\n180:     - id: ac-1\n181:       text: Round-trip preserves criterion text\n182:       isSatisfied: false\n183:     - id: ac-2\n184:       text: Evidence is retained when supplied\n185:       isSatisfied: true\n186:       evidence: tests/Services/RequirementAcceptanceCriteriaTests.cs\n187: ```\n188: \n189: To copy criteria from an execution TODO onto a requirement, call `workflow.requirements.copyAcceptanceCriteriaFromTodo`:\n190: \n191: ```yaml\n192: method: workflow.requirements.copyAcceptanceCriteriaFromTodo\n193: params:\n194:   kind: fr\n195:   id: FR-MCP-XXX-001\n196:   todoId: PLAN-MCP-001\n197: ```\n198: \n199: This maps to `POST /mcpserver/requirements/{kind}/{id}/acceptance-criteria/copy-from-todo` with body `{ \"todoId\": \"PLAN-MCP-001\" }`.\n200: \n201: ### Batch Create Or Update\n202: \n203: When creating or updating multiple records, prefer the atomic batch commands instead of Markdown ingestion. Batch commands accept YAML `records:` arrays and fail all records if any record is invalid, conflicts, or is missing during update.\n204: \n205: Use per-kind commands when every record is the same type:\n206: \n207: ```yaml\n208: type: request\n209: payload:\n210:   requestId: req-20260409T120002Z-createfr-batch-001\n211:   method: workflow.requirements.createFrBatch\n212:   params:\n213:     records:\n214:       - id: FR-MCP-004\n215:         title: Batch requirements\n216:         description: The system must accept multiple requirement records in one request.\n217:         priority: high\n218:       - id: FR-MCP-005\n219:         title: Atomic validation\n220:         description: The system must reject the whole batch when one record is invalid.\n221:         priority: high\n222: ```\n223: \n224: Use `workflow.requirements.updateFrBatch`, `createTrBatch`, `updateTrBatch`, `createTestBatch`, and `updateTestBatch` for per-kind batches. For mixed FR/TR/TEST arrays, use `workflow.requirements.createBatch` or `workflow.requirements.updateBatch` and include `kind: fr|tr|test` on each record.\n225: \n226: ### Updating an FR\n227: \n228: To modify an existing FR:\n229: \n230: ```yaml\n231: type: request\n232: payload:\n233:   requestId: req-20260409T120003Z-updatefr-001\n234:   method: workflow.requirements.updateFr\n235:   params:\n236:     id: FR-MCP-003\n237:     status: completed\n238:     notes: Implemented with HybridSearchService using all-MiniLM-L6-v2 embeddings\n239: ```\n240: \n241: ### Deleting an FR\n242: \n243: ```yaml\n244: type: request\n245: payload:\n246:   requestId: req-20260409T120004Z-deletefr-001\n247:   method: workflow.requirements.deleteFr\n248:   params:\n249:     id: FR-MCP-003\n250: ```\n251: \n252: ## Technical Requirements (TR)\n253: \n254: TR IDs require both `area` and `subarea` segments. The full ID format is `TR-<AREA>-<SUBAREA>-###`.\n255: \n256: ### Listing TRs\n257: \n258: ```yaml\n259: type: request\n260: payload:\n261:   requestId: req-20260409T120005Z-listtr-001\n262:   method: workflow.requirements.listTr\n263:   params:\n264:     area: MCP\n265:     subarea: PERF\n266: ```\n267: \n268: ### Creating a TR\n269: \n270: ```yaml\n271: type: request\n272: payload:\n273:   requestId: req-20260409T120006Z-createtr-001\n274:   method: workflow.requirements.createTr\n275:   params:\n276:     id: TR-MCP-PERF-001\n277:     title: API response time SLA\n278:     description: All API endpoints must respond within 500ms at p99\n279:     priority: high\n280:     area: MCP\n281:     subarea: PERF\n282:     notes: Measured at gateway, excluding network transit time\n283: ```\n284: \n285: ### Updating a TR\n286: \n287: ```yaml\n288: type: request\n289: payload:\n290:   requestId: req-20260409T120007Z-updatetr-001\n291:   method: workflow.requirements.updateTr\n292:   params:\n293:     id: TR-MCP-PERF-001\n294:     status: in_progress\n295:     notes: Baseline established at 320ms p99 under load test\n296: ```\n297: \n298: ### Deleting a TR\n299: \n300: ```yaml\n301: type: request\n302: payload:\n303:   requestId: req-20260409T120008Z-deletetr-001\n304:   method: workflow.requirements.deleteTr\n305:   params:\n306:     id: TR-MCP-PERF-001\n307: ```\n308: \n309: ## Test Requirements (TEST)\n310: \n311: ### Listing TEST Requirements\n312: \n313: ```yaml\n314: type: request\n315: payload:\n316:   requestId: req-20260409T120009Z-listtest-001\n317:   method: workflow.requirements.listTest\n318:   params:\n319:     area: MCP\n320: ```\n321: \n322: ### Creating a TEST Requirement\n323: \n324: ```yaml\n325: type: request\n326: payload:\n327:   requestId: req-20260409T120010Z-createtest-001\n328:   method: workflow.requirements.createTest\n329:   params:\n330:     id: TEST-MCP-001\n331:     title: Agent authentication unit test\n332:     description: Verify API key authentication rejects invalid tokens with 401\n333:     priority: critical\n334:     area: MCP\n335:     notes: Uses in-memory EF provider with CustomWebApplicationFactory\n336: ```\n337: \n338: ### Updating and Deleting TEST Requirements\n339: \n340: Follow the same pattern as FR: use `workflow.requirements.updateTest` and `workflow.requirements.deleteTest` with the same parameter shapes.\n341: \n342: ## Requirement Mappings\n343: \n344: Mappings link an FR to one or more TRs and TEST IDs in the current workspace, forming the traceability matrix.\n345: \n346: ### Listing Mappings\n347: \n348: ```yaml\n349: type: request\n350: payload:\n351:   requestId: req-20260409T120011Z-listmap-001\n352:   method: workflow.requirements.listMappings\n353:   params:\n354:     frId: FR-MCP-001\n355: ```\n356: \n357: ### Creating a Mapping\n358: \n359: ```yaml\n360: type: request\n361: payload:\n362:   requestId: req-20260409T120012Z-createmap-001\n363:   method: workflow.requirements.createMapping\n364:   params:\n365:     frId: FR-MCP-001\n366:     trIds:\n367:       - TR-MCP-ARCH-001\n368:     testIds:\n369:       - TEST-MCP-001\n370:     notes: Core authentication flow covered by ARCH constraint and unit test\n371: ```\n372: \n373: Legacy single-link `trId` and `testId` inputs are accepted for compatibility. Prefer `trIds` and `testIds` for all new calls.\n374: \n375: The result confirms the stored mapping:\n376: \n377: ```yaml\n378: type: result\n379: payload:\n380:   requestId: req-20260409T120012Z-createmap-001\n381:   result:\n382:     item:\n383:       frId: FR-MCP-001\n384:       trId: TR-MCP-ARCH-001\n385:       testId: TEST-MCP-001\n386:       createdAt: 2026-04-09T12:00:12Z\n387:       notes: Core authentication flow covered by ARCH constraint and unit test\n388: ```\n389: \n390: ### Deleting a Mapping\n391: \n392: ```yaml\n393: type: request\n394: payload:\n395:   requestId: req-20260409T120013Z-deletemap-001\n396:   method: workflow.requirements.deleteMapping\n397:   params:\n398:     frId: FR-MCP-001\n399:     trId: TR-MCP-ARCH-001\n400: ```\nEXIT_CODE=0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_42","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -Command \"pwsh.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -File './docs/receipts/hv/hv-r1-inspect.ps1' -Task Native -CommandName git -ArgumentText 'log:::36783b79..d3a0beb10b0879f20fb307f205a3bff25ac3a441:::--format=%H%n%B:::--:::docs/architecture'\"","aggregated_output":"d3a0beb10b0879f20fb307f205a3bff25ac3a441\ndocs(architecture): amendment 1, D-07 and D-08 settled by the operator\n\nOperator, 2026-10-17, verbatim: \"1. D-07 B: this plan fixes all six (P10 to\nP15) itself. 2. HV ids keep the UTC yyyyMMddTHHmmssZ stamp: timestamps are\nUTC in the data and local only for display. 3. Yes, launch the HV round\nnow.\"\n\n- D-07 settled (B): this plan fixes P10 to P15, each under the owning\n  plan's approved design. Section 20.4 assigns P10, P12, and P13 to\n  HvStorageSchema before any HV entity (P10 and P13 as the federation\n  plan's P1.5 decided them: rekey maintenance command, startup guard,\n  token-store rewrite, its D5 and D7; never an automatic migration or a\n  dual-key lookup), and P11, P14, and P15 to H1 in the PowerShell plugin\n  core, synced to every plugin, before FR-MCP-HVPIPE-008-AC010 is bound\n  (P11 and P15 to the foundation's stop gate, FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005,\n  never Codex's complete-in-hook suggestion). Where an owner already\n  delivered the same fix, the phase proves it with the named test and does\n  not change that code again (DRY). H0 imports FR, TR, and\n  TEST-MCP-PATHNORM-001 into this workspace's store (not held here today;\n  the federation handoff places them in the F:\\GitHub\\McpServer store) and\n  strengthens the acceptance criteria P10 to P15 are fixed through.\n- D-08 settled: hvr-, hvprobe-, and hvi- keep the UTC yyyyMMddTHHmmssZ\n  stamp; every stored timestamp is UTC with offset; local time is for\n  display only (section 12 cites it).\n\nLint PASSED at 2026-10-17T09:15:49 local (114 acceptance criteria, 15\nproblems, 8 decisions). Status: proposed, not approved, not\nhostile-validated.\n\nSigned: claude-opus-5-5-xhigh@ClaudeCode | Confidence: correctness 95%, accuracy 96%, completeness 94%\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01QPEG9wEPfbfj4qgB3eeK2N\n\n0a449314528c5bfc07e9b386c97034716e2ddbd4\ndocs(architecture): amendment 1, turns already HV-reviewed migrate Final (AC007)\n\nWhat-if per requirement before the hostile validation (profile 7.5,\nlegacy and upgrade): the freeze keys on HvState only, so a turn that\nalready holds a Pass or Fail hostile review when A-11 adds the column\nwould start None, stay writable, and could be admitted again, against\nS-07 (\"Session log may not allow updates to a turn with an HV review\")\nand S-20. Such turns exist if the foundation's Phase B ships its manual\nhostile reviews before A-06 retires them.\n\n- A-11: the migration that adds HvState and HvRequestId sets Final (with\n  no request id) on every turn whose hostile review is Pass or Fail.\n- Section 7.4 and section 12 state it; HvAdmissionEligibility names the\n  null request id of such a turn; HvStorageSchema owns the rule.\n- FR-MCP-HVPIPE-012-AC007 (MigrationMarksHvReviewedTurnsFinalThreeProviders,\n  three providers); 19.2 maps S-07 to it. FR, TR, and TEST-MCP-HVPIPE-012\n  updated in the store in one atomic updateBatch; all 63 records equal\n  section 19.1 (2026-10-17T08:55:01 local).\n\nLint PASSED at 2026-10-17T08:54:21 local (114 acceptance criteria).\nStatus: proposed, not approved, not hostile-validated.\n\nSigned: claude-opus-5-5-xhigh@ClaudeCode | Confidence: correctness 94%, accuracy 95%, completeness 94%\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01QPEG9wEPfbfj4qgB3eeK2N\n\n31904493381cfef56c57790950e23e72f3ecc39b\ndocs(architecture): amendment 1, pre-HV holistic review fixes and AC011\n\nHolistic read of the whole amendment before the hostile validation (profile\n7.5), with what-if and propagation checks. Implementer fixes:\n\n- A-02 cited section 20.4 for the rule that the pipeline does not validate\n  its own plan; the rule is in section 20.3.\n- Section 7.3 still read \"not the submitter\" after D-05; eligibility now\n  names the author too, as R-08, I-07, and HvReviewerEligibility do.\n- I-06 described the log hash as the SHA-256 of the concatenation, while\n  section 12 and V-1 use the chain hash over the chunks' SHA-256 values;\n  I-06 now states the chain hash, and section 12 no longer cites R-12 for\n  a definition R-12 does not hold.\n- GetHvEvidenceQuery listed a runId of another request as 400 while its\n  diagram and sequence 17.10 answer 404 not_found run; the list now says 404.\n- HvIncident.DedupeKey and R-17 named only the reviewer form; both now name\n  hv-server-<failureCode>, which FailHvRunCommand, the lease sweep, and\n  FR-MCP-HVPIPE-009-AC006 already use.\n- Section 13.3 called hv_probe the only record of a probe brief, while\n  ReadinessProbeVerdict also reads hv_reviewer_turn; the probe brief now\n  asks for both.\n- HvWorkerHost \"dispatches only internal units\" contradicted its startup\n  StartHvReviewerNonceCommand; it is now the only dispatcher of the\n  internal units.\n\nNew acceptance criterion from the what-if pass (a sibling of P14 in the HV\ndesign, and a deadlock of P-02 with read-to-end output): killing a reviewer\nends its whole process tree, also after the root exited, and a descendant\nholding the output pipes is ended so the run cannot be held open forever\n(section 16.2; FR-MCP-HVPIPE-008-AC011, ReviewerStopEndsWholeProcessTree,\nH3; 19.2 maps P-02 to it). FR, TR, and TEST-MCP-HVPIPE-008 were updated in\nthe store in one atomic updateBatch; all 63 records equal section 19.1\n(Test-HvpipeStoreEquality, 2026-10-17T08:48:33 local, 63 of 63).\n\nChecks: lint PASSED at 2026-10-17T08:45:03 local (113 acceptance criteria,\n15 problems, 7 decisions); foundation SHA-256 at 36783b79 equals the\nclaimed 6E5D4F41...295E; 0 of 94 Mermaid blocks changed since 6dbc3843.\nStatus: proposed, not approved, not hostile-validated.\n\nSigned: claude-opus-5-5-xhigh@ClaudeCode | Confidence: correctness 95%, accuracy 95%, completeness 94%\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01QPEG9wEPfbfj4qgB3eeK2N\n\nf5fd5ab24ad00b09053daf07ed3c0d850f70498d\ndocs(architecture): amendment 1, P15 names a plan-created requirement as such\n\nP15 cited FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005 as if it were a stored requirement. A\nrequirement read at 2026-10-17T08:36:10 local answered not_found: it is\ncreated by PLAN-SESSIONTURNREVIEW-001 (slice A7) and the store does not\nhold it yet. P15 now says so, and quotes that plan's change to\nTR-MCP-SESSIONLIFE-005 (\"Close-PluginTurnIfNeeded blocks instead of\nclosing\"), which is a stored record.\n\nLint PASSED at 2026-10-17T08:36:32 local.\nStatus: proposed, not approved, not hostile-validated.\n\nSigned: claude-opus-5-5-xhigh@ClaudeCode | Confidence: correctness 97%, accuracy 97%, completeness 95%\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01QPEG9wEPfbfj4qgB3eeK2N\n\nbd6e37b33f043b1ede3a1feeaf7073d357ea21cf\ndocs(architecture): amendment 1, PR 81 findings P10 to P15 and D-04 settled\n\nOperator, 2026-10-17: \"1. Yes, 2. A\".\n\n1. Yes: the six findings of the Codex review of PR 81 (review 5554472359 on\n   6dbc384323, posted 2026-10-16T17:48:12 local, after the merge) are\n   registered as P10 to P15 in section 21, each checked against this\n   branch's source:\n   - P10 legacy workspace keys are not rewritten (WorkspaceKeyConversion\n     reads stored values unchanged; no migration or other code rewrites\n     them). This is the federation plan's undelivered P1.5 (rekey command,\n     startup guard, token-store rewrite; its D5 and D7). Codex's suggested\n   