{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_44","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -Command \"pwsh.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -File './docs/receipts/hv/hv-r1-inspect.ps1' -Task Read -Path './docs/architecture/SessionTurn-Review-AgentContext-Architecture-Amendment-1-Durable-HV.md' -Start 156 -Count 145\"","aggregated_output":"SOURCE ./docs/architecture/SessionTurn-Review-AgentContext-Architecture-Amendment-1-Durable-HV.md totalLines=2785\n156: - R-14 Evidence in the relational store, GZIP at rest (S-13, S-29, S-30). The accepted request is one GZIP blob written at admission; the response log is the run's ordered GZIP chunks; the result document is one GZIP blob written at finalization. No uncompressed copy of an evidence item is stored in the evidence tables, and each evidence item is stored there once (the structured rows of section 12, such as the request's `Context` and the findings, are records the units derive, not copies of the evidence blobs). The audit ledger keeps its own history of every evidence write, as it does for every audited entity: develop's ledger snapshots every property of an audited entity, binary columns included (`src/QBrainAi.Storage/McpDbContext.cs:1540-1589`), compressed with GZIP (`src/QBrainAi.Storage/Entities/DataAuditLogEntity.cs:119-147`), and a removal never changes it (R-20, I-13). Retrieval returns `application/gzip` (download name `<hvRequestId>-<kind>.jsonl.gz` or `.json.gz`) or, when asked, the decompressed bytes. The pattern is develop's audit ledger, which stores GZIP payloads in the database (`src/QBrainAi.Storage/Entities/DataAuditLogEntity.cs:66-147`). Rationale: S-23 (\"Single trx at end\") and S-29 (the database audit log) both hold only when the evidence is in the same transactional store as the result, and no crash window between a file system and the database remains. Rejected: `.jsonl.gz` files beside the database, which would need a two-store protocol, keep evidence outside the audit ledger, and split backups.\n157: - R-15 Atomic finalization (S-23). FinalizeHvRequestCommand verifies the evidence and the reviewer output before it opens a transaction, then commits in one SerializableWrite: the result document, the per-turn assessments, the holistic evaluation, the failures, each turn's `hostileReview` through policy HvVerdictMapping (origin `HvFinalization`), each turn's `Final` HV state, the linked responses' model grades and trust decisions (when Mode is not `Off`), the request's `Completed` state, its notification, and the audit rows. The transaction re-checks everything the earlier reads relied on (the run's fence, the chunk manifest, the request and turn states). Crash windows are in section 9.\n158: - R-16 Transactional notification outbox (S-19). Every state change that callers observe writes an `HvNotification` row in its own transaction, numbered by a per-workspace sequence that the same transaction advances, so the sequence has no gaps and no row is visible before its change commits. The notification stream replays rows after the caller's `Last-Event-ID` and then follows new ones (OrderedEventReplay). Delivery is at least once; consumers deduplicate by sequence. `IChangeEventBus` (develop's in-memory bus, `src/QBrainAi.Services/Notifications/IChangeEventBus.cs:4`) is used only to wake waiting streams and the worker, never as the record.\n159: - R-17 Incidents through the triage endpoint (S-15, S-26). An execution failure writes an `HvIncident` row in the failure's transaction; FileHvIncidentCommand then submits it through `ITriageService` with its `dedupeKey` (`hv-reviewer-<reviewerId>-<failureCode>` for a failure attributable to the reviewer, `hv-server-<failureCode>` otherwise, section 12), which triage groups as `dedupe:<key>` (`src/QBrainAi.Services/Services/TriageService.cs:1475-1476`), and records the returned report and group ids. A filing that fails stays `Pending` and is retried by the worker host's sweep. Rejected: new incident tables with their own deduplication (S-26: \"File through endpoint\").\n160: - R-18 Honest ETA (S-18, P-03). The ETA a caller sees is the reviewer's own latest progress record (`percentComplete`, `etaSeconds`), with `etaStatus` `Reported` and the record's time; with no progress record it is `etaStatus` `Unknown` and no number. The server never estimates.\n161: - R-19 Audit participation and hard stop (S-29). Every HV entity carries `IsDeleted`, so `McpDbContext` writes its ledger rows for every HV mutation in the same `SaveChanges` (develop audits only entities with that property, `src/QBrainAi.Storage/McpDbContext.cs:1620-1627`). The ledger is append-only for HV: no HV unit or process updates or deletes a ledger row (I-13). Because develop skips the ledger when the audit table is unavailable (`McpDbContext.cs:1507-1538`), every HV unit first calls the shared component AuditLedgerGuard, which refuses with 503 `audit_unavailable` and writes nothing when the ledger cannot be written. Evidence reads (GetHvEvidenceQuery, GetHvResultQuery, and the output stream) write an explicit `read` ledger row before returning bytes, and a read whose row cannot be written is refused (the \"protected-log access\" of draft 213 participates in the audit log, S-29).\n162: - R-20 Retention until removal (S-29). No unit deletes, expires, or compacts HV evidence except RemoveHvEvidenceCommand, an explicit call that removes the evidence bytes of one request, keeps its result rows, hashes, and sizes, records who removed it and why, and is audited. It clears the evidence tables only and never changes or deletes an audit ledger row: the ledger keeps the history of the evidence's writes and gains the record of the removal, because an audit log is never purged (I-13). There are no roles: any caller with the workspace key can call it, as S-29 requires.\n163: - R-21 Self-review independence (S-28). No HV unit, policy, or process reads `agentReview`. The turn snapshot sent to a reviewer omits both review records (shared component TurnSnapshotBuilder), so a reviewer never sees a self-review grade. S-12 (a plugin list of missing agent reviews) is met by the foundation's Stop-gate remedy, which already lists each open turn's missing `agentReview` (foundation section 3.4, Stop gate, and `Get-CloseRemedy`, section 3.5.6), and is not an HV input. Reconciliation with the foundation's completion gate: HV admits only terminal turns (R-05), and the foundation's gate needs an `agentReview` status of `Pass`, `Fail`, or `Other` before a turn can close `completed` (foundation section 5.3, CompletionPolicy); that is a rule of closing a turn, not of HV, it is met by `Other` with a note as well as by a grade, and a turn closed `failed` or `canceled` needs no agent review at all; HV admission, execution, and verdict read none of it, and FR-MCP-HVPIPE-013 proves that every terminal turn is admitted and judged identically whatever its self-review.\n164: - R-22 Verdict only (S-25). No HV unit writes TODO state. A result names the reviewed turns' `todoId` values for information only.\n165: - R-23 Requirement references (S-14). The submitted context is stored as written (Markdown, YAML, or text, declared by `contextFormat`) and parsed for requirement references: FR, TR, and TEST ids of the form `<KIND>-<AREA>-<NNN>` with optional further dash-separated area segments, acceptance criteria written as `<FR id>-AC<NNN>` or `<FR id> ac-<n>`, and use cases by their store id or exact title. Each reference is resolved against the workspace's effective requirements and use cases and handed to the reviewer as a delineated list with its resolution status; an unresolved reference is listed as unresolved, never refused (shared component RequirementReferenceExtractor).\n166: - R-24 The reviewer's own turn (P-06). The brief tells the reviewer to log its review as its own session-log turn, classified `Chore` so that its turn never needs an HV of its own (foundation completion policy: Chore needs no hostile review), and to store its entire result there. Finalization requires that turn to exist, to be persisted (not a failsafe or queued record), to have been created after the run started, and to contain the run's `hv_verdict` record byte for byte in its stored normal form (V-6). A reviewer whose host cannot log turns fails its NONCE with `reviewer_turn_logging_unsupported`. The reviewer's plugin runs as a host session of its own: a review or probe run never reads or changes the plugin state or the session-log session of a live session of the same agent in the workspace (FR-MCP-HVPIPE-008-AC008), which matters because a reviewer starts in the workspace (section 16.2) and Grok's headless hooks write their host state under the working directory's `.mcpServer/grok/` (VI-04).\n167: - R-25 The HostileReview subsystem stays separate. FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW-001 to -006 (its queue, artifact links, findings, and query surface) are not changed by this amendment, as the foundation already states (foundation section 5.4); the problems found in it (section 21, P1 to P3) are tracked in REMED-HVPIPELINE-001. It stays separate (D-02, settled): it reviews artifacts (TODO, FR/TR/TEST, plans, files, commits; FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW-002) while this pipeline reviews session turns (S-03), the approved foundation keeps it separate, and consolidating it is scope the operator has not named. Its missing worker (P1) is built on this amendment's execution components (process HvWorkerHost and the reviewer adapters), so reviewer execution exists once (the DRY ruling).\n168: - R-26 Scope limits. One request names turns of one MCP session. `MaxTurnsPerRequest` (default 200, the foundation's `HvRequest:MaxTurns` default) bounds every scope; a session scope above it is refused with 400 `hv_scope_too_large` naming the count, never truncated.\n169: - R-27 One open review per turn. A turn is `Pending` in at most one open request (a unique filtered index on the turn key where the item is `Pending`); admission over a pending turn is refused with 409 `hv_turns_pending` naming the turns and their requests, and over a final turn with 409 `hv_turns_already_reviewed` (S-06, S-20).\n170: - R-28 Cancellation by anyone (S-18). `Queued` becomes `Canceled` at once; `Running` becomes `CancelRequested`, and the worker stops the process at its next heartbeat and closes the run `Canceled`; `Finalizing`, `Completed`, `Failed`, and `Canceled` refuse with 409 `hv_request_not_cancelable`. A cancel and a finalization of the same request are serialized on the request row, so exactly one of them commits.\n171: - R-29 Worker units are internal. ClaimHvRunCommand, RecordHvRunHeartbeatCommand, AppendHvRunOutputCommand, CloseHvRunOutputCommand, FailHvRunCommand, ExpireHvRunLeasesCommand, FinalizeHvRequestCommand, and FileHvIncidentCommand have no REST route, MCP tool, or client method; only the worker host dispatches them, in-process, through `IDispatcher`. The public mutations are submit, cancel, reviewer update, NONCE, and evidence removal.\n172: - R-30 Plugins consume notifications through the REPL. Plugins never call REST (foundation section 3); the REPL gains a streaming verb that follows the notification stream, and the PowerShell core runs it in the background per host session (process WatchHvNotifications), keeping the last sequence in the host session's state and handing completions of requests that host session submitted to the next prompt as context.\n173: - R-31 The 98/98 bar for the overall verdict (P-05). The overall verdict is `AGREE` only when the holistic correctness and completeness both meet `max(configured hostile threshold, 0.98)` (ScoreVerdict, foundation section 3.5.4) and no failure is FAIL or UNKNOWN; per-turn hostile reviews keep the foundation's configured hostile thresholds through HvVerdictMapping.\n174: \n175: ## 6. Amended foundation clauses\n176: \n177: Each amendment names the foundation clause it replaces and states the replacement; nothing else in the named section changes.\n178: \n179: - A-01 Foundation section 3.1, \"Trust\" and \"Model grade\", and section 6.1, fourth bullet: \"model grades come only from the configured grader (default Codex, `gpt-6-astra`, `max`, the plan's HV reviewer)\" and \"this effort builds no grading worker or HV pipeline\" are replaced by: model grades come only from FinalizeHvRequestCommand, for the reviewer the pipeline assigned to the request (R-03); the HV pipeline is this amendment.\n180: - A-02 Foundation section 3 (\"Process\") and section 8.1: \"a Codex gpt-6-astra max hostile validation\" for every gate is replaced by: a hostile validation by the reviewer the operator names for the session, never of the requesting agent's family (P-01). The HV of a plan's own work does not use the pipeline that plan is building until that pipeline has passed its own end-to-end verification (section 20.3).\n181: - A-03 Foundation section 6.2: `ModelGrader:AgentName`, `ModelGrader:RequiredModel`, `ModelGrader:RequiredEffort`, `ModelGrader:ReceiptRoot`, and `HvRequest:MaxTurns` are removed; the reviewer pool and `MaxTurnsPerRequest` of `HostileValidationOptions` (section 10) replace them. The startup validation of an empty grader identity and receipt root is removed with them.\n182: - A-04 Foundation sections 6.3 (`AgentContextHvRequestEntity`, `AgentContextHvRequestItemEntity`), 6.4 (`CreateAgentContextHvRequestRequest`, `AgentContextHvRequestResult`, `AgentContextHvRequestQuery`), 6.5 (CreateAgentContextHvRequestCommand, GetAgentContextHvRequestQuery, QueryAgentContextHvRequestsQuery with their nested activities), 6.7 (their sequences), and Addendum A.9 are superseded by `HvRequest`, `HvRequestTurn`, `HvRequestResponse` (section 12), SubmitHvRequestCommand, GetHvRequestQuery, QueryHvRequestsQuery (section 14), and Addendum B. Every foundation reference to an HV request's `HvRequestId` (the grade's root id and foreign key, the finding's root id) now names `HvRequest`. ResponseStatusTransition's side effect (\"the response's `Pending` items in `Open` HV requests become `Closed` with that reason, and each request left with no `Pending` item becomes `Completed`\") becomes: the response's `Pending` `HvRequestResponse` items become `Closed` with that reason; the HV request's own state is decided by its turns and is never completed by a response transition.\n183: - A-05 Foundation section 6.5, RecordModelGradeCommand: its caller becomes FinalizeHvRequestCommand only (internal dispatch inside the finalization transaction, origin `HvFinalization`); the REST route `POST /qbrainai/agent-context/responses/{responseId}/grades/model`, the MCP tool `agent_context_record_model_grade`, the client and REPL methods, and `New-McpAgentContextRecordModelGradeParams` are removed; 403 `agent_context_grader_not_allowed` becomes \"grader is not the reviewer assigned to the HV request\"; the eight receipt and reviewer checks of foundation section 6.3 are replaced by the verification steps V-1 to V-8 of policy HvFinalVerification (section 15), which finalization runs before it dispatches the grade; the receipt path, rollout, and thread columns of `AgentResponseGradeEntity` become the run reference `RunId` and the evidence hashes (section 12).\n184: - A-06 Foundation section 3.1, \"Review record\" (\"One record per kind per turn; latest write wins\") and section 5.2, RecordSessionTurnReviewCommand: on agent surfaces a `hostileReview` write may carry only `status` `Other`; `Pass`, `Fail`, `Running`, and `hvVerdict` are refused with 403 `review_hostile_pipeline_only` (R-06); `Unnecessary` and `Unstarted` stay server-managed as before. Policy HvVerdictMapping is applied only by FinalizeHvRequestCommand. Section 5.4, process ConvertHvReceiptToReview, and its plugin function `ConvertFrom-McpHvReceipt` are retired, with the `hvReceiptPath` parameter of the plugin verbs.\n185: - A-07 Foundation section 3.1, \"Reviews remain writable on terminal turns\", section 3.4 (section PUT and DELETE), and section 3.5.3, TurnWritePipeline: every turn write, review write, review reset, section replace or clear, dialog append, title change, and session-level replace or delete that would change a turn whose HV state is `Pending` or `Final`, or any of that turn's children, is refused with 409 `turn_immutable` (`details.reason` `hv_pending` or `hv_final`, `details.hvRequestId`), from every origin except `HvFinalization` (policy HvTurnFreeze, R-04). TurnWritePipeline runs that policy after TurnLocator and before every other policy.\n186: - A-08 Foundation section 3.5.3, `WriteOrigin`: a fifth origin `HvFinalization` is added; it is exempt from the completion gate (it never moves a turn's status) and is the only origin that may write a turn whose HV state is `Pending`.\n187: - A-09 Foundation section 3.1, check 7 (`reviewer_independent`, \"not a different agent name, so an independently launched Codex reviewer grades a Codex-authored response\"): replaced by V-5, which requires both a separate session and a reviewer id different from the submitter's and the author's (R-08, S-17, P-01, P-07).\n188: - A-10 Foundation section 7.8 (UC-7, \"Record a hostile validation review with receipts\") is replaced by the use cases of section 18 (UC-HV-03 to UC-HV-05); foundation section 7.12 (UC \"Submit and grade an agent response\") keeps its flow, and its model grade now comes from UC-HV-05.\n189: - A-11 Foundation section 5.1, `SessionLogTurnEntity`: two columns are added, `HvState` (16: `None`, `Pending`, `Final`; database default `None`) and `HvRequestId` (128, null while `None`), written only by the migration that adds them, which sets `HvState` `Final` (with `HvRequestId` null) on every turn whose hostile review is already `Pass` or `Fail`, because S-07 forbids updates to a turn with an HV review, and then by SubmitHvRequestCommand, CancelHvRequestCommand, FailHvRunCommand and ExpireHvRunLeasesCommand (both through policy HvRetryDecision), and FinalizeHvRequestCommand, through policy HvTurnFreeze's state changes (Addendum B.8).\n190: - A-12 Foundation section 6.1, \"Existing records and stores\", the FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW bullet: unchanged in substance (R-25); its phrase \"because this effort builds no grading worker\" is replaced by \"because turn HV runs through the HostileValidation pipeline of Amendment 1\".\n191: \n192: ## 7. Lifecycles\n193: \n194: ### 7.1 HV request\n195: \n196: ```mermaid\n197: stateDiagram-v2\n198:     [*] --> Queued: SubmitHvRequestCommand, turns frozen Pending\n199:     Queued --> Running: ClaimHvRunCommand, run attempt claimed\n200:     Queued --> Canceled: CancelHvRequestCommand\n201:     Running --> Queued: attempt Failed or Abandoned, attempts left\n202:     Running --> CancelRequested: CancelHvRequestCommand\n203:     CancelRequested --> Canceled: worker stops the process, or the lease expires\n204:     Running --> Finalizing: CloseHvRunOutputCommand, output closed\n205:     Finalizing --> Completed: FinalizeHvRequestCommand commits, turns Final\n206:     Finalizing --> Queued: verification failed, attempts left\n207:     Running --> Failed: attempts spent\n208:     Finalizing --> Failed: verification failed, attempts spent\n209:     Canceled --> [*]: turns released to None\n210:     Failed --> [*]: turns released to None\n211:     Completed --> [*]\n212: ```\n213: \n214: - `Queued` and `Running` hold the turns `Pending`. `Completed`, `Failed`, and `Canceled` are terminal and never change again (S-06, S-20). A request whose attempt fails returns to `Queued` with its assignment re-evaluated (R-07); with no eligible reviewer it stays `Queued`, its ETA `Unknown`, until a reviewer passes a NONCE or the request is canceled, so no work is lost to an outage.\n215: - `CancelRequested` is not terminal: the request is canceled when the worker has stopped the process or when the lease of a dead worker expires.\n216: \n217: ### 7.2 Run attempt\n218: \n219: ```mermaid\n220: stateDiagram-v2\n221:     [*] --> Claimed: ClaimHvRunCommand, lease and fence\n222:     Claimed --> Running: worker started the reviewer process\n223:     Running --> Running: heartbeat renews the lease, output appended\n224:     Running --> OutputClosed: CloseHvRunOutputCommand\n225:     Running --> Failed: FailHvRunCommand, execution failure\n226:     Running --> Canceled: cancel observed at a heartbeat\n227:     Claimed --> Abandoned: lease expired, ExpireHvRunLeasesCommand\n228:     Running --> Abandoned: lease expired, ExpireHvRunLeasesCommand\n229:     OutputClosed --> Succeeded: FinalizeHvRequestCommand committed, or probe passed\n230:     OutputClosed --> Failed: verification failed, or probe failed\n231:     Succeeded --> [*]\n232:     Failed --> [*]\n233:     Abandoned --> [*]\n234:     Canceled --> [*]\n235: ```\n236: \n237: A run is either a `Review` run of an HV request or a `Probe` run of a reviewer's NONCE (R-10); both use the same lease, fence, output, and close units, so the probe needs no second execution path.\n238: \n239: ### 7.3 Reviewer readiness\n240: \n241: ```mermaid\n242: stateDiagram-v2\n243:     [*] --> Unverified: seeded from configuration\n244:     Unverified --> Online: NONCE probe passed\n245:     Unverified --> Offline: NONCE probe failed\n246:     Online --> Offline: execution failure, incident filed\n247:     Offline --> Online: NONCE probe passed\n248:     Online --> Unverified: strategy, model, or effort changed\n249:     Offline --> Unverified: strategy, model, or effort changed\n250: ```\n251: \n252: `Enabled` is a separate flag set by UpdateHvReviewerCommand: a disabled reviewer is never eligible and is never NONCEd automatically, whatever its readiness. Eligibility (policy HvReviewerEligibility) is enabled, `Online`, and neither the submitter nor the author of the reviewed turns (R-08).\n253: \n254: ### 7.4 HV state of a turn\n255: \n256: ```mermaid\n257: stateDiagram-v2\n258:     [*] --> None\n259:     None --> Pending: admitted by SubmitHvRequestCommand\n260:     Pending --> None: request Canceled or Failed\n261:     Pending --> Final: FinalizeHvRequestCommand\n262:     Final --> [*]\n263: ```\n264: \n265: While `Pending` or `Final`, policy HvTurnFreeze refuses every write to the turn and its children except finalization's own (A-07, A-08). A turn that already holds a `Pass` or `Fail` hostile review when the column is added starts `Final`, with no request id (A-11).\n266: \n267: ## 8. Invariants and limits\n268: \n269: Invariants, each owned by the named component and proved by the acceptance criteria of section 19:\n270: \n271: - I-01 A turn is `Pending` in at most one open request, and a `Final` turn is never admitted again (R-27, HvAdmissionEligibility).\n272: - I-02 The content a reviewer reviewed is the content finalized: no write reaches a `Pending` turn between admission and finalization (HvTurnFreeze), and finalization re-hashes each turn's snapshot and refuses on any difference (V-2).\n273: - I-03 A request is finalized at most once; a second finalization returns the stored result and writes nothing (FinalizeHvRequestCommand).\n274: - I-04 Nothing of a finalization is visible unless all of it is: result, per-turn assessments, holistic evaluation, failures, turn reviews, turn finality, grades, trust decisions, request state, notification, and audit rows commit together (R-15, S-23).\n275: - I-05 Only the run that holds the current fence can append output, close output, or fail; a stale fence changes nothing (R-13).\n276: - I-06 The response log of a finalized run is complete: its chunks are contiguous from sequence 1, each chunk's SHA-256 matches its decompressed bytes, the chain hash over the chunks' SHA-256 values equals the run's `OutputChainSha256` (section 12), every line parses as one JSON object, and the log contains exactly one `hv_verdict` record (V-1).\n277: - I-07 The reviewer is never the submitter or the author of the reviewed turns, by canonical id (V-5, R-08).\n278: - I-08 Varying a turn's `agentReview` (absent, any status, any score) changes neither admission, nor assignment, nor the brief's bytes, nor the verdict mapping (R-21).\n279: - I-09 No HV unit changes TODO state (R-22).\n280: - I-10 No HV write happens without its audit ledger rows (R-19).\n281: - I-11 Evidence bytes are removed from the evidence tables only by RemoveHvEvidenceCommand (R-20).\n282: - I-12 Every notification row is committed with the change it reports, and sequences are gap-free per workspace (R-16).\n283: - I-13 The audit ledger is append-only: no HV unit or process updates or deletes a ledger row, and a removal of evidence leaves every earlier ledger row unchanged (R-19, R-20).\n284: \n285: Limits stated plainly:\n286: \n287: - L-01 Agent identity rests on the workspace key: every caller holds the same key, so the server cannot prove which agent sent a request. Exclusion is enforced against the agent of the submitting turn the server finds and the agent of the reviewed session; an `Operator` submission excludes only the author (R-09). This is the foundation's existing identity model (foundation section 6.3: \"every agent of a workspace authenticates with the workspace key\"), not a new weakness.\n288: - L-02 A reviewer can run more than once for one request (at-least-once execution): a worker crash after the reviewer finished but before its output was closed leads to a new attempt. The request still finalizes at most once (I-03), and every attempt's output stays as evidence.\n289: - L-03 Incident deduplication is per triage group: a filing retried after a crash between the triage commit and the recording of its report id can add a second report to the same group, never a second group (R-17).\n290: - L-04 A reviewer CLI that cannot report its runtime model and effort cannot pass a NONCE, so it stays `Unverified` or `Offline` (R-10, P-04). Verified on 2026-10-16 (VI-01): Codex, Claude, Grok, and Copilot record both in their own run records; Cline records the model and only whether thinking was on, never the effort level, so Cline cannot pass a NONCE until it records the effort of a run (D-06, settled: P-04 fails closed).\n291: \n292: ## 9. Crash windows and recovery\n293: \n294: Each window names what is durable at the crash and what recovers it. \"Sweep\" is the worker host's periodic pass (process HvWorkerHost).\n295: \n296: - W-01 Crash after the submit commit, before the worker wakes: the request is `Queued` in the store; the next poll claims it.\n297: - W-02 Crash after a claim, before the process starts: the run's lease expires; the sweep marks the attempt `Abandoned` and the request `Queued`, or `Failed` or `Canceled` with its turns released when its attempts are spent or a cancel was requested (ExpireHvRunLeasesCommand, policy HvRetryDecision).\n298: - W-03 Worker crash while the reviewer runs: the reviewer's output pipe closes with the worker; the output already appended is durable; the lease expires and the sweep queues a new attempt. A worker that is alive but lost its lease (a pause longer than the lease) gets `stale_fence` on its next write and stops its process (I-05).\n299: - W-04 Crash after the reviewer exits, before CloseHvRunOutputCommand commits: as W-03, a new attempt runs (L-02).\n300: - W-05 Crash after CloseHvRunOutputCommand commits (request `Finalizing`), before finalization: the sweep dispatches FinalizeHvRequestCommand for every `Finalizing` request (idempotent, I-03).\nEXIT_CODE=0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_45","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -Command \"pwsh.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -File './docs/receipts/hv/hv-r1-inspect.ps1' -Task Read -Path './.github/copilot-instructions.md' -Count 350\"","aggregated_output":"SOURCE ./.github/copilot-instructions.md totalLines=81\n1: # Copilot Instructions — QBrainAi\n2: \n3: **Agent Identity:** When posting to the MCP session log, use the agent's actual identity in Pascal-Case for `sourceType` and the session ID prefix. Do not use inaccurate aliases, lowercase names, or legacy placeholders. Example: `Codex`.\n4: For specific operational instructions (session bootstrap, turn logging lifecycle, helper command order), follow `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml`.\n5: \n6: ## Response Formatting\n7: \n8: - Do not use table-style output in responses.\n9: - Use concise bullets or short paragraphs instead.\n10: \n11: ## Terminal Usage\n12: \n13: - Do NOT set focus to the terminal window.\n14: - Do NOT write multiline commands to the terminal; use temporary scripts instead.\n15: - Use **only `pwsh.exe`** (PowerShell 7+). Do not invoke `powershell.exe` (Windows PowerShell 5.1) for scripts, examples, or tooling.\n16: \n17: ## Session Logging Requirements\n18: \n19: - Follow `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml` for workflow and helper command order.\n20: - Persist session log changes immediately after each meaningful update; do not batch unsaved turn changes.\n21: - For each turn, include as much detail as available: interpretation, response/status, actions (type/status/filePath), files modified, design decisions, requirements discovered, blockers, and context references.\n22: \n23: ## Build, Test, Lint\n24: \n25: ```powershell\n26: # Build (via Nuke)\n27: ./build.ps1 Compile\n28: # or: dotnet build src\\QBrainAi.Support.Mcp -c Debug\n29: \n30: # Run all unit tests (via Nuke)\n31: ./build.ps1 Test\n32: # or individual projects:\n33: # dotnet test tests\\QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.Tests -c Debug\n34: # dotnet test tests\\QBrainAi.Client.Tests -c Debug\n35: # dotnet test tests\\Build.Tests -c Debug\n36: \n37: # Run integration tests (uses CustomWebApplicationFactory, in-memory EF)\n38: dotnet test tests\\QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.IntegrationTests -c Debug\n39: \n40: # Run a single test by fully-qualified name\n41: dotnet test tests\\QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.Tests -c Debug --filter \"FullyQualifiedName~TodoServiceTests.QueryAsync_NoFilters_ReturnsAllItems\"\n42: \n43: # Run tests in a single class\n44: dotnet test tests\\QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.Tests -c Debug --filter \"FullyQualifiedName~TodoServiceTests\"\n45: \n46: # Validate appsettings config (via Nuke)\n47: ./build.ps1 ValidateConfig\n48: # or: pwsh.exe -NoLogo -NoProfile -NonInteractive -File ./scripts/Validate-McpConfig.ps1\n49: \n50: # Validate requirements traceability\n51: ./build.ps1 ValidateTraceability\n52: \n53: # Markdown lint (docs only)\n54: # CI uses markdownlint-cli2 with .markdownlint-cli2.yaml\n55: ```\n56: \n57: ## Architecture\n58: \n59: **QBrainAi** is a standalone ASP.NET Core 9 server providing context retrieval, TODO management, session logging, repository operations, and GitHub issue sync for AI agents. It exposes functionality via two transports:\n60: \n61: - **HTTP REST API** — Controllers under `src/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp/Controllers/` (routes at `/qbrainai/*`).\n62: - **MCP Streamable HTTP** — `app.MapMcp(\"/mcp-transport\")` using ModelContextProtocol.AspNetCore.\n63: - **MCP STDIO** — `--transport stdio` flag; same tools as HTTP via `McpStdio/FwhMcpTools.cs`.\n64: \n65: **Workspace model**: The primary host manages multiple workspaces, each getting its own in-process Kestrel `WebApplication` (not child processes). Workspace config lives in `appsettings.json` under `Mcp:Workspaces` (not in the database). One workspace is the \"primary\" — served by the host process directly; others get child `WebApplication` instances via `WorkspaceAppFactory`.\n66: \n67: **Search pipeline**: Hybrid search combines SQLite FTS5 full-text with HNSW vector similarity (384-dim all-MiniLM-L6-v2 ONNX embeddings). `HybridSearchService` fuses both with BM25 scoring.\n68: \n69: **Storage**: EF Core with SQLite (`McpDbContext`). TODO items use a pluggable backend — either YAML file-backed (`TodoService`) or SQLite table-backed (`SqliteTodoService`), selected via `Mcp:TodoStorage:Provider`.\n70: \n71: **Multi-instance**: `McpInstanceResolver` overlays per-instance config from `Mcp:Instances:{name}` onto base `Mcp:*` settings.\n72: \n73: ## Key Conventions\n74: \n75: ### XML Documentation Required\n76: \n77: `TreatWarningsAsErrors` and `GenerateDocumentationFile` are enabled globally in `Directory.Build.props`. All public types and members must have XML doc comments or the build fails (CS1591). Use `/// <inheritdoc />` for interface implementations. Test projects are not exempt: test classes and test methods must include XML docs that state what is being tested, what data/fixtures are used, why that data/fixtures are used, and which requirement IDs are validated.\n78: \n79: ### Requirement Traceability Comments\n80: \n81: All source files reference their FR/TR requirement IDs in doc comments (e.g., `/// <summary>TR-PLANNED-CORE-013: Constructor.</summary>`). When adding new functionality, reference the relevant requirement ID from `docs/Project/Functional-Requirements.md` and `docs/Project/Technical-Requirements.md`.\nEXIT_CODE=0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_43","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -Command \"pwsh.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -File './docs/receipts/hv/hv-r1-inspect.ps1' -Task Read -Path '/home/sharpninja/.codex/plugins/cache/mcpserver-codex-plugin/mcpserver/1.120.0/skills/requirements/SKILL.md' -Count 170\"","aggregated_output":"SOURCE /home/sharpninja/.codex/plugins/cache/mcpserver-codex-plugin/mcpserver/1.120.0/skills/requirements/SKILL.md totalLines=548\n1: ---\n2: name: Requirements Management\n3: description: Use when the user asks to \"list requirements\", \"add requirement\", \"create FR\", \"create TR\", \"create test requirement\", \"generate requirements document\", or \"ingest requirements\".\n4: version: 0.1.0\n5: ---\n6: \n7: # Requirements Management\n8: \n9: ## Overview\n10: \n11: To manage functional requirements (FR), technical requirements (TR), test requirements (TEST), and their traceability mappings, use this plugin's declared hook/wrapper (or the REPL transport) for the `workflow.requirements.*` namespace. Do not substitute raw REST calls, generic `PowerShell.MCP wrapper`, helper modules, or another agent's plugin for normal requirements work.\n12: \n13: The plugin wrapper validates documented params and emits single-line JSON to REPL stdio. Any direct REPL diagnostic call must use one single-line JSON request envelope, not formatted YAML.\n14: \n15: The database is the source of truth for requirements. Markdown files are import/export projections only. Every operation is scoped to the workspace resolved from the signed marker, and generated workspace output must contain only the requested workspace's FR, TR, TEST, and traceability links.\n16: \n17: ## Initialization\n18: \n19: Call `workflow.sessionlog.bootstrap` through this plugin's declared hook/wrapper to initialize the session log subsystem before issuing any workflow commands. This call is idempotent and should be made once per conversation context.\n20: \n21: ```yaml\n22: type: request\n23: payload:\n24:   requestId: req-20260409T120000Z-bootstrap-001\n25:   method: workflow.sessionlog.bootstrap\n26:   params: {}\n27: ```\n28: \n29: ## Requirement Scope Layers\n30: \n31: Plugin version 1.13.0 and newer exposes native layer operations. Treat this section as the layer API reference when plugin status reports these methods; do not ask the operator or inspect wrapper source just to learn the parameter shapes.\n32: \n33: - `workflow.requirements.listLayers` / `req_list_layers` / `client.Requirements.ListRequirementLayersAsync`: omit params.\n34: - `workflow.requirements.createLayer` / `req_create_layer` / `client.Requirements.CreateRequirementLayerAsync`: required params are `key`, `order`, and `name`; optional params are `description` and `scopeEndLayerKey`.\n35: - `workflow.requirements.updateLayer` / `req_update_layer` / `client.Requirements.UpdateRequirementLayerAsync`: required param is `key`; optional params are `name`, `description`, and `scopeEndLayerKey`. Layer `order` is immutable; create a new layer rather than attempting to update `order`.\n36: - `workflow.requirements.effective` / `req_effective` / `client.Requirements.GetEffectiveRequirementsAsync`: optional param is `layerKey`; omit `layerKey` to use the active workspace layer.\n37: \n38: Use camelCase exactly in YAML:\n39: \n40: ```yaml\n41: type: request\n42: payload:\n43:   requestId: req-20260409T115900Z-listlayers-001\n44:   method: workflow.requirements.listLayers\n45:   params: {}\n46: ```\n47: \n48: ```yaml\n49: type: request\n50: payload:\n51:   requestId: req-20260409T115901Z-createlayer-001\n52:   method: workflow.requirements.createLayer\n53:   params:\n54:     key: future\n55:     order: 20\n56:     name: Future Layer\n57:     description: Requirements that are documented but not currently enforceable\n58: ```\n59: \n60: ```yaml\n61: type: request\n62: payload:\n63:   requestId: req-20260409T115902Z-updatelayer-001\n64:   method: workflow.requirements.updateLayer\n65:   params:\n66:     key: future\n67:     name: Future Layer\n68:     scopeEndLayerKey: release\n69: ```\n70: \n71: ```yaml\n72: type: request\n73: payload:\n74:   requestId: req-20260409T115903Z-effective-001\n75:   method: workflow.requirements.effective\n76:   params:\n77:     layerKey: future\n78: ```\n79: \n80: FR, TR, TEST, and batch create/update commands also accept requirement scope fields:\n81: \n82: - `scopeStartLayerKey`: first layer where the requirement is effective.\n83: - `scopeEndLayerKey`: last layer where the requirement is effective; omit for open-ended scope.\n84: \n85: Omit both fields for requirements that are effective in the default/current layer.\n86: \n87: ## Requirement ID Conventions\n88: \n89: Three ID spaces are in use:\n90: \n91: - **FR** - Functional Requirements: `^FR-[A-Z]+-\\d{3}$`, e.g. `FR-MCP-001`, `FR-AUTH-042`\n92: - **TR** - Technical Requirements: `^TR-[A-Z]+-[A-Z]+-\\d{3}$`, e.g. `TR-MCP-ARCH-001`, `TR-AUTH-SEC-002`\n93: - **TEST** - Test Requirements: `^TEST-[A-Z]+-\\d{3}$`, e.g. `TEST-MCP-001`, `TEST-AUTH-003`\n94: \n95: All IDs must be uppercase. TR IDs require both an area and a subarea segment; FR and TEST IDs require only an area segment.\n96: \n97: ## Functional Requirements (FR)\n98: \n99: ### Listing FRs\n100: \n101: To retrieve a filtered list of functional requirements:\n102: \n103: ```yaml\n104: type: request\n105: payload:\n106:   requestId: req-20260409T120000Z-listfr-001\n107:   method: workflow.requirements.listFr\n108:   params:\n109:     area: MCP\n110:     status: in_progress\n111: ```\n112: \n113: All params are optional; omit to return all FRs. The result contains an `items` array:\n114: \n115: ```yaml\n116: type: result\n117: payload:\n118:   requestId: req-20260409T120000Z-listfr-001\n119:   result:\n120:     items:\n121:       - id: FR-MCP-001\n122:         title: Agent authentication\n123:         description: System must authenticate AI agents via API key\n124:         status: completed\n125:         priority: critical\n126:         area: MCP\n127:         createdAt: 2026-03-01T10:00:00Z\n128:         updatedAt: 2026-04-09T12:00:00Z\n129:     totalCount: 1\n130: ```\n131: \n132: Valid `status` values: `pending`, `in_progress`, `completed`, `deferred`.\n133: \n134: ### Getting a Single FR\n135: \n136: To fetch one functional requirement by ID:\n137: \n138: ```yaml\n139: type: request\n140: payload:\n141:   requestId: req-20260409T120001Z-getfr-001\n142:   method: workflow.requirements.getFr\n143:   params:\n144:     id: FR-MCP-001\n145: ```\n146: \n147: ### Creating an FR\n148: \n149: To record a new functional requirement:\n150: \n151: ```yaml\n152: type: request\n153: payload:\n154:   requestId: req-20260409T120002Z-createfr-001\n155:   method: workflow.requirements.createFr\n156:   params:\n157:     id: FR-MCP-003\n158:     title: Context search\n159:     description: System must support semantic search across workspace documents\n160:     priority: high\n161:     area: MCP\n162:     notes: Use hybrid search combining BM25 and vector embeddings\n163: ```\n164: \n165: Required fields: `id`, `title`, `description`, `priority`, `area`. The `notes` field is optional.\n166: \n167: ### Acceptance Criteria\n168: \n169: FR, TR, and TEST create/update commands accept structured `acceptanceCriteria` entries with the same shape used by TODO execution criteria:\n170: \nEXIT_CODE=0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
