ce` call was refused, \"a headless run cannot ask for approval\", and the run ended `stopReason` `cancelled` with exit 0); structural for Copilot `--available-tools view grep glob` (its session lists `bash`, `create`, and `edit` as disabled, `tool_count` 3); not established for Claude `--permission-mode plan` (its model declined before any write call; a read-only Bash call was accepted) or Cline `--plan` (a `run_commands` shell call ran in plan mode); Copilot's `--deny-tool write --deny-tool shell` and `--mode plan` exited 0 with no output at all and are not used. Open for H1: whether each plugin logs its turn behind its barrier, verified in the live check on a separate server instance (section 20.2), never against the live server, because the operator's rule puts integration checks on a temporary instance and the probe writes reviewer turns.\n2499: - VI-04 That each reviewer host's plugin logs a `Chore` turn when the server launches it (R-24, V-6). Observed: a headless Grok run loads the MCP plugin and its hooks and wrote `.mcpServer/grok/session-state.yaml` into its working directory (FR-MCP-HVPIPE-008-AC008); `claude -p` loads plugins and hooks unless `--bare`, and its `--safe-mode` init still listed the mcpserver plugin; Codex runs hooks only with persisted trust or `--dangerously-bypass-hook-trust` (its help); Cline reads `~/.cline/hooks`; Copilot not observed. Open for H1: the logged turn itself.\n2500: - VI-05 The storage cost of appending at the default flush interval with `MaxConcurrentRuns` runs on SQLite, measured before the default is kept. Bound for the measurement: at most one append per run per `OutputFlushInterval`, so at most 14400 chunks per run hour at 250 ms, each a chunk row, the run's manifest update, and their ledger rows, the chunk's row carrying its own compressed copy of the chunk (R-14). Open for H1: the measurement.\n2501: - VI-06 The column or ordinal that gives a session's turn order. Settled: develop has no ordinal column on `SessionLogTurnEntity` and reads a session's turns by ascending row `Id` (`src/QBrainAi.Services/Services/SessionLogService.cs:504,693,2002`; `src/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp/Services/TransactionGatedSessionLogService.cs:445,476,694`); one federation adapter orders by `Timestamp` instead (`src/QBrainAi.Services/Services/FederationAdapters/FederationStateAdapterImplementations.cs:1063`), which HvScopeResolution does not use.\n2502: - VI-07 That a multi-member response log reads back as one stream. Settled on .NET 10.0.13: one `GZipStream` over three concatenated members returned the exact concatenation (305136 bytes, equal SHA-256), and the first member alone did not (negative control); FR-MCP-HVPIPE-011-AC004 repeats the check on the server's runtime.\n2503: - VI-08 The highest effort each CLI accepts. Settled: Codex `max` (accepted and recorded in its rollout), Claude `max` (recorded in its transcript), Grok `xhigh` (Grok 1.0.60 refuses `max` and lists `xhigh`, `high`, `medium`, `low`), Cline `xhigh` (its `--thinking` levels end at `xhigh`), Copilot `max` (its `--reasoning-effort` values, subject to the configured model); section 10 holds the table, and P9 records where develop's constants differ.\n2504: \n2505: ## 21. Problem register (REMED-HVPIPELINE-001)\n2506: \n2507: The single TODO of problems to fix found while writing this amendment (operator, 2026-10-16: \"keep track of problems to fix as a single todo item that will be pulled into the plan.\"), created and read back through the plugin on 2026-10-16; P10 to P15, the six findings of the Codex review of pull request 81 (review 5554472359 on commit `6dbc384323`, posted 2026-10-16T17:48:12 local, after the merge), were added on 2026-10-17 at the operator's answer \"1. Yes\", each checked against the source of this branch. Each item's full text and receipts are in the TODO; summaries:\n2508: \n2509: - P1 Requirement drift: FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW-003 acceptance criterion 4 (a server-owned leased worker) is checked as satisfied, but no worker is registered and `AcceptReviewerOutputAsync` has no caller in `src` (`HostileReviewServiceCollectionExtensions.cs:11-42`; lease options unread, `HostileReviewWorkerOptions.cs:30-46`).\n2510: - P2 Defect: `AcceptReviewerOutputAsync` overwrites the verdict on every call with no lease, attempt, or fence (`HostileReviewService.cs:196-273`).\n2511: - P3 Requirement drift: FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW-002 lists many artifact types, but only `todo` resolves, and the stored hash is of the TODO id, not its content (`HostileReviewService.cs:294-314`).\n2512: - P4 Audit gap: the ledger is skipped when the audit table is unavailable, and entities without `IsDeleted` are never audited (`McpDbContext.cs:1507-1538,1620-1627`).\n2513: - P5 Reviewer pin drift: the foundation and the HostileReview options pin one reviewer and model, contrary to P-01 and S-02 (A-01 to A-03).\n2514: - P6 Capability gap: the one-shot strategy has no Copilot branch; Copilot runs only through the interactive `copilot-cli` strategy, unqualified for HV (`OneShotCliAgentExecutionStrategy.cs:238-262`, `CopilotCliAgentExecutionStrategy.cs:5-45`).\n2515: - P7 Traceability drift: `EventStreamController` cites FR-MCP-045, which is \"Cross-Workspace TODO Move\" in the store (`EventStreamController.cs:11`).\n2516: - P8 No true live streaming: the one-shot strategy yields output only after the process ends (`OneShotCliAgentExecutionStrategy.cs:57-75`); operator: \"Explains a lot.  Gotta be fixed in the plan.\" Proven fixed only by FR-MCP-HVPIPE-008-AC003.\n2517: - P9 Defect and duplicated constants: develop's highest-effort constants disagree, and one is refused. The one-shot strategy passes Grok `--effort max` and `--reasoning-effort max` (`src/QBrainAi.Services/Services/OneShotCliAgentExecutionStrategy.cs:26,333,335`), which Grok 1.0.60 refuses with exit 1 (\"unknown effort level 'max'; use one of: xhigh, high, medium, low\", probe of 2026-10-16), while `src/QBrainAi.Services/Services/GrokCliAgentExecutionStrategy.cs:25` uses `high`; Codex is pinned to `xhigh` twice (`OneShotCliAgentExecutionStrategy.cs:23`, `src/QBrainAi.Services/Services/CodexCliAgentExecutionStrategy.cs:21`) although Codex accepts and records `max`; Cline's `xhigh` is defined twice (`src/QBrainAi.Common.AgentCli/AgentCliClient.cs:17`, `OneShotCliAgentExecutionStrategy.cs:25`).\n2518: - P10 Unconverted legacy workspace keys (Codex priority 1): `WorkspaceKeyConversion` canonicalizes workspace keys on write and in query parameters but reads stored values unchanged (`src/QBrainAi.Storage/WorkspaceKeyConversion.cs:24-26`), and nothing rewrites stored backslash keys: no migration since 2026-07-01 on any of the three providers, and no other code in `src`, rewrites them (content searches of 2026-10-17). This is the federation plan's undelivered P1.5, the `--workspace-path-rekey` command with its startup guard and token-store key rewrite, by its decisions D5 (a maintenance command, no automatic startup migration) and D7 (no dual-key lookup; the startup guard refuses an unconverted store) (`docs/plans/2026-10-07-federation-fixes.md:166-175`); its handoff says P1.5 \"has not started\" and not to deploy `develop` to a server whose database still holds backslash workspace ids (`docs/handoffs/federation-fixes-handoff-20261016T121400.md:14-16`). Codex's suggested provider migration contradicts D5.\n2519: - P11 Stop-hook proof unusable (Codex priority 2): the stale-cache proof calls `client.SessionLog.GetAsync` (`plugins/core/lib-ps/plugin-hook.ps1:1065`), whose `Task<UnifiedSessionLogDto>` is not among the result types the REPL passthrough awaits, so the call ends in \"Unsupported task result type\" (`src/QBrainAi.Repl.Core/GenericClientPassthrough.cs:243-640`) and the hook reads no proof and answers `Active` (`plugin-hook.ps1:1063-1087`); a supported call would still not carry the `workspacePath`, `requestId`, `outcome`, and `serverStatus` the hook reads (`plugin-hook.ps1:1089-1110`; `src/QBrainAi.Client/Models/SessionLogModels.cs:8-85`). Inference (Codex's, not reproduced): a completed turn behind a stale cache keeps the Stop gate blocked. The code is under FR-MCP-SESSIONLIFE-004, in the stop path the foundation's slice A7 rewrites.\n2520: - P12 Root-relative Windows segment not rooted (Codex priority 2): `IsWindowsRooted` accepts a drive-qualified path and the two UNC spellings only (`src/QBrainAi.Client/WorkspacePathNormalizer.cs:161-164`), so `Combine` (`:120-144`) appends a segment `\\repo` to the root `C:/content` as `C:/content/repo` instead of resolving it to `C:/repo` (inferred from the code, not run). The federation plan delivered `Combine` and `ResolveRepoRootWorkspacePath` (P1.3b, P1.4d, `docs/plans/2026-10-07-federation-fixes.md:109,157`).\n2521: - P13 Legacy GitHub token entries unreadable (Codex priority 2): the token store compares stored entry paths with the normalized key without normalizing the stored side (`src/QBrainAi.Services/Services/FileGitHubWorkspaceTokenStore.cs:53,89,125`; the document read at `:142` normalizes nothing), so an entry stored as `C:\\repo` no longer matches `C:/repo`. The federation plan's P1.5 includes this rewrite (`docs/plans/2026-10-07-federation-fixes.md:172`).\n2522: - P14 Descendants survive the root (Codex priority 2): `Stop-ReplChildTree` calls `Kill(true)` only while the root process is still running (`plugins/core/lib-ps/repl-invoke.ps1:736-738`), so a descendant that outlives the root is not killed by it, contrary to FR-MCP-SESSIONLIFE-004 acceptance criterion 1 (\"process-tree cleanup without a stranded child\"). No open TODO in this workspace names FR-MCP-SESSIONLIFE-004 (keyword queries of 2026-10-17); the foundation plan lists it as related and unchanged.\n2523: - P15 Stop override returns before the turn is handled (Codex priority 2): when the stop hook consumes an acceptance marker for a failed build or an incomplete audit (`plugins/core/lib-ps/plugin-hook.ps1:991-994,1009-1012`), it returns `allow` at `:1172-1174`, before the completion branch at `:1253`, so the stop passes while the turn is still in progress. Codex suggests continuing to the completion; the approved foundation plan instead makes the stop gate block while a delivered request is in progress and never close a turn (PLAN-SESSIONTURNREVIEW-001, slice A7: its FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005 acceptance criteria 1 and 4, which that plan creates and the store does not hold yet (requirement read of 2026-10-17), and its change to TR-MCP-SESSIONLIFE-005, \"`Close-PluginTurnIfNeeded` blocks instead of closing\").\n2524: \n2525: ## 22. Decisions\n2526: \n2527: On 2026-10-16 the operator asked the drafting agent to settle every decision it could from the operator's own instructions (\"look at the totality of the project, of all of my instructions, and see if you can solve them yourself\"). Four are settled from those instructions, each with its source; the operator settled D-04, D-07, and D-08 on 2026-10-17; D-03 stays with the operator, because the operator's rules place it there. A settled decision is part of this proposed amendment and is approved with it (D-03).\n2528: \n2529: - D-01 (settled) Reviewer exclusion by model vendor: exclusion is by agent family and its aliases, not by model vendor (R-08). Sources: S-17 (\"excluding the agent submitting the review\"); P-01, which constrains the agent family and asks separately for the agent and the model; the operator's own configuration, which runs Cline on `gpt-6-astra` (provider `openai-codex`, VI-01 receipt) while keeping Cline and Codex as two reviewers (S-02). Inference, labeled: the operator treats the agent, not the model vendor, as the unit of independence.\n2530: - D-02 (settled) The HostileReview subsystem stays separate (R-25), and P1 to P3 are fixed in it (section 20.4). Sources: FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW-002 (it reviews artifacts) against S-03 (this pipeline reviews session turns); the approved foundation's position (foundation section 5.4); the operator rule that new scope the operator has not named needs approval, which consolidation would be. Its missing worker reuses this amendment's execution components (the DRY ruling).\n2531: - D-03 (open, the operator's) The foundation amendments A-01 to A-12, with this amendment and its plan: approve them as a change to PLAN-SESSIONTURNREVIEW-001, which then needs its new hostile validation (section 1). The operator's rules make plan approval the operator's act: a requirement change is planned, presented, and implemented only after explicit approval.\n2532: - D-04 (settled by the operator, 2026-10-17) The reviewer for the hostile validation of this amendment and its plan is Codex `gpt-6-astra` at effort `max`. Source: the operator's answer \"2. A\" to the question asked under P-01 (\"If not specified by operator, ask what agent and model to use for HV in a session.\"). Codex is neither the requesting agent's family (ClaudeCode) nor the agent that drafted the first version (Cline); its model and effort are verified from its rollout (VI-01).\n2533: - D-05 (settled) Exclusion covers the author of the reviewed turns as well as the submitter (R-08, FR-MCP-HVPIPE-002-AC008). Sources: S-28 (a self-review \"is not relevant to the HV\"), P-07 (\"Self-check alone is not validation.\"), and the handoff's settled requirement that self-review is independent of HV; a review by the agent that wrote the turns is a self-review. Because one request names turns of one MCP session (R-26), the author is that session's agent.\n2534: - D-06 (settled) Cline stays unqualified until it records the effort of a run: its NONCE fails `runtime_identity_mismatch` (L-04). Sources: P-04 (\"Verify the reviewer's model and effort from its own runtime receipts\"), which leaves no accepted remedy for a declared, unobserved effort; the operator rule to fail closed under an existing rule when the inputs cannot prove a fact; the handoff's settled requirement that not every request needs all five reviewers.\n2535: - D-07 (settled by the operator, 2026-10-17) This plan fixes P10 to P15 itself, in HvStorageSchema and H1, each under the owning plan's approved design (the federation plan's D5 and D7; the foundation's FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005), as section 20.4 assigns them (operator, verbatim: \"D-07 B: this plan fixes all six (P10 to P15) itself.\"). The question arose because each sits in code another plan owns or has redesigned: P10 and P13 are the federation plan's undelivered P1.5, P12 is in that plan's normalizer code, P11 and P15 are in the stop path the foundation's slice A7 rewrites, and P14 is under FR-MCP-SESSIONLIFE-004, which the foundation lists as related and unchanged. Rejected: option A, which left each fix to its owning plan with this plan holding only entry conditions.\n2536: - D-08 (settled by the operator, 2026-10-17) Timestamps in HV data: the identifiers `hvr-`, `hvprobe-`, and `hvi-` keep the UTC `yyyyMMddTHHmmssZ` stamp, as session and request ids do, and every stored timestamp is UTC with offset (section 12); local time is for display only (operator, verbatim: \"HV ids keep the UTC yyyyMMddTHHmmssZ stamp: timestamps are UTC in the data and local only for display.\"). The question arose because the operator profile's rule of 2026-10-13 (\"Local everywhere\") asks that a UTC identifier be raised to the operator.\n2537: \n2538: ## 23. Diagram index\n2539: \n2540: - Section 4: the system context.\n2541: - Section 7: the state diagrams of the HV request (7.1), the run attempt (7.2), reviewer readiness (7.3), and a turn's HV state (7.4).\n2542: - Section 11, the shared components, each drawn once: FencedLease, GzipCodec, AuditLedgerGuard, AgentIdentity, TurnSnapshotBuilder, RequirementReferenceExtractor, NotificationOutbox, OrderedEventReplay.\n2543: - Section 14, units of work: ListHvReviewersQuery, GetHvReviewerQuery, UpdateHvReviewerCommand, StartHvReviewerNonceCommand; SubmitHvRequestCommand (nested activities AdmitRequestChecks and InsertAdmittedRequest), GetHvRequestQuery, QueryHvRequestsQuery, CancelHvRequestCommand; GetHvResultQuery, GetHvEvidenceQuery, RemoveHvEvidenceCommand, StreamHvRunOutputQuery, StreamHvNotificationsQuery, QueryHvNotificationsQuery, QueryHvIncidentsQuery; ClaimHvRunCommand, RecordHvRunHeartbeatCommand, AppendHvRunOutputCommand, CloseHvRunOutputCommand, FailHvRunCommand, ExpireHvRunLeasesCommand, FinalizeHvRequestCommand (nested activities ReadFinalizationInputs and CommitFinalResult), FileHvIncidentCommand.\n2544: - Section 15, policies: HvScopeResolution, HvAdmissionEligibility, HvReviewerEligibility, RoundRobinAssignment, HvTurnFreeze, HvFinalVerification (nested activities VerifyLogAndBinding and VerifyVerdictAndReviewer), ReadinessProbeVerdict, HvRetryDecision.\n2545: - Section 16, processes: HvWorkerHost, RunReviewerProcess (nested activity StreamReviewerOutput), SubmitHvFromPlugin, WatchHvNotifications.\n2546: - Section 17, sequences: 17.1 to 17.15, with the nested sequences StreamOneAttempt and FinalizeOneRequest after 17.7.\n2547: - Section 18, use cases: the overview and UC-HV-01 to UC-HV-12.\n2548: - Section 20: the delivery order.\n2549: - Addendum B, entity diagrams by functionality: B.1 references between the groups, B.2 reviewer pool, B.3 requests and membership, B.4 runs and output, B.5 results and findings, B.6 evidence, B.7 notifications and incidents, B.8 changed foundation entities.\n2550: \n2551: ## Addendum B. Entity diagrams with relationships\n2552: \n2553: Grouped by functionality, the foundation's rule (Addendum A, \"Grouping\"): each entity's columns appear in exactly one group; an entity of another group appears without columns only as a relationship target. Solid lines are database foreign keys (each the composite (`WorkspaceId`, parent key)); dotted lines are logical references by root ids, enforced by the units. Diagrams draw key columns and the columns this amendment adds; section 12 lists every column.\n2554: \n2555: ### B.1 References between the groups\n2556: \n2557: - An HV request names its reviewed session by `Agent` and `SessionId`, its submitting turn by the submitter turn key, and each member turn by its turn key; a run names the reviewer's own turn by `ReviewerAgent`, `ReviewerSessionId`, and `ReviewerRequestId`; a requirement reference names a requirement or use case by `RefId`; an incident names its triage report by `TriageReportId`.\n2558: \n2559: ```mermaid\n2560: erDiagram\n2561:     SessionLog ||..o{ HvRequest : \"reviewed session, by Agent and SessionId\"\n2562:     SessionLogTurn ||..o{ HvRequest : \"submitting turn key\"\n2563:     SessionLogTurn ||..o{ HvRequestTurn : \"member turn key\"\n2564:     SessionLogTurn ||..o{ HvRun : \"reviewer turn key\"\n2565:     Requirement ||..o{ HvRequestRequirementRef : \"RefId\"\n2566:     TriageReport ||..o{ HvIncident : \"TriageReportId\"\n2567:     HvReviewer ||..o{ HvRequest : \"AssignedReviewerId\"\n2568:     HvReviewer ||..o{ HvRun : \"ReviewerId\"\n2569: ```\n2570: \n2571: ### B.2 Reviewer pool\n2572: \n2573: Not drawn: `HvReviewer` `ExecutionStrategy`, `AgentPath`, `ConfiguredModel`, `ConfiguredEffort`, `ResolvedModel`, `ResolvedEffort`, `ReadinessReason`, `ReadinessChangedUtc`, `LastProbeRunId`, `OpenIncidentId`, `ProbeRequestedUtc`, `MaxConcurrentRuns`, `CreatedUtc`, `UpdatedUtc`.\n2574: \n2575: ```mermaid\n2576: erDiagram\n2577:     HvWorkspaceState {\n2578:         string WorkspaceId PK\n2579:         string LastAssignedReviewerId\n2580:         long NextNotificationSequence\n2581:         int StateVersion\n2582:     }\n2583:     HvReviewer {\nEXIT_CODE=0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
