ccess\": true\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      ],\n      \"ts\": 1792165983348\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"Index\": 28,\n    \"Message\": {\n      \"id\": \"msg_M-lgM3g0\",\n      \"role\": \"user\",\n      \"content\": [\n        {\n          \"type\": \"tool_result\",\n          \"tool_use_id\": \"call_01a1456aa66770ea94dfa33aa72d45fe\",\n          \"name\": \"read_files\",\n          \"content\": [\n            {\n              \"query\": \"/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/.github/copilot-instructions.md\",\n              \"result\": \" 1 | # Copilot Instructions — QBrainAi\\n 2 | \\n 3 | **Agent Identity:** When posting to the MCP session log, use the agent's actual identity in Pascal-Case for `sourceType` and the session ID prefix. Do not use inaccurate aliases, lowercase names, or legacy placeholders. Example: `Codex`.\\n 4 | For specific operational instructions (session bootstrap, turn logging lifecycle, helper command order), follow `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml`.\\n 5 | \\n 6 | ## Response Formatting\\n 7 | \\n 8 | - Do not use table-style output in responses.\\n 9 | - Use concise bullets or short paragraphs instead.\\n10 | \\n11 | ## Terminal Usage\\n12 | \\n13 | - Do NOT set focus to the terminal window.\\n14 | - Do NOT write multiline commands to the terminal; use temporary scripts instead.\\n15 | - Use **only `pwsh.exe`** (PowerShell 7+). Do not invoke `powershell.exe` (Windows PowerShell 5.1) for scripts, examples, or tooling.\\n16 | \\n17 | ## Session Logging Requirements\\n18 | \\n19 | - Follow `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml` for workflow and helper command order.\\n20 | - Persist session log changes immediately after each meaningful update; do not batch unsaved turn changes.\\n21 | - For each turn, include as much detail as available: interpretation, response/status, actions (type/status/filePath), files modified, design decisions, requirements discovered, blockers, and context references.\\n22 | \\n23 | ## Build, Test, Lint\\n24 | \\n25 | ```powershell\\n26 | # Build (via Nuke)\\n27 | ./build.ps1 Compile\\n28 | # or: dotnet build src\\\\QBrainAi.Support.Mcp -c Debug\\n29 | \\n30 | # Run all unit tests (via Nuke)\\n31 | ./build.ps1 Test\\n32 | # or individual projects:\\n33 | # dotnet test tests\\\\QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.Tests -c Debug\\n34 | # dotnet test tests\\\\QBrainAi.Client.Tests -c Debug\\n35 | # dotnet test tests\\\\Build.Tests -c Debug\\n36 | \\n37 | # Run integration tests (uses CustomWebApplicationFactory, in-memory EF)\\n38 | dotnet test tests\\\\QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.IntegrationTests -c Debug\\n39 | \\n40 | # Run a single test by fully-qualified name\\n41 | dotnet test tests\\\\QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.Tests -c Debug --filter \\\"FullyQualifiedName~TodoServiceTests.QueryAsync_NoFilters_ReturnsAllItems\\\"\\n42 | \\n43 | # Run tests in a single class\\n44 | dotnet test tests\\\\QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.Tests -c Debug --filter \\\"FullyQualifiedName~TodoServiceTests\\\"\\n45 | \\n46 | # Validate appsettings config (via Nuke)\\n47 | ./build.ps1 ValidateConfig\\n48 | # or: pwsh.exe -NoLogo -NoProfile -NonInteractive -File ./scripts/Validate-McpConfig.ps1\\n49 | \\n50 | # Validate requirements traceability\\n51 | ./build.ps1 ValidateTraceability\\n52 | \\n53 | # Markdown lint (docs only)\\n54 | # CI uses markdownlint-cli2 with .markdownlint-cli2.yaml\\n55 | ```\\n56 | \\n57 | ## Architecture\\n58 | \\n59 | **QBrainAi** is a standalone ASP.NET Core 9 server providing context retrieval, TODO management, session logging, repository operations, and GitHub issue sync for AI agents. It exposes functionality via two transports:\\n60 | \\n61 | - **HTTP REST API** — Controllers under `src/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp/Controllers/` (routes at `/qbrainai/*`).\\n62 | - **MCP Streamable HTTP** — `app.MapMcp(\\\"/mcp-transport\\\")` using ModelContextProtocol.AspNetCore.\\n63 | - **MCP STDIO** — `--transport stdio` flag; same tools as HTTP via `McpStdio/FwhMcpTools.cs`.\\n64 | \\n65 | **Workspace model**: The primary host manages multiple workspaces, each getting its own in-process Kestrel `WebApplication` (not child processes). Workspace config lives in `appsettings.json` under `Mcp:Workspaces` (not in the database). One workspace is the \\\"primary\\\" — served by the host process directly; others get child `WebApplication` instances via `WorkspaceAppFactory`.\\n66 | \\n67 | **Search pipeline**: Hybrid search combines SQLite FTS5 full-text with HNSW vector similarity (384-dim all-MiniLM-L6-v2 ONNX embeddings). `HybridSearchService` fuses both with BM25 scoring.\\n68 | \\n69 | **Storage**: EF Core with SQLite (`McpDbContext`). TODO items use a pluggable backend — either YAML file-backed (`TodoService`) or SQLite table-backed (`SqliteTodoService`), selected via `Mcp:TodoStorage:Provider`.\\n70 | \\n71 | **Multi-instance**: `McpInstanceResolver` overlays per-instance config from `Mcp:Instances:{name}` onto base `Mcp:*` settings.\\n72 | \\n73 | ## Key Conventions\\n74 | \\n75 | ### XML Documentation Required\\n76 | \\n77 | `TreatWarningsAsErrors` and `GenerateDocumentationFile` are enabled globally in `Directory.Build.props`. All public types and members must have XML doc comm\n... 8285568 bytes omitted ...\nparate holistic evaluation.\\n\\nA turn without a self-review is no less eligible for HV than one with a self-review. This resolves that open question; it is not a readiness blocker.\\n\\nNo project files changed. The audit write again failed with `Session not found`; the tool reported a local failsafe copy.\\nMESSAGE 230\\nUnderstood. I’ll simplify the plan: “NONCE the agent” is a check that the agent is available and can execute an HV—not a separate authorization or recovery workflow.\\nMESSAGE 238\\nUnderstood. The planning definition is simply:\\n\\n> **“NONCE the agent” means checking that the agent is available and can execute an HV.**\\n\\n- A successful check establishes **availability and HV readiness**, not an HV verdict.\\n- No separate invocation-authority policy or recovery-proof workflow is implied.\\n- Self-review remains entirely unrelated to this check and to HV.\\n\\nThat clarification is **resolved**, not a readiness blocker. No files were changed or reviewers launched.\\n\\n*Audit note: MCP session persistence still failed with `Session not found`; the tool reported local failsafe saves.*\\nMESSAGE 240\\nUnderstood: HV evidence and protected-log access must participate in the database’s audit log. I’ll record that clarification without making implementation changes.\\nMESSAGE 264\\nUnderstood. **HV evidence retention and protected-log access must participate in the database’s canonical audit log—not merely agent session logs or separate HV logs.**\\n\\nThe proposed acceptance criteria are:\\n\\n- **Evidence lifecycle:** Audit evidence creation/finalization, retention-policy changes, and expiration or deletion.\\n- **Protected access:** Audit read/export attempts, including allowed and denied access, plus permission changes.\\n- **Traceability:** Identify the actor, UTC timestamp, workspace, HV run, evidence reference, action, and outcome.\\n- **Confidentiality:** Record audit metadata without duplicating protected evidence or secrets into general logs.\\n- **Verification:** Test audit coverage and audit-write failure behavior; missing audit records must not silently count as successful audited operations.\\n\\nThis establishes the audit-integration requirement; it does not select a retention duration or access-role policy, or authorize implementation.\\n\\n**Persistence remains blocked:** session completion again returned `Session not found`; history and TODO queries returned HTTP 404. The tool reported a local failsafe, but I cannot claim this decision reached the database. No project artifacts were changed.\\n\",\n              \"success\": true\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      ],\n      \"ts\": 1792173980033\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"Index\": 459,\n    \"Message\": {\n      \"id\": \"msg_TQq8dPG5\",\n      \"role\": \"user\",\n      \"content\": [\n        {\n          \"type\": \"tool_result\",\n          \"tool_use_id\": \"call_Nk7m6N5dO6nOkVNubvBlBNgl\",\n          \"name\": \"run_commands\",\n          \"content\": [\n            {\n              \"query\": \"pwsh.exe -NoLogo -NoProfile -NonInteractive -Command '$ErrorActionPreference=\\\"Stop\\\"; $p=\\\"/home/sharpninja/.cline/data/sessions/1792165626722_56f98/1792165626722_56f98.messages.json\\\"; $d=Get-Content -L ... [command truncated: 472 more chars; full command is in the tool call input]\",\n              \"result\": \"DRAFT_SECTION_INDEX\\n0 # Reassembled plan: durable, multi-agent hostile validation\\n444 ## 1. Verified foundation and current state\\n1928 ### Worktree\\n2454 ## 2. Required behavior\\n2479 ### Submission and eligibility\\n3492 ### Reviewer selection\\n4123 ### Results and finality\\n4968 ## 3. Architecture amendments required before implementation\\n5127 ### A. Turn coverage versus response coverage\\n5551 ### B. Finality and turn closure\\n6517 ### C. Five reviewers versus one configured grader\\n7205 ### D. Atomic completion versus per-response commits\\n8090 ### E. Existing hostile-review storage\\n8658 ## 4. Delivery slices and dependency order\\n8909 ### H0 — Readiness, requirements, and interface baseline\\n9989 ### H1 — Reviewer configuration and execution adapters\\n10762 ### H2 — Admission, snapshots, and durable ownership\\n11368 ### H3 — Execution lifecycle and verified acceptance\\n12114 ### H7 — Per-turn results, holistic evaluation, and atomic finalization\\n12855 ### H4 — REST management and query surfaces\\n13414 ### H5 — Client, REPL, Director, MCP, and documentation parity\\n13876 ### H6 — SSE, incidents, and recovery operations\\n14705 ## 5. Byrd execution and validation gates\\n16652 ### Bootstrap validation\\n17137 ## 6. Decisions still required\\nDELIVERY_SLICES\\n#\\n\",\n              \"success\": true\n            },\n            {\n              \"query\": \"pwsh.exe -NoLogo -NoProfile -NonInteractive -Command '$ErrorActionPreference=\\\"Stop\\\"; $p=\\\"/home/sharpninja/.cline/data/sessions/1792165626722_56f98/1792165626722_56f98.messages.json\\\"; $d=Get-Content -L ... [command truncated: 302 more chars; full command is in the tool call input]\",\n              \"result\": \"DESIGN_CONSTRAINTS\\n### Reviewer selection\\n\\nConfigure the five reviewers:\\n\\n- `cline`\\n- `copilot`\\n- `claude`\\n- `codex`\\n- `grok`\\n\\nUse round-robin selection over eligible, healthy reviewers, excluding the submitter. Normalize identities and aliases so an override or persona name cannot bypass exclusion.\\n\\nThis is a **pool of five reviewers**, not an implied requirement that all five review every request.\\n\\nAuto-start and readiness must be tested under the service’s actual execution identity. All five command names are present in this environment, but that does **not** establish authentication, model availability, usable permissions, or readiness.\\n\\n### Results and finality\\n\\nEvery successful review returns:\\n\\n- Individual turn scores and findings.\\n- A **separate holistic evaluation** of the submitted work as a whole.\\n- An overall verdict consistent with the findings.\\n- The complete accepted request.\\n- The complete JSONL response log.\\n- Actionable failures identifying the affected turn, claim or requirement, evidence, and corrective action.\\n\\nThe holistic evaluation is not an average, minimum, or other mathematical roll-up of turn scores. It must examine cross-turn consistency, omissions, contradictory decisions, and end-to-end satisfaction.\\n\\nFinal reviews are immutable. HV-reviewed turns cannot subsequently be edited, reset, or force-reviewed. Remediation produces new work and new turns, preserving the original record.\\n\\n**HV returns a verdict. It does not mark a TODO complete.**\\n\\n## 3. Architecture amendments required before implementation\\n\\nKeep the designated architecture authoritative, but amend its conflicting contracts explicitly.\\n\\n### A. Turn coverage versus response coverage\\n\\nArchitecture A.9 currently selects agent-context responses. That does not guarantee coverage of every requested turn.\\n\\nExtend the canonical request/item model to represent the complete selected turn set, including turns without an agent-context response. Link applicable responses to those items for grading. Do not silently exclude a turn because it lacks a response record.\\n\\n### B. Finality and turn closure\\n\\nThe architecture currently permits review writes on terminal turns and describes reset operations. Those paths must respect the new finality requirement across **every write surface**, not only the HV endpoint.\\n\\nProposed lifecycle:\\n\\n- Admission freezes the reviewed content and self-review versions.\\n- Execution works against that snapshot.\\n- Finalization verifies that the bound versions remain valid.\\n- Finalization writes the hostile review and makes the reviewed turn immutable.\\n\\nFor a turn still awaiting closure, define its intended terminal transition before review and apply any authorized closure **inside the finalization transaction**. Do not require an ordinary turn edit after the turn has become immutable.\\n\\nThe reviewer’s own audit lifecycle must also avoid recursively requiring another HV review merely to finish recording its receipt. Document the narrowly scoped protocol rather than adding a broad gate bypass.\\n\\n### C. Five reviewers versus one configured grader\\n\\nRetain the architecture’s verification checks:\\n\\n- Receipt location and integrity.\\n- Request metadata.\\n- Verdict extraction and request binding.\\n- Exact agreement between submitted grades and reviewer output.\\n- Reviewer-turn evidence.\\n- Reviewer independence.\\n- Actual runtime identity.\\n\\nReplace the single-grader assumption with a request-bound assignment from the configured reviewer pool. Each adapter must produce verifiable provenance; configured model names alone are not proof of the runtime used.\\n\\nThe existing Codex-specific two-step receipt/grade protocol needs an equivalent, documented protocol for each supported reviewer.\\n\\n### D. Atomic completion versus per-response commits\\n\\nThe architecture currently describes individual grade submissions. Calling those independently would expose partial completion.\\n\\nIntroduce one completion unit that reuses the canonical validation and trust policies and commits together:\\n\\n- Final result and complete response-log representation.\\n- Holistic evaluation.\\n- Per-turn reviews and findings.\\n- Applicable response grades and trust changes.\\n- Request/item completion.\\n- Turn finalization.\\n- A durable completion-notification record.\\n\\nExternal receipt reads and validation happen before the database transaction; the transaction rechecks the versions and ownership those checks depended on.\\n\\nQueue admission, leases, heartbeats, attempt records, and staged output may be persisted earlier. **Atomic completion does not mean keeping the entire run inside one transaction.**\\n\\n### E. Existing hostile-review storage\\n\\nThe existing hostile-review entities explicitly avoid storing raw artifact bodies and assembled prompts. The new complete-request/log requirement therefore needs an approved retention and compatibility amendment.\\n\\nUse one authoritative model. Existing hostile-review APIs should delegate or project into that model where appropriate—not maintain a second independent verdict store.\\n\\nDefine protected canonical evidence, outward secret redaction, size limits, and retention. Never silently truncate a log and label it complete.\\n\\n## 4. Delivery slices and dependency order\\n\\nRetain H1–H7 for continuity, but change their execution order:\\n\\n**H0 → H1 → H2 → H3 → H7 → H4 → H5/H6 → release validation**\\n\\nH5 and H6 may overlap only where their contracts and ownership are independent.\\n\\n### H0 — Readiness, requirements, and interface baseline\\n\\nBefore production changes:\\n\\n- Resolve worktree ownership and the exact develop base.\\n- Verify the foundation’s implementation status and integration dependencies.\\n- Restore usable MCP history/TODO/audit access.\\n- Query effective workspace requirements and verify existing TODO IDs.\\n- Reconcile requirement-store ownership with the documentation projections.\\n- Record the architecture amendments above.\\n- Define all request, response, error, authorization, and idempotency contracts.\\n- Provide activity diagrams per CQRS unit and sequence diagrams for submission and response handling.\\n- Decompose the approved design into persisted, dependency-linked TODOs.\\n\\nOrganize requirements by problem domain: admission, evidence, scheduling, execution, finalization, observability, incidents, and surface parity.\\n\\nDo not invent a 1:1 FR/TR/TEST rule. Preserve explicit traceability, AC ownership, UC links, and test evidence.\\n\\n**Exit:** approved interfaces and requirements, verified dependencies, and a usable audit/task system.\\n\\n###\\n\",\n              \"success\": true\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      ],\n      \"ts\": 1792173995809\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"Index\": 461,\n    \"Message\": {\n      \"id\": \"msg_gRM916hu\",\n      \"role\": \"user\",\n      \"content\": [\n        {\n          \"type\": \"tool_result\",\n          \"tool_use_id\": \"call_VesNNG3oVyd7leJbMexu0BTh\",\n          \"name\": \"run_commands\",\n          \"content\": [\n            {\n              \"query\": \"pwsh.exe -NoLogo -NoProfile -NonInteractive -Command '$ErrorActionPreference=\\\"Stop\\\"; $p=\\\"/home/sharpninja/.cline/data/sessions/1792165626722_56f98/1792165626722_56f98.messages.json\\\"; $d=Get-Content -L ... [command truncated: 261 more chars; full command is in the tool call input]\",\n              \"result\": \"### H1 — Reviewer configuration and execution adapters\\n\\n- Replace single-reviewer constraints with five configurable reviewer definitions.\\n- Define startup, readiness, health, concurrency, and shutdown behavior.\\n- Implement identity normalization and submitter exclusion.\\n- Address adapter gaps, including Copilot support.\\n- Validate each CLI’s actual output and runtime-evidence capabilities.\\n- Reuse existing process abstractions rather than creating unrelated launchers.\\n\\nThe current one-shot strategy has output callbacks, but its streaming-return methods wait for completion. Reuse suitable callbacks or refactor the shared abstraction to provide genuine live output.\\n\\n**Exit:** adapter contracts and mocked behavior pass; live qualification remains a separate gate.\\n\\n### H2 — Admission, snapshots, and durable ownership\\n\\n- Implement turn/session/range resolution.\\n- Enumerate missing self-reviews.\\n- Resolve FR/TR/AC/UC references with provenance.\\n- Freeze selected turns, context, configuration, and evidence versions.\\n- Add durable queue state, execution attempts, leases, heartbeats, and fencing.\\n- Prevent duplicate or overlapping ownership.\\n- Persist round-robin selection safely across concurrent workers and restarts.\\n- Recheck availability and independence at assignment.\\n\\n**Exit:** admission and ownership are deterministic, restart-safe, and concurrency-tested.\\n\\n### H3 — Execution lifecycle and verified acceptance\\n\\n- Launch the assigned reviewer under bounded authority.\\n- Capture ordered output and execution metadata.\\n- Handle timeout, cancellation, process failure, and lease loss.\\n- Reject stale-worker output using the attempt’s ownership token.\\n- Implement receipt verification and reviewer-specific provenance.\\n- Distinguish an adverse review verdict from an execution failure.\\n- Define retry behavior for execution incidents without enabling re-review of finalized work.\\n\\nIncident detection and offline transitions belong here; endpoint delivery and operational recovery are completed in H6.\\n\\n**Exit:** executions can produce verified completion candidates—not yet publicly accepted final reviews.\\n\\n### H7 — Per-turn results, holistic evaluation, and atomic finalization\\n\\nMove this ahead of public rollout.\\n\\n- Validate complete coverage of the selected turns.\\n- Preserve lossless correctness/completeness scores.\\n- Require a distinct holistic assessment.\\n- Validate actionable failures and full request/log coverage.\\n- Apply canonical grade and trust policies.\\n- Commit all final results and turn changes in one transaction.\\n- Enforce immutable, idempotent completion.\\n- Resolve completion/cancellation and completion/lease-expiry races.\\n- Publish completion notifications from a durable post-commit mechanism.\\n\\n**Exit:** rollback cannot leave some turns graded and others ungraded; reconnecting clients can discover committed completion.\\n\\n### H4 — REST management and query surfaces\\n\\nDocument and implement canonical operations for:\\n\\n- Submission.\\n- Request/result retrieval.\\n- Queue and running-review queries.\\n- Latest output and progress.\\n- ETA, including an explicit unavailable/unknown state.\\n- Cancellation.\\n- Reviewer status and authorized management.\\n\\nRetain compatibility aliases where required. Assign exact routes and error codes during H0 rather than treating proposed routes as existing APIs.\\n\\n**Exit:** REST behavior matches the domain contracts, including actual HTTP status codes.\\n\\n### H5 — Client, REPL, Director, MCP, and documentation parity\\n\\nExpose the same semantics through each supported surface:\\n\\n- Typed client.\\n- REPL.\\n- Director.\\n- MCP tools.\\n- Shared plugin helpers and their consumers.\\n\\nNo surface may bypass reviewer exclusion, finality, eligibility, or completion validation. Follow the workspace’s PowerShell scripting policy.\\n\\n**Exit:** contract tests demonstrate parity, and documentation matches the implemented interfaces.\\n\\n### H6 — SSE, incidents, and recovery operations\\n\\n- Per-run live-output SSE with ordered event identifiers and reconnect behavior.\\n- Workspace-scoped completion-notification SSE.\\n- Running-review visibility, latest output, and honest ETA.\\n- Durable incident deduplication.\\n- Affected-reviewer offline transitions.\\n- Incident submission **through an endpoint**.\\n\\nAn existing candidate is `POST /qbrainai/triage/reports`, with its compatibility alias. Verify its schema and deduplication behavior before adopting it.\\n\\nKeep the recovery instruction exactly **“NONCE the agent.”** Do not implement restart, credential rotation, nonce challenge, or re-enablement semantics until that phrase is defined.\\n\\n**Exit:** failures are visible and recoverable without duplicate incidents, false completion, or unauthorized reviewer re-entry.\\n\\n\\n\",\n              \"success\": true\n            },\n            {\n              \"query\": \"pwsh.exe -NoLogo -NoProfile -NonInteractive -Command '$ErrorActionPreference=\\\"Stop\\\"; $p=\\\"/home/sharpninja/.cline/data/sessions/1792165626722_56f98/1792165626722_56f98.messages.json\\\"; $d=Get-Content -L ... [command truncated: 200 more chars; full command is in the tool call input]\",\n              \"result\": \"## 5. Byrd execution and validation gates\\n\\nFor each approved TODO:\\n\\n1. Hydrate its requirements, acceptance criteria, dependencies, and relevant context.\\n2. Write the next behavioral test and demonstrate the intended failure.\\n3. Validate that increment against mocks.\\n4. Implement the real behavior.\\n5. Refactor tests and production code.\\n6. Run the complete required unit regression scope.\\n7. Run configuration and traceability checks.\\n8. Produce an implementer receipt and stop at `Validating`.\\n9. Have an independent validator reproduce the claims.\\n10. Let the coordinator decide completion only after the required evidence agrees.\\n\\nThe verified build targets include `Test`, `ValidateConfig`, `ValidateTraceability`, and `MigrationIntegrationTests`. However:\\n\\n- `Test` excludes more than integration tests, including `Build.Tests`, plugin-integration, and review-test projects.\\n- `MigrationIntegrationTests` does not run every dedicated integration-test project.\\n- Traceability defaults allow some TR/TEST gaps as warnings.\\n\\nTherefore H0 must establish a **complete gate inventory**, including separately required suites and strict traceability checks. A successful default target alone is insufficient evidence.\\n\\nRequired adversarial coverage includes:\\n\\n- Missing self-reviews and explicitly included `Unnecessary` turns.\\n- Whole-session scope and stable snapshots.\\n- Concurrent submissions, claims, and finalization.\\n- Submitter aliases and reviewer-override attempts.\\n- All reviewers unavailable.\\n- Restart, stale leases, and late output.\\n- Cancellation/completion races.\\n- Invalid, missing, or mismatched receipts.\\n- Transaction rollback.\\n- Cross-workspace access.\\n- SSE reconnection and notification delivery.\\n- Incident deduplication.\\n- Attempts to modify finalized turns.\\n\\nThe gate requires **zero failures, zero skips, and no omitted required tests**. Integration validation follows the green unit gate, with human-guided refinement.\\n\\n### Bootstrap validation\\n\\nThe existing launcher is present at:\\n\\n`/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/tools/powershell/Invoke-HostileGrok.ps1`\\n\\nIt is a candidate bootstrap mechanism, **not established proof of readiness**. Verify its permissions, receipt handling, independence, and evidence completeness before relying on it.\\n\\nDo not switch to the new pipeline merely because H1–H3 are finished. Switch only after its required end-to-end capabilities have been independently validated.\\n\\n## 6. Decisions still required\\n\\nBefore implementation starts:\\n\\n- Confirm the worktree and foundation integration point.\\n- Approve the architecture amendments for finality, turn coverage, reviewer authorization, and atomic completion.\\n- Define which existing self-review states satisfy admission.\\n- Define **“NONCE the agent”**, including who may invoke it and what proves recovery.\\n- Approve evidence retention and protected-log access.\\n\\n**This turn completed the read-only plan reconstruction.** No project files were edited, no worktree was created, and no builds, tests, or reviewer executions were run. MCP history/TODO queries failed, and session writes reported local failsafe records rather than confirmed server persistence; verified TODO links and durable planning artifacts remain prerequisites.\\n\",\n              \"success\": true\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      ],\n      \"ts\": 1792174012376\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"Index\": 463,\n    \"Message\": {\n      \"id\": \"6b8ee28b-7681-4862-b04d-afb8d020828b\",\n      \"role\": \"user\",\n      \"content\": [\n        {\n          \"type\": \"text\",\n          \"text\": \"<user_input mode=\\\"plan\\\">> Confirm the worktree/base and foundation integration point.\\n\\nI don't understand the question.  I've already specified both.</user_input>\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"ts\": 1792174133974\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"Index\": 465,\n    \"Message\": {\n      \"id\": \"msg_cB08jHu4\",\n      \"role\": \"user\",\n      \"content\": [\n        {\n          \"type\": \"tool_result\",\n          \"tool_use_id\": \"call_JT40GqGC8tOYa96HWvGzVJ94\",\n          \"name\": \"run_commands\",\n          \"content\": [\n            {\n              \"query\": \"pwsh.exe -NoLogo -NoProfile -NonInteractive -Command '$ErrorActionPreference=\\\"Stop\\\"; $path=\\\"/home/sharpninja/.cline/data/sessions/1792165626722_56f98/1792165626722_56f98.messages.json\\\"; $data=Get-Cont ... [command truncated: 502 more chars; full command is in the tool call input]\",\n              \"result\": \"USER_MESSAGE=0\\n<user_input mode=\\\"act\\\"><mode_notice>The user switched from plan mode to act mode before sending this message.</mode_notice>\\ncreate worktree from develop</user_input>\\nUSER_MESSAGE=76\\n<user_input mode=\\\"plan\\\">worktree session-20261009 should have updates to the session log schema for storing both agent self-review and hv review outcome on the turn.</user_input>\\nUSER_MESSAGE=96\\n<user_input mode=\\\"plan\\\">> Found the architecture doc \\n\\nThat document is the base we are building on in this plan.</user_input>\\n\",\n              \"success\": true\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      ],\n      \"ts\": 1792174153319\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"Index\": 467,\n    \"Message\": {\n      \"id\": \"50b7c630-5bea-4c2c-b2ae-fb91bb0b1783\",\n      \"role\": \"user\",\n      \"content\": [\n        {\n          \"type\": \"text\",\n          \"text\": \"<user_input mode=\\\"plan\\\">> Choose evidence-retention duration, protected-access roles, and exact audit-failure policy.\\n\\nRetain until told to remove.  There are no access roles.  Audit-failure is a hard stop.</user_input>\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"ts\": 1792174305831\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"Index\": 469,\n    \"Message\": {\n      \"id\": \"7f942e6d-c2fd-4d70-b2f8-c35bf3bda06d\",\n      \"role\": \"user\",\n      \"content\": [\n        {\n          \"type\": \"text\",\n          \"text\": \"<user_input mode=\\\"plan\\\">> Verify task/requirement links and persist the approved plan.\\n\\nThat's your job.</user_input>\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"ts\": 1792174335655\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"Index\": 471,\n    \"Message\": {\n      \"id\": \"msg_DWjBBuv2\",\n      \"role\": \"user\",\n      \"content\": [\n        {\n          \"type\": \"tool_result\",\n          \"tool_use_id\": \"call_HgP6wnpSppGCdeLFBP6K4OGQ\",\n          \"name\": \"read_files\",\n          \"content\": [\n            {\n              \"query\": \"/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml:1-28\",\n              \"result\": \" 1 | port: 7147\\n 2 | baseUrl: http://LAB-OMARCHY:7147\\n 3 | apiKey: [REDACTED:api-key]\\n 4 | endpoints:\\n 5 |   health: /health\\n 6 |   swagger: /swagger/v1/swagger.json\\n 7 |   swaggerUi: /swagger\\n 8 |   mcpTransport: /mcp-transport\\n 9 |   sessionLog: /mcpserver/sessionlog\\n10 |   sessionLogDialog: /mcpserver/sessionlog/{agent}/{sessionId}/{requestId}/dialog\\n11 |   contextSearch: /mcpserver/context/search\\n12 |   contextPack: /mcpserver/context/pack\\n13 |   contextSources: /mcpserver/context/sources\\n14 |   todo: /mcpserver/todo\\n15 |   repo: /mcpserver/repo\\n16 |   desktop: /mcpserver/desktop\\n17 |   gitHub: /mcpserver/gh\\n18 |   tools: /mcpserver/tools\\n19 |   workspace: /mcpserver/workspace\\n20 |   serverStartupUtc: /server-startup-utc\\n21 |   markerFileTimestamp: /marker-file-timestamp?repoPath={workspacePath}\\n22 | workspace: McpServer\\n23 | workspacePath: /home/sharpninja/github/McpServer\\n24 | pid: 597684\\n25 | startedAt: 2026-10-09T20:03:47.7882776+00:00\\n26 | markerWrittenAtUtc: 2026-10-09T20:03:47.7882776+00:00\\n27 | serverStartedAtUtc: 2026-10-09T20:03:24.7968517+00:00\\n28 | signature:\",\n              \"success\": true\n            },\n            {\n              \"query\": \"/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/docs/plans/PLAN-SESSIONTURN-SESSIONREVIEW-001.md:1-180\",\n              \"result\": \"  1 | <!-- SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 -->\\n  2 | # Plan: Correct turn logging, request class, agent/hostile reviews on session-log turns, and stateless agent context (BDPv4)\\n  3 | \\n  4 | Status of the merged plan: NOT APPROVED, awaiting operator approval (operator, 2026-10-11: \\\"Integrate attached to current plan.\\\" with the scoping answers \\\"Reconstruct plan with added requirements\\\", \\\"Also publish the doc to develop\\\", and \\\"Outline now, catalog in P0\\\", then \\\"No new code until I approve merged plan.\\\"). This revision integrates the Stateless Agent Context Management plan as Phase C (sections \\\"Locked operator decisions (2026-10-11)\\\", \\\"Scope catalog: Phase C, stateless agent context (delivered through the four execution phases)\\\", and the Phase C parts of P0, the catalog, wire contracts, rollout, risks, verification, and critical files), records the 2026-10-11 process rulings, and is described as architecture, with every model, unit-of-work contract, endpoint, and activity, sequence, and use case diagram, in `docs/architecture/SessionTurn-Review-AgentContext-Architecture.md`; the agent-context specification is `docs/Stateless-Agent-Context-Management.md`. No code is written under this plan until the operator approves the merged plan, and the merged plan passes a plan-readiness hostile validation before it is presented for approval; the one exception to that code freeze is operator-directed work outside this plan, the analyzer branch (see \\\"Code freeze\\\" under \\\"Locked operator decisions (2026-10-11)\\\"). The plan derives from the architecture document, which is the authority and is settled first (operator, 2026-10-12, verbatim: \\\"The plan should depend on the architecture, not the other way around.\\\"): the plan cites the architecture's decisions, contracts, units of work, named test cases, and delivery structure (its section 8), adds no design decision of its own, and a change to the architecture after approval is a plan change that needs a new hostile validation (architecture introduction). The requirements catalog is divided into problem domains (operator, 2026-10-13; \\\"Locked operator decisions (2026-10-13)\\\"). History of the pre-merge plan (Phases P0, A, B): APP [line truncated]\\n  5 | \\n  6 | Worktree `/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/.claude/worktrees/session-20261009` (branch `worktree-session-20261009`). Source baseline: `f56dcf70` (== `origin/develop` on 2026-10-09, QBrainAi rename); every source anchor in this plan was verified against it. The branch has since gained only documentation and receipt commits (`git diff --name-only f56dcf70..HEAD` lists only `AGENTS.md`, removed handoff files `CODEX-HANDOFF.md`, `HANDOFF.md`, `docs/handoffs/*`, `docs/plans/handoff-*`, `docs/Project/Plugin-Simplification-Handoff.md`, plus `docs/setup/*` and `docs/receipts/*`; no `src/`, `tests/`, `build/`, or `plugins/` change). The approval HEAD is recorded by P0.1 (`git rev-parse HEAD` at approval) and P0.1 re-runs that diff to confirm the baseline still holds. Merged-plan update (2026-10-11): that statement held at the 2026-10-10 approval but no longer holds: after it, the P0.1 baseline fixes landed source, test, build, and plugin changes on this branch (among them BUG-BUILD-001..010, BUG-PLUGINCORE-001, BUG-BASELINE-001..011 and -018, MAINT-CIREMOVAL-001; `git diff --name-only f56dcf70..HEAD` now lists paths under `src/`, `tests/`, `build/`, and `plugins/`). P0.1 therefore records the merged-plan approval HEAD and re-verifies every source anchor this plan cites (file and line) against that HEAD before any work package relies on it, correcting each anchor that moved. Round-5 and round-6 anchors (2026-10-12 and 2026-10-13) are cited at `a8d3934b` (`origin/develop` on 2026-10-11, a descendant of `f56dcf70`) unless they say otherwise, as the architecture's are. The analyzer branch `chore/netanalyzers-all` (worktree `/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/.claude/worktrees/netanalyzers-all`, head `fe5879ba`, which is `a8d3934b` plus seven commits, with 515 uncommitted paths on 2026-10-13) moves namespaces to RootNamespace plus folder, renames test methods without underscores, and moves `tests/Build.Tests` to `build/Build.Tests` (commit `fe5879ba`); it merges first (architectu [line truncated]\\n  7 | \\n  8 | ## Context\\n  9 | \\n 10 | The operator requires an agent self-review and a hostile validation (HV) on every result, plus truthful, per-request audit in the MCP session log. Today:\\n 11 | \\n 12 | - Reviews have no structured home. They live in free-text turns, `docs/receipts/hv/*` (integer `accuracy`/`completeness` 0-100), and the unlinked HostileReview subsystem (`double` 0-1). No score in the codebase is a lossless decimal.\\n 13 | - Turn logging is not trustworthy, so anything attached to a turn would attach to the wrong request. Receipts observed in this session (plugin 1.118.0):\\n 14 |   - Twin turns per prompt (e.g. `req-20261009T011653Z-prompt-b776` and `-c9a3`): MCP hooks were then registered twice (`~/.claude/settings.json` bridge hooks plus the enabled plugin `mcpserver@mcpserver-local` `hooks/hooks.json`). The bridge hooks were removed later on 2026-10-09 (MEMORY-FACT-003); a read-only check at 2026-10-09T17:54Z found no `hooks` key in `~/.claude/settings.json` or either `settings.local.json`, one plugin install, and one registration per event. The defect that let the duplicate go unreported remains: `validate-claude-mcp-hooks.ps1` wraps the `enabledPlugins` map as one item (line ~72), so it never sees the plugin as enabled and would report `valid` for a duplicate. A2 is therefore a regression fix verified against a fresh live inventory, not a cleanup of a current duplicate. Observed later the same day with hooks active: the prompt hook opens phantom turns for background task notifications, and each such open supersede-cancels the real turn in progress (`req-20261009T175347Z-prompt-dc63`, `req-20261009T175421Z-prompt-79dd`).\\n 15 |   - A new prompt cancels the in-progress turn: `Invoke-ReplSupersedeCurrentTurnIfInProgress` (`plugins/core/lib-ps/repl-invoke.ps1:2131`, called at `:2300`). Canceled while being worked: `6067`, `5360`, `bc34`, `8ab5`, `11dd`, `ea25`, `9a7d`, and the plan request `e18e`.\\n 16 |   - Background `<task-notification>` events open turns (`-986f`, `-06ab`, `-290b`, `-4ced`, `-695f`).\\n 17 |   - False durable-write receipt: `appendActions` on `req-20261009T005031Z-prompt-d3a5` returned `code: persisted, persisted: true`, but the server turn has 0 actions. The submit terminal guard `continue`s silently (`src/QBrainAi.Services/Services/SessionLogService.cs:1606-1618`) while the controller hard-codes `persisted = true` (`SessionLogController.cs:71-86`).\\n 18 |   - The stop gate auto-closes turns as `'Auto-closed by PowerShell stop gate.'` (`plugins/core/lib-ps/plugin-hook.ps1:1017`).\\n 19 |   - A single shared `current-turn.yaml` couples all turn edits, so edits to one request land on another.\\n 20 |   - Cross-session interference: this session's UserPromptSubmit hook reported `req-20261009T012640Z-prompt-b2ff` (a turn of a different ClaudeCode session, likely the Plan subagent's prompt) as \\\"the root work turn\\\"; hooks of subagents and parallel sessions share the workspace cache.\\n 21 |   - Hook-current turns can exist only locally (`req-20261009T013814Z-prompt-7ee0`: server PATCH returned \\\"planFile is omitted\\\", turn absent server-side).\\n 22 | \\n 23 | - Agents carry conversation history forward between invocations, so what a model knows depends on what happened to stay in its window: the operator profile, standing rules, applicable requirements, and earlier verified results are not guaranteed to be present, and nothing records which context an answer was produced from (operator-attached plan \\\"Stateless Agent Context Management\\\", 2026-10-11).\\n 24 | \\n 25 | Intended outcome: every user request is exactly one turn whose edits are independent of every other turn; every write receipt is truthful; every turn carries a `requestClass` and two review records (`agentReview`, `hostileReview`) with lossless decimal scores, a status lifecycle, and configurable pass thresholds; the server enforces completion rules; every agent invocation can run stateless from a bounded, reproducible context package whose provenance is recorded, and only responses graded at 98/98 with zero FAIL and zero UNKNOWN are reused as trusted context; FR/TR/TEST/AC/UC are complete and traceable.\\n 26 | \\n 27 | Changes to existing behavior: no phase is purely additive, Phase C included (architecture section 3, \\\"Changes to existing behavior\\\", and section 8.1, \\\"Published packages\\\"). Phase C adds tables, endpoints, and the `Mcp:AgentContext:Mode` setting, but the shared components it brings change what every existing caller sees in every Mode: `IDispatcher` in the published `QBrainAI.Cqrs` package becomes scoped and WorkspaceResolutionBehavior runs on every command and query; a deadlock or busy store that outlasts SerializableWrite's attempts answers 409 `conflict` with `retryable: true` where develop answers 503; and the published `QBrainAI.Client` package gains public API. Phases A and B change turn identity, turn writes, and the plugin's per-turn store. \\\"Breaking changes, rollout, backfill\\\" lists each change to existing behavior once, and each domain's \\\"Updated existing requirements\\\" and \\\"Test churn\\\" carry the records and tests it changes.\\n 28 | \\n 29 | ## Locked operator decisions (2026-10-09)\\n 30 | \\n 31 | Design decisions: the turn-logging decisions (D1, D2, D5 to D9, D11, D12, plugin turn independence, the unsupported-session failsafe, the clear-session closing rule), the request class, the review record, and the completion rules are defined in the architecture document section 3.1, with their verbatim operator quotes.\\n 32 | \\n 33 | Scope and process\\n 34 | - Server + client + REPL + MCP tools + plugin core, and every agent plugin updated and tested as needed (matrix below), in three scope areas: A (turn logging), B (class and reviews), C (stateless agent context), delivered through the four execution phases of \\\"Execution organization\\\" (requirements; interfaces and code stubs; TDD unit tests with mocks; implementation by bounded context with parallel coding agents).\\n 35 | - Strict BDPv4 per work package (below). Complete FR/TR/TEST with ACs and Use Cases (UC) for every new FR.\\n 36 | - Hostile validation runs on Codex CLI with model `gpt-6-astra` at reasoning effort `max` (operator, 2026-10-12: \\\"I want the HV executed by Astra 6.1 with the highest level of effort.\\\", resolved with the operator as mod