iles, not as a substitute for live model-authored logging.\\n210 |   ## MCP Requirements Visibility\\n211 |   - When implementing or enforcing requirements, query current effective requirements for the active workspace layer through the active agent's requirements surface (`workflow.requirements.effective`, `client.Requirements.GetEffectiveRequirementsAsync`, `req_effective`, or equivalent plugin tool). Use the layer catalog surface (`workflow.requirements.listLayers/createLayer/updateLayer`, `client.Requirements.ListRequirementLayersAsync/CreateRequirementLayerAsync/UpdateRequirementLayerAsync`, `req_list_layers/req_create_layer/req_update_layer`, or equivalent plugin tools) when the user asks to inspect or manage requirement layers. Do not treat raw all-requirements lists, exported markdown, or future-layer requirements as currently enforceable unless the user explicitly asks to inspect or plan those layers.\\n212 |   ## YAML Mutation Rule\\n213 | \\n214 |   - Never edit YAML files by appending, replacing, or removing text lines. This includes one-key configuration changes.\\n215 | \\n216 |   - Always deserialize the complete document into an object, mutate the object, serialize it, and save the serialized result.\\n217 | \\n218 |   - For PowerShell work, use `plugins/core/lib-ps/yaml-object-mutation.ps1` and call `Set-McpYamlObjectValue` or `Update-McpYamlObject`. Do not build YAML snippets as strings or use line-oriented edit helpers for YAML.\\n219 | \\n220 |   ## MCP and Plugin Failure Reporting\\n221 |   - MCP Server failures and required-plugin failures discovered while working must always be written as a normal failsafe YAML report. Use the plugin/REPL failsafe or pending YAML queue and preserve the triage method (`workflow.triage.report`, `triage_report`, or equivalent plugin triage tool) plus title, summary, observed behavior, expected behavior, severity, component, workspace path, dedupe key or error signature, affected paths, evidence, reproduction hints, tags, reporter agent, session id, turn id, and current TODO id when known.\\n222 |   - After the failsafe YAML is written and triage submission succeeds, continue the user's active task. Do not wait for triage research, TODO creation, or resolution.\\n223 |   - Do not create TODOs, requirements, GitHub issues, manual repair plans, or alternate reports for MCP Server/plugin failures unless the user's active request is to fix that failure.\\n224 |   - If the MCP Server or required-plugin failure reveals that code, docs, requirements, plugin skills, package sync, deployment, configuration, or other artifacts need to be updated as a separate repair workflow, write a detailed triage report that includes the needed changes, acceptance criteria, and validation evidence, then continue the active task after the failsafe YAML is written and triage submission succeeds.\\n225 |   - If triage submission fails, stop work and notify the user. Keep the failsafe YAML report for later replay; do not use raw REST or another reporting channel as a substitute.\\n226 | \\n227 |   ## Agent Help (MCP Server issues)\\n228 |   When you are stuck on any MCP Server surface (marker trust, plugin bootstrap, session log, TODO, requirements, triage, memory, context search, federation, hooks, or API behavior), request expert help instead of improvising raw REST bypasses.\\n229 |   - **When to invoke:** signature or health nonce failures you cannot resolve; unexpected tool or plugin behavior; `MCP_PLUGIN_UNAVAILABLE`; repeated 401/503 after re-reading this marker; documentation contradictions.\\n230 |   - **How to invoke:** preferred `workflow.agenthelp.createSession` or MCP tool `agent_help_create_session` with caller linkage (`callerAgent`, `callerSessionId`, `callerRequestId`) and a factual issue summary (observation vs inference separated). REST: `POST /mcpserver/agent-help/session` then WebSocket `/mcpserver/agent-help/session/{id}/stream` (SSE fallback: `POST .../turn/stream`).\\n231 |   - **Caller obligations:** include reproduction steps, exact error text, and files already consulted. Do not instruct the helper to ignore guardrails, skip triage, or mutate TODO/session-log/requirements storage directly.\\n232 |   - Agent help is for diagnosis and coordination, not a substitute for the required plugin route for normal MCP work.\\n233 | \\n234 |   ## MCP Memories\\n235 |   MCP memories are durable operator guidance managed by the MCP Server. Global memories apply to every workspace; Workspace memories apply only to the active workspace. Memory IDs use `MEMORY-{CATEGORY}-{NNN}`.\\n236 |   Agents that support request-boundary injection must render required memories exactly as:\\n237 |   REQUIRED MEMORIES - MEMORY-REQ-001: Raw memory text.\\n238 |   If no required memories are visible, render:\\n239 |   REQUIRED MEMORIES - None.\\n240 |   Preserve raw memory text exactly; do not summarize, paraphrase, reorder within a scope, silently generate new memories, or add secrets. MCP memories are not a secret store. Effective memory order is Global memories first sorted by ID, then current Workspace memories sorted by ID.\\n241 |   Existing agent-local memory stores are caches or migration sources only; the MCP memory store is the authoritative shared record. Import agent-local memories only when the operator approves the scope, the content belongs to this workspace or is intentionally Global, and the import does not leak secrets, private notes, or unrelated workspace guidance.\\n242 |   Attribute memory mutations with the real agent or user identity via `updatedBy` when supported, and record successful memory add/update/remove operations in the active session log through `workflow.sessionlog.appendActions`. Keep import provenance in the session log or dialog instead of adding private source paths to memory text.\\n243 |   Manage memories through the required plugin or MCP tool surface: `memory_add`, `memory_list`, `memory_update`, `memory_remove`, `memory_remember`, `memory_recall`, `memory_explore`, `memory_consolidate`, `memory_promote`, `memory_revert`; REPL `workflow.memory.add`, `workflow.memory.list`, `workflow.memory.update`, `workflow.memory.remove`, `workflow.memory.remember`, `workflow.memory.recall`, `workflow.memory.explore`, `workflow.memory.consolidate`, `workflow.memory.promote`, `workflow.memory.revert`; or REST `/mcpserver/memory` when explicitly allowed for non-plugin diagnostics.\\n244 | \\n245 |   ## Codex MCP Quickstart\\n246 |   If you are Codex, use the Codex plugin wrappers before direct REPL calls:\\n247 |   1. Run `Invoke-CodexMcpPlugin.ps1 -Command Status` from the `mcpserver-codex-plugin` root to verify plugin root, marker trust, health nonce, workspace path, session id, current turn, and supported namespaces.\\n248 |   2. Invoke MCP workflow methods with `Invoke-CodexMcpPlugin.ps1 -Command Invoke -Method <method> -Params <yaml>` so YAML parameters are passed through stdin instead of fragile nested shell quoting.\\n249 |   3. Complete the session-log turn intentionally with `Invoke-CodexMcpPlugin.ps1 -Command CompleteTurn -Response <final response>` before the final answer when the stop gate cannot infer completion.\\n250 |   4. For `mcpserver-repl --agent-stdio`, send one single-line JSON request envelope per stdin line. Do not send formatted YAML or a single `type: batch` envelope; batch envelopes are rejected with `unsupported_batch_envelope`.\\n251 | \\n252 |   ## PowerShell.MCP Command Routing\\n253 |   For every PowerShell Core (`pwsh`) invocation on every operating system:\\n254 |   - Use PowerShell.MCP 1.14.0 or newer from PSGallery as the command runner. Install or update it with `Install-PSResource PowerShell.MCP` when the agent environment does not provide a current version.\\n255 |   - `execute_command` is the primary MCP tool for PowerShell cmdlets, CLI tools, navigation, builds, tests, file-system work, and process management. Do not use the removed `invoke_expression` name. Host-qualified names depend on the configured server key: for example, `mcp__powershell__execute_command` for `powershell` and `mcp__pwsh__execute_command` for `pwsh`.\\n256 |   - Calling `start_console` first is optional because `execute_command` auto-starts or reuses a console. Call `start_console` explicitly only to pre-warm at `start_location`, show a banner, allocate a sub-agent console, or force an additional console by supplying `reason`; omit `reason` when reuse is preferred.\\n257 |   - Use `get_current_location` when the current PowerShell context matters. For a sub-agent, call `start_console` with `is_subagent=true`, retain the returned `agent_id`, and pass it on every later PowerShell.MCP tool call.\\n258 |   - When a command continues after the call returns, use `wait_for_completion` to retrieve the cached result instead of running `Start-Sleep`. Use `cancel` for an interruptible pipeline or native CLI, and `close_console` for a non-interruptible command or PowerShell host prompt. If `close_console` reports that a human is typing, only repeat the same PID call when confirming that the console should be closed.\\n259 |   - In 1.14.0, blocking timeouts are end-to-end deadlines and are capped at 170 seconds for Claude Code and 50 seconds for other or unidentified clients unless a lower limit applies. A timed-out command keeps running and its result is cached for a later tool call.\\n260 |   - Route `pwsh` commands through a PowerShell.MCP session instead of launching ad hoc PowerShell processes. On Windows, reuse one session for the workspace and route `node` invocations through it as well.\\n261 |   - When PowerShell.MCP must launch PowerShell Core directly, invoke `pwsh.exe` with `-NoProfile -NonInteractive`. Do not use `powershell.exe`.\\n262 |   - The PowerShell escape character is the backtick `` ` ``, not the backslash. Never use C or bash-style `\\\\\\\"` escaping inside PowerShell command strings.\\n263 |   - Prefer single-quoted PowerShell strings for literal regex, paths, JSON/YAML snippets, and text containing pipeline characters such as `|`.\\n264 |   - When a command needs nested quoting, choose one of these patterns before improvising: `-File` with explicit arguments; piping stdin to the called tool or script; single-quoted or double-quoted here-strings; `-EncodedCommand` for complex inline PowerShell; or correct backtick escaping for embedded PowerShell quotes.\\n265 |   - If a regex or literal argument contains `|`, `&`, `;`, parentheses, braces, or quotes, verify it is inside a single PowerShell argument before running.\\n266 |   - For text reads and edits inside PowerShell.MCP, use `Show-TextFiles`, `Add-LinesToFile`, `Update-LinesInFile`, `Update-MatchInFile`, and `Remove-LinesFromFile`; pass literal content through `var1` to `var4`. Version 1.12.0 and newer stage replacements beside the target, so do not apply the obsolete same-volume `TEMP`/`TMP` workaround. Re-read or diff every edit before claiming it landed.\\n267 |   ## Byrd Development Process\\n268 |   `Byrd Dev Process`, `BDP`, `BPDv4`, and `Byrd Development Process` refer to `F:\\\\GitHub\\\\McpServer\\\\docs\\\\Development-Process-draft-v4.md`.\\n269 | \\n270 |   ## Federation Topology\\n271 |   - To identify hub/proxy topology, call the supported federation status surface, preferably through your required plugin or client wrapper: `/mcpserver/federation/status`. - Status fields to report when relevant: `role`, `configuredRole`, `hubBaseUrl`, `proxyId`, `proxyCount`, `hostedWorkspaceCount`, `queueDepth`, `fanoutDepth`, `conflictCount`, and `staleReadStatus`. - `Standalone` serves local workspaces only. `DirectProxy` is the existing point-to-point proxy mode. `Hub` is authoritative for enrolled proxies and global workspace inventory. `LocalProxy` forwards MCP requests to `hubBaseUrl` and queues mutating requests during hub outages. - LocalProxy queued writes return `202 Accepted` with `X-Mcp-Queued: true` and `X-Mcp-Operation-Id`. Treat the write as optimistic until the hub acknowledges it. - If `queueDepth`, `fanoutDepth`, `conflictCount`, or stale-read status is non-zero/non-clear, include that in session log status and avoid claiming federation synchronization is complete.\\n272 | \\n273 |   ## Per User Message\\n274 |   1. Post a new session log turn before starting work.\\n275 |   2. Complete the user's request.\\n276 |   3. Update the turn with results and actions when done.\\n277 | \\n278 |   ## Re-run Full Session Start Only If\\n279 |   - The user explicitly says \\\"Start Session\\\". - Signature verification fails. - /health fails. - /health nonce verification fails. - Any /mcpserver/* call returns 401. - The marker endpoint/key changes after a server restart.\\n280 | \\n281 |   ## Continuing After a Server Restart\\n282 |   The workspace API key rotates on every server restart, so an in-flight agent will begin seeing 401 on /mcpserver/* calls. When that happens, resume through your required agent plugin - do not hand-roll raw REST for session, TODO, or requirements work:\\n283 |   1. Treat the first 401 (or a /health failure) as a restart signal and stop using the cached API key immediately.\\n284 |   2. Re-read this marker file (AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml) for the rotated apiKey and current endpoints.\\n285 |   3. Re-verify trust before resuming: recompute the marker signature with the new key and call /health with a fresh nonce.\\n286 |   4. Re-bootstrap and resume through your required agent plugin. Codex: run `Invoke-CodexMcpPlugin.ps1 -Command Status` to re-establish marker trust, health nonce, workspace, and the current session/turn, then continue with `Invoke-CodexMcpPlugin.ps1 -Command Invoke -Method <method> -Params <yaml>`. Other agents: re-run the plugin session-start hook/command, then continue via the plugin session tools.\\n287 |   5. Recover the open turn instead of starting over: query session history through the plugin (workflow.sessionlog.queryHistory), find the in-progress turn, then keep updating and complete it via the plugin. Do not open a duplicate session for the same work.\\n288 |   5a. Treat `deprecated: true` on successful `workflow.*` results as metadata, not wrapper failure. Treat an empty `workflow.sessionlog.queryHistory` result as a valid no-match result. Re-check the workspace current directory, explicit `agent` or `sourceType`, and plugin cache/session state through the wrapper; do not use raw REST merely because history is empty or marked deprecated.\\n289 |   6. If the plugin cannot re-establish trust after the restart, record `MCP_PLUGIN_UNAVAILABLE:<Agent>` and continue with non-MCP local diagnosis only.\\n290 | \\n291 |   ## Rules\\n292 |   0. NEVER write to TODO.yaml directly.\\n293 |   1. Generate SessionId values using `session.init` (REPL) or `New-McpSessionLogSlug -Agent <SourceType> -Model <Model>` (PowerShell module), then create session logs with `session.new` (REPL) or `New-McpSessionLog -SessionId` (PowerShell). Do not handcraft SessionId values.\\n294 |   1a. Agents must identify themselves accurately in session logs. `sourceType` and the SessionId `<Agent>` prefix must use the agent's real identity in Pascal-Case. Do not use lowercase forms, placeholders, or legacy aliases.\\n295 |   2. Post a new session log turn (`session.turn.add` for REPL or `Add-McpSessionTurn` for PowerShell) before starting work. Update it with results (`Response`) and actions (`Add-McpAction` / `session.action.add`) when done. Persist after each meaningful update, not just at the end.\\n296 |   2a. Before any compaction step, persist the current session log state. After compaction, update the session log again to record the compaction outcome and recovered context.\\n297 |   3. If signature verification, the /health request, or nonce verification fails, log `MCP_UNTRUSTED`, continue without the MCP server, and do not probe additional MCP endpoints.\\n298 |   4. Marker signatures use HMAC-SHA256 with the workspace API key in this file as the verifier. Recompute the canonical payload exactly as described by the marker metadata before trusting the file.\\n299 |   5. Use your required agent plugin for session log, TODO, requirements, import/export, and traceability operations. Do not use raw API calls or generic helper paths except for read-only diagnosis after plugin failure.\\n300 |   6. Write decisions, requirements, and state to the session log, not just conversation. Capture rich turn detail: interpretation, response/status, actions (type/status/filePath), contextList, filesModified, designDecisions, requirementsDiscovered, blockers, and key processingDialog updates.\\n301 |   7. Follow workspace conventions in AGENTS.md and .github/copilot-instructions.md.\\n302 |   8. When you need API schemas, module examples, or compliance rules, load them from docs/context/ or use context_search.\\n303 |   9. Do not fabricate information. Acknowledge mistakes. Distinguish facts from speculation.\\n304 |   10. Prioritize correctness over speed. Do not ship code you have not verified compiles.\\n305 | \\n306 |   11. Use the Byrd Development Process V4 for all plans: organize work into small gated implementation slices, define validation before broadening scope, and keep session-log/TODO updates current between meaningful steps. A Byrd gate requires 100% test success before moving to the next iteration: zero failed tests and zero skipped tests in the executed validation scope. Tests are the progress ledger; do not hide unfinished work with skipped tests. Track deferred scope in MCP TODO/requirements and add its tests when that slice begins.\\n307 | \\n308 |   ## Commit Signing\\n309 |   ALL COMMITS MUST BE SIGNED WITH `model-effort@agent` and Model's Confidence in the Correctness, Accuracy, and Completeness of the commit's contents.\\n310 | \\n311 |   ## Planning Standard\\n312 |   Plans are frontier-model handoff artifacts. Write them so a less expensive implementation model can execute successfully without resolving unstated product, architecture, interface, data, testing, rollout, or validation decisions.\\n313 |   Every plan must be decision-complete. Lock the intended behavior, public interfaces, data shapes, migration/backfill strategy, failure modes, rollout or deployment path, validation commands, and acceptance criteria before implementation starts.\\n314 |   Every plan must capture requirements before implementation: create or update FR/TR/TEST records through the required MCP requirements workflow, include the requirement IDs in the TODO and session log, and map FR to TR and TEST coverage.\\n315 |   Every implementation plan must name the TDD unit tests to add or update first, the expected red state, the green criteria, and the exact validation scope for each Byrd gate.\\n316 | \\n317 |   ## Naming Conventions\\n318 | \\n319 |   - Persisted TODO IDs must be uppercase canonical ids in either <SDLC-PHASE>-<AREA>-### form (regex: ^[A-Z]+-[A-Z0-9]+-\\\\d{3}$) or ISSUE-{number} form (regex: ^ISSUE-\\\\d+$).\\n320 |   - Valid TODO IDs: PLAN-NAMINGCONVENTIONS-001, MCP-API-042, ISSUE-17.\\n321 |   - Invalid TODO IDs: plan-api-001, MCP-API-42, ISSUE-ABC, MCPAPI001.\\n322 |   - Create requests may use ISSUE-NEW only when the intent is to create a new GitHub-backed TODO. The server will create the GitHub issue immediately and persist the TODO using the canonical ISSUE-{number} id.\\n323 |   - Session IDs must use <Agent>-<yyyyMMddTHHmmssZ>-<suffix> and start with the exact agent/source type prefix.\\n324 |   - `<Agent>` / `sourceType` must be the agent's real identity in Pascal-Case. Valid example: `Codex`. Invalid examples: `codex`, `copilotcli` (when the agent is Codex), `Assistant`.\\n325 |   - Valid Session ID: Copilot-20260304T113901Z-namingconv.\\n326 |   - Invalid Session IDs: copilot-20260304T113901Z-namingconv, Copilot-2026-03-04-namingconv.\\n327 |   - Request IDs must use req-<yyyyMMddTHHmmssZ>-<slugOrOrdinal> and be unique within a session.\\n328 |   - Valid Request ID: req-20260304T113901Z-plan-namingconventions-001.\\n329 |   - Invalid Request IDs: req-plan-namingconventions-001, request-20260304T113901Z-task-01.\\n330 | \\n331 |   ## Workspace\\n332 | \\n333 |   - Name: McpServer\\n334 |   - Path: /home/sharpninja/github/McpServer\\n335 |   - Primary: True\\n336 |   - Data Directory: /home/sharpninja/github/McpServer\\n337 |   - Todo Path: docs/Project/TODO.yaml\\n338 | \\n339 | \\n340 |   ## Authentication\\n341 | \\n342 |   All /mcpserver/* endpoints require a per-workspace auth token:\\n343 |   - Header: X-Api-Key: [REDACTED:api-key]\\n344 |   - Or query param: ?api_key=[REDACTED:api-key]\\n345 |   If you receive a 401, re-read this marker file — the token rotates on each server restart.\\n346 | \\n347 |   ## Where Things Live\\n348 | \\n349 |   - AGENTS.md — agent conduct, requirements tracking, session continuity, glossary\\n350 |   - .github/copilot-instructions.md — build/test commands, architecture, coding conventions\\n351 |   - docs/context/ — on-demand reference (schemas, module docs, compliance rules, action types)\\n352 |   - docs/Project/ — requirements docs, TODO.yaml, mapping matrices\\n353 |   - templates/ — prompt templates\\n354 | \\n355 |   ## Context Loading by Task Type\\n356 | \\n357 |   - Session logging → docs/context/session-log-schema.md + docs/context/module-bootstrap.md (pull module-bootstrap via MCP context)\\n358 |   - TODO management → docs/context/todo-schema.md + docs/context/module-bootstrap.md (pull module-bootstrap via MCP context)\\n359 |   - API integration → docs/context/api-capabilities.md (or GET http://LAB-OMARCHY:7147/swagger/v1/swagger.json)\\n360 |   - Adding dependencies → docs/context/compliance-rules.md\\n361 |   - Logging actions → docs/context/action-types.md\\n362 | \\n363 |   ## Protocols\\n364 | \\n365 |   - REST API: http://LAB-OMARCHY:7147/mcpserver/* (requires X-Api-Key). Use this for typed client/API integration and read-only diagnosis after the required plugin/tool route fails. Do not bypass the required plugin for session log, TODO, requirements, import/export, or traceability operations. Swagger UI: http://LAB-OMARCHY:7147/swagger\\n366 |   - MCP Streamable HTTP: POST http://LAB-OMARCHY:7147/mcp-transport (no API key required)\\n367 |   - Health: GET http://LAB-OMARCHY:7147/health\\n368 | \\n369 |   ## Repository Access\\n370 | \\n371 |   - Hosted agents expose built-in repository tools: `mcp_repo_list`, `mcp_repo_read`, and `mcp_repo_write`.\\n372 |   - All repository paths are relative to /home/sharpninja/github/McpServer.\\n373 |   - REST clients can browse and edit files through http://LAB-OMARCHY:7147/mcpserver/repo/list and http://LAB-OMARCHY:7147/mcpserver/repo/file using the API key above.\\n374 |   - If you need to browse or edit files, use these repository tools instead of saying that filesystem access is unavailable.\\n375 |   ## Local Program Execution\\n376 | \\n377 |   - Hosted agents expose a built-in local program execution tool: `mcp_desktop_launch`.\\n378 |   - Use it when you need to start a local Windows program through MCP Server instead of claiming local program execution is unavailable.\\n379 |   - Prefer `createNoWindow: true` and `waitForExit: true` for short non-interactive verification commands.\\n380 |   - REST clients can launch programs through http://LAB-OMARCHY:7147/mcpserver/desktop/launch using the API key above.\\n381 | \\n382 | \\n383 | \\n384 | \\n385 | \\n386 |   ## Before Delivering Output\\n387 | \\n388 |   Verify: session log is current, decisions are recorded, requirements are tracked, code compiles, action types are correct (see docs/context/action-types.md).\\n389 | \\n390 |   ## Available Capabilities\\n391 | \\n392 |   The following core libraries are indexed and available for semantic search/context retrieval:\\n393 |   - McpServer.Cqrs (CQRS framework)\\n394 |   - McpServer.Cqrs.Mvvm (MVVM support)\\n395 | \\n396 |   ---\\n397 |   MCP Server version: 1.4.41-recovery+b53ce6b8.preserved-grok.b53ce6b8e498d9d12982c1d64ff989217383ba17\",\n              \"success\": true\n            },\n            {\n              \"query\": \"/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/docs/context/module-bootstrap.md\",\n              \"result\": \"  1 | # Module Bootstrap Reference\\n  2 | \\n  3 | Load this file when setting up helper modules at session start.\\n  4 | \\n  5 | ## Overview\\n  6 | \\n  7 | **Preferred: `qbrain-ai-repl` (single-line JSON envelopes)** - The `qbrain-ai-repl` CLI tool is the recommended agent entrypoint. Direct `--agent-stdio` callers send one single-line JSON request envelope per stdin line and receive result, error, or event envelopes on stdout. Do not send formatted YAML or wrap multiple requests in `type: batch`; unsupported batch envelopes are rejected with `unsupported_batch_envelope`.\\n  8 | \\n  9 | **Supported: PowerShell helper modules** - `McpSession.psm1` and `McpTodo.psm1` remain supported until feature parity is validated. Use these modules when you need direct PowerShell integration or when REPL usage is not practical.\\n 10 | \\n 11 | Helper modules handle workspace routing (`X-Workspace-Path` header) automatically. Raw `Invoke-RestMethod` / `curl` calls to `/qbrainai/sessionlog` and `/qbrainai/todo` endpoints will target the wrong workspace. Use REPL or modules instead.\\n 12 | \\n 13 | ## REPL Bootstrap (Preferred)\\n 14 | \\n 15 | After handshake, send one single-line JSON request envelope per stdin line. Build JSON from native objects and serialize. Do not handwrite fragile JSON string literals when a serializer is available.\\n 16 | \\n 17 | ### Starting the REPL\\n 18 | \\n 19 | ```powershell\\n 20 | # Launch the REPL in the workspace directory\\n 21 | qbrain-ai-repl --workspace \\\"C:\\\\projects\\\\MyWorkspace\\\"\\n 22 | \\n 23 | # STDIO mode for agent integration\\n 24 | qbrain-ai-repl --agent-stdio\\n 25 | \\n 26 | # Or use the default workspace if registered as primary\\n 27 | qbrain-ai-repl\\n 28 | ```\\n 29 | \\n 30 | ### Handshake\\n 31 | \\n 32 | Client to server hello request:\\n 33 | \\n 34 | ```json\\n 35 | {\\\"type\\\":\\\"hello\\\",\\\"payload\\\":{\\\"protocolVersion\\\":\\\"1.0\\\",\\\"clientName\\\":\\\"Copilot\\\",\\\"clientVersion\\\":\\\"2.0.0\\\",\\\"capabilities\\\":[\\\"sessionlog\\\",\\\"todo\\\",\\\"requirements\\\",\\\"streaming\\\"],\\\"metadata\\\":{\\\"workspacePath\\\":\\\"C:\\\\\\\\workspace\\\\\\\\project\\\"}}}\\n 36 | ```\\n 37 | \\n 38 | Server hello response:\\n 39 | \\n 40 | ```json\\n 41 | {\\\"type\\\":\\\"hello\\\",\\\"payload\\\":{\\\"protocolVersion\\\":\\\"1.0\\\",\\\"capabilities\\\":[\\\"client-passthrough\\\",\\\"sessionlog\\\",\\\"todo\\\",\\\"requirements\\\",\\\"streaming\\\"]}}\\n 42 | ```\\n 43 | \\n 44 | ### REPL Request Format\\n 45 | \\n 46 | All REPL requests use a typed envelope. The request body is `type: request` with a `payload` that carries a unique `requestId`, the namespaced `method`, and its `params`:\\n 47 | \\n 48 | ```json\\n 49 | {\\\"type\\\":\\\"request\\\",\\\"payload\\\":{\\\"requestId\\\":\\\"<unique-request-id>\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"workflow.sessionlog.beginTurn\\\",\\\"params\\\":{\\\"requestId\\\":\\\"<unique-request-id>\\\",\\\"queryTitle\\\":\\\"Implement feature X\\\",\\\"queryText\\\":\\\"User requested feature X\\\",\\\"planFile\\\":\\\"None\\\",\\\"todoId\\\":\\\"None\\\"}}}\\n 50 | ```\\n 51 | \\n 52 | Methods are namespaced: `workflow.sessionlog.*` and `workflow.todo.*` are plugin-local workflow verbs that update the cache and `current-turn.yaml` and persist through the real client; `client.<Client>.<Method>` is a passthrough to any typed sub-client method.\\n 53 | \\n 54 | ### Session Log Operations\\n 55 | \\n 56 | ```json\\n 57 | {\\\"type\\\":\\\"request\\\",\\\"payload\\\":{\\\"requestId\\\":\\\"req-20260304T113901Z-001\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"workflow.sessionlog.bootstrap\\\",\\\"params\\\":{}}}\\n 58 | ```\\n 59 | \\n 60 | ```json\\n 61 | {\\\"type\\\":\\\"request\\\",\\\"payload\\\":{\\\"requestId\\\":\\\"req-20260304T113901Z-002\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"workflow.sessionlog.openSession\\\",\\\"params\\\":{\\\"sessionId\\\":\\\"Copilot-20260304T113901Z-example\\\",\\\"title\\\":\\\"Implement new feature\\\",\\\"agent\\\":\\\"Copilot\\\",\\\"sourceType\\\":\\\"Copilot\\\",\\\"model\\\":\\\"gpt-5.3-codex\\\"}}}\\n 62 | ```\\n 63 | \\n 64 | ```json\\n 65 | {\\\"type\\\":\\\"request\\\",\\\"payload\\\":{\\\"requestId\\\":\\\"req-20260304T113901Z-003\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"workflow.sessionlog.beginTurn\\\",\\\"params\\\":{\\\"requestId\\\":\\\"req-20260304T113901Z-003\\\",\\\"queryTitle\\\":\\\"Implement feature X\\\",\\\"queryText\\\":\\\"User requested feature X\\\",\\\"planFile\\\":\\\"None\\\",\\\"todoId\\\":\\\"None\\\"}}}\\n 66 | ```\\n 67 | \\n 68 | `workflow.sessionlog.beginTurn` metadata precedence is explicit `planFile` and `todoId`, then verified cache values, then exact `None` for both fields. Ordinary first persistence requires that pair: a missing, null, empty, or whitespace field is not a valid raw first-persist payload. Supersession accepts both `canceled` and `cancelled` and stores exact `None` when either field is omitted. A same-request durable reopen omits an absent field so the stored value is preserved.\\n 69 | \\n 70 | ```json\\n 71 | {\\\"type\\\":\\\"request\\\",\\\"payload\\\":{\\\"requestId\\\":\\\"req-20260304T113901Z-003\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"workflow.sessionlog.updateTurn\\\",\\\"params\\\":{\\\"response\\\":\\\"Implementing feature X\\\",\\\"interpretation\\\":\\\"User requested feature X\\\"}}}\\n 72 | ```\\n 73 | \\n 74 | ```json\\n 75 | {\\\"type\\\":\\\"request\\\",\\\"payload\\\":{\\\"requestId\\\":\\\"req-20260304T113901Z-003\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"workflow.sessionlog.appendActions\\\",\\\"params\\\":{\\\"actions\\\":[{\\\"description\\\":\\\"Create the feature module\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"create\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"filePath\\\":\\\"src/feature-x.ts\\\"},{\\\"description\\\":\\\"Wire the feature into main\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"edit\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"filePath\\\":\\\"src/main.ts\\\"}]}}}\\n 76 | ```\\n 77 | \\n 78 | ```json\\n 79 | {\\\"type\\\":\\\"request\\\",\\\"payload\\\":{\\\"requestId\\\":\\\"req-20260304T113901Z-003\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"workflow.sessionlog.completeTurn\\\",\\\"params\\\":{\\\"response\\\":\\\"Feature X implemented successfully\\\"}}}\\n 80 | ```\\n 81 | \\n 82 | ### TODO Operations\\n 83 | \\n 84 | ```json\\n 85 | {\\\"type\\\":\\\"request\\\",\\\"payload\\\":{\\\"requestId\\\":\\\"req-20260304T113901Z-010\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"workflow.todo.query\\\",\\\"params\\\":{}}}\\n 86 | ```\\n 87 | \\n 88 | ```json\\n 89 | {\\\"type\\\":\\\"request\\\",\\\"payload\\\":{\\\"requestId\\\":\\\"req-20260304T113901Z-011\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"workflow.todo.get\\\",\\\"params\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"MVP-MCP-001\\\"}}}\\n 90 | ```\\n 91 | \\n 92 | ```json\\n 93 | {\\\"type\\\":\\\"request\\\",\\\"payload\\\":{\\\"requestId\\\":\\\"req-20260304T113901Z-012\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"workflow.todo.create\\\",\\\"params\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"MVP-MCP-042\\\",\\\"title\\\":\\\"Implement feature Y\\\",\\\"section\\\":\\\"Development\\\",\\\"priority\\\":\\\"high\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"Feature Y implementation\\\"}}}\\n 94 | ```\\n 95 | \\n 96 | ```json\\n 97 | {\\\"type\\\":\\\"request\\\",\\\"payload\\\":{\\\"requestId\\\":\\\"req-20260304T113901Z-013\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"workflow.todo.update\\\",\\\"params\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"MVP-MCP-042\\\",\\\"done\\\":true}}}\\n 98 | ```\\n 99 | \\n100 | ### REPL Benefits\\n101 | \\n102 | - **No Shell Quoting Issues**: JSON is a single line per request, so agents avoid PowerShell and bash quoting of nested YAML documents.\\n103 | - **Structured Data**: Nested arrays and objects are native JSON values.\\n104 | - **Idempotent**: REPL state is isolated; no global shell variables or module imports to manage.\\n105 | - **Cross-Platform**: Works identically on Windows (PowerShell), Linux (bash), and macOS.\\n106 | - **Error Handling**: Error envelopes include structured `code`, `message`, `retryable`, and `details`.\\n107 | \\n108 | ### REPL Response Format\\n109 | \\n110 | A successful response is `type: result` with the matching `requestId` and a command-specific `result`:\\n111 | \\n112 | ```json\\n113 | {\\\"type\\\":\\\"result\\\",\\\"payload\\\":{\\\"requestId\\\":\\\"<matching-request-id>\\\",\\\"result\\\":{}}}\\n114 | ```\\n115 | \\n116 | On error the response is `type: error` with a structured code, message, and details:\\n117 | \\n118 | ```json\\n119 | {\\\"type\\\":\\\"error\\\",\\\"payload\\\":{\\\"requestId\\\":\\\"<matching-request-id>\\\",\\\"code\\\":\\\"invalid_request\\\",\\\"message\\\":\\\"Request envelope failed validation\\\",\\\"retryable\\\":false,\\\"details\\\":{}}}\\n120 | ```\\n121 | \\n122 | Streaming uses `type: event` envelopes on stdout.\\n123 | \\n124 | ## PowerShell Bootstrap\\n125 | \\n126 | ```powershell\\n127 | # 1. Discover and download modules from the Tool Registry\\n128 | $headers = @{ \\\"X-Api-Key\\\" = \\\"<apiKey from AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml>\\\" }\\n129 | Invoke-RestMethod -Uri \\\"http://localhost:7147/qbrainai/tools/search?keyword=session\\\" -Headers $headers\\n130 | Invoke-RestMethod -Uri \\\"http://localhost:7147/qbrainai/tools/search?keyword=todo\\\" -Headers $headers\\n131 | # Save the downloaded files as McpSession.psm1 and McpTodo.psm1\\n132 | \\n133 | # 2. Import and initialize\\n134 | Import-Module ./McpSession.psm1\\n135 | Import-Module ./McpTodo.psm1\\n136 | $sessionSlug = Initialize-McpSession -Agent \\\"Copilotcli\\\" -Model \\\"gpt-5.3-codex\\\"  # returns only the reusable session-slug string; does not create a session object\\n137 | Initialize-McpTodo             # reads the marker file, verifies the marker signature, and performs the /health nonce handshake\\n138 | ```\\n139 | \\n140 | `Initialize-McpSession` configures module-scoped connection state, verifies the marker\\n141 | signature when a marker file is used, performs the `/health` nonce handshake, and persists or\\n142 | reuses the session-slug metadata in local state files. It does not create a session-log\\n143 | record and it does not return a session object. Call\\n144 | `New-McpSessionLog` when you need the actual session object that `Add-McpSessionTurn`,\\n145 | `Set-McpSessionTurn`, and `Update-McpSessionLog` operate on explicitly.\\n146 | \\n147 | The marker signature is self-verifiable: bootstrap modules recompute the HMAC-SHA256 marker\\n148 | signature by using the workspace API key already present in `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml` as the\\n149 | verifier. After signature verification succeeds, the module calls `/health?nonce=<random>`\\n150 | and requires the response to echo that exact nonce before any MCP endpoint is trusted.\\n151 | \\n152 | ## Bash Bootstrap\\n153 | \\n154 | ```bash\\n155 | source ./mcp-session.sh && mcp_session_init\\n156 | source ./mcp-todo.sh   && mcp_todo_init\\n157 | ```\\n158 | \\n159 | ## Error Recovery\\n160 | \\n161 | If signature verification, the `/health` request, or nonce verification fails, the helper\\n162 | modules emit `MCP_UNTRUSTED`, clear their MCP connection state, and stop before probing any\\n163 | additional MCP endpoints.\\n164 | \\n165 | If module initialization or session log push fails after a previously trusted bootstrap\\n166 | (e.g., 401), re-read the `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml` marker file for a fresh API key,\\n167 | re-initialize the modules, and retry. The API key rotates on each server restart.\\n168 | \\n169 | If module download fails, retry with exponential backoff.\\n170 | \\n171 | ## Comparison: REPL vs. PowerShell Module\\n172 | \\n173 | | Feature | REPL | PowerShell Module |\\n174 | |---------|------|-------------------|\\n175 | | Shell quoting issues | None (single-line JSON) | Frequent (JSON in strings) |\\n176 | | Cross-platform | Identical on all OS | PowerShell only |\\n177 | | Setup complexity | Launch one process | Import + Initialize |\\n178 | | State management | Self-contained | Module-scoped globals |\\n179 | | Nested data | Native JSON objects | Hashtable/PSObject |\\n180 | | Preferred for agents | Yes | No (legacy) |\\n181 | \\n182 | `McpSession.psm1` and `McpTodo.psm1` remain supported until parity is validated. New agent workflows should prefer `qbrain-ai-repl`.\",\n              \"success\": true\n            },\n            {\n              \"query\": \"/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/docs/context/todo-schema.md\",\n              \"result\": \"  1 | # TODO Management Schema Reference\\n  2 | \\n  3 | Load this file when you need to create, update, query, or manage project TODOs.\\n  4 | \\n  5 | \\n  6 | TODO mutations persist without the turn-transaction coordinator or keyserver on `develop` and on\\n  7 | the live Linux box MCP (FR-MCP-173, PLAN-TXNKEYSERVER-001). Keep live\\n  8 | `Mcp:TurnTransactions:Enabled=true` for QuadBrain. Do not edit `docs/Project/TODO.yaml` directly.\\n  9 | \\n 10 | ## Endpoints\\n 11 | \\n 12 | - `GET /qbrainai/todo` — list all todos\\n 13 | - `POST /qbrainai/todo` — create a new todo\\n 14 | - `GET /qbrainai/todo/{id}` — get a specific todo\\n 15 | - `PUT /qbrainai/todo/{id}` — update a todo\\n 16 | - `DELETE /qbrainai/todo/{id}` — delete a todo\\n 17 | - `GET /qbrainai/todo/{id}/prompt/implement` — get implementation prompt\\n 18 | - `GET /qbrainai/todo/{id}/prompt/plan` — get planning prompt\\n 19 | - `GET /qbrainai/todo/{id}/prompt/status` — get status prompt\\n 20 | - `POST /qbrainai/todo/{id}/requirements` — add requirements to a todo\\n 21 | \\n 22 | ## Naming Conventions (Normative)\\n 23 | \\n 24 | - Persisted TODO IDs must match either uppercase kebab-case ending in a three-digit sequence suffix\\n 25 |   or canonical GitHub issue IDs `ISSUE-{number}`.\\n 26 | - Required persisted-ID regex set: `^[A-Z][A-Z0-9]*(?:-[A-Z0-9]+)+-\\\\d{3}$` or `^ISSUE-\\\\d+$`\\n 27 | - Valid persisted-ID examples: `PLAN-NAMINGCONVENTIONS-001`, `PHASE0-REMOTE-001`, `MCP-TODO-CREATE-001`, `ISSUE-17`\\n 28 | - Invalid persisted-ID examples: `plan-api-001`, `MCP-API-42`, `MCP-001`, `ISSUE-ABC`, `MCPAPI001`\\n 29 | - Create requests may also use the special id `ISSUE-NEW`. The server immediately creates a GitHub\\n 30 |   issue, rewrites the saved TODO id to the canonical `ISSUE-{number}` value, and returns that canonical id.\\n 31 | - After the first sync, `ISSUE-{number}` descriptions are immutable. Server-side TODO update surfaces sync\\n 32 |   subsequent ISSUE changes to GitHub, keep the local description/body unchanged, and append a GitHub issue\\n 33 |   comment describing the applied change set.\\n 34 | - When creating/updating `dependsOn`, each dependency ID must follow the same persisted TODO ID convention.\\n 35 | \\n 36 | ## TodoFlatItem (returned by GET)\\n 37 | \\n 38 | ```json\\n 39 | {\\n 40 |   \\\"id\\\": \\\"string — unique TODO ID in uppercase kebab-case ending in -### or ISSUE-{number} (e.g. 'MCP-AUTH-001', 'MCP-TODO-CREATE-001', 'ISSUE-17')\\\",\\n 41 |   \\\"title\\\": \\\"string — brief title\\\",\\n 42 |   \\\"section\\\": \\\"string — grouping category (e.g. 'Backend', 'Frontend', 'Infrastructure')\\\",\\n 43 |   \\\"priority\\\": \\\"string — 'critical', 'high', 'medium', or 'low'\\\",\\n 44 |   \\\"done\\\": \\\"boolean — whether the task is complete\\\",\\n 45 |   \\\"estimate\\\": \\\"string|null — effort estimate (e.g. '2h', '1d')\\\",\\n 46 |   \\\"note\\\": \\\"string|null — additional context\\\",\\n 47 |   \\\"description\\\": [\\\"string array — detailed description lines\\\"],\\n 48 |   \\\"technicalDetails\\\": [\\\"string array — technical implementation notes\\\"],\\n 49 |   \\\"implementationTasks\\\": [\\n 50 |     { \\\"task\\\": \\\"string — subtask description\\\", \\\"done\\\": \\\"boolean\\\" }\\n 51 |   ],\\n 52 |   \\\"completedDate\\\": \\\"string|null — ISO 8601 when completed\\\",\\n 53 |   \\\"doneSummary\\\": \\\"string|null — summary of what was done\\\",\\n 54 |   \\\"remaining\\\": \\\"string|null — what work remains\\\",\\n 55 |   \\\"priorityNote\\\": \\\"string|null — why this priority\\\",\\n 56 |   \\\"reference\\\": \\\"string|null — link or reference\\\",\\n 57 |   \\\"dependsOn\\\": [\\\"string array — IDs of prerequisite todos\\\"],\\n 58 |   \\\"functionalRequirements\\\": [\\\"string array — FR IDs\\\"],\\n 59 |   \\\"technicalRequirements\\\": [\\\"string array — TR IDs\\\"]\\n 60 | }\\n 61 | ```\\n 62 | \\n 63 | ## TodoCreateRequest (POST body)\\n 64 | \\n 65 | ```json\\n 66 | {\\n 67 |   \\\"id\\\": \\\"string — REQUIRED TODO ID matching ^[A-Z][A-Z0-9]*(?:-[A-Z0-9]+)+-\\\\\\\\d{3}$ or ^ISSUE-\\\\\\\\d+$; create requests may also use ISSUE-NEW to create and persist a canonical GitHub-backed todo\\\",\\n 68 |   \\\"title\\\": \\\"string — REQUIRED brief title\\\",\\n 69 |   \\\"section\\\": \\\"string — REQUIRED grouping category\\\",\\n 70 |   \\\"priority\\\": \\\"string — REQUIRED: 'critical', 'high', 'medium', or 'low'\\\",\\n 71 |   \\\"estimate\\\": \\\"string|null\\\",\\n 72 |   \\\"description\\\": [\\\"string array|null\\\"],\\n 73 |   \\\"technicalDetails\\\": [\\\"string array|null\\\"],\\n 74 |   \\\"implementationTasks\\\": [{ \\\"task\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"done\\\": false }],\\n 75 |   \\\"note\\\": \\\"string|null\\\",\\n 76 |   \\\"remaining\\\": \\\"string|null\\\",\\n 77 |   \\\"dependsOn\\\": [\\\"string array|null — IDs of prerequisite todos\\\"],\\n 78 |   \\\"functionalRequirements\\\": [\\\"string array|null\\\"],\\n 79 |   \\\"technicalRequirements\\\": [\\\"string array|null\\\"]\\n 80 | }\\n 81 | ```\\n 82 | \\n 83 | ## TodoUpdateRequest (PUT body)\\n 84 | \\n 85 | ```json\\n 86 | {\\n 87 |   \\\"title\\\": \\\"string|null — only include fields you want to change\\\",\\n 88 |   \\\"priority\\\": \\\"string|null\\\",\\n 89 |   \\\"section\\\": \\\"string|null\\\",\\n 90 |   \\\"done\\\": \\\"boolean|null — set true to mark complete\\\",\\n 91 |   \\\"estimate\\\": \\\"string|null\\\",\\n 92 |   \\\"description\\\": [\\\"string array|null\\\"],\\n 93 |   \\\"technicalDetails\\\": [\\\"string array|null\\\"],\\n 94 |   \\\"implementationTasks\\\": [{ \\\"task\\\": \\\"string\\\", \\\"done\\\": true }],\\n 95 |   \\\"note\\\": \\\"string|null\\\",\\n 96 |   \\\"completedDate\\\": \\\"string|null\\\",\\n 97 |   \\\"doneSummary\\\": \\\"string|null\\\",\\n 98 |   \\\"remaining\\\": \\\"string|null\\\",\\n 99 |   \\\"dependsOn\\\": [\\\"string array|null\\\"],\\n100 |   \\\"functionalRequirements\\\": [\\\"string array|null\\\"],\\n101 |   \\\"technicalRequirements\\\": [\\\"string array|null\\\"]\\n102 | }\\n103 | ```\\n104 | \\n105 | ## McpTodo Module — PowerShell Lifecycle\\n106 | \\n107 | ```powershell\\n108 | # List todos at session start\\n109 | Get-McpTodo | Format-Table id, title, priority, done\\n110 | \\n111 | # Get a specific todo\\n112 | Get-McpTodo -Id \\\"MCP-AUTH-001\\\"\\n113 | \\n114 | # Create a new todo\\n115 | New-McpTodo -Id \\\"MCP-AUTH-001\\\" -Title \\\"Add JWT auth\\\" -Section \\\"Backend\\\" -Priority high `\\n116 |   -Description @(\\\"Implement JWT bearer tokens\\\") -Estimate \\\"4h\\\"\\n117 | \\n118 | # Create a GitHub-backed todo and let the server rewrite it to ISSUE-{number}\\n119 | New-McpTodo -Id \\\"ISSUE-NEW\\\" -Title \\\"Capture sync regression\\\" -Section \\\"issues\\\" -Priority high\\n120 | \\n121 | # Update fields\\n122 | Update-McpTodo -Id \\\"MCP-AUTH-001\\\" -Remaining \\\"Need tests\\\"\\n123 | \\n124 | # Mark complete\\n125 | Complete-McpTodo -Id \\\"MCP-AUTH-001\\\" -DoneSummary \\\"JWT auth complete\\\"\\n126 | \\n127 | # Get implementation guidance\\n128 | Get-McpTodoPrompt -Id \\\"MCP-AUTH-001\\\" -Type implement\\n129 | \\n130 | # Add requirements\\n131 | Add-McpTodoRequirements -Id \\\"MCP-AUTH-001\\\" -FunctionalRequirements @(\\\"FR-AUTH-001\\\") `\\n132 |   -TechnicalRequirements @(\\\"TR-AUTH-001\\\")\\n133 | \\n134 | # Delete\\n135 | Remove-McpTodo -Id \\\"MCP-AUTH-001\\\"\\n136 | ```\\n137 | \\n138 | ## McpTodo Module — Bash Lifecycle\\n139 | \\n140 | ```bash\\n141 | source ./mcp-todo.sh\\n142 | mcp_todo_init\\n143 | mcp_todo_list | jq '.items[] | {id, title, done}'\\n144 | mcp_todo_get \\\"MCP-AUTH-001\\\"\\n145 | mcp_todo_create \\\"MCP-AUTH-001\\\" \\\"Add JWT auth\\\" \\\"Backend\\\" \\\"high\\\" '{\\\"estimate\\\":\\\"4h\\\"}'\\n146 | mcp_todo_create \\\"ISSUE-NEW\\\" \\\"Capture sync regression\\\" \\\"issues\\\" \\\"high\\\" '{}'\\n147 | mcp_todo_update \\\"MCP-AUTH-001\\\" '{\\\"remaining\\\":\\\"Need tests\\\"}'\\n148 | mcp_todo_complete \\\"MCP-AUTH-001\\\" \\\"JWT auth complete\\\"\\n149 | mcp_todo_prompt \\\"MCP-AUTH-001\\\" \\\"implement\\\"\\n150 | mcp_todo_add_requirements \\\"MCP-AUTH-001\\\" '{\\\"functionalRequirements\\\":[\\\"FR-AUTH-001\\\"]}'\\n151 | mcp_todo_delete \\\"MCP-AUTH-001\\\"\\n152 | ```\\n153 | \\n154 | ## Raw API (for understanding only — use modules)\\n155 | \\n156 | The modules automatically include the `X-Workspace-Path` header for correct workspace routing. Raw calls will target the wrong workspace.\",\n              \"success\": true\n            },\n            {\n              \"query\": \"/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/docs/context/session-log-schema.md\",\n              \"result\": \"  1 | # Session Log Schema Reference\\n  2 | \\n  3 | Load this file when you need to create, update, or query session logs.\\n  4 | For specific agent operational instructions, follow `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml`.\\n  5 | \\n  6 | \\n  7 | Session-log mutations (every source type, including QBAgent) persist without the turn-transaction\\n  8 | coordinator or keyserver on `develop` and on the live Linux box MCP (FR-MCP-173). Keep live\\n  9 | `Mcp:TurnTransactions:Enabled=true` for QuadBrain; do not disable it to make session-log writes\\n 10 | succeed. Workspace-stamp repair remains fail-closed.\\n 11 | \\n 12 | ## Endpoints\\n 13 | \\n 14 | - `POST /qbrainai/sessionlog` — create or update a session log\\n 15 | - `GET /qbrainai/sessionlog?limit=N&offset=M&planFile=&todoId=&turnStatus=&staleOlderThanHours=` — query recent session logs; optional exact `planFile` and `todoId` filters after the same normalize/expand rules as persist; optional `turnStatus` plus `staleOlderThanHours` list sessions that still have matching turns older than N hours (BUG-TRIAGE-121). The query is read-only and does not cancel or complete those turns.\\n 16 | - `POST /qbrainai/sessionlog/{agent}/{sessionId}/{requestId}/begin` - first persist of a turn; body `SessionLifecycleBeginRequest` requires `planFile` and `todoId` (`None` when none)\\n 17 | - `POST /qbrainai/sessionlog/{agent}/{sessionId}/{requestId}/dialog` - stream reasoning dialog (incremental persist; not a full-session upsert)\\n 18 | \\n 19 | ## Submit acknowledgement (FR-MCP-SESSIONLIFE-003)\\n 20 | \\n 21 | `POST /qbrainai/sessionlog` returns a durable-write receipt only after the storage service\\n 22 | has completed its awaited save. The receipt retains `id`, `sourceType`, and `sessionId`,\\n 23 | and adds `persisted: true`, `degraded: false`, and `queued: false`. It includes `requestId`\\n 24 | for a single-turn submission; zero-turn and multi-turn submissions return a null request ID.\\n 25 | Failed or canceled persistence never produces this successful receipt.\\n 26 | \\n 27 | `SessionLogSubmitResult` preserves these fields through the typed client and REPL YAML\\n 28 | transport. A legacy response that omits `persisted` is not durable-write confirmation.\\n 29 | Update both the service and the installed REPL when deploying this contract. Failsafes\\n 30 | must remain until the plugin receives the required persistence acknowledgement.\\n 31 | \\n 32 | Whole-session snapshots cannot reopen a terminal turn with a supplied nonterminal status\\n 33 | or replace it with a different cancellation status. This prevents a late hook or failsafe\\n 34 | replay from overwriting completed history after an acknowledgement failure. Same-status\\n 35 | and omitted-status enrichment, cancellation of active turns, and recovery from canceled\\n 36 | to completed remain supported. Deliberate corrections and reopening use the explicit\\n 37 | single-turn update API. Relational submissions protect the read/check/save sequence with\\n 38 | serializable isolation and acknowledge after the owned transaction commits. A conflicting\\n 39 | write that cannot serialize fails rather than acknowledging overwritten terminal history;\\n 40 | its failsafe remains available for retry. Caller-owned, ambient, and enlisted transactions\\n 41 | are rejected before mutation because submission cannot acknowledge their uncommitted writes.\\n 42 | Reused contexts refresh the target session graph inside the transaction; pending edits to\\n 43 | that graph are rejected without discarding them, and unrelated tracked changes are retained.\\n 44 | \\n 45 | ## Outbound sanitization (FR-MCP-SESSIONLOGSAN-001)\\n 46 | \\n 47 | Query, GET, MCP, and stdio reads clone the DTO and apply `Mcp:SessionLogSanitization` rules before the payload leaves the process. Raw storage is unchanged. Replacement tokens include the rule Id (for example `[REDACTED:example-internal-token]`). A secret in a stored turn still participates in text filtering and does not change paging metadata. Invalid or duplicate rule Ids fail options validation. Regex timeout redacts fail-closed and does not log the input.\\n 48 | \\n 49 | ## Stale in_progress turns (BUG-TRIAGE-121)\\n 50 | \\n 51 | Forward-only. Do not mass-close historical `in_progress` turns. Recurrence of completed-then-canceled is the UserPromptSubmit isolation path: background briefs `reuse` an in-progress root work turn and `isolate-skip` a completed root turn.\\n 52 | \\n 53 | Operator listing of stale open turns (sessions that contain at least one matching turn; inspect `turns[].status` and `turns[].timestamp`; complete or fail individually):\\n 54 | \\n 55 | ```\\n 56 | GET /qbrainai/sessionlog?turnStatus=in_progress&staleOlderThanHours=24\\n 57 | ```\\n 58 | \\n 59 | MCP tool `sessionlog_query` with the same fields: `turnStatus=in_progress`, `staleOlderThanHours=24` (any positive hour count). Omit either field to relax that half of the filter. Mass close is out of scope.\\n 60 | \\n 61 | ## Required turn context (normative)\\n 62 | \\n 63 | - First persist and replace of a turn require both `planFile` and `todoId`.\\n 64 | - Accepted `planFile`: workspace-relative path, exact absolute path, `~/` home-relative path, or the exact sentinel `None` (case-sensitive). `..` is rejected.\\n 65 | - Accepted `todoId`: canonical MCP TODO id (`PHASE-AREA-###` or `ISSUE-N`) or `None`. FR/TR/TEST ids are rejected.\\n 66 | - Additive updates that omit either field keep the stored value. Replace that omits either field is rejected, except the canceled/cancelled hook-supersede persist: when the incoming turn status is `canceled` or `cancelled` and either field is omitted or blank, the server stamps `None` then validates. That is the only first-persist omission path (FR-MCP-SESSIONLOGCTX-001 AC-003 plus STORE-006).\\n 67 | - Reads never return null for these fields. Import, ingest, and federation persist a validated pair (`None` if extraction finds nothing).\\n 68 | \\n 69 | ## Naming Conventions (Normative)\\n 70 | \\n 71 | - `sessionId` must match `<Agent>-<yyyyMMddTHHmmssZ>-<suffix>`.\\n 72 | - `sessionId` regex: `^[A-Z][A-Za-z0-9]*-\\\\d{8}T\\\\d{6}Z-[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)*$`\\n 73 | - `sessionId` must start with the exact `sourceType`/`agent` prefix (case-sensitive).\\n 74 | - `requestId` must match `req-<yyyyMMddTHHmmssZ>-<slugOrOrdinal>`.\\n 75 | - `requestId` regex: `^req-\\\\d{8}T\\\\d{6}Z-[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)*$`\\n 76 | - Valid IDs:\\n 77 |   - `sessionId`: `Copilot-20260304T113901Z-namingconv`\\n 78 |   - `requestId`: `req-20260304T113901Z-plan-namingconventions-001`\\n 79 | - Invalid IDs:\\n 80 |   - `sessionId`: `copilot-20260304T113901Z-namingconv`, `Copilot-2026-03-04-namingconv`\\n 81 |   - `requestId`: `req-plan-namingconventions-001`, `request-20260304T113901Z-task-01`\\n 82 | \\n 83 | ## SessionLog (POST body)\\n 84 | \\n 85 | ```json\\n 86 | {\\n 87 |   \\\"sourceType\\\": \\\"string — YOUR agent name (e.g. 'Copilot', 'Cline', 'Cursor')\\\",\\n 88 |   \\\"sessionId\\\": \\\"string — required format <Agent>-<yyyyMMddTHHmmssZ>-<suffix> (e.g. 'Copilot-20260304T113901Z-feature-audit')\\\",\\n 89 |   \\\"agentSessionId\\\": \\\"string|null — provider-native root session identifier from the host payload; empty when unknown (the MCP sessionId is never echoed here)\\\",\\n 90 |   \\\"agentSessionTranscriptFile\\\": \\\"string|null — provider-native transcript path; only set when the file exists on disk, empty otherwise (never synthesized)\\\",\\n 91 |   \\\"agentExecutablePath\\\": \\\"string|null — resolved executable path for the agent host\\\",\\n 92 |   \\\"agentExecutableVersion\\\": \\\"string|null — executable version resolved from the agent host, or 'unknown' when discovery fails (never the plugin version)\\\",\\n 93 |   \\\"title\\\": \\\"string — brief session summary, keep updated\\\",\\n 94 |   \\\"model\\\": \\\"string — AI model name (e.g. 'claude-sonnet-4-20250514')\\\",\\n 95 |   \\\"started\\\": \\\"string — ISO 8601 timestamp when session began\\\",\\n 96 |   \\\"lastUpdated\\\": \\\"string — ISO 8601 timestamp of latest activity\\\",\\n 97 |   \\\"status\\\": \\\"string — 'in_progress', 'completed', 'failed', 'canceled', or 'cancelled'\\\",\\n 98 |   \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"string array — session-scoped tags; persist and return on query (not silently dropped)\\\"],\\n 99 |   \\\"turns\\\": [ \\\"array of RequestTurn objects (see below)\\\" ]\\n100 | }\\n101 | ```\\n102 | \\n103 | ## RequestTurn (each element in `turns`)\\n104 | \\n105 | ```json\\n106 | {\\n107 |   \\\"requestId\\\": \\\"string — required format req-<yyyyMMddTHHmmssZ>-<slugOrOrdinal>\\\",\\n108 |   \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"string — ISO 8601\\\",\\n109 |   \\\"queryText\\\": \\\"string — full user query or task description\\\",\\n110 |   \\\"queryTitle\\\": \\\"string — short summary of the query\\\",\\n111 |   \\\"response\\\": \\\"string — your response text\\\",\\n112 |   \\\"interpretation\\\": \\\"string — your understanding of what was asked\\\",\\n113 |   \\\"status\\\": \\\"string — 'completed', 'in_progress', 'failed', 'canceled', or 'cancelled' (canceled/cancelled are first-class terminal statuses)\\\",\\n114 |   \\\"model\\\": \\\"string — model used for this turn\\\",\\n115 |   \\\"tokenCount\\\": \\\"integer|null — approximate token count\\\",\\n116 |   \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"string array — e.g. 'refactor', 'bugfix', 'feature'\\\"],\\n117 |   \\\"contextList\\\": [\\\"string array — files or resources referenced\\\"],\\n118 |   \\\"designDecisions\\\": [\\\"string array — decisions made during this turn\\\"],\\n119 |   \\\"requirementsDiscovered\\\": [\\\"string array — requirement IDs e.g. 'TR-MCP-001'\\\"],\\n120 |   \\\"filesModified\\\": [\\\"string array - file paths changed. Paths that resolve outside the workspace root require a foreign: / foreign-repo: / cross-workspace: prefix or a turn tag foreign-repo / cross-workspace / foreign-workspace (FR-MCP-SESSIONATTR-001). Completeness audits can filter those prefixes and tags. Forward-only: historical turns are not rewritten.\\\"],\\n121 |   \\\"blockers\\\": [\\\"string array — issues preventing progress\\\"],\\n122 |   \\\"actions\\\": [ \\\"array of Action objects (see below)\\\" ],\\n123 |   \\\"processingDialog\\\": [ \\\"array of DialogItem objects (see below)\\\" ],\\n124 |   \\\"planFile\\\": \\\"string — required on new persist and replace: current plan file path (workspace-relative, exact, or ~/...) or the exact sentinel None\\\",\\n125 |   \\\"todoId\\\": \\\"string - required on new persist and replace: canonical MCP TODO id (PHASE-AREA-### or ISSUE-N) or the exact sentinel None\\\"\\n126 | }\\n127 | ```\\n128 | \\n129 | ## Foreign filesModified and commits (FR-MCP-SESSIONATTR-001)\\n130 | \\n131 | A session-log turn for workspace W must not list `filesModified` or commit `filesChanged` paths that resolve outside W unless the turn explicitly marks them:\\n132 | \\n133 | - Item prefix on the path: `foreign:`, `foreign-repo:`, or `cross-workspace:`\\n134 | - Turn tags: `foreign-repo`, `cross-workspace`, or `foreign-workspace`\\n135 | \\n136 | Unmarked foreign paths are rejected with `validation_error`. Relative paths are resolved against the workspace root (`..` that escapes is foreign). Empty workspace context (import/ingest) skips this check. Forward-only: existing rows are not rewritten. Completeness audits filter the prefixes and tags above.\\n137 | \\n138 | Commit SHA/message without `filesChanged` cannot be proven foreign; mark the turn with `foreign-repo` when attributing another repository's commit.\\n139 | \\n140 | ## Action (each element in `actions`)\\n141 | \\n142 | ```json\\n143 | {\\n144 |   \\\"order\\\": \\\"integer — sequence number\\\",\\n145 |   \\\"description\\\": \\\"string — what was done\\\",\\n146 |   \\\"type\\\": \\\"string — action type (see action-types.md)\\\",\\n147 |   \\\"status\\\": \\\"string — 'completed', 'in_progress', or 'failed'\\\",\\n148 |   \\\"filePath\\\": \\\"string — affected file path, or empty string\\\"\\n149 | }\\n150 | ```\\n151 | \\n152 | ## DialogItem (each element in `processingDialog`, or POST body to dialog endpoint)\\n153 | \\n154 | ```json\\n155 | {\\n156 |   \\\"timestamp\\\": \\\"string — ISO 8601\\\",\\n157 |   \\\"role\\\": \\\"string — 'model', 'tool', 'system', or 'user'\\\",\\n158 |   \\\"content\\\": \\\"string — reasoning text, tool output, or observation\\\",\\n159 |   \\\"category\\\": \\\"string — 'reasoning', 'tool_call', 'tool_result', 'observation', or 'decision'\\\"\\n160 | }\\n161 | ```\\n162 | \\n163 | ## McpSession Module — PowerShell Lifecycle\\n164 | \\n165 | ```powershell\\n166 | # Query recent logs at session start\\n167 | Get-McpSessionLog -Limit 5\\n168 | \\n169 | # Create session\\n170 | $s = New-McpSessionLog -SourceType \\\"Copilot\\\" -Title \\\"Implementing feature X\\\" -Model \\\"claude-sonnet-4\\\"\\n171 | \\n172 | # Add turn for each user request\\n173 | $t = Add-McpSessionTurn -Session $s -QueryTitle \\\"Add auth\\\" -QueryText \\\"Add JWT authentication\\\"\\n174 | \\n175 | # Record actions during work\\n176 | Add-McpAction -Turn $t -Description \\\"Created TokenService\\\" -Type create -FilePath \\\"src/TokenService.cs\\\"\\n177 | \\n178 | # Stream reasoning dialog as you work\\n179 | Send-McpDialog -Session $s -RequestId $t.requestId -Content \\\"Analyzing the issue...\\\" -Category reasoning\\n180 | \\n181 | # Complete the turn\\n182 | Set-McpSessionTurn -Turn $t -Session $s -Response \\\"Done\\\" -Status completed\\n183 | \\n184 | # Before compaction, persist the current session state.\\n185 | Update-McpSessionLog -Session $s\\n186 | \\n187 | # After compaction, record the compaction outcome and push again.\\n188 | Send-McpDialog -Session $s -RequestId $t.requestId -Content \\\"Compaction completed; recovered context has been restored.\\\" -Category observation\\n189 | Update-McpSessionLog -Session $s\\n190 | \\n191 | # Final push at session end\\n192 | Update-McpSessionLog -Session $s -Status completed\\n193 | ```\\n194 | \\n195 | ## McpSession Module — Bash Lifecycle\\n196 | \\n197 | ```bash\\n198 | # Query recent logs at session start\\n199 | mcp_session_query 5\\n200 | \\n201 | # Create session\\n202 | mcp_session_create \\\"Copilot\\\" \\\"Implementing feature X\\\" \\\"claude-sonnet-4\\\"\\n203 | \\n204 | # Add turn, record actions, stream dialog, complete\\n205 | mcp_session_add_turn \\\"req-001\\\" \\\"Add auth\\\" \\\"Add JWT authentication\\\" \\\"in_progress\\\"\\n206 | mcp_session_add_action \\\"req-001\\\" \\\"Created TokenService\\\" \\\"create\\\" \\\"src/TokenService.cs\\\"\\n207 | mcp_session_send_dialog \\\"req-001\\\" \\\"Analyzing the issue...\\\" \\\"reasoning\\\"\\n208 | mcp_session_update_turn \\\"req-001\\\" \\\"status\\\" \\\"completed\\\"\\n209 | mcp_session_complete\\n210 | ```\",\n              \"success\": true\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      ],\n      \"ts\": 1792174535660\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"Index\": 486,\n    \"Message\": {\n      \"id\": \"msg_YDgSWoeS\",\n      \"role\": \"user\",\n      \"content\": [\n        {\n          \"type\": \"tool_result\",\n          \"tool_use_id\": \"call_4yZCmeOUCCk0vayhfN6auDac\",\n          \"name\": \"composio_search_tools\",\n          \"content\": \"{\\\"result_type\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"data\\\":{\\\"results\\\":[{\\\"index\\\":1,\\\"use_case\\\":\\\"Create and retrieve a high-priority TODO containing an implementation plan in the local McpServer workspace\\\",\\\"execution_guidance\\\":\\\"IMPORTANT: Follow the recommended plan below. Extract steps before execution, adapt to your current context, execute sequentially with current_step parameter, and check known pitfalls to avoid errors.\\\",\\\"recommended_plan_steps\\\":[\\\"Required Prerequisite (if tasklist_id unknown): Identify the destination list using GOOGLETASKS_LIST_TASK_LISTS (capture tasklist_id).\\\",\\\"Optional (if the desired list doesn’t exist): Create it using GOOGLETASKS_CREATE_TASK_LIST (use returned tasklist_id).\\\",\\\"Optional (if avoiding duplicates by updating instead of creating): Search existing tasks using GOOGLETASKS_LIST_TASKS (capture task_id for matches).\\\",\\\"Required Step: Create a high-priority task due “today” using GOOGLETASKS_INSERT_TASK (set title/notes, priority cues, and due; store task_id).\\\",\\\"Optional Next Step (if you intended to update an existing item or refine after creation): Update fields using GOOGLETASKS_PATCH_TASK (requires tasklist_id + task_id).\\\",\\\"Optional Fallback (if the correct list/task is unclear from per-list results): Discover tasks across lists using GOOGLETASKS_LIST_ALL_TASKS, then re-apply GOOGLETASKS_GET_TASK before the final GOOGLETASKS_PATCH_TASK edit.\\\"],\\\"known_pitfalls\\\":[\\\"[GOOGLETASKS_INSERT_TASK] Due values may be treated as date-only (time-of-day can be discarded in UTC), so don’t rely on precise timing.\\\",\\\"[GOOGLETASKS_LIST_TASKS] Response shape can be non-intuitive (tasks are nested under a tasks array), so parse the correct path before extracting task_id.\\\",\\\"[GOOGLETASKS_INSERT_TASK] Creating without checking can introduce duplicates; list and match existing tasks when you intend to update.\\\",\\\"[GOOGLETASKS_PATCH_TASK] Requires both tasklist_id and task_id; persist IDs from GOOGLETASKS_INSERT_TASK/GOOGLETASKS_LIST_TASKS to avoid editing the wrong item.\\\"],\\\"difficulty\\\":\\\"easy\\\",\\\"primary_tool_slugs\\\":[\\\"GOOGLETASKS_LIST_TASK_LISTS\\\",\\\"GOOGLETASKS_INSERT_TASK\\\"],\\\"related_tool_slugs\\\":[\\\"GOOGLETASKS_LIST_TASKS\\\",\\\"GOOGLETASKS_CREATE_TASK_LIST\\\",\\\"GOOGLETASKS_GET_TASK\\\",\\\"GOOGLETASKS_LIST_ALL_TASKS\\\",\\\"GOOGLETASKS_PATCH_TASK\\\"],\\\"toolkits\\\":[\\\"googletasks\\\"],\\\"plan_id\\\":\\\"c938ee0ea9ef4cf3bf9a32a12c03d716\\\",\\\"cached_plan_usecase\\\":\\\"usecase: List Google Tasks task lists then add a high priority task for today.. known: task, priority, due. apps: googletasks\\\"},{\\\"index\\\":2,\\\"use_case\\\":\\\"Update the local McpServer agent session log and verify server health without invoking a disabled Cline plugin\\\",\\\"execution_guidance\\\":\\\"IMPORTANT: Follow the recommended plan below. Extract steps before execution, adapt to your current context, execute sequentially with current_step parameter, and check known pitfalls to avoid errors.\\\",\\\"recommended_plan_steps\\\":[\\\"[Optional (if target project unknown)] [Prerequisite]: Identify the correct project using SUPABASE_LIST_ALL_PROJECTS (capture the project ref for later calls).\\\",\\\"[Optional (if destination relation/schema uncertain)] [Prerequisite]: Confirm the destination table exists using SUPABASE_LIST_TABLES (avoid writing to the wrong relation).\\\",\\\"[Required] [Prerequisite]: Inspect columns/defaults using SUPABASE_GET_TABLE_SCHEMAS (provide only required fields; omit server-defaulted fields unless overriding).\\\",\\\"[Optional (if a schema change might already be tracked)] [Prerequisite]: Review prior schema changes using SUPABASE_LIST_MIGRATION_HISTORY (avoid duplicating/conflicting DDL).\\\",\\\"[Optional (if table/columns/constraints are missing)] [Prerequisite]: Create/alter the log table using SUPABASE_APPLY_A_MIGRATION (keep DDL separate from routine logging).\\\",\\\"[Required] [Step]: After user confirmation, insert one status row using SUPABASE_BETA_RUN_SQL_QUERY (read_only=false; use INSERT…RETURNING; confirm data.command='INSERT' and data.rows_affected=1; capture returned key/row).\\\",\\\"[Optional (for independent verification or if RETURNING is unavailable)] [Next Step]: Validate persistence using SUPABASE_RUN_READ_ONLY_QUERY (SELECT by returned key; otherwise deterministic ORDER BY with LIMIT 1).\\\",\\\"[Optional (if you must correct values post-write)] [Next Step]: Update the inserted row using SUPABASE_BETA_RUN_SQL_QUERY (target by returned key; confirm data.rows_affected>0).\\\",\\\"[Optional (if write fails with common SQLSTATEs, incl. CHECK violations)] [Fallback]: Diagnose via catalog/constraint SELECTs and retry using SUPABASE_BETA_RUN_SQL_QUERY (handle 42P01, 42703, 23514; fix quoting, casts, and value alignment).\\\"],\\\"known_pitfalls\\\":[\\\"[SUPABASE_BETA_RUN_SQL_QUERY] Writes require read_only=false; read-only mode can block DML or fail depending on context.\\\",\\\"[SUPABASE_RUN_READ_ONLY_QUERY] Using it for INSERT can fail with SQLSTATE 25006 (read-only transaction); reserve it for SELECT-only validation.\\\",\\\"[SUPABASE_BETA_RUN_SQL_QUERY] String literals with apostrophes must be escaped using doubled single-quotes (''); array-typed columns require ARRAY[...] to avoid 42601/22P02.\\\",\\\"[SUPABASE_BETA_RUN_SQL_QUERY] INSERT can fail with SQLSTATE 23514 when a CHECK constraint restricts allowed values; inspect constraint definitions via catalog queries instead of guessing.\\\",\\\"[SUPABASE_GET_TABLE_SCHEMAS] Schema inspection may not expose CHECK constraint allowed-values; rely on catalog/constraint inspection when 23514 occurs.\\\"],\\\"difficulty\\\":\\\"easy\\\",\\\"primary_tool_slugs\\\":[\\\"SUPABASE_BETA_RUN_SQL_QUERY\\\"],\\\"related_tool_slugs\\\":[\\\"SUPABASE_GET_TABLE_SCHEMAS\\\",\\\"SUPABASE_LIST_TABLES\\\",\\\"SUPABASE_LIST_ALL_PROJECTS\\\",\\\"SUPABASE_RUN_READ_ONLY_QUERY\\\",\\\"SUPABASE_LIST_MIGRATION_HISTORY\\\",\\\"SUPABASE_APPLY_A_MIGRATION\\\"],\\\"toolkits\\\":[\\\"supabase\\\"],\\\"plan_id\\\":\\\"b2f0d1bb189949a2a7255ce1e3de907b\\\",\\\"cached_plan_usecase\\\":\\\"usecase: Insert a heartbeat/health status log record into a Supabase heartbeat_logs table.. known: None. apps: supabase\\\"}],\\\"toolkit_connection_statuses\\\":[{\\\"toolkit\\\":\\\"googletasks\\\",\\\"has_active_connection\\\":false,\\\"description\\\":\\\"Google Tasks provides a simple to-do list and task management system integrated into Gmail and Google Calendar for quick and easy tracking\\\",\\\"status_message\\\":\\\"No Active connection for toolkit=googletasks. You MUST call COMPOSIO_MANAGE_CONNECTIONS (toolkit=\\\\\\\"googletasks\\\\\\\") to create a connection BEFORE executing any googletasks tools.\\\"},{\\\"toolkit\\\":\\\"supabase\\\",\\\"has_active_connection\\\":false,\\\"description\\\":\\\"Supabase is an open-source backend-as-a-service providing a Postgres database, authentication, storage, and real-time subscription APIs for building modern applications\\\",\\\"status_message\\\":\\\"No Active connection for toolkit=supabase. You MUST call COMPOSIO_MANAGE_CONNECTIONS (toolkit=\\\\\\\"supabase\\\\\\\") to create a connection BEFORE executing any supabase tools.\\\"}],\\\"tool_schemas\\\":{\\\"GOOGLETASKS_LIST_TASK_LISTS\\\":{\\\"toolkit\\\":\\\"GOOGLETASKS\\\",\\\"tool_slug\\\":\\\"GOOGLETASKS_LIST_TASK_LISTS\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"Fetches the authenticated user's task lists from Google Tasks; results may be paginated. Response contains task lists under the `items` key. Multiple lists may share similar names — confirm the correct list by ID before passing to other tools.\\\",\\\"input_schema\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"object\\\",\\\"properties\\\":{\\\"pageToken\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"string\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"Token for the page of results to return; omit for the first page, use `nextPageToken` from a previous response for subsequent pages.\\\"},\\\"maxResults\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"integer\\\",\\\"default\\\":20,\\\"examples\\\":[\\\"10\\\",\\\"20\\\",\\\"50\\\"],\\\"description\\\":\\\"Maximum number of task lists to return per page. Capped at 100; values above 100 are silently truncated to 100.\\\"}}},\\\"hasFullSchema\\\":true},\\\"GOOGLETASKS_INSERT_TASK\\\":{\\\"toolkit\\\":\\\"GOOGLETASKS\\\",\\\"tool_slug\\\":\\\"GOOGLETASKS_INSERT_TASK\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"Creates a new task in a given `tasklist_id`, optionally as a subtask of an existing `task_parent` or positioned after an existing `task_previous` sibling, where both `task_parent` and `task_previous` must belong to the same `tasklist_id` if specified. IMPORTANT: Date fields (due, completed) accept various formats like '28 Sep 2025', '11:59 PM, 22 Sep 2025', or ISO format '2025-09-21T15:30:00Z' and will automatically convert them to RFC3339 format required by the API. Not idempotent — repeated calls with identical parameters create duplicate tasks; track returned task IDs to avoid duplication. High-volume inserts may trigger 403 rateLimitExceeded or 429; apply exponential backoff.\\\",\\\"input_schema\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"object\\\",\\\"required\\\":[\\\"title\\\",\\\"tasklist_id\\\"],\\\"properties\\\":{\\\"id\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"string\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"Unique identifier for the task. Generated by Google Tasks API when creating a task. Used for updating or deleting existing tasks.\\\"},\\\"due\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"string\\\",\\\"examples\\\":[\\\"2025-09-28T23:59:00Z\\\",\\\"28 Sep 2025\\\",\\\"11:59 PM, 22 Sep 2025\\\",\\\"1:00 PM, 21 Sep 2025\\\",\\\"UTC-5:30, 6:50 PM\\\",\\\"UTC+1, 11:59 PM, 31 Dec\\\",\\\"2025-09-21T15:30:00+02:00\\\"],\\\"description\\\":\\\"Due date for the task (optional). NOTE: Google Tasks only stores the date portion (YYYY-MM-DD) - any time component provided will be automatically stripped before sending to the API. \\\\nGoogle Tasks API requires dates in RFC3339 format (e.g., 2025-09-22T13:48:27Z).\\\\nWe automatically convert various human-readable formats to RFC3339:\\\\n\\\\nAccepted formats:\\\\n• 2025-09-28T23:59:00Z → RFC3339/ISO-8601 format (preferred) - September 28, 2025 at 11:59 PM UTC\\\\n• 28 Sep 2025 → Simple date format - Will be converted to midnight UTC on that date\\\\n• 11:59 PM, 22 Sep 2025 → Time with date - Assumes UTC timezone if not specified\\\\n• 1:00 PM, 21 Sep 2025 → 12-hour format with date - Converted to 13:00 UTC\\\\n• UTC-5:30, 6:50 PM → With timezone offset - Converted to UTC automatically\\\\n• UTC+1, 11:59 PM, 31 Dec → Timezone with date - Next occurrence of Dec 31\\\\n• 2025-09-21T15:30:00+02:00 → RFC3339 with timezone offset\\\\n\\\\nNote: If no timezone is specified, UTC is assumed. Dates without times default to midnight UTC.\\\"},\\\"etag\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"string\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"ETag of the resource for concurrency control. Prevents accidental overwrites when multiple clients modify the same task. Auto-generated by the API.\\\"},\\\"notes\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"string\\\",\\\"maxLength\\\":8192,\\\"description\\\":\\\"Additional details or description for the task. Supports plain text only (no HTML/markdown). Maximum 8192 characters. Example: 'Include Q3 sales data and projections'\\\"},\\\"title\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"string\\\",\\\"maxLength\\\":1024,\\\"description\\\":\\\"Title/name of the task (REQUIRED). This is the main text displayed for the task. Maximum 1024 characters. Example: 'Complete quarterly report'\\\"},\\\"hidden\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"boolean\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"Whether the task is hidden from the default view. Hidden tasks can still be accessed via API but won't show in UI. Default: false\\\"},\\\"status\\\":{\\\"enum\\\":[\\\"needsAction\\\",\\\"completed\\\"],\\\"type\\\":\\\"string\\\",\\\"default\\\":\\\"needsAction\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"Status of the task. Must be either 'needsAction' (task is pending) or 'completed' (task is done). Defaults to 'needsAction' if not provided.\\\"},\\\"deleted\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"boolean\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"Whether the task has been deleted. Deleted tasks are marked but not immediately removed, allowing for recovery. Default: false\\\"},\\\"completed\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"string\\\",\\\"examples\\\":[\\\"2025-09-28T23:59:00Z\\\",\\\"28 Sep 2025\\\",\\\"11:59 PM, 22 Sep 2025\\\",\\\"1:00 PM, 21 Sep 2025\\\",\\\"UTC-5:30, 6:50 PM\\\",\\\"UTC+1, 11:59 PM, 31 Dec\\\",\\\"2025-09-21T15:30:00+02:00\\\"],\\\"description\\\":\\\"Date/time when the task was marked as completed. Only applicable when status='completed'. Unlike 'due', this field preserves the time component. \\\\nGoogle Tasks API requires dates in RFC3339 format (e.g., 2025-09-22T13:48:27Z).\\\\nWe automatically convert various human-readable formats to RFC3339:\\\\n\\\\nAccepted formats:\\\\n• 2025-09-28T23:59:00Z → RFC3339/ISO-8601 format (preferred) - September 28, 2025 at 11:59 PM UTC\\\\n• 28 Sep 2025 → Simple date format - Will be converted to midnight UTC on that date\\\\n• 11:59 PM, 22 Sep 2025 → Time with date - Assumes UTC timezone if not specified\\\\n• 1:00 PM, 21 Sep 2025 → 12-hour format with date - Converted to 13:00 UTC\\\\n• UTC-5:30, 6:50 PM → With timezone offset - Converted to UTC automatically\\\\n• UTC+1, 11:59 PM, 31 Dec → Timezone with date - Next occurrence of Dec 31\\\\n• 2025-09-21T15:30:00+02:00 → RFC3339 with timezone offset\\\\n\\\\nNote: If no timezone is specified, UTC is assumed. Dates without times default to midnight UTC.\\\"},\\\"task_parent\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"string\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"Identifier of an existing task to serve as parent; if provided, creates a subtask, otherwise a top-level task in the specified list.\\\"},\\\"tasklist_id\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"string\\\",\\\"examples\\\":[\\\"@default\\\",\\\"MDQ1NTEzMjc4OTM5MzM0NTY4NzE6MDow\\\",\\\"N1FNZHM5Y0xYN3g5TzJBVQ\\\"],\\\"description\\\":\\\"Task list identifier where the new task will be created. Valid values: (1) '@default' - special identifier that references the user's default/primary task list, or (2) an actual task list ID obtained from the 'List Task Lists' action (GOOGLETASKS_LIST_TASK_LISTS). Task list IDs are opaque strings generated by Google (typically base64-encoded, e.g., 'MDQ1NTEzMjc4OTM5MzM0NTY4NzE6MDow').\\\"},\\\"task_previous\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"string\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"Identifier of an existing task after which the new task will be placed, at the same hierarchical level (as a sibling).\\\"}}},\\\"hasFullSchema\\\":true},\\\"SUPABASE_BETA_RUN_SQL_QUERY\\\":{\\\"toolkit\\\":\\\"SUPABASE\\\",\\\"tool_slug\\\":\\\"SUPABASE_BETA_RUN_SQL_QUERY\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"Executes a given SQL query against the project's database; use for advanced data operations or when standard API endpoints are insufficient, ensuring queries are valid PostgreSQL and sanitized. Use the get_table_schemas or generate_type_script_types tool to retrieve the table schema, then base your query on it.\\\",\\\"input_schema\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"object\\\",\\\"required\\\":[\\\"ref\\\",\\\"query\\\"],\\\"properties\\\":{\\\"ref\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"string\\\",\\\"pattern\\\":\\\"^[a-z]{20}$\\\",\\\"examples\\\":[\\\"abcdefghijklmnopqrst\\\",\\\"xvljpkujuwroxcuvossw\\\"],\\\"maxLength\\\":20,\\\"minLength\\\":20,\\\"description\\\":\\\"The unique reference ID of the Supabase project (used in the URL path). Must be exactly 20 lowercase letters (a-z). Can be found in Project Settings > General > Reference ID in the Supabase Dashboard, or from the dashboard URL: https://supabase.com/dashboard/project/<project-ref>. Can also be provided as 'project_ref'.\\\"},\\\"query\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"string\\\",\\\"examples\\\":[\\\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = 1;\\\",\\\"INSERT INTO products (name, price) VALUES ('New Product', 99.99);\\\",\\\"INSERT INTO users (bio) VALUES ('I''m a developer');\\\",\\\"INSERT INTO tasks (tags) VALUES (ARRAY['urgent', 'backend']);\\\",\\\"INSERT INTO items (categories) VALUES ('{\\\\\\\"electronics\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"sale\\\\\\\"}');\\\",\\\"SELECT NOW() + INTERVAL '3 days' AS future_date;\\\"],\\\"description\\\":\\\"The SQL query to be executed against the project's database. Can also be provided as 'sql'. CRITICAL - Single Quote Handling: Single quotes that delimit SQL string literals (e.g., 'John', '3 days') work normally when transmitted via JSON. However, apostrophes and single quotes WITHIN string content MUST be escaped using PostgreSQL's standard escaping (doubling the quote: ''). Examples: ✓ CORRECT: SELECT 'I''m here' (apostrophe in content is escaped as '') ✓ CORRECT: INSERT INTO users (bio) VALUES ('She said ''hello''') ✓ CORRECT: SELECT '{ \\\\\\\"msg\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"I''m ready\\\\\\\" }'::jsonb (apostrophe inside JSON string is escaped) ✗ WRONG: SELECT 'I'm here' (syntax error - apostrophe breaks the string) ✗ WRONG: SELECT '{ \\\\\\\"msg\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"I'm ready\\\\\\\" }'::jsonb (syntax error in JSON content) IMPORTANT - PostgreSQL Array Syntax: For columns of type text[], integer[], etc., do NOT use JSON array syntax like '[\\\\\\\"item1\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"item2\\\\\\\"]' as PostgreSQL will reject it. Use PostgreSQL array syntax instead: (1) ARRAY constructor: ARRAY['item1', 'item2'], or (2) Curly brace literal: '{\\\\\\\"item1\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"item2\\\\\\\"}'. IMPORTANT - PostgreSQL Configuration Parameters: To reference Supabase secrets or config parameters, use current_setting() with the correct parameter name (e.g., current_setting('app.settings.my_secret')). Parameter names must not contain sanitization placeholders like '<URL>', '<IP_ADDRESS>', etc. Note: Complex DDL operations (CREATE TABLE with multiple indexes, CREATE VIEW with joins, large data migrations) may timeout if they exceed the Supabase API's internal timeout limit (~60 seconds). For long-running operations, consider breaking them into smaller queries or using a direct database connection.\\\"},\\\"read_only\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"boolean\\\",\\\"examples\\\":[true,false],\\\"description\\\":\\\"If true, executes the query in a read-only transaction. Useful for safety when only fetching data without modifying the database. IMPORTANT: This parameter is incompatible with data modification statements. For INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE statements, if set to true, it will be automatically overridden to false to allow the operation to proceed. For DDL statements (CREATE, ALTER, DROP, etc.), read_only=true will cause an error - you must set it to false or omit it.\\\"}}},\\\"hasFullSchema\\\":true},\\\"GOOGLETASKS_LIST_TASKS\\\":{\\\"toolkit\\\":\\\"GOOGLETASKS\\\",\\\"tool_slug\\\":\\\"GOOGLETASKS_LIST_TASKS\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"Retrieves tasks from a Google Tasks list; all date/time strings must be RFC3339 UTC, and `showCompleted` must be true if `completedMin` or `completedMax` are specified. Response key for tasks is `tasks` (not `items`). No full-text search; filter client-side by title/notes. Results ordered by position, not by date.\\\",\\\"input_schema\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"object\\\",\\\"required\\\":[\\\"tasklist_id\\\"],\\\"properties\\\":{\\\"dueMax\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"string\\\",\\\"examples\\\":[\\\"2025-09-28T23:59:00Z\\\",\\\"28 Sep 2025\\\",\\\"11:59 PM, 22 Sep 2025\\\",\\\"1:00 PM, 21 Sep 2025\\\",\\\"UTC-5:30, 6:50 PM\\\",\\\"UTC+1, 11:59 PM, 31 Dec\\\",\\\"2025-09-21T15:30:00+02:00\\\"],\\\"description\\\":\\\"Exclude tasks due after this date. \\\\nGoogle Tasks API requires dates in RFC3339 format (e.g., 2025-09-22T13:48:27Z).\\\\nWe automatically convert various human-readable formats to RFC3339:\\\\n\\\\nAccepted formats:\\\\n• 2025-09-28T23:59:00Z → RFC3339/ISO-8601 format (preferred) - September 28, 2025 at 11:59 PM UTC\\\\n• 28 Sep 2025 → Simple date format - Will be converted to midnight UTC on that date\\\\n• 11:59 PM, 22 Sep 2025 → Time with date - Assumes UTC timezone if not specified\\\\n• 1:00 PM, 21 Sep 2025 → 12-hour format with date - Converted to 13:00 UTC\\\\n• UTC-5:30, 6:50 PM → With timezone offset - Converted to UTC automatically\\\\n• UTC+1, 11:59 PM, 31 Dec → Timezone with date - Next occurrence of Dec 31\\\\n• 2025-09-21T15:30:00+02:00 → RFC3339 with timezone offset\\\\n\\\\nNote: If no timezone is specified, UTC is assumed. Dates without times default to midnight UTC.\\\"},\\\"dueMin\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"string\\\",\\\"examples\\\":[\\\"2025-09-28T23:59:00Z\\\",\\\"28 Sep 2025\\\",\\\"11:59 PM, 22 Sep 2025\\\",\\\"1:00 PM, 21 Sep 2025\\\",\\\"UTC-5:30, 6:50 PM\\\",\\\"UTC+1, 11:59 PM, 31 Dec\\\",\\\"2025-09-21T15:30:00+02:00\\\"],\\\"description\\\":\\\"Exclude tasks due before this date. \\\\nGoogle Tasks API requires dates in RFC3339 format (e.g., 2025-09-22T13:48:27Z).\\\\nWe automatically convert various human-readable formats to RFC3339:\\\\n\\\\nAccepted formats:\\\\n• 2025-09-28T23:59:00Z → RFC3339/ISO-8601 format (preferred) - September 28, 2025 at 11:59 PM UTC\\\\n• 28 Sep 2025 → Simple date format - Will be converted to midnight UTC on that date\\\\n• 11:59 PM, 22 Sep 2025 → Time with date - Assumes UTC timezone if not specified\\\\n• 1:00 PM, 21 Sep 2025 → 12-hour format with date - Converted to 13:00 UTC\\\\n• UTC-5:30, 6:50 PM → With timezone offset - Converted to UTC automatically\\\\n• UTC+1, 11:59 PM, 31 Dec → Timezone with date - Next occurrence of Dec 31\\\\n• 2025-09-21T15:30:00+02:00 → RFC3339 with timezone offset\\\\n\\\\nNote: If no timezone is specified, UTC is assumed. Dates without times default to midnight UTC.\\\"},\\\"pageToken\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"string\\\",\\\"examples\\\":[\\\"CiAKGjBpbmRXUTA1Y1cxVWF6b2hVbkprZWxTelNVUWdRMBoOCL2q0qcCELqGmgsQ0KG3jAE=\\\"],\\\"description\\\":\\\"Token from a previous list operation for fetching a specific page; if omitted, retrieves the first page.\\\"},\\\"maxResults\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"integer\\\",\\\"examples\\\":[\\\"20\\\",\\\"50\\\",\\\"100\\\"],\\\"description\\\":\\\"Maximum number of tasks to return. API default: 20, maximum: 100. Use `nextPageToken` from the response in successive calls until absent to avoid missing tasks beyond the first page.\\\"},\\\"showHidden\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"boolean\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"Include hidden tasks. Defaults to false.\\\"},\\\"updatedMin\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"string\\\",\\\"examples\\\":[\\\"2025-09-28T23:59:00Z\\\",\\\"28 Sep 2025\\\",\\\"11:59 PM, 22 Sep 2025\\\",\\\"1:00 PM, 21 Sep 2025\\\",\\\"UTC-5:30, 6:50 PM\\\",\\\"UTC+1, 11:59 PM, 31 Dec\\\",\\\"2025-09-21T15:30:00+02:00\\\"],\\\"description\\\":\\\"Lower bound for task's last modification time (for syncing). \\\\nGoogle Tasks API requires dates in RFC3339 format (e.g., 2025-09-22T13:48:27Z).\\\\nWe automatically convert various human-readable formats to RFC3339:\\\\n\\\\nAccepted formats:\\\\n• 2025-09-28T23:59:00Z → RFC3339/ISO-8601 format (preferred) - September 28, 2025 at 11:59 PM UTC\\\\n• 28 Sep 2025 → Simple date format - Will be converted to midnight UTC on that date\\\\n• 11:59 PM, 22 Sep 2025 → Time with date - Assumes UTC timezone if not specified\\\\n• 1:00 PM, 21 Sep 2025 → 12-hour format with date - Converted to 13:00 UTC\\\\n• UTC-5:30, 6:50 PM → With timezone offset - Converted to UTC automatically\\\\n• UTC+1, 11:59 PM, 31 Dec → Timezone with date - Next occurrence of Dec 31\\\\n• 2025-09-21T15:30:00+02:00 → RFC3339 with timezone offset\\\\n\\\\nNote: If no timezone is specified, UTC is assumed. Dates without times default to midnight UTC.\\\"},\\\"showDeleted\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"boolean\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"Include deleted tasks. Defaults to false.\\\"},\\\"tasklist_id\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"string\\\",\\\"examples\\\":[\\\"@default\\\",\\\"MdXxc1BhcEt4NVJhcEVXMQ\\\"],\\\"description\\\":\\\"Identifier of the task list. Use '@default' for the user's primary list, or provide a task list ID from GOOGLETASKS_LIST_TASK_LISTS. The common aliases 'primary', 'default', and '@primary' are normalized to '@default'.\\\"},\\\"completedMax\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"string\\\",\\\"examples\\\":[\\\"2025-09-28T23:59:00Z\\\",\\\"28 Sep 2025\\\",\\\"11:59 PM, 22 Sep 2025\\\",\\\"1:00 PM, 21 Sep 2025\\\",\\\"UTC-5:30, 6:50 PM\\\",\\\"UTC+1, 11:59 PM, 31 Dec\\\",\\\"2025-09-21T15:30:00+02:00\\\"],\\\"description\\\":\\\"Exclude tasks completed after this date. \\\\nGoogle Tasks API requires dates in RFC3339 format (e.g., 2025-09-22T13:48:27Z).\\\\nWe automatically convert various human-readable formats to RFC3339:\\\\n\\\\nAccepted formats:\\\\n• 2025-09-28T23:59:00Z → RFC3339/ISO-8601 format (preferred) - September 28, 2025 at 11:59 PM UTC\\\\n• 28 Sep 2025 → Simple date format - Will be converted to midnight UTC on that date\\\\n• 11:59 PM, 22 Sep 2025 → Time with date - Assumes UTC timezone if not specified\\\\n• 1:00 PM, 21 Sep 2025 → 12-hour format with date - Converted to 13:00 UTC\\\\n• UTC-5:30, 6:50 PM → With timezone offset - Converted to UTC automatically\\\\n• UTC+1, 11:59 PM, 31 Dec → Timezone with date - Next occurrence of Dec 31\\\\n• 2025-09-21T15:30:00+02:00 → RFC3339 with timezone offset\\\\n\\\\nNote: If no timezone is specified, UTC is assumed. Dates without times default to midnight UTC.\\\"},\\\"completedMin\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"string\\\",\\\"examples\\\":[\\\"2025-09-28T23:59:00Z\\\",\\\"28 Sep 2025\\\",\\\"11:59 PM, 22 Sep 2025\\\",\\\"1:00 PM, 21 Sep 2025\\\",\\\"UTC-5:30, 6:50 PM\\\",\\\"UTC+1, 11:59 PM, 31 Dec\\\",\\\"2025-09-21T15:30:00+02:00\\\"],\\\"description\\\":\\\"Exclude tasks completed before this date. \\\\nGoogle Tasks API requires dates in RFC3339 format (e.g., 2025-09-22T13:48:27Z).\\\\nWe automatically convert various human-readable formats to RFC3339:\\\\n\\\\nAccepted formats:\\\\n• 2025-09-28T23:59:00Z → RFC3339/ISO-8601 format (preferred) - September 28, 2025 at 11:59 PM UTC\\\\n• 28 Sep 2025 → Simple date format - Will be converted to midnight UTC on that date\\\\n• 11:59 PM, 22 Sep 2025 → Time with date - Assumes UTC timezone if not specified\\\\n• 1:00 PM, 21 Sep 2025 → 12-hour format with date - Converted to 13:00 UTC\\\\n• UTC-5:30, 6:50 PM → With timezone offset - Converted to UTC automatically\\\\n• UTC+1, 11:59 PM, 31 Dec → Timezone with date - Next occurrence of Dec 31\\\\n• 2025-09-21T15:30:00+02:00 → RFC3339 with timezone offset\\\\n\\\\nNote: If no timezone is specified, UTC is assumed. Dates without times default to midnight UTC.\\\"},\\\"showCompleted\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"boolean\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"Include completed tasks. Defaults to true. (See action docstring for interaction with `completedMin`/`Max`).\\\"}}},\\\"hasFullSchema\\\":true},\\\"SUPABASE_GET_TABLE_SCHEMAS\\\":{\\\"toolkit\\\":\\\"SUPABASE\\\",\\\"tool_slug\\\":\\\"SUPABASE_GET_TABLE_SCHEMAS\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"Retrieves column details, types, and constraints for multiple database tables to help debug schema issues and write accurate SQL queries. Use the SUPABASE_LIST_TABLES action first to discover available tables, the fetch their detailed schemas.\\\",\\\"input_schema\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"object\\\",\\\"required\\\":[\\\"project_ref\\\",\\\"table_names\\\"],\\\"properties\\\":{\\\"project_ref\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"string\\\",\\\"examples\\\":[\\\"huqmqtilymjtstzxbuww\\\",\\\"abcdefghijklmnopqrst\\\"],\\\"description\\\":\\\"The unique reference ID of the Supabase project. This is visible in your Supabase project URL (e.g., https://supabase.com/dashboard/project/{project_ref}).\\\"},\\\"table_names\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"array\\\",\\\"items\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"string\\\"},\\\"examples\\\":[[\\\"users\\\",\\\"posts\\\",\\\"comments\\\"],[\\\"operations.call_queue_items\\\",\\\"public.profiles\\\"],[\\\"auth.users\\\",\\\"storage.objects\\\"]],\\\"maxItems\\\":20,\\\"minItems\\\":1,\\\"description\\\":\\\"List of table names to retrieve schemas for. At least one table name is required (empty lists are not allowed). Tables can be from different schemas (e.g., 'public.users', 'operations.call_queue_items'). Without schema prefix, 'public' is assumed. Maximum 20 tables per request.\\\"},\\\"include_indexes\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"boolean\\\",\\\"default\\\":true,\\\"description\\\":\\\"Whether to include index information in the response\\\"},\\\"exclude_null_values\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"boolean\\\",\\\"default\\\":true,\\\"description\\\":\\\"Whether to exclude properties with null values from the response for cleaner output\\\"},\\\"include_relationships\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"boolean\\\",\\\"default\\\":true,\\\"description\\\":\\\"Whether to include foreign key relationships in the response\\\"}}},\\\"hasFullSchema\\\":true},\\\"SUPABASE_LIST_TABLES\\\":{\\\"toolkit\\\":\\\"SUPABASE\\\",\\\"tool_slug\\\":\\\"SUPABASE_LIST_TABLES\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"Lists all tables and views in specified database schemas, providing a quick overview of database structure to help identify available tables before fetching detailed schemas.\\\",\\\"input_schema\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"object\\\",\\\"required\\\":[\\\"project_ref\\\"],\\\"properties\\\":{\\\"schemas\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"array\\\",\\\"items\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"string\\\"},\\\"examples\\\":[null,[\\\"public\\\"],[\\\"public\\\",\\\"auth\\\",\\\"storage\\\"]],\\\"description\\\":\\\"List of schemas to search for tables (maximum 10). If not provided, lists tables from all non-system schemas. Specify schema names to limit the search to specific schemas.\\\"},\\\"project_ref\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"string\\\",\\\"pattern\\\":\\\"^[a-z]+$\\\",\\\"examples\\\":[\\\"huqmqtilymjtstzxbuww\\\",\\\"abcdefghijklmnopqrst\\\"],\\\"maxLength\\\":20,\\\"minLength\\\":20,\\\"description\\\":\\\"The unique reference ID of the Supabase project. Must be exactly 20 lowercase letters (a-z only, no numbers or hyphens). This is visible in your Supabase project URL (e.g., https://supabase.com/dashboard/project/{project_ref}).\\\"},\\\"include_views\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"boolean\\\",\\\"default\\\":true,\\\"description\\\":\\\"Whether to include views along with tables in the results\\\"},\\\"include_metadata\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"boolean\\\",\\\"default\\\":true,\\\"description\\\":\\\"Whether to include basic metadata like estimated row count and table size\\\"},\\\"include_system_schemas\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"boolean\\\",\\\"default\\\":false,\\\"description\\\":\\\"Whether to include system schemas (pg_catalog, information_schema, etc.) when listing all schemas. When set to false, temporary and internal schemas such as pg_temp_*, pg_toast_temp_* and pgbouncer are excluded from the results.\\\"}}},\\\"hasFullSchema\\\":true},\\\"GOOGLETASKS_CREATE_TASK_LIST\\\":{\\\"toolkit\\\":\\\"GOOGLETASKS\\\",\\\"tool_slug\\\":\\\"GOOGLETASKS_CREATE_TASK_LIST\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"Creates a new task list with the specified title and returns a tasklist_id. Use the returned tasklis...\\\",\\\"hasFullSchema\\\":false,\\\"schemaRef\\\":{\\\"tool\\\":\\\"COMPOSIO_GET_TOOL_SCHEMAS\\\",\\\"args\\\":{\\\"tool_slugs\\\":[\\\"GOOGLETASKS_CREATE_TASK_LIST\\\"]}}},\\\"GOOGLETASKS_GET_TASK\\\":{\\\"toolkit\\\":\\\"GOOGLETASKS\\\",\\\"tool_slug\\\":\\\"GOOGLETASKS_GET_TASK\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"Retrieve a specific Google Task. REQUIRES both `tasklist_id` and `task_id`. Tasks cannot be retrieve...\\\",\\\"hasFullSchema\\\":false,\\\"schemaRef\\\":{\\\"tool\\\":\\\"COMPOSIO_GET_TOOL_SCHEMAS\\\",\\\"args\\\":{\\\"tool_slugs\\\":[\\\"GOOGLETASKS_GET_TASK\\\"]}}},\\\"GOOGLETASKS_LIST_ALL_TASKS\\\":{\\\"toolkit\\\":\\