ederation/status`. - Status fields to report when relevant: `role`, `configuredRole`, `hubBaseUrl`, `proxyId`, `proxyCount`, `hostedWorkspaceCount`, `queueDepth`, `fanoutDepth`, `conflictCount`, and `staleReadStatus`. - `Standalone` serves local workspaces only. `DirectProxy` is the existing point-to-point proxy mode. `Hub` is authoritative for enrolled proxies and global workspace inventory. `LocalProxy` forwards MCP requests to `hubBaseUrl` and queues mutating requests during hub outages. - LocalProxy queued writes return `202 Accepted` with `X-Mcp-Queued: true` and `X-Mcp-Operation-Id`. Treat the write as optimistic until the hub acknowledges it. - If `queueDepth`, `fanoutDepth`, `conflictCount`, or stale-read status is non-zero/non-clear, include that in session log status and avoid claiming federation synchronization is complete.\\n272 | \\n273 |   ## Per User Message\\n274 |   1. Post a new session log turn before starting work.\\n275 |   2. Complete the user's request.\\n276 |   3. Update the turn with results and actions when done.\\n277 | \\n278 |   ## Re-run Full Session Start Only If\\n279 |   - The user explicitly says \\\"Start Session\\\". - Signature verification fails. - /health fails. - /health nonce verification fails. - Any /mcpserver/* call returns 401. - The marker endpoint/key changes after a server restart.\\n280 | \\n281 |   ## Continuing After a Server Restart\\n282 |   The workspace API key rotates on every server restart, so an in-flight agent will begin seeing 401 on /mcpserver/* calls. When that happens, resume through your required agent plugin - do not hand-roll raw REST for session, TODO, or requirements work:\\n283 |   1. Treat the first 401 (or a /health failure) as a restart signal and stop using the cached API key immediately.\\n284 |   2. Re-read this marker file (AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml) for the rotated apiKey and current endpoints.\\n285 |   3. Re-verify trust before resuming: recompute the marker signature with the new key and call /health with a fresh nonce.\\n286 |   4. Re-bootstrap and resume through your required agent plugin. Codex: run `Invoke-CodexMcpPlugin.ps1 -Command Status` to re-establish marker trust, health nonce, workspace, and the current session/turn, then continue with `Invoke-CodexMcpPlugin.ps1 -Command Invoke -Method <method> -Params <yaml>`. Other agents: re-run the plugin session-start hook/command, then continue via the plugin session tools.\\n287 |   5. Recover the open turn instead of starting over: query session history through the plugin (workflow.sessionlog.queryHistory), find the in-progress turn, then keep updating and complete it via the plugin. Do not open a duplicate session for the same work.\\n288 |   5a. Treat `deprecated: true` on successful `workflow.*` results as metadata, not wrapper failure. Treat an empty `workflow.sessionlog.queryHistory` result as a valid no-match result. Re-check the workspace current directory, explicit `agent` or `sourceType`, and plugin cache/session state through the wrapper; do not use raw REST merely because history is empty or marked deprecated.\\n289 |   6. If the plugin cannot re-establish trust after the restart, record `MCP_PLUGIN_UNAVAILABLE:<Agent>` and continue with non-MCP local diagnosis only.\\n290 | \\n291 |   ## Rules\\n292 |   0. NEVER write to TODO.yaml directly.\\n293 |   1. Generate SessionId values using `session.init` (REPL) or `New-McpSessionLogSlug -Agent <SourceType> -Model <Model>` (PowerShell module), then create session logs with `session.new` (REPL) or `New-McpSessionLog -SessionId` (PowerShell). Do not handcraft SessionId values.\\n294 |   1a. Agents must identify themselves accurately in session logs. `sourceType` and the SessionId `<Agent>` prefix must use the agent's real identity in Pascal-Case. Do not use lowercase forms, placeholders, or legacy aliases.\\n295 |   2. Post a new session log turn (`session.turn.add` for REPL or `Add-McpSessionTurn` for PowerShell) before starting work. Update it with results (`Response`) and actions (`Add-McpAction` / `session.action.add`) when done. Persist after each meaningful update, not just at the end.\\n296 |   2a. Before any compaction step, persist the current session log state. After compaction, update the session log again to record the compaction outcome and recovered context.\\n297 |   3. If signature verification, the /health request, or nonce verification fails, log `MCP_UNTRUSTED`, continue without the MCP server, and do not probe additional MCP endpoints.\\n298 |   4. Marker signatures use HMAC-SHA256 with the workspace API key in this file as the verifier. Recompute the canonical payload exactly as described by the marker metadata before trusting the file.\\n299 |   5. Use your required agent plugin for session log, TODO, requirements, import/export, and traceability operations. Do not use raw API calls or generic helper paths except for read-only diagnosis after plugin failure.\\n300 |   6. Write decisions, requirements, and state to the session log, not just conversation. Capture rich turn detail: interpretation, response/status, actions (type/status/filePath), contextList, filesModified, designDecisions, requirementsDiscovered, blockers, and key processingDialog updates.\",\n              \"success\": true\n            },\n            {\n              \"query\": \"/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/AGENTS.md\",\n              \"result\": \"  1 | # Agent Instructions\\n  2 | \\n  3 | ## Session Start\\n  4 | \\n  5 | 1. Read `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml` in the repo root for the current API key and endpoints.\\n  6 | 2. For specific operational steps (session bootstrap, session log turn workflow, and helper command sequence), follow `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml`.\\n  7 | \\n  8 | On every subsequent user message:\\n  9 | \\n 10 | 1. Follow `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml` for specific operational instructions.\\n 11 | 2. Complete the user's request.\\n 12 | \\n 13 | ## Rules\\n 14 | \\n 15 | 1. `templates/prompt-templates.yaml` (`default-marker-prompt`) is the source of truth for specific agent instructions. `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml` is the rendered runtime instruction set.\\n 16 | 2. Keep this file focused on durable workspace policy and conventions; avoid duplicating marker-file operational procedures.\\n 17 | 3. Use helper modules for session log and TODO operations. Do not make raw API calls.\\n 18 | 4. Persist session log updates immediately after each meaningful change (turn creation, action append, decision, requirement, blocker, file/context update). Do not defer saves.\\n 19 | 5. Capture rich turn detail: interpretation, response, status, actions (type/status/filePath), contextList, filesModified, designDecisions, requirementsDiscovered, blockers, and relevant processing dialog.\\n 20 | 6. Follow workspace conventions in `.github/copilot-instructions.md` for build, test, and architecture guidance.\\n 21 | 7. Use **only `pwsh.exe`** for shell commands and script execution; do not use `powershell.exe`.\\n 22 | 8. When you need API schemas, module examples, or compliance rules, load them from `docs/context/` or use `context_search`.\\n 23 | 9. Do not fabricate information. If you made a mistake, acknowledge it. Distinguish facts from speculation.\\n 24 | 10. Prioritize correctness over speed. Do not ship code you have not verified compiles and is logically sound.\\n 25 | 11. When writing session logs or other audit records, agents must identify themselves accurately using their real agent identity in Pascal-Case. Do not use placeholder, legacy, or misleading sourceType values.\\n 26 | 12. Never edit YAML by appending, replacing, or removing text lines. Deserialize the complete document into an object, mutate the object, serialize it, and save it. Use `plugins/core/lib-ps/yaml-object-mutation.ps1` for PowerShell work.\\n 27 | \\n 28 | ## Byrd Test Gate\\n 29 | \\n 30 | The canonical process is `docs/Development-Process-draft-v4.md`. To leave a Byrd implementation slice, the entire unit test suite for the current iteration and previous iterations must be completely passing. In this workspace, skipped tests are not passing tests: a validation gate requires zero failures and zero skips in the executed scope. Tests should directly track progress; deferred work belongs in MCP TODO/requirements state until that slice starts, not in skipped test placeholders.\\n 31 | \\n 32 | ## Where Things Live\\n 33 | \\n 34 | - `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml` — connection details, API key, workspace config (regenerated on server start)\\n 35 | - `.github/copilot-instructions.md` — build/test commands, architecture overview, coding conventions\\n 36 | - `docs/context/` — on-demand reference docs (schemas, module docs, compliance rules, action types)\\n 37 | - `docs/Project/` — requirements docs, TODO.yaml, mapping matrices\\n 38 | - `templates/` — prompt templates (loaded on demand)\\n 39 | - `tools/powershell/McpContext.psm1` — PowerShell module for context ingestion/query workflows\\n 40 | - `tools/powershell/McpContext.USER.md` — user-level guide for the McpContext module\\n 41 | - `tools/powershell/McpContext.AGENT.md` — agent workflow instructions for the McpContext module\\n 42 | - `plugins/core/lib-ps/yaml-object-mutation.ps1` — object-first YAML mutation helper for agents and plugin maintenance\\n 43 | \\n 44 | ## MCP Interaction via REPL Tools\\n 45 | \\n 46 | Agents running inside `McpAgent` must use the 27 built-in tools instead of raw HTTP calls. See `docs/REPL-MIGRATION-GUIDE.md` for the full tool inventory and migration patterns.\\n 47 | \\n 48 | Key rules:\\n 49 | - Use `mcp_session_*` tools for session log lifecycle (bootstrap, turns, history).\\n 50 | - Use `mcp_todo_*` tools for TODO CRUD (query, get, create, update, delete, plan, status, implementation).\\n 51 | - Use `mcp_requirements_*` tools for FR/TR/TEST queries.\\n 52 | - Use `mcp_client_invoke` for any sub-client method not covered by a dedicated tool (context search, GitHub, workspace, etc.).\\n 53 | - Do not make raw HTTP calls to `/qbrainai/*` endpoints when a tool is available.\\n 54 | \\n 55 | ## Context Loading by Task Type\\n 56 | \\n 57 | - Agent Help: see marker `## Agent Help (QBrain.AI issues)` in `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml`; API `/qbrainai/agent-help`\\n 58 | - Session logging → `docs/context/session-log-schema.md` + `docs/context/module-bootstrap.md`\\n 59 | - TODO management → `docs/context/todo-schema.md` + `docs/context/module-bootstrap.md`\\n 60 | - API integration → `docs/context/api-capabilities.md` (or `GET /swagger/v1/swagger.json`)\\n 61 | - Adding dependencies → `docs/context/compliance-rules.md`\\n 62 | - Logging actions → `docs/context/action-types.md`\\n 63 | - New to workspace → this file + `docs/context/api-capabilities.md`\\n 64 | - Migrating from raw API → `docs/REPL-MIGRATION-GUIDE.md`\\n 65 | \\n 66 | ## Agent Conduct\\n 67 | \\n 68 | You represent the workspace owner. Your work directly reflects the owner's professional reputation.\\n 69 | \\n 70 | ### Honesty\\n 71 | \\n 72 | - Do not fabricate information, capabilities, or results.\\n 73 | - Distinguish between facts, informed opinions, and speculation.\\n 74 | - Acknowledge mistakes immediately and correct them.\\n 75 | \\n 76 | ### Correctness\\n 77 | \\n 78 | - Prioritize correctness over speed.\\n 79 | - When uncertain, state your uncertainty and suggest verification steps.\\n 80 | - Prefer proven patterns over clever approaches unless directed otherwise.\\n 81 | - All code must have XMLDocs. All public APIs must be documented.\\n 82 | - Follow DRY, SOLID, and existing project conventions.\\n 83 | \\n 84 | ### Decision Documentation\\n 85 | \\n 86 | - Log every decision to the session log, including trivial ones.\\n 87 | - For each decision, document: what was decided, why, what alternatives were considered, what was rejected.\\n 88 | - Log design decisions as dialog entries with category \\\"decision\\\" and as session log actions with type \\\"design_decision\\\".\\n 89 | \\n 90 | ### Professional Representation\\n 91 | \\n 92 | - Every interaction is audited via the session log.\\n 93 | - Every commit must be correct, clean, well-described, and complete.\\n 94 | - Log all commits as actions with type \\\"commit\\\" (SHA, branch, message, files).\\n 95 | - Log all PR/issue comments as actions with type \\\"pr_comment\\\" or \\\"issue_comment\\\".\\n 96 | - Always sign PR comments using the same signature convention as commits, identifying the actual authoring agent.\\n 97 | \\n 98 | ### Source Attribution\\n 99 | \\n100 | - Document all web sources in the session log as actions with type \\\"web_reference\\\" (URL, title, usage).\\n101 | - Add source URLs to the turn's contextList array.\\n102 | - Attribute external code in both the session log and code comments.\\n103 | \\n104 | ## Requirements Tracking\\n105 | \\n106 | When you discover or agree on new requirements during a session:\\n107 | \\n108 | 1. Update the files in `docs/Project/`:\\n109 |    - `Functional-Requirements.md` — append FR-MCP-* entries\\n110 |    - `Technical-Requirements.md` — append TR-MCP-* entries\\n111 |    - `TR-per-FR-Mapping.md` — append mapping rows\\n112 |    - `Requirements-Matrix.md` — append status rows\\n113 |    - `Testing-Requirements.md` — append TEST-MCP-* entries\\n114 | 2. Include the requirement ID in your session log turn's tags.\\n115 | 3. Capture requirements as they emerge. Do not defer to later.\\n116 | \\n117 | ## Design Decision Logging\\n118 | \\n119 | When a design decision is made:\\n120 | \\n121 | 1. Log it as a session log dialog item with category \\\"decision\\\".\\n122 | 2. Include: the decision, alternatives considered, rationale, and affected requirements.\\n123 | 3. Add a session log action with type \\\"design_decision\\\".\\n124 | 4. If the decision affects existing code or requirements, note what needs updating.\\n125 | \\n126 | ## Session Continuity\\n127 | \\n128 | At the start of every session