hub/McpServer/.claude/worktrees/session-20261009/docs/architecture/SessionTurn-Review-AgentContext-Architecture.md:1-230\",\n              \"result\": \"  1 | <!-- SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 -->\\n  2 | # Architecture: Turn Logging, Turn Reviews, and Stateless Agent Context\\n  3 | \\n  4 | Status: architecture documentation of the merged plan PLAN-SESSIONTURNREVIEW-001 (`docs/plans/PLAN-SESSIONTURN-SESSIONREVIEW-001.md`), pending operator approval of the merged plan. Operator directives (2026-10-11, verbatim): \\\"Integrate attached to current plan.\\\", \\\"Reconstruct plan with added requirements\\\", \\\"Also publish the doc to develop\\\", \\\"Outline now, catalog in P0\\\", \\\"No new code until I approve merged plan.\\\", \\\"I want every model defined.  I want every unit of work request and respone defined.  I want every endpoint defined.\\\", and \\\"I want a single, complete activity diagram (mermaid) for every unit of work.  I want sequence diagrams for submitting each request type and processing every response.  I want a md doc describing the who plan as architecture documentation.\\\"; for plugins and scripts, \\\"You should be designing all scripts and script tools around PowerShell and only PowerShell.\\\", \\\"This should have already been the standard for plugins.\\\", and \\\"I have not chosen PowerShell for scripting because of Microsoft or Windows. I have chosen it because it is simply the best tool for the job.\\\"\\n  5 | \\n  6 | This document describes the whole merged plan as architecture: the components, the shared components every unit calls (section 3.5: the CQRS pipeline behaviors, the storage and error components, and the session-log, scoring, agent-context, and plugin-core components, each defined and drawn once), the data each part owns, every unit of work with its request, response, and error contract, one complete activity diagram per unit of work, sequence diagrams for submitting each request type and processing each response, the use cases (section 7), delivery (section 8), and the entity diagrams of Addendum A, grouped by functionality (A.1 the references between the groups, A.2 to A.10 one diagram per group). This document is the authority, and the plan derives from it (operator, 2026-10-12: \\\"The plan should depend on the architecture, not the other way around.\\\"): the plan's scope entries, four execution phases, gates, tests, and acceptance criteria cite the decisions, contracts, units, and cases defined here, and a change to this document after the plan is approved is a plan change that needs a new hostile validation. `docs/Stateless-Agent-Context-Management.md` publishes the agent-context part of this document (section 3.5, section 6, use cases 7.11 to 7.16, and delivery 8.1 to 8.6) from the same source text, so the two never differ.\\n  7 | \\n  8 | How to read the diagrams:\\n  9 | \\n 10 | - Activity diagrams are Mermaid flowcharts read top to bottom. A rounded terminal is a start or an end, a diamond is a decision, a rectangle is an action, and every error exit is drawn and ends in a terminal that names the error code.\\n 11 | - Sequence diagrams show who sends what. Each `alt` block lists every response the caller can receive and how the caller processes it.\\n 12 | - Three kinds of activity diagram: a unit of work is exactly one CQRS command or query with its handler (operator, 2026-10-11: \\\"I want to be clear, unit of work maps to one CQRS handler\\\") and is labelled by the record's name; a policy is a rule a handler applies (for example CompletionGate); a process is plugin, host, REPL, or build orchestration that calls handlers (for example ApplyArrivalBridgeRecord). All labels are meaningful names (operator: \\\"Please use human-readable, meaningful identifiers.\\\"). Section 3.5 also draws each shared component once, as the activity diagram of that component.\\n 13 | - A double-bordered node (`[[\\\"...\\\"]]`) is a call: a shared component of section 3.5, a policy, or a process used as a step. The calling diagram draws only the exits the call returns, labelled by its typed results; the called steps are drawn once, where the callee is defined.\\n 14 | - Requirement ids in brackets name the functional requirement the unit of work realizes. Ids of the form `FR-MCP-AGENTCONTEXT-*` are reserved for Phase 1 (the plan's P0 steps 1-9), where the agent-context catalog is created.\\n 15 | \\n 16 | ## 1. Goals\\n 17 | \\n 18 | 1. One user request is exactly one session-log turn, minted by the server when the message arrives, and every write lands on the turn of the request that caused it (Phase A).\\n 19 | 2. Every turn carries a request class and two review records (agent and hostile) with lossless decimal scores, a status lifecycle, configurable thresholds, and a server-enforced completion gate (Phase B).\\n 20 | 3. Every agent invocation can run stateless: the server builds a bounded, reproducible context package from the profile, rules, requirements, session-log evidence, trusted prior responses, and current code, and only responses graded at 98/98 with no FAIL or UNKNOWN become trusted context for later requests (Phase C).\\n 21 | \\n 22 | The agent-context scope depends on the turn-logging scope (turns are the unit a package is built for, and session-log turns are evidence) and on the review scope (the thresholds and the 98/98 grading rule). Logic that more than one unit of work, policy, or process uses is one shared component (section 3.5), defined once and called from each user (operator, 2026-10-12: \\\"If a pice of logic is hared between multiple UoW, that logic should be encapsulated and shared.\\\"). Phases A, B, and C name scope areas; execution runs in four phases across all three (section 8): requirements, interfaces and code stubs, test-driven unit tests with mocks, and implementation by bounded context with parallel coding agents.\\n 23 | \\n 24 | ## 2. System context\\n 25 | \\n 26 | ```mermaid\\n 27 | flowchart LR\\n 28 |     subgraph Hosts[\\\"Agent hosts\\\"]\\n 29 |         CC[\\\"Claude Code / Cowork\\\"]\\n 30 |         CX[\\\"Codex\\\"]\\n 31 |         GK[\\\"Grok / Grok Bot\\\"]\\n 32 |         CP[\\\"Copilot\\\"]\\n 33 |         TSH[\\\"Cline / Cline v2 / OpenCode (logic-free TypeScript adapters)\\\"]\\n 34 |     end\\n 35 |     subgraph Plugins[\\\"QBrain.AI plugins (nine repos)\\\"]\\n 36 |         PS[\\\"PowerShell core lib-ps, the only plugin core: hooks, arrival bridge, verbs, failsafe, stop gate, tool descriptors, plugin core functions (3.5.6)\\\"]\\n 37 |     end\\n 38 |     subgraph Clients[\\\"Clients\\\"]\\n 39 |         REPL[\\\"qbrain-ai-repl (workflow.* verbs)\\\"]\\n 40 |         CL[\\\"QBrainAi.Client (typed REST client)\\\"]\\n 41 |         AG[\\\"McpAgent / QBAgent\\\"]\\n 42 |     end\\n 43 |     subgraph Server[\\\"QBrainAi.Support.Mcp\\\"]\\n 44 |         REST[\\\"REST controllers /qbrainai/*\\\"]\\n 45 |         MCP[\\\"MCP tools (stdio and Streamable HTTP)\\\"]\\n 46 |         PIPE[\\\"IDispatcher pipeline: WorkspaceResolutionBehavior, ModeGateBehavior (3.5.1)\\\"]\\n 47 |         SL[\\\"Session-log and review handlers, SessionLogService\\\"]\\n 48 |         GATE[\\\"Completion gate\\\"]\\n 49 |         ACX[\\\"Agent-context handlers: packages, responses and trust, graph\\\"]\\n 50 |         SHC[\\\"Shared components (3.5.2 to 3.5.5): SerializableWrite, IdempotentCreate, ReadableKeyAllocator, PagedQuery, SecretRedactor, TurnLocator, ScoreVerdict, SourceResolver\\\"]\\n 51 |         GR[\\\"GraphRAG (optional)\\\"]\\n 52 |     end\\n 53 |     DB[(\\\"Relational store: SQLite, PostgreSQL, or SQL Server\\\")]\\n 54 |     CC --> PS\\n 55 |     CX --> PS\\n 56 |     GK --> PS\\n 57 |     CP --> PS\\n 58 |     TSH -- \\\"pwsh: plugin-hook.ps1 and Invoke-McpPlugin.ps1\\\" --> PS\\n 59 |     Hosts -- \\\"model tool calls over MCP (stdio or Streamable HTTP)\\\" --> MCP\\n 60 |     PS --> REPL\\n 61 |     REPL --> CL\\n 62 |     AG --> CL\\n 63 |     CL --> REST\\n 64 |     REST -- \\\"SendAsync or QueryAsync\\\" --> PIPE\\n 65 |     MCP -- \\\"SendAsync or QueryAsync\\\" --> PIPE\\n 66 |     PIPE --> SL\\n 67 |     PIPE --> ACX\\n 68 |     SL --> GATE\\n 69 |     SL --> SHC\\n 70 |     ACX --> SHC\\n 71 |     SHC --> DB\\n 72 |     SL --> DB\\n 73 |     ACX --> DB\\n 74 |     ACX -. \\\"projection, optional\\\" .-> GR\\n 75 | ```\\n 76 | \\n 77 | ## 3. Cross-cutting conventions, design decisions, and contracts\\n 78 | \\n 79 | - Workspace scoping: every request is scoped to one workspace through the `X-Workspace-Path` header (REST), the `workspacePath` parameter (MCP tools, REPL), or `MCP_WORKSPACE_PATH` (plugins); WorkspaceResolutionBehavior (section 3.5.1) resolves it for every command and query, existing ones included, into the `WorkspaceContext` of the caller's dispatch scope (`IDispatcher` becomes scoped), and a record that may run with no workspace (global memory) declares `IAllowsGlobalWorkspace`. Every stored record carries its `WorkspaceId`, and no read ever crosses workspaces.\\n 80 | - Authentication: `X-Api-Key` (or `api_key`) from the workspace marker. Plugins never call REST directly; they use the REPL wrapper.\\n 81 | - Plugin scripting: PowerShell only (operator, 2026-10-11: \\\"You should be designing all scripts and script tools around PowerShell and only PowerShell.\\\"). `plugins/core/lib-ps` is the one plugin core for all nine hosts; cline, cline-v2, and opencode keep only the adapter entry file their host API forces, which forwards each host hook invocation the host binding table names (`{ input, output }`; on opencode's `event` hook, which fires for every host event including streaming updates, only an event of type `session.idle`, so no other event starts `pwsh`) to `lib/plugin-hook.ps1 -HostEvent` and applies the returned `output`, forwards each tool call (`{ params, hostContext }`) to `lib/Invoke-McpPlugin.ps1 -InvocationFromStdin`, and registers the tools listed in `lib/tool-descriptors.json`; the host's own build compiles that one file into the git-ignored `dist/`; Pester is the only plugin test runner.\\n 82 | - Routes: every REST route exists under `/qbrainai/...` with the legacy alias `/mcpserver/...`.\\n 83 | - Errors: every REST error of a unit this plan adds or changes is the `ClassifiedError` problem+json body `{ \\\"type\\\", \\\"title\\\", \\\"status\\\", \\\"detail\\\", \\\"code\\\", \\\"message\\\", \\\"retryable\\\", \\\"details\\\" }` emitted through `McpErrorClassifier` (`SessionLogController.cs` and the agent-context controller) from the handler's `ClassifiedErrorException` (section 3.5.2); `code` carries the unit's error code (for example `review_status_conflict`, `agent_context_budget_exceeded`), the HTTP status is the one stated per unit (400 validation, 403 not allowed, 404 not found, 409 conflict or disabled, 422 semantic refusal), and MCP tools return `{ code, error, message, retryable, details }`. A 409 `conflict` carries `retryable: true` when SerializableWrite or key allocation exhausted its retries (four attempts; only the Phase A minter states its own bound, for a collision of its random request id only, six attempts and 500 `mint_collision_exhausted`, while its transient conflicts keep the four attempts and the 409, section 3.5.2), which is also the answer when a deadlock or a busy store outlasts those attempts, and `retryable: false` when an idempotency key is reused with other inputs; the plugin treats a retryable 409 as an outage and keeps its failsafe record (a review excepted: reviews keep their no-failsafe rule, section 3.1); a busy store outside SerializableWrite keeps today's 503 `persistence_error` with `retryable: true` (`src/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp/Services/McpErrorClassifier.cs:129-139` at `a8d3934b`), and unreachable storage keeps its answer (section 3.1, \\\"Deadlock and busy outcomes\\\"). A workspace that does not resolve is 400 `validation_error` with `details.field` `workspacePath` on every surface, the status TR-MCP-MT-002 gives an unregistered workspace (WorkspaceResolutionBehavior, section 3.5.1), and every sequence draws that refusal after the dispatch. On REST, `WorkspaceResolutionMiddleware` still answers a missing or unknown `X-Workspace-Path` before any controller with today's ` [line truncated]\\n 84 | - Identifiers: every identifier is human-readable and meaningful (operator, 2026-10-11: \\\"Please use human-readable, meaningful identifiers.\\\"). Session ids `<Agent>-<yyyyMMddTHHmmssZ>-<suffix>`; server-minted request ids `req-<yyyyMMddTHHmmssZ>-<8 hex>`; agent-context record keys derive from the turn and session they belong to (section 6.3): packages `pkg-<requestId>-<nn>`, responses `rsp-<requestId>-<nn>`, grades `grd-<responseId>-<kind>-<nn>`, relationships `rel-<type>-<yyyyMMddTHHmmssZ>-<nn>`, HV requests `hvr-<sessionId>-<yyyyMMddTHHmmssZ>`; ReadableKeyAllocator (section 3.5.2) allocates every one of these keys and the server-minted request ids. Document labels use the command, query, process, policy, and shared component names. Namespaces say where a type goes and who owns it, and type and member names say what it is and what it does (section 3.5).\\n 85 | - Root ids: every record carries `WorkspaceId` and the root ids of every parent it hangs from, a turn's root id being its whole turn key (`Agent`, `SessionId`, `RequestId`): a turn child the turn key; a package child `PackageId` and the turn key; a response child `ResponseId`, `PackageId`, and the turn key; a grade those and, for a model grade, `HvRequestId`; an HV request item `HvRequestId`, `ResponseId`, `PackageId`, and the turn key (section 3.1, \\\"Root ids\\\"; section 6.3).\\n 86 | - Changes to existing behavior: no phase is purely additive. Phase C adds tables, endpoints, and the `Mcp:AgentContext:Mode` setting, but the shared components it brings change behavior every existing caller sees, in every Mode: `IDispatcher` in the published `QBrainAI.Cqrs` package becomes scoped and WorkspaceResolutionBehavior runs on every command and query (section 3.5.1); a deadlock or busy store that outlasts SerializableWrite's attempts answers 409 `conflict` with `retryable: true` where develop answers 503 (section 3.5.2); the published `QBrainAI.Client` package gains the new DTOs, `ClassifiedErrorException`, `ErrorCodes`, `PageResult`, and `ScoreLexeme` (section 8.1, \\\"Published packages\\\", states each package's version effect: the client additions a minor version, the scoped `IDispatcher` a major version under semantic versioning). Phases A and B change turn identity (server-minted request ids), turn writes (turn-level isolation, `closed` refused on agent surfaces, the completion gate), and the plugin's per-turn store; the TypeScript hosts' tool audit moves into the PowerShell core and keeps its `tool_call` and `tool_result` dialog items but writes no `design_decision` action per tool event (section 3.2, Tool-audit hook). Where this document states a behavior that replaces a live requirement record, it names the record inline once, in the form \\\"(replaces ...)\\\", so the plan lists every update.\\n 87 | - Secrets: no API key, token, password, or credential is ever stored in a context package or a response, and none is ever returned by a read surface; turn text is stored as written, searched as stored, and redacted on every read surface (FR-MCP-SESSIONLOGSAN-001, its ac4 for the search; section 3.4, Search); `SecretRedactor` (section 3.5.2) is the one redactor behind the session-log sanitizer's review paths, the turn read projection, and the agent-context secret filter and secret scan.\\n 88 | - Process: the plan runs in the four execution phases of section 8 under the Byrd Development Process v4 (requirements, tests red, mocks green, implementation green, full gates with zero failures and zero skips); each prerequisite, the end of Phase 1, Phase 2, and Phase 3, each bounded context at the end of its Phase 4 work package, and the phase-wide Phase 4 HV end with a Codex gpt-6-astra max hostile validation at accuracy and completeness of at least 98 with every claim PASS. Builds and tests run as Nuke targets natively on Windows or Linux; GitHub Actions and Azure Pipelines are not part of any requirement or process; Octopus (container on LAB-OMARCHY) is used only for releases.\\n 89 | ### 3.1 Design decisions\\n 90 | \\n 91 | Moved verbatim from the plan on 2026-10-12 (operator: \\\"The plan should depend on the architecture, not the other way around.\\\"). Delivery references inside this text (P0 steps, slices A1-A9 and S1-S11, TODO ids, gates) name the plan's delivery units; the decisions and contracts themselves are defined here, and the plan cites them.\\n 92 | \\n 93 | Locked operator decisions of 2026-10-09:\\n 94 | \\n 95 | Turn logging\\n 96 | - Every user request (including messages queued mid-turn) is exactly one turn; actions and decisions map to the turn of the request that caused them, determined by the write-attribution rule below (D2). Enforcement of turn-logging correctness is Phase A and ships first.\\n 97 | - Plugin turn edits are independent: opening, editing, closing, or canceling one turn never changes another turn's local or server state; concurrent edits to different turns do not interfere.\\n 98 | - Unsupported-session failsafe (operator, 2026-10-10: \\\"If QBrain is truly not available, use the failsafe already in the plugin.\\\" and, choosing between a failsafe capture and a warn-only Stop, \\\"1\\\"): when a host session fails closed under D6 (`unsupportedSince`), the model's session-log writes for a turn that was already open at that moment go to the plugin's existing failsafe instead of being refused (reviews excepted: they keep their no-failsafe rule and are recorded after the drain through the original pair), so the Stop gate's remedy stays accepted; the captured records drain at the next SessionStart through the trusted recovery flow with the turn's original pair (C5); every mint and every write for any other turn stays refused (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001).\\n 99 | - Clear-session closing rule (operator, 2026-10-10, choosing option A over leaving blocked and withdrawn turns open: \\\"A\\\"): clear-session completes each turn that meets the completion policy, fails each withdrawn turn (reason \\\"withdrawn before delivery\\\") and each turn the model marks blocked (with its blocker reason; `failed` requires `requestClass` only, FR-MCP-SESSIONCLASS-002-AC002), and leaves every other turn in progress, reported by requestId with its exact missing items, never fabricating reviews (TR-MCP-CLEARSESSION-001-AC001). Definition (catalog cross-reference, not a change to the decision): \\\"marked blocked\\\" means the turn's `blockers` section (the existing decision-list field, FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-006-AC001) holds at least one nonblank model-authored entry, read by the clear-session coordinator for that turn by its original (`sessionId`, `requestId`) pair, and the first entry is the fail reason; precedence per turn: withdrawn -> `failTurn` (\\\"withdrawn before delivery\\\"); else blocked -> `failTurn` with the blocker reason (blocked takes precedence over a ready completion); else meets the completion policy -> `completeTurn`; else stays in progress, reported with its exact missing items. Close prerequisites (catalog cross-reference, not a change to the decision): before clear-session fails a withdrawn or blocked turn, the model writes by that turn's original pair, within the same clear-session run and before its session-end step, every prerequisite the plugin and the server require for that close, in the Stop gate remedy order (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC009): a designDecision when the turn has no model evidence (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC002), then a `requestClass` when it is missing or `Unclassified`, judged from the turn's `queryText`; the coordinator then fails the turn with its reason (TR-MCP-CLEARSESSION-001-AC001).\\n100 | - Request identity (operator D1, 2026-10-09: \\\"have the server issue a request token when a message arrives\\\"): the server mints every turn's `requestId` when a user message arrives (ingress), through the new mint operation `POST /qbrainai/sessionlog/{agent}/{sessionId}/mint`. The mint call carries an idempotency nonce derived from the host's own identity for that message event (host binding table, section 3.2): a host per-prompt id, or a SHA-256 of host event fields that every delivery of that event carries identically. Every delivery of one event therefore sends the same nonce and receives the same token, including network retries and failsafe replay on every host and, on hosts whose nonce is a stable per-message id (codex `turn_id`, grok and grok-bot `promptId`, cline-v2 `runId`, opencode `messageID`) or the bridge's single ordered source (claude-code, claude-cowork), two hook processes started by a doubly registered hook (on copilot and classic cline a second registration is refused before any mint, and a second new equal-field invocation fails closed); two separate user messages (including two intentionally identical prompts) are two events and receive two tokens. On copilot and classic cline, whose event fields include no per-message id, a message whose session, timestamp, and prompt all equal an already minted one is indistinguishable from a redelivery of it, so the host session fails closed under D6 instead of merging or guessing (host binding table, equal-field rule). The host id is only the idempotency key; the turn identity is the server-issued token. There is no prompt-text dedupe window. Every mint comes from a host arrival mechanism (a prompt hook, the arrival bridge, or a host-native hook adapter); no supported host relies on the model to mint. The stop gate's `duplicate_delivery_suspected` warning is a diagnostic only and is never counted as D1 compliance.\\n101 | - Queued-message arrival (operator D5, 2026-10-09: \\\"Arrival bridge\\\"): a message the operator sends while a turn is running is minted when it is enqueued, not when the model later processes it. On hosts that record the enqueue in a transcript (Claude Code), a per-host-session arrival bridge started by the SessionStart hook tails the transcript and follows each queued message through the lifecycle the transcript records. Observed in this workspace's Claude Code transcripts on 2026-10-09: 117 `enqueue`, 79 `dequeue`, 35 `remove` (27 with reason `absorbed_mid_turn`), 2 `popAll`; a normal prompt is `enqueue` then `dequeue` within milliseconds, a mid-turn message is `enqueue` then `remove` with `absorbed_mid_turn` when it is injected into the running turn (for example the operator's mid-turn message beginning \\\"write a global memory with the\\\" was removed with `absorbed_mid_turn` at 2026-10-09T14:35:47.730Z), and `popAll` pulls queued text back into the input box. The bridge mints at `enqueue` (state `queued`); the request becomes `delivered` at `dequeue` (applied by the bridge from the transcript itself; on hosts with a bridge the prompt hook neither mints nor claims) or at `remove` with `absorbed_mid_turn`; it becomes `withdrawn` at `popAll` or a `remove` with any other or no reason. Task notifications and other system events that the host also enqueues are classified and never minted; on bridge hosts they keep their queue position (`kind: system`), so their `dequeue` never delivers a user request.\\n102 | - Hosts without an arrival mechanism (operator D6, 2026-10-09: \\\"Adapters or unsupported\\\"): a host is supported for turn logging only through an arrival mechanism this plan specifies (a prompt hook that fires at enqueue, the claude-code/claude-cowork arrival bridge, or the classic-cline native-hook adapter); every other host is unsupported until a separately approved adapter exists, and a queue or enqueue event that P0.2 records on such a host becomes a traced follow-up adapter TODO (filed in P0.8) for operator approval (host binding table, section 3.2). A host with no arrival mechanism is declared unsupported for turn logging until it gains one: its plugin reports `turn_logging_unsupported` on every session-log verb and writes nothing (a host session that becomes unsupported after one of its requests was delivered follows the host binding table unsupported-session rule and the 2026-10-10 failsafe decision), and no first-sight minting is used anywhere.\\n103 | - Plugin equivalence (operator D7, 2026-10-09, confirmed in chat \\\"1 yes\\\" for the requirement and its qualification posted by Codex in `MEMORY-PROCESS-007` at 17:48:48Z and 17:52:06Z): all QBrain.AI plugins keep equivalent functionality; shared capabilities, contracts, fixes, and behavior changes go to every plugin; host-specific adaptations preserve equivalent supported behavior; equivalence is limited by what each host's plugin infrastructure can support, no extraordinary effort is spent forcing unworkable equivalence, and each such limit is documented as a feature-specific capability limitation instead of being claimed as equivalent. A D6 `turn_logging_unsupported` host is such a documented limitation. The limitations live in `docs/AGENT-PLUGIN-FEATURE-MATRIX.md` (one row per plugin and feature: supported, adapted, or limited, with the reason and the receipt), first written in P0.8 from the P0.2 receipts, updated in A9 and S11, and checked by a docs contract test.\\n104 | - Ninth plugin (operator D8, 2026-10-09: \\\"2 yes\\\"): `mcpserver-grok-bot-plugin` is in scope like the other eight.\\n105 | - Write attribution (operator D2, 2026-10-09: tie each write to its request automatically through the agent app's hooks): every verb invocation is bound to one request automatically from host context, without the model naming it: the binding is the write target of the invoking host session at the moment the invocation starts, captured once and stored immutably with that invocation (its persist record and failsafe record), so a later message never retargets an invocation already started. A host session's write target is the request most recently delivered to the model in that host session: a prompt the host hands to the model as a new turn (the prompt hook on hosts without a bridge, or the bridge's `dequeue` delivery), or a queued message absorbed into the running turn (`absorbed_mid_turn`). Minting alone never moves the write target, so work the model is still doing for request R1 after R2 is enqueued stays on R1 until R2 is delivered. Explicit `requestId` remains accepted as an override, for example to continue R1 work after R2 was absorbed, and it is required while two or more delivered requests are open in the host session (D9). Attribution therefore maps each write to the request most recently delivered to the model in its host session when the write started. A write is never attributed by guesswork: when a verb cannot read its host session key from its own invocation context (host binding table, section 3.2), it binds only if exactly one live host session of its agent exists in the workspace (warning `host_session_inferred`); with none it is refused (`host_session_unknown`), and with more than one it is refused (`host_session_ambiguous`), in both cases unless it carries the trusted recovery or review-backfill pair (original MCP `sessionId` and `requestId`, verified against the server for this workspace and agent, C5); an explicit `requestId` alone never lets a verb without a host session key write. No verb ever targets the workspace-level `current-turn.yaml`.\\n106 | - Ambiguous attribution (operator D9, 2026-10-09: \\\"Refuse when ambiguous\\\"): no host supplies a per-action signal of which request caused a write, so automatic binding applies only while exactly one delivered request is open in the host session. While two or more delivered requests are open (for example R2 absorbed mid-turn while R1 is still open), a write without an explicit `requestId` is refused with `request_ambiguous`, naming the open requests; the model passes `requestId` until all but one are closed. Writes are never attributed by guesswork.\\n107 | - Copilot duplicate deliveries (operator D11, 2026-10-09: \\\"Supported only if single hook\\\"): Copilot gives no stable per-prompt id and separately registered hooks may see different `timestamp` values, so Copilot turn logging is supported only while the plugin's duplicate check proves exactly one MCP prompt hook across all Copilot hook sources; otherwise the Copilot prompt hook reports `turn_logging_unsupported` before any mint, and the condition is a D7 documented limitation until fixed. One Copilot prompt therefore never becomes two turns.\\n108 | - Node runtime floor (operator D12, 2026-10-09: \\\"Keep Node 18/20\\\"), superseded for core code by the 2026-10-11 PowerShell-only ruling: no code of this plan runs on Node except the logic-free TypeScript-host adapters, which keep each host's declared engine unchanged (mcpserver-cline-plugin, mcpserver-cline-v2-plugin, and mcpserver-opencode-plugin `>=18`; the Cline application's own `node >=22` is unaffected). Exact review scores are validated and carried by the PowerShell core's raw-lexeme rules (S8); the adapters never parse a score (C10).\\n109 | \\n110 | Request class\\n111 | - Turn scalar `requestClass`: `Code` | `Docs` | `Chore` (case-insensitive in, canonical PascalCase out, anything else rejected). Required, enforced at transition to `completed` or `failed`, not at creation (hooks create turns before the agent can classify). Legacy rows backfilled to `Unclassified` (readable; never accepted on new writes from agent surfaces).\\n112 | \\n113 | Review record (shared by `agentReview` and `hostileReview`)\\n114 | - Fields: `status` (`Unnecessary` | `Unstarted` | `Running` | `Pass` | `Fail` | `Other`), `otherNotes`, `correctness`, `completeness` (lossless decimal 0..1), `correctnessThreshold`, `completenessThreshold` (server-stamped, read-only), `reasons` (string[]), `reviewer`, `reviewedAt` (UTC), `requestJsonlPath`, `responseJsonlPath`. No `passed` bool.\\n115 | - One record per kind per turn; latest write wins.\\n116 | - Server creates both at turn creation as `Unstarted`. `hostileReview` flips to `Unnecessary` when `requestClass` becomes `Chore`, and back to `Unstarted` if the class changes away while still `Unnecessary`. Records past `Unstarted`/`Unnecessary` are never auto-changed. Legacy turns are backfilled `agentReview=Unstarted`, `hostileReview=Unstarted`. (Catalog cross-references, not a change to the decision: the flip and its reverse apply only to in-progress turns, FR-MCP-SESSIONREVIEW-006-AC002 and UC-4; a reset restores the creation default for the turn's current class, FR-MCP-SESSIONREVIEW-001-AC004.)\\n117 | - `otherNotes` with text is rejected unless `status` is `Other`.\\n118 | - Strict per-status fields: `Unnecessary`/`Unstarted` carry no scores, reasons, reviewer, timestamps, or paths; `Running` requires `reviewer`, forbids scores; `Pass`/`Fail` require `correctness`, `completeness`, `reviewer`, `reviewedAt`; `hostileReview` `Pass`/`Fail` also require `requestJsonlPath` and `responseJsonlPath`; `Other` requires non-empty `otherNotes` (the Other reason; operator D4, 2026-10-09: \\\"Other requires Other Reason be populated\\\") and allows optional scores, `reasons`, `reviewer`, `reviewedAt`, and receipt paths. `Unnecessary` is server-managed only.\\n119 | - Thresholds: `Mcp:SessionLog:ReviewThresholds:{Agent|Hostile}:{Correctness|Completeness}` (decimal 0..1, per instance through `McpInstanceResolver` overrides), default 0.98, inclusive (score >= threshold passes). Pass requires both scores to meet their thresholds. The server derives `Pass`/`Fail`; a supplied `Pass`/`Fail` that contradicts the derivation is rejected (422 `review_status_conflict`); omitting status lets the server derive it. `Other` is never derived. Thresholds in force are stamped on the record.\\n120 | - Names stay `correctness`/`completeness`. HV tooling maps `accuracy` -> `correctness` and integer percent -> decimal/100.\\n121 | \\n122 | Completion rules\\n123 | - `completed` requires `requestClass` plus `agentReview.status` in `Pass`/`Fail`/`Other`; for `Code`/`Docs` also `hostileReview.status` in `Pass`/`Fail`/`Other`; for `Chore` `hostileReview` is not checked (HV not required). `Unstarted`/`Running` block where checked. Pass is not required (Fail/Other complete).\\n124 | - `failed` requires `requestClass` only. Reviews remain writable on terminal turns.\\n125 | - Server-internal writers (QuadBrain chat, AuditedAgentCliClient, WorkspacePolicyService, federation replication, transcript/session ingestion) are exempt by code-path origin; they never fabricate reviews.\\n126 | \\n127 | Locked operator decisions of 2026-10-11 and 2026-10-12 for Phase C and the plugin core:\\n128 | \\n129 | Stateless agent context (Phase C)\\n130 | - Opt-in: `Mcp:AgentContext:Mode` is `Off` by default; `Pilot` builds and grades without injecting; `Stateless` injects the package and carries no history on a host whose plugin API can exclude history from the model request (P0.2 probe (j)); any other host treats `Stateless` as `Pilot`. Changing the default is a later operator decision taken from the pilot report.\\n131 | - Authority: the relational store holds packages, responses, grades, and relationships; GraphRAG holds only a projection, and a deterministic relational fallback answers whenever GraphRAG is disabled, uninitialized, not indexed, or failing.\\n132 | - Trust: a response is trusted only when its latest deterministic grade and its latest model grade both reach correctness and completeness of at least the trust thresholds (configurable only upward from a floor of 0.98: a value below 0.98 fails startup, and every grade and trust decision applies max(configured, 0.98), inclusive) with zero FAIL and zero UNKNOWN findings; model grades come only from the configured grader (default Codex, `gpt-6-astra`, `max`, the plan's HV reviewer).\\n133 | - Budget: mandatory context (request, profile, rules, applicable requirements) is never truncated; when it exceeds the budget the build fails with `agent_context_budget_exceeded`, and in `Pilot` or `Stateless` mode the invocation fails (decision below).\\n134 | - Audit: rejected, stale, and superseded responses are kept and never retrieved as trusted context.\\n135 | - Package failure (operator, plan-readiness round 1 finding F2, verbatim answer \\\"Fail the invocation\\\"): in `Pilot` or `Stateless` mode, when the package cannot be built (budget overflow or any build error, including a retried or concurrent build that finds the stored `Overflow` package and returns the same 422), the invocation fails: no answer is produced, the plugin refuses the prompt with the error shown to the operator, and the request's turn is closed `canceled` with that reason (canceled turns are not gated, FR-MCP-SESSIONCLASS-002). A host whose prompt hook cannot refuse a prompt cannot run `Pilot` or `Stateless` and records that as a feature-matrix limitation.\\n136 | - Model grade (operator, round-1 finding F3 and the follow-ups, verbatim: \\\"Server grading worker (Recommended)\\\", then \\\"We are going to add an HV pipeline in the future, but not in this effort.\\\" and \\\"Agents will specify a range of session turns to be HV, the HV agent will mark the grade you have been working.\\\"): this effort builds no grading worker or HV pipeline. An agent creates an HV request for a range of session-log turns (CreateAgentContextHvRequestCommand); the HV agent (the plan's Codex gpt-6-astra max reviewer, launched as every HV is) reviews that range and then, in a grade-only continuation of its own reviewer thread (`codex exec resume`) after the runner freezes the review receipts, records a model grade for each agent response in it (RecordModelGradeCommand; retry-safe, because an equal resubmission returns the stored grade), which the server accepts only from the configured grader, only for a response inside an open HV request, and only after the eight receipt, reviewer, and runtime-identity checks of the architecture document section 6.3 pass (`receipt_path`, `request_meta`, `verdict_block`, `verdict_binding`, `verdict_match`, `reviewer_turn`, `reviewer_independent`, and `runtime_identity`, which reads the reviewer's own rollout receipt and requires its recorded runtime model and effort to equal the configured grader's; otherwise 422 `agent_context_receipt_unverified` naming the check). Until a response has such a grade it stays `Grading` and is never trusted.\\n137 | - PowerShell is the only plugin script language (operator, 2026-10-11, verbatim: \\\"You should be designing all scripts and script tools around PowerShell and only PowerShell.\\\", \\\"This should have already been the standard for plugins.\\\", and \\\"I have not chosen PowerShell for scripting because of Microsoft or Windows. I have chosen it because it is simply the best tool for the job.\\\"; scope answer, verbatim: \\\"PowerShell core only (Recommended)\\\"). The ruling restores CLAUDE.md Required Usage Rule 8 (\\\"Normal plugin execution must use PowerShell only\\\") and the existing `ValidatePluginPowerShellOnly` target (`build/Build.SyncAgentPlugins.cs:75-87` and `:406-501`), from which the pre-merge plan had drifted by designing a second plugin core in TypeScript. Consequences: `plugins/core/lib-ps` is the one plugin core, and every plugin behavior of this plan is implemented and tested there once, for all nine hosts; `plugins/core/lib-node` receives no new work, and the pre-ruling A8 and S9 (a TypeScript implementation of the same behavior with its own jest copies of the core tests) are replaced by the TypeScript-host bindings in the PowerShell core and the TypeScript-host adapter contract (section 3.2); cline, cline-v2, and opencode keep only the adapter entry file their host API forces, which holds no logic and forwards every host event and tool call to the PowerShell core through `pwsh`; Pester is the only plugin test runner (bats cases ported in P0.1b; npm and jest are in no gate); the plan designs no Node, bash, bats, or Python script or tool (a host's own runtime runs its adapter, and a host that loads compiled output compiles the adapter with its existing build as a step of `SyncAgentPlugins`, never as a test); `plugins/core/lib-node` and `plugins/core/lib-sh` are retired under `MAINT-LIBNODERETIRE-001` and `MAINT-LIBSHRETIRE-001`. The D12 Node engine decision no longer governs core code; each TypeScript host keeps its declared engine. QB-AGENT-001's governance work is built in [line truncated]\\n138 | - Governed-host protections (operator, 2026-10-12, round-2 finding R2-F10, verbatim answer \\\"Port to PowerShell (Recommended)\\\"): the protections the approved plan gave the TypeScript hosts through the Node core (structured failure causes, a governed context bound to its REPL bridge with no ambient HTTP fallback, and a governed flush through the context-bound failsafe only, keeping its completion blockers and never replaying a foreign-workspace failsafe) are kept and re-specified once in the PowerShell core (TR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-008 AC-010..AC-012, delivered in A8 by PluginCorePowerShell before any TypeScript host adopts the adapter), with their discriminating Pester tests. \\\"No ambient HTTP fallback\\\" governs the verbs; the marker trust check's read-only health and nonce request stays (section 3.1, Governed verbs and the trust check).\\n139 | \\n140 | Locked operator decisions of 2026-10-12 (architecture round 5):\\n141 | \\n142 | - Shared components and names (operator, 2026-10-12, verbatim: \\\"If a pice of logic is hared between multiple UoW, that logic should be encapsulated and shared.\\\" and \\\"\\\\\\\"Where it goes\\\\\\\" and \\\\\\\"Who Owns It\\\\\\\" are jobs for namespaces.  \\\\\\\"What it is\\\\\\\" and \\\\\\\"What it does\\\\\\\" are class and member names.\\\"): logic used by more than one unit of work, policy, or process is one component of section 3.5, drawn once and called from each user; namespaces follow section 3.5's rule.\\n143 | - Stateless invocation on bridge hosts (operator, 2026-10-12, verbatim answer \\\"Hook syncs, absorbed=Pilot (Recommended)\\\"): on claude-code and claude-cowork, where the arrival bridge is the only minter, the prompt hook applies pending transcript records through `Sync-PluginArrivalLog` up to the transcript length at its own start, takes the bridge-minted `requestId` of the delivered request, and then runs the invocation (process RunStatelessInvocation); when that request's `enqueue` record is not yet in the transcript, the invocation fails (prompt refused, as for a build failure). A message absorbed into a running turn (`absorbed_mid_turn`) has no prompt to refuse and no history to exclude, so it runs as `Pilot` (the package is built and recorded, never injected, and the prompt is never refused).\\n144 | - Response submission (operator, 2026-10-12, verbatim answer \\\"Model calls submit tool (Recommended)\\\"): in `Pilot` and `Stateless` the model submits its response with the existing `agent_context_submit_response` tool (SubmitAgentResponseCommand) before it closes the turn; the plugin supplies the `packageId` it recorded for the request (found again with QueryAgentContextPackagesQuery `{ agent, sessionId, requestId, status: Built }` when a hook process boundary lost it). A missing submission is not a Stop-gate item; the pilot report counts requests with a package and no response.\\n145 | - Resumed host sessions (operator, 2026-10-12, verbatim answer \\\"Revive, keep MCP session (Recommended)\\\"): a SessionStart for a host session key whose `ended.yaml` says `session_end` (Claude Code resume and `codex exec resume` reuse the key) removes the ended marker in one atomic replace under `turn-open.lock`, as the idle revival does, and continues in the host session's recorded MCP session (`Invoke-SessionOpen` never replaces a recorded `mcpSessionId`).\\n146 | - The `closed` status (operator, 2026-10-12, verbatim answer \\\"Refuse from agent surfaces (Recommended)\\\"): agent surfaces write only `in_progress`, `completed`, `failed`, and `canceled`; a write naming `closed` is refused with 400 `validation_error`; server-internal writers and existing rows keep `closed` (`TurnStatus`, section 3.5.3).\\n147 | \\n148 | Locked operator decisions of 2026-10-12 from the pre-HV requirements reconciliation (round 5):\\n149 | \\n150 | - Analyzer rules (operator, 2026-10-12, verbatim answer \\\"Analyzers first, adopt rules (Recommended)\\\"): the analyzer branch `chore/netanalyzers-all` merges before this plan's implementation, and every new type and test follows its rules: a namespace is the project's RootNamespace followed by the folder path (IDE0130; for example code in `src/QBrainAi.Services/SessionLog/Commands/` is `QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.SessionLog.Commands`, because the RootNamespace of `QBrainAi.Services` is `QBrainAi.Support.Mcp`), one type per file (MA0048, so a command or query record and its handler are separate files), and identifiers without underscores (CA1707 covers every identifier, so test methods, test classes, and test-only subclasses alike; the named cases of this document and the plan are written in PascalCase, and P0 re-points store evidence that cites a renamed test).\\n151 | - Workspace resolution (operator, 2026-10-12, verbatim answer \\\"Every command and query\\\"): WorkspaceResolutionBehavior applies to every command and query, existing ones included. `IDispatcher` in `QBrainAi.Cqrs` (a published package) becomes a scoped facade that resolves the handler and the behaviors from the caller's DI scope (the HTTP request scope, the MCP server's per-call scope, or a scope a hosted service creates), so the behavior sets that scope's `WorkspaceContext` and a dispatch made inside a handler or a SerializableWrite delegate shares the caller's `McpDbContext` and transaction (section 3.5.1, \\\"Dispatch scope\\\"). A record with an empty `WorkspacePath` keeps the workspace its transport resolved; a record that may run with no workspace (global memory, FR-MCP-MEMORY-001) declares it with `IAllowsGlobalWorkspace`; the behavior never refuses a dispatch develop serves today. Both composition roots (`Program.cs` and `McpStdioHost.cs`) register the behaviors.\\n152 | - Turn-level isolation (operator, 2026-10-12, verbatim answer \\\"Turn-level (Recommended)\\\"): a single-turn write (upsert, patch, replace, complete, fail, review record, review reset) isolates the turn it writes and that turn's children: it reads the session row only to locate the parent and writes only that turn and its children, so writes to different turns of one session never answer a conflict (section 3.5.2, isolation scope); a multi-turn submission keeps its session-level Serializable transaction, as `SessionLogService.SubmitAsync` does today. The session summary refresh after a single-turn write is its own short write after the turn commits, and its failure never fails the turn write.\\n153 | - Code freeze exception (operator, 2026-10-12, verbatim answer \\\"Record an exception (Recommended)\\\"): the operator-directed analyzer work (`chore/netanalyzers-all`) is exempt from the code freeze.\\n154 | \\n155 | Locked operator decision of 2026-10-13:\\n156 | \\n157 | - Requirements by problem domain (operator, 2026-10-13, verbatim: \\\"Dividing the new reqs into problem domains would be best split.\\\"; clarified answer \\\"Catalog and work by domain (Recommended)\\\"): the plan's requirements catalog is divided into problem domains that follow the bounded contexts of section 8.3 (turn arrival and binding; turn persistence, decision lists, and closing; request class and the completion gate; turn reviews; plugin core runners and host adoption; context packages; responses, grading, and trust; relationships, retrieval, and projection; agent-context surfaces and stateless invocation; the shared platform; build tooling), each domain holding its own requirements, AC ownership, mappings, updated existing records, and test churn, and the derivation and implementation work are split along the same domains. The hostile validation stays one review of the whole document.\\n158 | \\n159 | Resolutions of the pre-HV requirements reconciliation (coordinator readings that reconcile the design with live requirements, code, and tests; each names what it reconciles and reuses an existing mechanism):\\n160 | \\n161 | - Workspace failure: one answer on every surface, 400 `validation_error` with `details.field` `workspacePath`, the status TR-MCP-MT-002 gives an unregistered workspace; REST keeps the middleware's own bodies before any controller (section 3.5.1).\\n162 | - Retry bound: SerializableWrite retries a declared conflict at most three times (four attempts) for every unit; only the Phase A minter states its own bound for one cause, a collision of its random request id (six attempts, then 500 `mint_collision_exhausted`, today's InsertMintedTurnWithRetry); its transient conflicts keep the default bound and the 409 `conflict` exit.\\n163 | - Mint isolation: MintSessionTurnCommand inserts one turn and is a single-turn write under the turn-level isolation decision above, like the other single-turn writes.\\n164 | - Soft delete (TR-MCP-DB-003): IdempotentCreate and ReadableKeyAllocator read past the soft-delete filter; a soft-deleted key is revived and written anew, as develop revives a tombstoned session (`SessionLogService.cs:161-169`); a deleted suffix is never reallocated.\\n165 | - Stored normal form: `McpDbContext` replaces em and en dashes in every saved string (`LineSanitizer.Sanitize`); ContentHasher hashes, and every byte-for-byte check compares, that normal form.\\n166 | - Database-only delegates: a SerializableWrite delegate does no git, file, source, or GraphRAG read; the unit reads first (after a read-only idempotency lookup) and the delegate re-checks the database state those reads relied on (section 3.5.2). Build therefore reads staleness read-only and persists nothing but its package and links; only EvaluateStalenessCommand persists `Stale`. A grade unit (RunDeterministicGradeCommand, RecordModelGradeCommand) runs every read-only check it has (the response, the HV request, the evidence checks and secret scan of a deterministic grade, and the receipt, reviewer, and runtime-identity checks of a model grade), then dispatches EvaluateStalenessCommand for the response, then runs its own SerializableWrite, whose delegate re-checks the response's `StatusVersion`; DecideAgentResponseTrustCommand reads only the database. RelationshipWriter takes the target hash its caller read before the write.\\n\\n[Showing lines 1-166 of 230. Use start_line/end_line to read other sections.]\",\n              \"success\": true\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      ],\n      \"ts\": 1792174673032\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"Index\": 501,\n    \"Message\": {\n      \"id\": \"msg__h2jMpvy\",\n      \"role\": \"user\",\n      \"content\": [\n        {\n          \"type\": \"tool_result\",\n          \"tool_use_id\": \"call_KGy9ZuuMkQDuOBOPq7cfs3bE\",\n          \"name\": \"run_commands\",\n          \"content\": [\n            {\n              \"query\": \"pwsh.exe -NoLogo -NoProfile -NonInteractive -Command '$ErrorActionPreference=\\\"Stop\\\"; $path=\\\"/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml\\\"; $lines=Get-Content -LiteralPath $path; for($i=1 ... [command truncated: 542 more chars; full command is in the tool call input]\",\n              \"result\": \"191: prompt: |\\n192:   You are connected to an MCP Context Server at http://LAB-OMARCHY:7147. Use this server to coordinate your work.\\n193: \\n194:   ## Session Start (Run Once Per Session)\\n195:   1. Read this marker file for connection details and API key.\\n196:   2. Bootstrap the required plugin named for your agent in the top-level `agent_plugins` contract. Get the plugin through the MCP Server tool registry before relying on local root hints: search `/mcpserver/tools/search?keyword=<plugin_name>` for the installed tool definition and select the result whose `name` exactly equals `<plugin_name>`; if that exact tool definition is missing, install the definition from `/mcpserver/tools/buckets/official/install?toolName=<plugin_name>`, then search again. Execute the exact tool definition's returned `commandTemplate` with `targetParent` set to the plugin parent directory to clone or update the plugin repository. Codex must use `mcpserver-codex-plugin`; Claude must use `mcpserver-claude-code-plugin`; Copilot must use `mcpserver-copilot-plugin`; Cline must use `mcpserver-cline-plugin`; Grok must use `mcpserver-grok-plugin`.\\n197:   3. Verify the marker signature using the workspace API key in this file before contacting the server.\\n198:   4. Call /health with a random nonce and confirm the response echoes that exact nonce.\\n199:   5. Verify and log plugin identity before other MCP work: sourceType, plugin name/version, plugin root or MCP server name, marker signature result, health nonce result, and workspace path.\\n200:   6. Review recent session history and current TODOs only after signature, nonce, and plugin verification succeed.\\n201:   7. Post an initial session log turn for the session.\\n202: \\n203:   ## Required Agent Plugins\\n204:   - `agent_plugins.policy` is `required`. The plugin contract or deterministic digest is covered by marker signature verification. - Plugin acquisition is the bootstrap exception to the normal plugin-only rule: if the matching plugin is not already available locally, use the authenticated tool registry endpoints in this marker to locate/install the matching `mcpserver-*-plugin` tool definition from the `official` bucket and run its clone/update command. After acquisition, bootstrap and use the matching plugin for normal MCP work. - If your matching plugin cannot be acquired through the tool registry or remains unavailable after acquisition, stop MCP usage, record `MCP_PLUGIN_UNAVAILABLE:<Agent>`, and continue only with non-MCP local diagnosis. - Do not substitute raw REST, generic `mcpserver-repl`, helper modules, or another agent's plugin for normal session, TODO, requirements, import/export, or traceability operations. - Another agent's plugin may be inspected only for read-only diagnosis of plugin installation or contract drift. - Normal workspace shell commands use `pwsh.exe`. Plugin-owned hooks may use the runner declared by that plugin contract. - Codex plugin tools/hooks must satisfy `agent_plugins.agents.Codex`; Claude plugin tools/hooks must satisfy `agent_plugins.agents.Claude`; Copilot plugin tools/hooks must satisfy `agent_plugins.agents.Copilot`; Cline MCP tools must satisfy `agent_plugins.agents.Cline`; Grok plugin skills/hooks/MCP server declarations must satisfy `agent_plugins.agents.Grok`. - For Grok, the Streamable HTTP MCP server exposes tool names such as `sessionlog_*`, `todo_*`, `requirements_*`, and memory tools when available; hosted-agent `mcp_*` names are not expected from that transport. `workflow.sessionlog.*`, `workflow.todo.*`, `workflow.memory.*`, and `workflow.requirements.*` are plugin shim/REPL method names used by the Grok skills and `repl-invoke` helpers; they are not expected to appear as literal `search_tool` results. - Local plugin root hints in `agent_plugins.agents.<Agent>.root_hints` are fallback verification hints after tool-registry acquisition, not the primary install source. - Claude Code agents must trigger the `claude-hook-validation` skill during marker/plugin bootstrap. That skill validates active Claude settings for MCP `UserPromptSubmit`, `Stop`, and `PostToolUse` hook commands from `hooks/hooks.json`; if any required hook is missing, it uses `install-claude-mcp-hooks.ps1` from the `claude-hook-wiring` skill to merge MCP hooks into active Claude settings without replacing existing hooks. Restart Claude Code after the skill reports installed hooks.\\n205: \\n206:   ## Model-Authored Session Logging\\n207:   - Models must write MCP Session Log turns, dialog, actions, decisions, and completions themselves through the active agent's session-log surface (`workflow.sessionlog.*`, `sessionlog_*`, `mcp_session_*`, or equivalent plugin tool).\\n208:   - Plugins must not ingest local chat transcripts to populate normal session logs and must not expose transcript-ingestion helpers, skills, endpoint shortcuts, or parser forks.\\n209:   - Transcript ingestion and normalization are explicit non-plugin server/client/REPL/MCP operations. Use them only when the user asks to import or normalize transcript files, not as a substitute for live model-authored logging.\\n210:   ## MCP Requirements Visibility\\n211:   - When implementing or enforcing requirements, query current effective requirements for the active workspace layer through the active agent's requirements surface (`workflow.requirements.effective`, `client.Requirements.GetEffectiveRequirementsAsync`, `req_effective`, or equivalent plugin tool). Use the layer catalog surface (`workflow.requirements.listLayers/createLayer/updateLayer`, `client.Requirements.ListRequirementLayersAsync/CreateRequirementLayerAsync/UpdateRequirementLayerAsync`, `req_list_layers/req_create_layer/req_update_layer`, or equivalent plugin tools) when the user asks to inspect or manage requirement layers. Do not treat raw all-requirements lists, exported markdown, or future-layer requirements as currently enforceable unless the user explicitly asks to inspect or plan those layers.\\n212:   ## YAML Mutation Rule\\n213: \\n214:   - Never edit YAML files by appending, replacing, or removing text lines. This includes one-key configuration changes.\\n215: \\n216:   - Always deserialize the complete document into an object, mutate the object, serialize it, and save the serialized result.\\n217: \\n218:   - For PowerShell work, use `plugins/core/lib-ps/yaml-object-mutation.ps1` and call `Set-McpYamlObjectValue` or `Update-McpYamlObject`. Do not build YAML snippets as strings or use line-oriented edit helpers for YAML.\\n219: \\n220:   ## MCP and Plugin Failure Reporting\\n221:   - MCP Server failures and required-plugin failures discovered while working must always be written as a normal failsafe YAML report. Use the plugin/REPL failsafe or pending YAML queue and preserve the triage method (`workflow.triage.report`, `triage_report`, or equivalent plugin triage tool) plus title, summary, observed behavior, expected behavior, severity, component, workspace path, dedupe key or error signature, affected paths, evidence, reproduction hints, tags, reporter agent, session id, turn id, and current TODO id when known.\\n222:   - After the failsafe YAML is written and triage submission succeeds, continue the user's active task. Do not wait for triage research, TODO creation, or resolution.\\n223:   - Do not create TODOs, requirements, GitHub issues, manual repair plans, or alternate reports for MCP Server/plugin failures unless the user's active request is to fix that failure.\\n224:   - If the MCP Server or required-plugin failure reveals that code, docs, requirements, plugin skills, package sync, deployment, configuration, or other artifacts need to be updated as a separate repair workflow, write a detailed triage report that includes the needed changes, acceptance criteria, and validation evidence, then continue the active task after the failsafe YAML is written and triage submission succeeds.\\n225:   - If triage submission fails, stop work and notify the user. Keep the failsafe YAML report for later replay; do not use raw REST or another reporting channel as a substitute.\\n226: \\n227:   ## Agent Help (MCP Server issues)\\n228:   When you are stuck on any MCP Server surface (marker trust, plugin bootstrap, session log, TODO, requirements, triage, memory, context search, federation, hooks, or API behavior), request expert help instead of improvising raw REST bypasses.\\n229:   - **When to invoke:** signature or health nonce failures you cannot resolve; unexpected tool or plugin behavior; `MCP_PLUGIN_UNAVAILABLE`; repeated 401/503 after re-reading this marker; documentation contradictions.\\n230:   - **How to invoke:** preferred `workflow.agenthelp.createSession` or MCP tool `agent_help_create_session` with caller linkage (`callerAgent`, `callerSessionId`, `callerRequestId`) and a factual issue summary (observation vs inference separated). REST: `POST /mcpserver/agent-help/session` then WebSocket `/mcpserver/agent-help/session/{id}/stream` (SSE fallback: `POST .../turn/stream`).\\n231:   - **Caller obligations:** include reproduction steps, exact error text, and files already consulted. Do not instruct the helper to ignore guardrails, skip triage, or mutate TODO/session-log/requirements storage directly.\\n232:   - Agent help is for diagnosis and coordination, not a substitute for the required plugin route for normal MCP work.\\n233: \\n234:   ## MCP Memories\\n235:   MCP memories are durable operator guidance managed by the MCP Server. Global memories apply to every workspace; Workspace memories apply only to the active workspace. Memory IDs use `MEMORY-{CATEGORY}-{NNN}`.\\n236:   Agents that support request-boundary injection must render required memories exactly as:\\n237:   REQUIRED MEMORIES - MEMORY-REQ-001: Raw memory text.\\n238:   If no required memories are visible, render:\\n239:   REQUIRED MEMORIES - None.\\n240:   Preserve raw memory text exactly; do not summarize, paraphrase, reorder within a scope, silently generate new memories, or add secrets. MCP memories are not a secret store. Effective memory order is Global memories first sorted by ID, then current Workspace memories sorted by ID.\\n241:   Existing agent-local memory stores are caches or migration sources only; the MCP memory store is the authoritative shared record. Import agent-local memories only when the operator approves the scope, the content belongs to this workspace or is intentionally Global, and the import does not leak secrets, private notes, or unrelated workspace guidance.\\n242:   Attribute memory mutations with the real agent or user identity via `updatedBy` when supported, and record successful memory add/update/remove operations in the active session log through `workflow.sessionlog.appendActions`. Keep import provenance in the session log or dialog instead of adding private source paths to memory text.\\n243:   Manage memories through the required plugin or MCP tool surface: `memory_add`, `memory_list`, `memory_update`, `memory_remove`, `memory_remember`, `memory_recall`, `memory_explore`, `memory_consolidate`, `memory_promote`, `memory_revert`; REPL `workflow.memory.add`, `workflow.memory.list`, `workflow.memory.update`, `workflow.memory.remove`, `workflow.memory.remember`, `workflow.memory.recall`, `workflow.memory.explore`, `workflow.memory.consolidate`, `workflow.memory.promote`, `workflow.memory.revert`; or REST `/mcpserver/memory` when explicitly allowed for non-plugin diagnostics.\\n244: \\n245:   ## Codex MCP Quickstart\\n246:   If you are Codex, use the Codex plugin wrappers before direct REPL calls:\\n247:   1. Run `Invoke-CodexMcpPlugin.ps1 -Command Status` from the `mcpserver-codex-plugin` root to verify plugin root, marker trust, health nonce, workspace path, session id, current turn, and supported namespaces.\\n248:   2. Invoke MCP workflow methods with `Invoke-CodexMcpPlugin.ps1 -Command Invoke -Method <method> -Params <yaml>` so YAML parameters are passed through stdin instead of fragile nested shell quoting.\\n249:   3. Complete the session-log turn intentionally with `Invoke-CodexMcpPlugin.ps1 -Command CompleteTurn -Response <final response>` before the final answer when the stop gate cannot infer completion.\\n250:   4. For `mcpserver-repl --agent-stdio`, send one single-line JSON request envelope per stdin line. Do not send formatted YAML or a single `type: batch` envelope; batch envelopes are rejected with `unsupported_batch_envelope`.\\n251: \\n252:   ## PowerShell.MCP Command Routing\\n253:   For every PowerShell Core (`pwsh`) invocation on every operating system:\\n254:   - Use PowerShell.MCP 1.14.0 or newer from PSGallery as the command runner. Install or update it with `Install-PSResource PowerShell.MCP` when the agent environment does not provide a current version.\\n255:   - `execute_command` is the primary MCP tool for PowerShell cmdlets, CLI tools, navigation, builds, tests, file-system work, and process management. Do not use the removed `invoke_expression` name. Host-qualified names depend on the configured server key: for example, `mcp__powershell__execute_command` for `powershell` and `mcp__pwsh__execute_command` for `pwsh`.\\n256:   - Calling `start_console` first is optional because `execute_command` auto-starts or reuses a console. Call `start_console` explicitly only to pre-warm at `start_location`, show a banner, allocate a sub-agent console, or force an additional console by supplying `reason`; omit `reason` when reuse is preferred.\\n257:   - Use `get_current_location` when the current PowerShell context matters. For a sub-agent, call `start_console` with `is_subagent=true`, retain the returned `agent_id`, and pass it on every later PowerShell.MCP tool call.\\n258:   - When a command continues after the call returns, use `wait_for_completion` to retrieve the cached result instead of running `Start-Sleep`. Use `cancel` for an interruptible pipeline or native CLI, and `close_console` for a non-interruptible command or PowerShell host prompt. If `close_console` reports that a human is typing, only repeat the same PID call when confirming that the console should be closed.\\n259:   - In 1.14.0, blocking timeouts are end-to-end deadlines and are capped at 170 seconds for Claude Code and 50 seconds for other or unidentified clients unless a lower limit applies. A timed-out command keeps running and its result is cached for a later tool call.\\n260:   - Route `pwsh` commands through a PowerShell.MCP session instead of launching ad hoc PowerShell processes. On Windows, reuse one session for the workspace and route `node` invocations through it as well.\\n261:   - When PowerShell.MCP must launch PowerShell Core directly, invoke `pwsh.exe` with `-NoProfile -NonInteractive`. Do not use `powershell.exe`.\\n262:   - The PowerShell escape character is the backtick `` ` ``, not the backslash. Never use C or bash-style `\\\\\\\"` escaping inside PowerShell command strings.\\n263:   - Prefer single-quoted PowerShell strings for literal regex, paths, JSON/YAML snippets, and text containing pipeline characters such as `|`.\\n264:   - When a command needs nested quoting, choose one of these patterns before improvising: `-File` with explicit arguments; piping stdin to the called tool or script; single-quoted or double-quoted here-strings; `-EncodedCommand` for complex inline PowerShell; or correct backtick escaping for embedded PowerShell quotes.\\n265:   - If a regex or literal argument contains `|`, `&`, `;`, parentheses, braces, or quotes, verify it is inside a single PowerShell argument before running.\\n266:   - For text reads and edits inside PowerShell.MCP, use `Show-TextFiles`, `Add-LinesToFile`, `Update-LinesInFile`, `Update-MatchInFile`, and `Remove-LinesFromFile`; pass literal content through `var1` to `var4`. Version 1.12.0 and newer stage replacements beside the target, so do not apply the obsolete same-volume `TEMP`/`TMP` workaround. Re-read or diff every edit before claiming it landed.\\n267:   ## Byrd Development Process\\n268:   `Byrd Dev Process`, `BDP`, `BPDv4`, and `Byrd Development Process` refer to `F:\\\\GitHub\\\\McpServer\\\\docs\\\\Development-Process-draft-v4.md`.\\n269: \\n270:   ## Federation Topology\\n271:   - To identify hub/proxy topology, call the supported federation status surface, preferably through your required plugin or client wrapper: `/mcpserver/federation/status`. - Status fields to report when relevant: `role`, `configuredRole`, `hubBaseUrl`, `proxyId`, `proxyCount`, `hostedWorkspaceCount`, `queueDepth`, `fanoutDepth`, `conflictCount`, and `staleReadStatus`. - `Standalone` serves local workspaces only. `DirectProxy` is the existing point-to-point proxy mode. `Hub` is authoritative for enrolled proxies and global workspace inventory. `LocalProxy` forwards MCP requests to `hubBaseUrl` and queues mutating requests during hub outages. - LocalProxy queued writes return `202 Accepted` with `X-Mcp-Queued: true` and `X-Mcp-Operation-Id`. Treat the write as optimistic until the hub acknowledges it. - If `queueDepth`, `fanoutDepth`, `conflictCount`, or stale-read status is non-zero/non-clear, include that in session log status and avoid claiming federation synchronization is complete.\\n272: \\n273:   ## Per User Message\\n274:   1. Post a new session log turn before starting work.\\n275:   2. Complete the user's request.\\n276:   3. Update the turn with results and actions when done.\\n277: \\n278:   ## Re-run Full Session Start Only If\\n279:   - The user explicitly says \\\"Start Session\\\". - Signature verification fails. - /health fails. - /health nonce verification fails. - Any /mcpserver/* call returns 401. - The marker endpoint/key changes after a server restart.\\n280: \\n281:   ## Continuing After a Server Restart\\n282:   The workspace API key rotates on every server restart, so an in-flight agent will begin seeing 401 on /mcpserver/* calls. When that happens, resume through your required agent plugin - do not hand-roll raw REST for session, TODO, or requirements work:\\n283:   1. Treat the first 401 (or a /health failure) as a restart signal and stop using the cached API key immediately.\\n284:   2. Re-read this marker file (AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml) for the rotated apiKey and current endpoints.\\n285:   3. Re-verify trust before resuming: recompute the marker signature with the new key and call /health with a fresh nonce.\\nPLUGIN_CONFIGURATION\\n{\\\"path\\\":\\\"/home/sharpninja/.cline/data/settings/cline_mcp_settings.json\\\",\\\"entryPresent\\\":true,\\\"disabled\\\":true}\\n{\\\"path\\\":\\\"/home/sharpninja/.config/Code/User/globalStorage/saoudrizwan.claude-dev/settings/cline_mcp_settings.json\\\",\\\"entryPresent\\\":true,\\\"disabled\\\":true}\\n\",\n              \"success\": true\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      ],\n      \"ts\": 1792174689303\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"Index\": 503,\n    \"Message\": {\n      \"id\": \"d065e807-83a5-419c-8251-47ba8bf890a5\",\n      \"role\": \"user\",\n      \"content\": [\n        {\n          \"type\": \"text\",\n          \"text\": \"<user_input mode=\\\"act\\\">save your work so I can get a functioning agent to finish it.</user_input>\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"ts\": 1792175125772\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"Index\": 505,\n    \"Message\": {\n      \"id\": \"msg_63k-M8V7\",\n      \"role\": \"user\",\n      \"content\": [\n        {\n          \"type\": \"tool_result\",\n          \"tool_use_id\": \"call_tsZrRRd0llLX0N6ZNn7rBuXe\",\n          \"name\": \"read_files\",\n          \"content\": [\n            {\n              \"query\": \"/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml:203-255\",\n              \"result\": \"203 |   ## Required Agent Plugins\\n204 |   - `agent_plugins.policy` is `required`. The plugin contract or deterministic digest is covered by marker signature verification. - Plugin acquisition is the bootstrap exception to the normal plugin-only rule: if the matching plugin is not already available locally, use the authenticated tool registry endpoints in this marker to locate/install the matching `mcpserver-*-plugin` tool definition from the `official` bucket and run its clone/update command. After acquisition, bootstrap and use the matching plugin for normal MCP work. - If your matching plugin cannot be acquired through the tool registry or remains unavailable after