dation has occurred.\\n208 | \\n209 | ## 7. Architecture-amendment completion brief\\n210 | \\n211 | This section is a coverage checklist for the next agent, not an already-approved concrete interface design. Derive actual names, fields, endpoints, errors, IDs, and transaction boundaries from the foundation and verified repository contracts. Do not present invented APIs as existing APIs.\\n212 | \\n213 | ### Descriptive architecture and decisions\\n214 | \\n215 | - State amendment scope, status, relationship to the foundation, and precedence for any explicitly amended clause.\\n216 | - Describe problem domains, component ownership, data ownership, invariants, trust boundaries, lifecycle, and failure/recovery behavior.\\n217 | - Explain the rationale and alternatives for design choices. Separate settled user requirements from proposed design resolutions.\\n218 | - Reconcile self-review independence with any existing grading, review, completion-gate, or agent-context dependencies.\\n219 | - Explain how atomic database finalization relates to externally stored compressed evidence and reliable notifications. Identify crash windows and recovery behavior rather than assuming a single transaction covers unrelated storage systems.\\n220 | - Define snapshot membership and reviewed-turn immutability precisely, including remediation and concurrent mutation handling.\\n221 | \\n222 | ### Models, interfaces, and unit-of-work catalog\\n223 | \\n224 | - Inventory every model and every CQRS command/query and its handler; distinguish handlers from policies and orchestration processes.\\n225 | - Define every request, response, validation rule, error contract, and endpoint in scope, including internal units needed by workers and recovery.\\n226 | - Cover reviewer configuration/readiness, identity normalization, submission/admission, scheduling/lease ownership, execution/capture, cancellation/timeouts, evidence verification, grading, finalization, result retrieval, streaming/reconnect, notifications, and audit operations.\\n227 | - For each interface, specify workspace routing, required/optional fields, invariants, version/concurrency checks, idempotency, retry semantics, cancellation, and terminal/nonterminal results where applicable.\\n228 | - Specify storage/provenance references and completeness checks for accepted requests, JSONL output, per-turn assessments, holistic evaluation, and execution receipts.\\n229 | - Keep public REST, typed client, REPL, Director, and MCP/helper contracts consistent. Define exact error mappings after inspecting existing patterns.\\n230 | - Define unknown ETA behavior explicitly. Do not fabricate endpoint availability or deployment status.\\n231 | \\n232 | ### Diagrams: match the foundation's rigor\\n233 | \\n234 | - Include component/context and lifecycle diagrams sufficient to explain the full design.\\n235 | - Include grouped entity diagrams and the relationships between groups.\\n236 | - Provide one complete Mermaid activity diagram for every unit of work, with meaningful labels, all decisions, and every typed success/error exit.\\n237 | - Define shared-component activities once and use explicit calls from unit diagrams rather than duplicating inconsistent logic.\\n238 | - Provide sequence diagrams for submitting each request type and processing every response branch, including validation errors, unavailable reviewers, cancellation, timeout, stale ownership, audit failure, corrupt/incomplete evidence, and recovery where applicable.\\n239 | - Distinguish unit-of-work diagrams from policy and orchestration diagrams.\\n240 | \\n241 | ### Use cases (UC)\\n242 | \\n243 | - Give each use case a stable, verified identifier and descriptive name.\\n244 | - Define participants, triggers, preconditions, main flow, alternate/error flows, postconditions, and invariant preservation.\\n245 | - Cover successful and negative paths for each selection type, reviewer exclusion/readiness, fair scheduling, retries/restarts, cancellation, complete evidence, finalization, retrieval, SSE reconnection, notification recovery, and explicit retention/removal decisions.\\n246 | - Explicitly cover missing or failed self-review not blocking HV, audit failure stopping progress, and verdicts never completing TODOs.\\n247 | - Do not turn participants into access roles or add unrequested role-based gates.\\n248 | \\n249 | ### Acceptance criteria (AC) and traceability\\n250 | \\n251 | - Use testable criteria with explicit Given/When/Then or equally precise precondition/action/outcome structure.\\n252 | - Map each settled requirement to use cases, units/interfaces, diagrams, acceptance criteria, and required tests.\\n253 | - Cover alias exclusion, round-robin persistence, snapshot consistency, stale-worker rejection, cancellation/finalization races, evidence completeness, lossless GZIP round trips, corrupt/truncated input rejection, and audit failures.\\n254 | - Cover separate per-turn and holistic outputs, indefinite retention, no permanent raw duplicate, streaming preservation, immutable results/turns, and remediation as new work.\\n255 | - Prove that varying self-review presence, grade, or status cannot change HV admission, execution, or verdict.\\n256 | - Prove that no verdict path automatically completes a TODO.\\n257 | - Verify requirement and task identifiers against their canonical records. Do not invent populated mappings or claim unverified links are valid.\\n258 | - Preserve the zero-failure/zero-skip gate, independent validation, and coordinator-controlled completion.\\n259 | \\n260 | ## 8. Pending high-priority TODO\\n261 | \\n262 | The earlier request was: **write plan to high todo**.\\n263 | \\n264 | When a permitted, audited route is available:\\n265 | \\n266 | 1. Query existing tasks to avoid duplicating the HV plan or overwriting the foundation task.\\n267 | 2. Verify foundation dependencies and requirement links before assigning them.\\n268 | 3. Persist the complete corrected plan with priority `high`, linked to the architecture amendment once created.\\n269 | 4. Keep implementation unfinished. Neither saving documentation nor an HV verdict authorizes marking the implementation TODO complete.\\n270 | 5. Read the saved record back and compare priority, full plan text, dependencies, links, and every settled correction, especially GZIP, retention, auditing, and self-review independence.\\n271 | 6. Report the exact canonical TODO ID and retrieval/storage location only after durable persistence and verification succeed.\\n272 | \\n273 | `/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/docs/context/todo-schema.md` explicitly warns not to edit `/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/docs/Project/TODO.yaml` directly. Repository instructions describe pluggable TODO storage, so that file's presence does not establish it as the authoritative active backend.\\n274 | \\n275 | The session-log schema requires a durable receipt with `persisted: true`, `degraded: false`, and `queued: false`; a missing persistence flag or an optimistic queued acknowledgement is not durable-write confirmation. Revalidate the applicable runtime contract rather than assuming the installed service/client matches the documentation.\\n276 | \\n277 | No TODO ID has been reserved by this handoff. No canonical requirements have been allocated or modified by this handoff.\\n278 | \\n279 | ## 9. Resume sequence\\n280 | \\n281 | 1. Read this handoff and the current workspace instructions. Establish a permitted, healthy canonical audit path without invoking or altering the disabled plugin contrary to the user's direction. If unavailable, report the precise blocker; do not claim persistence.\\n282 | 2. Read the full foundation architecture, original HV draft and corrections, foundation plan, relevant session-log contracts, and existing requirements/tasks.\\n283 | 3. Establish the amendment's correct location and numbering from the existing document structure. Preserve the foundation and other agents' work.\\n284 | 4. Create the documentation-only amendment with the scope and rigor in sections 5-7. Do not create an implementation worktree or implementation code.\\n285 | 5. Verify diagrams, references, UC/AC coverage, interface consistency, and requirement/task mappings. Use an independent review path that does not rely on the unfinished HV pipeline.\\n286 | 6. Persist or update the requested high-priority TODO through the permitted audited route, with complete corrected plan text and verified amendment/dependency links; read it back.\\n287 | 7. Report exact files changed, validation actually performed, canonical TODO ID if saved, and any remaining blockers. Do not mark implementation complete.\\n288 | \\n289 | ## 10. Handoff verification and limitations\\n290 | \",\n              \"success\": true\n            }\n          ]\n        }\n      ],\n      \"ts\": 1792175334055\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"Index\": 514,\n    \"Message\": {\n      \"id\": \"msg_5xIkq2mr\",\n      \"role\": \"user\",\n      \"content\": [\n        {\n          \"type\": \"tool_result\",\n          \"tool_use_id\": \"call_6vvpa6zM4sGrQYdcHphST9wG\",\n          \"name\": \"run_commands\",\n          \"content\": [\n            {\n              \"query\": \"pwsh.exe -NoLogo -NoProfile -NonInteractive -Command '$ErrorActionPreference=\\\"Stop\\\"; $path=\\\"/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/docs/handoffs/hv-architecture-amendment-handoff-20261016T182551Z.md\\\"; $tex ... [command truncated: 1353 more chars; full command is in the tool call input]\",\n              \"result\": \"[Command exited with code 1]\\nContent checks passed: 16\\nLine count: 296\\nBytes: 23444\\n\\nAlgorithm : SHA256\\nHash      : 76260C1C5B666A783250F418C5759D4BD7A4E2CC317692CC8C32BA149F728DDD\\nPath      : /home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/docs/handoffs/hv-architecture-amendment-handoff-20261016T182551Z.md\\n\\n\\n\\n[stderr]\\n\\u001b[31;1mException: \\u001b[31;1mWhitespace validation failed\\u001b[0m\\n\",\n              \"error\":