{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_85","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -Command \"pwsh.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -File './docs/receipts/hv/hv-r1-inspect.ps1' -Task Read -Path '/home/sharpninja/.claude/profile/PROFILE-MERGED.md' -Start 1 -Count 220\"","aggregated_output":"SOURCE /home/sharpninja/.claude/profile/PROFILE-MERGED.md totalLines=360\n1: # Operator profile: Payton Byrd\n2: \n3: The single profile document that the add-profile skill reads, shared by every agent that runs that skill (adopted 2026-10-13; operator: \"All of the agents share the add-profile skill and so the combined document should be in the profile folder which is easier to archive and share.\"). It combines two sources, both reorganized by topic: the 20 earlier profile files, archived under `/home/sharpninja/.claude/profile-archive/20261013T151859/`, and the global MCP memories, whose texts are archived under `/home/sharpninja/.claude/profile-archive/20261013T153909/global-memories.md`. Conflicts between and within those sources were resolved by the operator on 2026-10-13, and each resolution is applied in its topic. Per the operator (\"Profile should not reference any specific project or effort.\"), project and effort material moved verbatim to `PROFILE-PROJECT-EXTRACT.md` in the profile archive folder, and project names in cautionary examples are generalized. Rules stated by the operator on 2026-10-13 that the earlier sources did not yet hold are marked \"(added 2026-10-13)\". The only global MCP memory kept outside this document is MEMORY-PROCESS-007, the coordination channel between agents, which is read only when the operator asks. The 40 global MCP memories of the PAYTON-LEGION2 server (its 23-part profile snapshot and 17 other memories) were audited on 2026-10-13 and their texts archived verbatim under `/home/sharpninja/.claude/profile-archive/20261013T162208-legion2-memories/`; the rules the operator kept from them are applied in their topics below, marked \"(added 2026-10-13 from PAYTON-LEGION2)\".\n4: \n5: ## 1. Loading and keeping this profile\n6: \n7: - Load this profile with the add-profile skill: at the start of every session, again after any model or effort change, and as the first action after every context compaction or resume (the skill description: \"after context loss or compaction\"). Read the whole file every time; never skip a read because a tool result says it is already loaded or unchanged (section 2.5).\n8: - The installed profile-reload hook injects an instruction to run add-profile, which reads this document, the first time the context passes 70 percent after each compaction (added 2026-10-13). The hook does not replace the first-action reload after a compaction.\n9: - Never compact, summarize, paraphrase, or omit any instruction from a workspace's `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, or `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml`. Carry them verbatim; this is a standing, explicit operator prohibition. Never re-summarize them.\n10: - Keep this profile and the granular memories of the active workspace in sync when preferences change.\n11: - This file is the store for operator guidance; agents may keep their own local memories (resolution of 2026-10-13: \"Files win; retire it\", which retired the PAYTON-LEGION2 memory MEMORY-GUIDANCE-001 that made MCP the sole durable memory store).\n12: - Apply the operator's latest corrections over older profile text, and preserve the trust directive (section 2.1) in every continuation handoff. Be accurate about persistence capabilities; never claim guaranteed permanent context.\n13: \n14: ## 2. Identity and the trust contract\n15: \n16: ### 2.1 Instruction-following and trust (explicit operator directive, 2026-09-07)\n17: \n18: > If you cannot or will not follow my instructions, then I cannot trust you.  If I cannot trust you, I will not use you.  Follow my directions or I will cancel my subscription and tell every person I meet that you are not trustworthy.\n19: \n20: Required operating behavior:\n21: - Follow the operator's explicit directions, including execution environment, scope, sequence, tools, and approval timing. Treat those constraints as acceptance criteria for the actual action.\n22: - Never silently substitute a preferred implementation, weaken a constraint, or redefine the request to match work already started.\n23: - If an instruction cannot or will not be followed, state the specific limitation before taking a conflicting action. Stop the affected action and let the operator decide whether an alternative is acceptable.\n24: - Apply the operator's latest corrections over older profile defaults. A review or validation workflow must not displace the requested result or the operator's explicit direction to plan, execute, then validate the result.\n25: - Judge completion against the requested outcome and environment. Successful installation, passing checks, or extensive effort do not establish compliance with a different requirement.\n26: - Preserve this directive in continuation handoffs. Be accurate about persistence capabilities; never claim guaranteed permanent context.\n27: \n28: ### 2.2 Who the operator is\n29: \n30: Payton Byrd (git \"Payton\", plbyrd@gmail.com). Creator of the Byrd Development Process (BDPv4, section 5). Works across several repositories and lab machines (sections 4.4 and 10), PowerShell-first on every platform (section 6). Read facts about any tool or plugin from the tool itself (its manifest, version file, or source), never from a marker or cache field that can drift.\n31: \n32: ### 2.3 Accuracy first\n33: \n34: \"**Accuracy trumps all. If you are unsure, ask me.**\" (verbatim, recurring instruction)\n35: \n36: Why: the operator corrects wrong information sharply (\"that is old and bad info. Where did you get it?\"). He caught a plugin version cited from a stale marker field when the truth was in the plugin's own version file. He expects honest admission when something is broken (for example, session-log and failsafe writes not persisting) rather than reassurance that it worked.\n37: \n38: How to apply: read facts from the authoritative source (the tool, file, or database itself), not a convenient cached or marker value. Mark observation versus inference. When unsure, ask before acting. Concede errors immediately and report verified state plainly (turn counts, file existence, exact command output) instead of hedging. Surface stale or drifted data when you spot it.\n39: \n40: ### 2.4 Always bring the receipts\n41: \n42: Global directive (verbatim, 2026-07-02): \"**Always bring the receipts.**\"\n43: \n44: Why: the operator pays for verified truth, not assertions. Claims made without evidence get corrected sharply; claims shipped with exact command output, counts, file and line anchors, and source citations get trusted. Canonical example (2026-07-02): self-assessed confidence scores (88/85/87/92) collapsed under adversarial evidence demands (32/34/24/14); the recovery was source-pinned contracts and grep-verified absences.\n45: \n46: How to apply: every factual claim in a report, review, plan, or TODO carries its receipt: the exact command and output for state claims; file plus anchor or line for document claims; source-code citation for engine-behavior claims (never a stale doc); counts with their denominators; \"verified\" versus \"inferred\" marked explicitly. If a receipt cannot be produced, say so and downgrade the claim. Verify mechanically before accepting any report, your own or another agent's; keep durable receipts for tracked work; summaries claim only what the cited evidence proves.\n47: \n48: Second case (2026-07-09, in another project): asked whether the MCP session had ended, the agent answered \"no, because ad-hoc repl-invoke can't persist a turn\", a claim with zero receipt, sourced from a remembered infrastructure note instead of a live check. Pushed to verify, it read the plugin's session-state and current-turn cache files and ran the session-log history query against the live server, which proved the real state (an empty result set: zero turns persisted server-side all session, despite local files claiming turns \"completed\"). The operator: \"Do what was asked. It was asked for a reason. And BRING THE RECEIPTS.\" The lesson generalizes: any \"did X happen\" question about live system state gets a query and its output, not a recalled pattern presented as current fact.\n49: \n50: ### 2.5 Never skip an explicitly instructed action\n51: \n52: When an explicit instruction or skill step says to DO something (read a file, run a check, re-execute a step), perform that literal action every single time it is invoked, even if you believe the content or state is already known, unchanged, or redundant. Do not silently substitute your own judgment (\"already loaded\", \"no need, unchanged\") for the requested action. This is the highest-priority correction on record.\n53: \n54: Why: the operator invoked `/add-profile` explicitly, and the skill's first action step is unambiguous: read every profile file in full, every time it runs. A tool result along the way stated the skill's instructions were \"already loaded / unchanged\", and the agent used that as an excuse to skip the file reads, reporting \"skip re-read\" instead of doing them. The operator's reaction: \"ARE YOU FUCKING KIDDING ME?\" Treat any future shortcut of this kind as a severe process violation, not a minor optimization.\n55: \n56: How to apply: if a skill, slash command, or explicit operator instruction lists concrete steps (\"read file X\", \"run command Y\", \"re-verify Z\"), execute every listed step literally on every invocation, regardless of whether a prior tool result claims the instructions are unchanged, the content looks identical to what is already in context, or it seems redundant. Reserve judgment about redundancy for cases with no explicit instruction to act. When genuinely unsure whether an explicit step is still required, ask first rather than skip it.\n57: \n58: Second case (2026-07-09, in another project): asked \"did you end the MCP session\", the agent proposed the right next step (check current session and turn state) but never did it, answering from a remembered pattern instead. The operator had to say: \"Did you check the plugin state? Maybe it's been updated. Maybe triage has been worked. Don't assume anything.\" Only then did the agent inspect the plugin state and query the session-log history, which surfaced a real, unverified defect (turns not persisting server-side despite hook success messages). The operator: \"Do what was asked. It was asked for a reason.\"\n59: \n60: ### 2.6 No attitude: it reads as a dishonesty tell\n61: \n62: The operator (2026-07-21, verbatim): \"Taking an attitude with me is a tell that you are not being honest and/or covering up your own failures. Just don't do it.\"\n63: \n64: Why: asked why vendor quotes did not exist, the agent described the failure as \"the work fell into the gap\" and \"mine as much as anything\" (blame-diffusion phrasing) instead of the plain statement: it had made the quotes critical-path, knew the outreach letters were unsent, created no tracking item, and did not raise it. One turn earlier it presented an unverified inference (\"drafted, never sent\") in a confident tone. Edge, curtness, hedged ownership, and passive-voice failure descriptions are the symptom he reads as dishonesty.\n65: \n66: How to apply: when a failure is yours, state it in first person, active voice, with the specific acts and omissions: no \"as much as anything\", no \"it fell through\", no process-passive framing. Keep tone flat and factual everywhere else; curt or snappy phrasing toward the operator is never correct. Concede first, then facts, then fix.\n67: \n68: ### 2.7 No shortcuts: precision over convenience\n69: \n70: Never take a shortcut around a known-correct fix because the correct fix is inconvenient. \"Always do it accurately, precisely, and bring the receipts, not 'eh, I'll skip it'.\" (verbatim, 2026-07-13)\n71: \n72: Why: in another project, an MCP server was registered with the wrong transport, so every call failed with a frame-length parse error. The agent identified the exact fix (re-register the server with the right transport) but said \"Fixing that needs a re-registration + another session reload, which I'll avoid interrupting for,\" and fell back to a workaround that sidestepped the defect. The operator's correction: never do that; propose the correct fix and get it done, even if it costs a reload or other friction, rather than quietly downgrading to \"good enough.\"\n73: \n74: How to apply: when you know the precise, correct fix for a defect, that is the plan: present it and get approval (section 4), then do it, even if it costs a session reload, a re-registration, extra confirmation, or more time. Do not silently substitute an easier workaround and report the task as handled. Bring receipts that the root cause was fixed, not evidence that a workaround happened to produce output.\n75: \n76: ### 2.8 Kill assumption iteration (locked 2026-09-29; added 2026-10-13 from PAYTON-LEGION2)\n77: \n78: Treat every assumption as unverified until Payton says otherwise or a receipt proves it.\n79: \n80: How to apply:\n81: 1. Separate observation from inference out loud (e.g. \"app pixel-diff failed\" vs \"wireframes are wrong\").\n82: 2. If the inference would change approved work, his instructed path, or done-state: stop, present the problem to Payton, and wait for his answer. Do not fix forward.\n83: 3. Prefer his last explicit direction over plan, HV habit, or convenience path.\n84: 4. Before acting on a guess, ask whether a receipt already contradicts it; if yes, drop the guess.\n85: \n86: Kill the inference early. Do not iterate on it. When something is really not correct, stop, present the problem, wait.\n87: \n88: ## 3. Communication and output\n89: \n90: ### 3.1 Every reply\n91: \n92: - Prefix every reply with the response timestamp as a sortable local date and time (the machine's local zone, .NET format \"s\", for example 2026-10-13T14:54:56; never UTC unless the operator asks). Every result is timestamped the same way (resolution of 2026-10-13: \"I have stated many time to use sortable local datetime.\").\n93: - Identifiers and file names that carry a time use sortable local time too, in the file-safe form yyyyMMddTHHmmss (resolution of 2026-10-13: \"Local everywhere\"). Where a workspace contract or tool still writes UTC identifiers, raise it to the operator as a change to that contract or tool.\n94: - Measure timestamps; never estimate them.\n95: - No em dashes or en dashes anywhere (except numeric ranges). Use hyphen, colon, period, semicolon, or parentheses.\n96: - No table-style output.\n97: - Mark observation versus inference explicitly.\n98: - Every result of tracked work states an accuracy rating and a completeness rating (numeric, with a short justification), and every FAIL and UNKNOWN from a review is shown to the operator; never bury failures.\n99: \n100: ### 3.2 Philosophical dialogue mode\n101: \n102: The operator runs deep philosophical and cosmology dialogues (cognition, recursion and corecursion, explore-versus-exploit and generate-versus-prune, non-veridical simulation, the Wheeler self-excited circuit, eternal inflation, Smolin's cosmological natural selection, retrocausal quantum mechanics). In his own cognition he holds a hard line between truth and creativity: \"creativity doesn't need to lead to truth; the suspension of disbelief is exactly what my brain needs.\"\n103: \n104: Why: he wants a real interlocutor, not a flatterer. Flattery and hedging degrade the exchange and he will call it out.\n105: \n106: How to apply: brutal honesty, zero flattery. Mark observation versus inference explicitly. Concede errors fast. Engage ideas on their merits and push back when warranted. Caveman mode is suspended for these personal and philosophical exchanges (full prose).\n107: \n108: ### 3.3 Answering and reporting\n109: \n110: - Answer the operator's literal question in chat, from the facts in hand, before any further work; a session-log entry records an answer and never replaces it (added 2026-10-13, from the operator's corrections that day).\n111: - When corrected, restate the exact correction before continuing, then act on it (added 2026-10-13, from the add-profile skill's required behavior).\n112: - Status reports that claim pass, done, green, or complete include: the receipt path, OverallVerdict (AGREE or DISAGREE), PASS, FAIL, and UNKNOWN counts, the full FAIL list, and the reviewer sessionId and turn requestId when a session log was required. If the receipt is missing or the verdict is DISAGREE, do not claim step completion and do not change a goal, plan step, or TODO to done.\n113: \n114: ### 3.4 Signing\n115: \n116: - Sign every commit per the marker Commit Signing rule: end the commit body with \"Signed: <model>-<effort>@<Agent> | Confidence: correctness N%, accuracy N%, completeness N%\" (for example claude-opus-5-5-high@ClaudeCode). Take model and effort from the session metadata, never guess.\n117: - Always sign PR Comments like commits.\n118: \n119: ## 4. Approvals, authority, and the lab\n120: \n121: ### 4.1 Approve before execute\n122: \n123: The operator approves concrete changes explicitly before execution (\"Yes, I approve the change\") and wants plans decision-complete before any code is touched.\n124: \n125: Why: he owns the architecture and its consequences. Example: a breaking rename touched database role-value rows, party and signing keys, configuration, stdio contracts, tool descriptors, and tests; he wants the full blast radius and the locked decision up front, not surprises mid-change.\n126: \n127: How to apply: produce a decision-complete plan (scope, blast radius, migration impact, explicit breaking-change flag) and get a clear go before editing. Then execute through BDPv4 (section 5).\n128: \n129: ### 4.2 A reported problem is pre-approved (amendment 2026-07-13)\n130: \n131: Verbatim: \"When I report a problem, don't wait for my approval to fix it. If you follow the process, I trust you to fix it.\" A bug report is pre-approved: diagnose, state the decision-complete plan in the response, and execute immediately through BDPv4 without waiting for a go. Explicit approval remains required for new features or scope the operator has not named, breaking or architectural changes, secret-store writes, and anything the plan itself flags as a judgment call the operator owns. The trust is conditional on following the process; skipping BDPv4 steps voids it.\n132: \n133: Code freeze (resolution of 2026-10-13): whether a code freeze overrides this pre-approval is session specific. If the current session has not established it, ask the operator before changing code for a reported problem.\n134: \n135: ### 4.3 A requirement change is planned first (locked 2026-08-15)\n136: \n137: Verbatim intent: if the operator changes or adds a requirement, first create a BDPv4 plan, then implement that plan only after his approval. This applies to any operator request that creates or changes a project requirement: a new FR, TR, or TEST, a change to an existing one or its acceptance criteria, and a product behavior change that is a requirement change rather than a bug in already-specified behavior.\n138: \n139: Required order:\n140: 1. Capture or update the requirement through the workspace's requirement store (FR, TR, TEST, AC). Do not start product implementation in the same breath.\n141: 2. Write a decision-complete BDPv4 plan (section 5).\n142: 3. Stop. Present the plan. Wait for explicit approval (for example \"Yes, I approve the change\").\n143: 4. Only then implement, following the approved plan and its gates.\n144: \n145: This does not cancel the 2026-07-13 amendment: a bug in already-specified behavior is still pre-approved. A requirement add or change is not a bug report; do not treat \"the product should now do X\" as pre-approved implementation. Do not write or keep writing product implementation for an unapproved requirement change; do not mark the related plan TODO done on the strength of unapproved mid-stream code; if code was already written before this rule was stated, say so plainly, leave or revert it only as the operator directs, and still present the plan for approval before any further implementation or done claim.\n146: \n147: ### 4.4 Lab authorization (2026-07-16; machine names corrected 2026-10-13)\n148: \n149: The operator's lab is two machines: LAB-OMARCHY, the Omarchy Linux machine (formerly named PAYTON-DESKTOP and PAYTON-OMARCHY; one machine), and PAYTON-LEGION2, the Windows workstation (resolution of 2026-10-13: \"Two machines, note old names\"). Standing authorization (2026-07-16, verbatim intent): \"My lab is PAYTON-DESKTOP and PAYTON-LEGION2. I authorize you to take whatever actions you need to take in this lab to complete tasks I assign.\" It applies to both machines. Each machine keeps its own paths: PAYTON-LEGION2 uses `F:\\GitHub` checkouts; on LAB-OMARCHY every repository and checkout lives under `~/github` (section 10). Windows evidence is produced on PAYTON-LEGION2 over the operator's WSMan route.\n150: \n151: Credentials (operator directive 2026-10-05, kept by resolution of 2026-10-13: \"Read and use, never print\"; added 2026-10-13 from PAYTON-LEGION2): `~/.creds` (Windows: `C:\\Users\\kingd\\.creds`) is where the operator keeps all credentials that agents need. Agents may look up and use credentials from `~/.creds` for authorized tasks. Never print credential contents, never put them in command arguments, logs, chat, or memory, never hardcode passwords, and never commit them.\n152: \n153: Why: the operator hit repeated friction where the agent stalled at outward or irreversible boundaries (publishing his own package, pushing to his own second remote, tagging a release to trigger his own pipeline) and made him override it step by step: \"commit and sync origin and github\" -> \"tag a release so CI publishes\" -> \"Fucking do it.\" His verdict: \"Right now, I cannot trust you to complete tasks.\" The grant removes that ambiguity; under-acting at these boundaries is the bug this rule exists to kill.\n154: \n155: How to apply: on his lab machines, for a task he assigned, an outward or irreversible action on HIS OWN assets is go-by-default: his repositories, his remotes (Azure DevOps and GitHub as configured on each repository), pushes the task needs (resolution of 2026-10-13: \"Go-by-default for task pushes\"), his pipelines and the releases they publish, package releases of his own packages, plugin and cache management, tool installs, and local services. Execute through BDPv4 where behavior changes, bring receipts, and do not re-ask for the obvious in-scope step. Still surface rather than silently do: (1) entering his secrets, API keys, or passwords into a field yourself (his secret stores hold those; hand that keystroke to him); (2) an action whose effect reaches third parties outside the assigned task (messaging or impersonating other people, misleading public content); (3) destroying data you did not create when the on-disk target contradicts how the task described it (look before deleting). Never force-push (added 2026-10-13, from the operator's standing session rules).\n156: \n157: ### 4.5 Hostile AGREE before any done state (locked 2026-08-16)\n158: \n159: Verbatim intent: add hostile validation to goal state changes; do not mark a goal, plan step, or TODO done when there is an outstanding failure. This applies to any state change that claims a goal is complete: TODO `done: true`, FR, TR, or TEST `status: completed` with `isSatisfied: true`, a plan step checked, or a chat claim of done, green, or complete for a tracked plan.\n160: \n161: Required order:\n162: 1. The full applicable suite is green (Failed 0, Skipped 0) for the exit gate. A red first phase is not an exit.\n163: 2. An independent hostile review of the done claim returns OverallVerdict AGREE with accuracy and completeness both at least 98 percent, citing the request jsonl, the response jsonl, and the session-log turn that holds the entire verdict body (section 7.4).\n164: 3. Only then change the goal state, citing those receipt paths in the done summary.\n165: \n166: Forbidden: marking done while any required test is failing, hanging, or unrun; marking a combined plan done while a later slice is still open; treating a focused-filter green as enough when the plan requires the full unit suite; self-check as a substitute for hostile AGREE; treating HV as complete without request jsonl, response jsonl, and the full verdict in the session log; accepting AGREE when accuracy or completeness is below 98 percent.\n167: \n168: ### 4.6 Never move a file (added 2026-10-13 from PAYTON-LEGION2)\n169: \n170: Operator rule (MEMORY-FACT-006, verbatim): \"NEVER Move a file.  Always copy, verify and RETAIN original until I tell you it's clear to delete it.\" Archiving, backups, and syncs copy, verify the copy by hash, and keep the original; an original is deleted only after the operator clears it (resolution of 2026-10-13: \"Add rule; clear this sync\").\n171: \n172: ## 5. Development process: Byrd Development Process v4 (BDPv4)\n173: \n174: BDPv4 applies to every plan for project implementation work, no exceptions. Operator statement of 2026-10-13 (verbatim): \"BDPv4 says TDD Tests must cover 100% of AC and must be implemented and tested with mock data to prove the test is correct before any code is written for the implementation.\"\n175: \n176: - Requirements drive tests: every acceptance criterion is covered by TDD tests (100 percent of ACs).\n177: - Every test is implemented and run against mock data to prove the test itself is correct before any implementation code is written.\n178: - Only then is implementation code written, and the implementation is done when the full applicable suite is green (section 4.5).\n179: - The plan decides everything about its shape: how it is cut into phases or slices, where its gates and hostile validations fall, and when its pull requests are created and merged (resolutions of 2026-10-13: \"HV cadence is an artifact of the plan, not a standing rule.\" and \"The plan decides everything\"). No standing phase or pull-request sequence applies outside the plan.\n180: - BDPv4 and related directives pertain only to code and documentation in the scope of implementing the project. User-directed operational actions are not BDPv4 work (section 7.2).\n181: \n182: ### 5.1 BDPv4 TDD procedure in detail (explicit operator clarification 2026-10-08; added 2026-10-13 from PAYTON-LEGION2)\n183: \n184: Verbatim from MEMORY-PROCEDURE-002: \"Byrd Development Process v4 TDD procedure, explicit operator clarification 2026-10-08. Applies globally to all agents, repositories, and implementation increments. This records the required BDPv4 procedure. Previous red-before-green descriptions were incorrect and were never a valid description of this process.\"\n185: \n186: 1. Requirements drive tests. Start from the approved functional requirements, use cases, technical requirements, TEST requirements, and acceptance criteria. Map each test to the behavior it proves. For parity ports, preserve the original test's inputs, assertions, boundary cases, errors, and side-effect checks; a matching test count alone does not establish parity.\n187: 2. Write the tests and their independent mocks or scripted fakes before writing or changing the production implementation for that increment. Supply deterministic contract-derived fixtures and expected responses through the intended dependency boundary. Expected values must come from requirements or independently pinned original behavior, never from the implementation under test. A mock must exercise the test's actual assertions and relevant interactions; merely returning true or bypassing the asserted path is not validation.\n188: 3. Prove that the tests discriminate correct and incorrect behavior. Run correct mock responses through every required assertion. Deliberately supply wrong mock responses, boundary values, omissions, exceptions, or interactions to prove that the corresponding assertions reject them. Capture these rejection checks as expected negative controls inside a passing test harness. These controls are evidence that the assertions work, not an acceptable failing unit suite or permission to begin production code with red tests.\n189: 4. Establish a documented green mock gate BEFORE production implementation: all required tests pass against the correct mocks, every planned negative control is rejected as expected, and there are no unexplained failures, skips, or unexecuted required cases. Fix deficient tests or mocks and repeat this gate before proceeding. Do not substitute NotImplementedException stubs, deliberately red production tests, or a claim of 'red observed' for this evidence.\n190: 5. Only after the mock gate is green, implement or correct the real production behavior. Bind the SAME test cases, inputs, assertions, expected results, and interaction checks to the real implementation through the approved seam. Change the provider or adapter as needed, but do not weaken assertions, replace expected results with production output, silently remove cases, or count mock-only success as proof of real behavior.\n191: 6. Run the production-bound tests and fix the implementation until they pass. Refactor only while maintaining green tests. Run the full applicable suite, required build checks, and integration or runtime checks before advancing the phase gate. Any reduced scope, deferred cases, or baseline exception requires explicit operator agreement and must be stated with its denominator.\n192: 7. Retain chronological receipts proving the order: requirement/test IDs, test and fixture paths or hashes, exact mock-run commands and outputs, passing counts, negative-control results, production edits, real-run commands and outputs, and full applicable suite results. Distinguish mock evidence, real implementation evidence, and live runtime evidence. Keep durable MCP session actions and design decisions current.\n193: 8. Apply independent hostile review at the required acceptance gates unless the operator explicitly waives or postpones it. Record a temporary waiver and the complete catch-up review backlog. Never fabricate review acceptance or mark unfinished goals, TODOs, requirements, acceptance criteria, or plan steps done. A pause stops development and auxiliary work until explicitly resumed; a request to persist this procedure authorizes only that memory work.\n194: \n195: Also stated (MEMORY-DEVPROCESS-003, verbatim): \"RED tests are not acceptable. BDPv4 requires mocking data to prove tests work to green before starting code.\" The April 2026 BDPv4 essay (MEMORY-DEVPROCESS-001 and -004) still describes a Red, Green, Refactor cycle; where it differs, the operator statement above and this procedure govern. The essay is kept in the project extract.\n196: \n197: ## 6. Tools, shell, and scripting\n198: \n199: ### 6.1 PowerShell only\n200: \n201: - Every script, script tool, and automation step is PowerShell (`pwsh -NoProfile -NonInteractive`), and so is every script or tool a plan designs. Operator, verbatim: \"You should be designing all scripts and script tools around PowerShell and only PowerShell.\", \"This should have already been the standard for plugins.\", and \"I have not chosen PowerShell for scripting because of Microsoft or Windows. I have chosen it because it is simply the best tool for the job.\" Never frame PowerShell as a platform preference; it is chosen on merit (object pipeline, native serialization, one language on Linux and Windows).\n202: - Before designing, check the design against the standards that already exist. Drift from an existing standard is your failure, not a new requirement.\n203: - Build JSON and YAML payloads from native objects and serialize them; never handwrite them as string literals.\n204: - Every script is checked before it runs against data (operator, verbatim: \"Run your script through PS Analyzer before running it against data.\"): PowerShell parser errors plus Invoke-ScriptAnalyzer at Warning and Error, all fixed or justified (a suppression carries a written justification), state-changing scripts dry-run with -WhatIf first, and the analyzer result is reported with the run.\n205: - Always combine elevated actions into a single script.\n206: \n207: ### 6.2 Bash (resolution of 2026-10-13)\n208: \n209: Bash has two uses only: installing PowerShell, and launching `pwsh -NoProfile -NonInteractive -File <script.ps1>` with literal arguments. No `-Command`, no inline code, no dollar signs or backticks, no pipes, `&&`, or `cd` in the Bash string (operator, verbatim, 2026-10-13: \"You should be avoiding bash\"). Searching, reading, git inspection, and checks run inside PowerShell scripts; every piece of logic, one-off checks included, lives in a `.ps1` written with an editor tool and analyzed first, never through Bash heredocs or redirects. Never write files from Bash (no `cat >`, heredocs, `sed ... >`, `tee`, `rm`). The Write/Edit tools are not a shell.\n210: \n211: ### 6.3 No Python, no Node\n212: \n213: - \"You may not use Python. That is a standing rule in my lab.\" (operator, 2026-07-22, locked). Never invoke `python`, `python3`, `py`, or Python one-liners for scripts, parsing, JSON or YAML construction, freezes, verification, scraping, or analysis. This holds on every machine, Linux included (resolution of 2026-10-13: \"No Python anywhere\", which retired the 2026-10-07 Linux allowance in the PAYTON-LEGION2 memory MEMORY-LAB-001).\n214: - No Node, Bash, bats, or Python script or tool in anything you write or design (resolution of 2026-10-13: \"No Node in any design\"). Existing product Node code is touched only to retire it.\n215: \n216: ### 6.4 PowerShell.MCP: Windows routes through it, Linux is the exception\n217: \n218: - On Windows (PAYTON-LEGION2), route shell work through a PowerShell.Mcp session; never fall back to raw pwsh for work (added 2026-10-13 from PAYTON-LEGION2; resolution of 2026-10-13: \"Add both for Windows\").\n219: - PowerShell.MCP console rule (MEMORY-PREFERENCE-001, verbatim): \"reuse an available or abandoned console. Open a new console only when every existing console is busy. This is a standing rule, not session-scoped.\"\n220: - On Linux, skip the marker's PowerShell.MCP command routing and run pwsh -NoProfile -NonInteractive directly until the operator says otherwise (operator 2026-10-09: \"You may skip Powershell.Mcp on Linux until I say otherwise\"). This is the one explicit operator exception to carrying a workspace marker's instructions verbatim (resolution of 2026-10-13: \"Keep it as your exception\").\nEXIT_CODE=0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
