ext agent must establish a genuinely permitted, functioning audit/persistence route before resuming canonical work. Do not silently reinterpret the handoff request as permission to enable the disabled plugin or bypass the policy.\n68: \n69: ## 4. Authoritative source locations and provenance\n70: \n71: ### Foundation architecture: primary design source\n72: \n73: `/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/.claude/worktrees/session-20261009/docs/architecture/SessionTurn-Review-AgentContext-Architecture.md`\n74: \n75: This is the architecture the user specified as the foundation. Build on it and incorporate required session-log schemas and contracts from `session-20261009`. Do not overwrite or replace the foundation with an unrelated HV design.\n76: \n77: Its introduction states that architecture is authoritative and the plan derives from it. It defines a unit of work as exactly one CQRS command or query and handler, distinguishes units from policies and orchestration processes, requires complete activity and sequence diagrams, and defines shared components once for reuse. It includes grouped entity diagrams and explicit use cases. Changes after plan approval require renewed hostile validation under that document's governance.\n78: \n79: ### Supporting plan and prior handoff\n80: \n81: - `/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/docs/plans/PLAN-SESSIONTURN-SESSIONREVIEW-001.md`\n82: - `/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/docs/handoffs/sessionturn-review-plan-20261009T022141Z.md`\n83: \n84: The architecture introduction references the plan identifier `PLAN-SESSIONTURNREVIEW-001`, while the plan filename contains `PLAN-SESSIONTURN-SESSIONREVIEW-001`. Verify the canonical task identity and references; do not assume these strings are interchangeable or allocate a duplicate.\n85: \n86: ### Recovered HV draft and subsequent corrections\n87: \n88: `/home/sharpninja/.cline/data/sessions/1792165626722_56f98/1792165626722_56f98.messages.json`\n89: \n90: The prior context summary locates the original draft in message 213. Inspect all text blocks rather than assuming `content[0]`; establish the file's actual message indexing before extracting. Later user messages contain binding corrections. This agent confirmed the file exists but did not re-extract the original draft during this handoff.\n91: \n92: ### Repository policy and tracking references\n93: \n94: - `/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/AGENTS.md`\n95: - `/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml`\n96: - `/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/.github/copilot-instructions.md`\n97: - `/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/docs/Development-Process-draft-v4.md`\n98: - `/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/docs/Project/Requirements-Matrix.md`\n99: - `/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/docs/Project/Functional-Requirements.md`\n100: - `/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/docs/Project/Technical-Requirements.md`\n101: - `/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/docs/Project/Testing-Requirements.md`\n102: - `/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/docs/Project/TR-per-FR-Mapping.md`\n103: - `/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/docs/context/todo-schema.md`\n104: - `/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/docs/context/session-log-schema.md`\n105: - `/home/sharpninja/github/McpServer/docs/context/module-bootstrap.md`\n106: \n107: The earlier context noted a targeted read of technical requirements lines 1190-1228. Recheck current contents rather than treating those line numbers as immutable.\n108: \n109: Protect `/home/sharpninja/.mcpServer/failsafe/cline-v2/` and its session snapshots. Do not clear it or replay it as part of this task. Never copy marker API keys or other credentials into the amendment, handoff, or TODO.\n110: \n111: ## 5. Settled HV requirements: preserve all of these\n112: \n113: ### Foundation and authorization\n114: \n115: 1. A future implementation worktree must be created from `develop`, not from the foundation worktree. Do not create that implementation worktree merely to write the amendment.\n116: 2. Build on the specified foundation architecture and incorporate the necessary session-log schema/contracts from `session-20261009`.\n117: 3. Checking integration requirements and validating links are agent preparation, not unresolved user decisions.\n118: 4. Current authorization covers documentation and plan persistence only. Saving either must not be presented as implementing or completing HV.\n119: \n120: ### Independence, reviewers, and scheduling\n121: \n122: 5. Self-review grading is independent of HV. Its presence, absence, grade, and status must not influence HV admission, execution, or verdict.\n123: 6. Configure five reviewers: Cline, Copilot, Claude, Codex, and Grok.\n124: 7. Select eligible reviewers round-robin. Normalize identities and aliases and exclude the submitter and every alias of the submitter.\n125: 8. Not every request requires all five reviewers. Do not silently turn the configurable reviewer pool into a mandatory five-reviewer quorum.\n126: 9. NONCE means checking an agent's availability and ability to execute HV. It is not merely a liveness ping or a repetition of the server health nonce handshake.\n127: 10. Scheduling and work ownership must survive restarts: durable queue, leases, fencing, and restart-safe round-robin state.\n128: \n129: ### Scope and evaluation\n130: \n131: 11. Support individual turns, inclusive turn ranges, and session snapshots captured at admission time.\n132: 12. Produce per-turn assessments and a separate holistic evaluation. The holistic evaluation is not a mathematical aggregation of the turn scores.\n133: 13. Preserve the complete accepted requests and complete response JSONL logs without silent truncation.\n134: \n135: ### Evidence, retention, and retrieval\n136: \n137: 14. Store result logs GZIP-compressed at rest as `.jsonl.gz`, losslessly.\n138: 15. Validate compression/decompression round trips and reject corrupt or incomplete evidence before finalization.\n139: 16. Support compressed retrieval without permanent uncompressed duplicates and preserve live streaming.\n140: 17. Retain evidence until explicitly told to remove it. No automatic expiry or retention-window cleanup is allowed.\n141: \n142: ### Audit, access, finalization, and completion\n143: \n144: 18. There are no access roles. Do not introduce HV-specific role-based gates. Actor names in use cases describe participants, not authorization roles.\n145: 19. Required canonical database auditing is mandatory. Audit failure is a hard stop; local or failsafe logging is not a substitute.\n146: 20. Finalization is atomic. Final results and reviewed turns are immutable; remediation creates new work rather than rewriting reviewed history.\n147: 21. HV verdicts must never automatically complete TODOs.\n148: 22. Link verification and plan persistence are agent responsibilities. Do not return them as outstanding user questions.\n149: \n150: ## 6. Consolidated delivery outline\n151: \n152: Required dependency order:\n153: \n154: **H0 -> H1 -> H2 -> H3 -> H7 -> H4 -> H5/H6 -> independent validation**\n155: \n156: ### H0: Foundation and integration baseline\n157: \n158: - Inspect the foundation architecture, required session-log changes, current code, and existing task/requirement mappings.\n159: - Reconcile interfaces, schemas, boundaries, and requirement ownership; verify audit readiness.\n160: - Persist the architecture-derived plan and high-priority TODO through the permitted canonical mechanism.\n161: - Do not overwrite the foundation plan or create duplicate tasks.\n162: \n163: ### H1: Reviewer configuration and readiness\n164: \n165: - Define reviewer configuration, identity normalization and alias exclusion, and eligibility.\n166: - Define NONCE readiness checks and execution adapters for Cline, Copilot, Claude, Codex, and Grok.\n167: \n168: ### H2: Admission and scheduling\n169: \n170: - Define immutable admission snapshots for individual turns, inclusive ranges, and session selections.\n171: - Define durable queueing, leases, fencing, and restart-safe round-robin scheduling.\n172: \n173: ### H3: Execution lifecycle\n174: \n175: - Define execution lifecycle, complete output capture, cancellation, and timeout handling.\n176: - Capture receipts and provenance; reject stale-worker actions and stale-fence writes.\n177: \n178: ### H7: Evidence and finalization, before external result surfaces\n179: \n180: - Require complete per-turn assessments and a separate holistic evaluation.\n181: - Define GZIP-at-rest storage and round-trip/completeness validation.\n182: - Define canonical grading contracts without making self-review a prerequisite or verdict input.\n183: - Define atomic immutable finalization and reliable notifications.\n184: \n185: ### H4: REST surfaces\n186: \n187: - Define submission, queries, results/output retrieval, cancellation, and reviewer status.\n188: - Report unknown ETA honestly rather than inventing estimates.\n189: \n190: ### H5: Client and tooling parity\n191: \n192: - Define typed client, REPL, Director, MCP/helper parity, and documentation.\n193: - Follow the foundation's PowerShell-only direction for scripts and script tools.\n194: - Documenting future MCP interfaces does not authorize invoking the currently disabled plugin.\n195: \n196: ### H6: Operations and recovery\n197: \n198: - Define reconnectable SSE, incidents, recovery, NONCE checks, and canonical audit operations.\n199: \n200: ### Validation and completion gate\n201: \n202: - Follow: failing behavioral test -> mocks -> implementation/refactor -> all required tests and applicable integration/parity checks -> independent verification.\n203: - The complete required unit suite covers the current iteration and previous iterations.\n204: - Zero failures, zero skips, and no omitted required tests. Deferred work belongs in TODO/requirements state, not skipped placeholders.\n205: - Stop implementation slices at `Validating`; the coordinator decides completion.\n206: - Do not prematurely use the new HV pipeline to validate itself.\n207: - These are future delivery requirements, not claims that implementation or validation has occurred.\n208: \n209: ## 7. Architecture-amendment completion brief\n210: \n211: This section is a coverage checklist for the next agent, not an already-approved concrete interface design. Derive actual names, fields, endpoints, errors, IDs, and transaction boundaries from the foundation and verified repository contracts. Do not present invented APIs as existing APIs.\n212: \n213: ### Descriptive architecture and decisions\n214: \n215: - State amendment scope, status, relationship to the foundation, and precedence for any explicitly amended clause.\n216: - Describe problem domains, component ownership, data ownership, invariants, trust boundaries, lifecycle, and failure/recovery behavior.\n217: - Explain the rationale and alternatives for design choices. Separate settled user requirements from proposed design resolutions.\n218: - Reconcile self-review independence with any existing grading, review, completion-gate, or agent-context dependencies.\n219: - Explain how atomic database finalization relates to externally stored compressed evidence and reliable notifications. Identify crash windows and recovery behavior rather than assuming a single transaction covers unrelated storage systems.\n220: - Define snapshot membership and reviewed-turn immutability precisely, including remediation and concurrent mutation handling.\n221: \n222: ### Models, interfaces, and unit-of-work catalog\n223: \n224: - Inventory every model and every CQRS command/query and its handler; distinguish handlers from policies and orchestration processes.\n225: - Define every request, response, validation rule, error contract, and endpoint in scope, including internal units needed by workers and recovery.\n226: - Cover reviewer configuration/readiness, identity normalization, submission/admission, scheduling/lease ownership, execution/capture, cancellation/timeouts, evidence verification, grading, finalization, result retrieval, streaming/reconnect, notifications, and audit operations.\n227: - For each interface, specify workspace routing, required/optional fields, invariants, version/concurrency checks, idempotency, retry semantics, cancellation, and terminal/nonterminal results where applicable.\n228: - Specify storage/provenance references and completeness checks for accepted requests, JSONL output, per-turn assessments, holistic evaluation, and execution receipts.\n229: - Keep public REST, typed client, REPL, Director, and MCP/helper contracts consistent. Define exact error mappings after inspecting existing patterns.\n230: - Define unknown ETA behavior explicitly. Do not fabricate endpoint availability or deployment status.\nEXIT_CODE=0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
