:     \"testId\": \"TEST-MCP-HVPIPE-005\"\n51:   },\n52:   {\n53:     \"createdAt\": \"2026-10-17T14:25:20.9716255+00:00\",\n54:     \"frId\": \"FR-MCP-HVPIPE-006\",\n55:     \"trId\": \"TR-MCP-HVPIPE-006\"\n56:   },\n57:   {\n58:     \"createdAt\": \"2026-10-17T14:25:20.9717466+00:00\",\n59:     \"frId\": \"FR-MCP-HVPIPE-006\",\n60:     \"testId\": \"TEST-MCP-HVPIPE-006\"\nEXIT_CODE=0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_103","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -Command \"pwsh.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -File './docs/receipts/hv/hv-r1-inspect.ps1' -Task Native -CommandName rg -ArgumentText 'ModelGrader|HvRequestMax|ConvertHvReceipt|hostileReview|FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005|Astra|grade-response|hv-request:::docs/architecture/SessionTurn-Review-AgentContext-Architecture.md:::-n'\"","aggregated_output":"83:- Errors: every REST error of a unit this plan adds or changes is the `ClassifiedError` problem+json body `{ \"type\", \"title\", \"status\", \"detail\", \"code\", \"message\", \"retryable\", \"details\" }` emitted through `McpErrorClassifier` (`SessionLogController.cs` and the agent-context controller) from the handler's `ClassifiedErrorException` (section 3.5.2); `code` carries the unit's error code (for example `review_status_conflict`, `agent_context_budget_exceeded`), the HTTP status is the one stated per unit (400 validation, 403 not allowed, 404 not found, 409 conflict or disabled, 422 semantic refusal), and MCP tools return `{ code, error, message, retryable, details }`. A 409 `conflict` carries `retryable: true` when SerializableWrite or key allocation exhausted its retries (four attempts; only the Phase A minter states its own bound, for a collision of its random request id only, six attempts and 500 `mint_collision_exhausted`, while its transient conflicts keep the four attempts and the 409, section 3.5.2), which is also the answer when a deadlock or a busy store outlasts those attempts, and `retryable: false` when an idempotency key is reused with other inputs; the plugin treats a retryable 409 as an outage and keeps its failsafe record (a review excepted: reviews keep their no-failsafe rule, section 3.1); a busy store outside SerializableWrite keeps today's 503 `persistence_error` with `retryable: true` (`src/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp/Services/McpErrorClassifier.cs:129-139` at `a8d3934b`), and unreachable storage keeps its answer (section 3.1, \"Deadlock and busy outcomes\"). A workspace that does not resolve is 400 `validation_error` with `details.field` `workspacePath` on every surface, the status TR-MCP-MT-002 gives an unregistered workspace (WorkspaceResolutionBehavior, section 3.5.1), and every sequence draws that refusal after the dispatch. On REST, `WorkspaceResolutionMiddleware` still answers a missing or unknown `X-Workspace-Path` before any controller with today's `{ error }` bodies (400 for an unknown path and 404 for a workspace-required route with none, `src/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp/Middleware/WorkspaceResolutionMiddleware.cs:106-111,171-177` at `a8d3934b`). Those middleware bodies, and every existing body a unit keeps as today (stated per unit, for example the dialog route's `{ error }` 400 and the \"Invalid turn body.\" problem), are outside this plan's error rule: they stay as they are on develop, and FR-MCP-TRIAGEERR-001's acceptance criterion of a machine-readable envelope on every REST failure is recorded as deliberately left open for them, as it is on develop. On success, `sessionlog_record_review` returns `{ success: true, review }` with the review record (`SessionLogTurnReviewDto`), each `agent_context_*` tool returns its unit's result DTO as camelCase JSON, and every existing tool keeps its shape (sections 4.2 and 5.2); MCP carries no HTTP status, so a status a diagram shows is the REST status, and an MCP caller tells a created record from an existing one only where the result says so (`created` on a relationship). The completion gate is the one exception to the code rule: its `code` is `validation_error` and its message starts with the token `session_turn_completion_gate` followed by the missing items in the order requestClass, agentReview, hostileReview (FR-MCP-SESSIONCLASS-002-AC006); the diagrams name that refusal by its token. Existing routes whose behavior this plan does not change keep today's error bodies (for example the `{ error }` 400 of the dialog route and the \"Invalid session identifier.\" problem of the open and title routes), stated per unit, and a body-binding failure keeps today's validation problem with `errors` keyed by JSON path (section 3.4, Errors, which reconciles FR-SUPPORT-012). The REPL error envelope is `type: error` with `code`, `message`, and `retryable`.\n98:- Unsupported-session failsafe (operator, 2026-10-10: \"If QBrain is truly not available, use the failsafe already in the plugin.\" and, choosing between a failsafe capture and a warn-only Stop, \"1\"): when a host session fails closed under D6 (`unsupportedSince`), the model's session-log writes for a turn that was already open at that moment go to the plugin's existing failsafe instead of being refused (reviews excepted: they keep their no-failsafe rule and are recorded after the drain through the original pair), so the Stop gate's remedy stays accepted; the captured records drain at the next SessionStart through the trusted recovery flow with the turn's original pair (C5); every mint and every write for any other turn stays refused (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001).\n99:- Clear-session closing rule (operator, 2026-10-10, choosing option A over leaving blocked and withdrawn turns open: \"A\"): clear-session completes each turn that meets the completion policy, fails each withdrawn turn (reason \"withdrawn before delivery\") and each turn the model marks blocked (with its blocker reason; `failed` requires `requestClass` only, FR-MCP-SESSIONCLASS-002-AC002), and leaves every other turn in progress, reported by requestId with its exact missing items, never fabricating reviews (TR-MCP-CLEARSESSION-001-AC001). Definition (catalog cross-reference, not a change to the decision): \"marked blocked\" means the turn's `blockers` section (the existing decision-list field, FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-006-AC001) holds at least one nonblank model-authored entry, read by the clear-session coordinator for that turn by its original (`sessionId`, `requestId`) pair, and the first entry is the fail reason; precedence per turn: withdrawn -> `failTurn` (\"withdrawn before delivery\"); else blocked -> `failTurn` with the blocker reason (blocked takes precedence over a ready completion); else meets the completion policy -> `completeTurn`; else stays in progress, reported with its exact missing items. Close prerequisites (catalog cross-reference, not a change to the decision): before clear-session fails a withdrawn or blocked turn, the model writes by that turn's original pair, within the same clear-session run and before its session-end step, every prerequisite the plugin and the server require for that close, in the Stop gate remedy order (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC009): a designDecision when the turn has no model evidence (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC002), then a `requestClass` when it is missing or `Unclassified`, judged from the turn's `queryText`; the coordinator then fails the turn with its reason (TR-MCP-CLEARSESSION-001-AC001).\n113:Review record (shared by `agentReview` and `hostileReview`)\n116:- Server creates both at turn creation as `Unstarted`. `hostileReview` flips to `Unnecessary` when `requestClass` becomes `Chore`, and back to `Unstarted` if the class changes away while still `Unnecessary`. Records past `Unstarted`/`Unnecessary` are never auto-changed. Legacy turns are backfilled `agentReview=Unstarted`, `hostileReview=Unstarted`. (Catalog cross-references, not a change to the decision: the flip and its reverse apply only to in-progress turns, FR-MCP-SESSIONREVIEW-006-AC002 and UC-4; a reset restores the creation default for the turn's current class, FR-MCP-SESSIONREVIEW-001-AC004.)\n118:- Strict per-status fields: `Unnecessary`/`Unstarted` carry no scores, reasons, reviewer, timestamps, or paths; `Running` requires `reviewer`, forbids scores; `Pass`/`Fail` require `correctness`, `completeness`, `reviewer`, `reviewedAt`; `hostileReview` `Pass`/`Fail` also require `requestJsonlPath` and `responseJsonlPath`; `Other` requires non-empty `otherNotes` (the Other reason; operator D4, 2026-10-09: \"Other requires Other Reason be populated\") and allows optional scores, `reasons`, `reviewer`, `reviewedAt`, and receipt paths. `Unnecessary` is server-managed only.\n123:- `completed` requires `requestClass` plus `agentReview.status` in `Pass`/`Fail`/`Other`; for `Code`/`Docs` also `hostileReview.status` in `Pass`/`Fail`/`Other`; for `Chore` `hostileReview` is not checked (HV not required). `Unstarted`/`Running` block where checked. Pass is not required (Fail/Other complete).\n171:- Completion gate writers: QBAgent's `mcp_session_turn_complete` (`QuadBrainInternalToolExecutor.cs:314-328`) stays gated and gains the optional `requestClass`, `agentReview`, and `hostileReview` arguments of CompleteSessionTurnCommand, so TR-MCP-SESSIONCLASS-004 covers it; REPL `importRecovery` stays gated: a recovered turn the gate refuses is reported in the import result with the gate's missing items and is never written with a fabricated review, and the REPL's status normalization stays as today (`SessionLogWorkflow.cs:725-750`: `completed-local` and `partial` become `completed`, `interrupted` becomes `failed`), so the one normalized value outside the agent write set, `superseded`, is refused with 400 `validation_error`.\n229:- Nonce: computed from the event itself, never from the hook process, so every delivery of one event yields the same nonce; a value outside `^[A-Za-z0-9._:-]{1,64}$` is replaced by the 64 lowercase hex characters of its SHA-256. Equal-field rule (copilot and classic cline, whose nonce hashes event fields with no per-message id): under `turn-open.lock`, a new prompt-hook invocation whose nonce already has a failsafe mint record or a `pending-<nonce>` turn in that host session, or whose mint returns `merged`, cannot be told apart from a separate message with equal session, timestamp, and prompt, so it mints and binds nothing and the host session fails closed under D6 exactly as the bridge does for an unattributable record (`unsupportedSince: { causeNonce, causeOperation: prompt_hook, reason: indistinguishable_arrival }` with the session-level envelope; every session-log verb then reports `turn_logging_unsupported` and writes nothing to the server until the session ends, except that writes for a turn already open at that moment go to the plugin failsafe under the unsupported-session failsafe rule, FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001; its open turns are listed at the next SessionStart). A network retry or failsafe replay of a stored mint record is a drain of that record, not a new invocation, and reuses its nonce without failing closed.\n230:- Unsupported-session rule (D6; operator option 1 of 2026-10-10): every check that makes a host session report `turn_logging_unsupported` after one of its requests was delivered records `unsupportedSince` with the session-level envelope in `turn-state.yaml` in the same atomic replace that makes the session unsupported, with `reason` set to that check's code: `queue_removal_ambiguous` (bridge), `indistinguishable_arrival` (equal-field rule), `arrival_bridge_format_unrecognized` (an unknown queue operation, a record missing a required field, or a transcript shorter than `appliedThroughOffset`), `duplicate_prompt_hook` (the copilot or classic-cline single-registration check starts failing; the first verb or hook in that host session that observes it records it), or, for any other D6 check, its own reason code; plus `causeOffset` or `causeNonce` where the cause has one, else `detectedAtUtc`. Whatever the reason, the unsupported-session failsafe of FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001 then applies: writes for a turn of that host session still in progress when `unsupportedSince` was recorded (a delivered open request, or a `withdrawn` queue entry still `in_progress`, addressed by its original pair; a minted request still `queued` is not covered and stays a warning) are captured, and every mint and every other write is refused. The state lasts until the host session ends, or until a SessionStart clears its cause where the cause's own rule allows (the bridge restarting on a valid transcript); the captured records drain at that SessionStart. A host that is unsupported from its first event mints nothing, has no open turn, and records no `unsupportedSince`.\n233:- Queued-message arrival on every host (D5, D6): P0.2 records, per host, whether a message sent while a turn is running reaches an arrival mechanism at enqueue. Where the prompt event itself fires at enqueue for a message the host takes into the running turn, it is the arrival mechanism; a prompt event that fires at enqueue for a message the host runs as its own later turn is not, because a prompt-hook mint is delivered at once (TR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001 AC-005) and would move the write target and the stop ledger to a request the model has not received (D2; FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001), so P0.2 records that result as only when processed. The only queue adapter this plan specifies is the claude-code/claude-cowork arrival bridge. For every other host whose P0.2 result is only when processed (under the rule above) (codex excepted: that result stops P0, because every HV reviewer persists its own turn through the Codex plugin), the host is unsupported for turn logging in this plan (`turn_logging_unsupported`, D6), whether or not it has a separate enqueue or message-queue event; when it has one, P0.2 records that event's observed payload, and P0.8 files a traced follow-up TODO proposing the host's adapter (payload, nonce, correlation, lifecycle, replay, tests) for operator approval; until that adapter is specified, approved, and delivered, the host stays `turn_logging_unsupported` in this plan and its `<Host>P02NotPassedTurnLoggingUnsupportedZeroMintsZeroWrites` case applies.\n235:- claude-code: nonce: the bridge's `q-` enqueue nonce (the bridge is the only minter, FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001-AC009); hook key payload `session_id`; verb key env `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` read at verb start in shell, PowerShell, and hook subprocesses (equals `session_id`; those subprocesses see the updated value after `/clear`); stdio MCP verbs use the live-session rule; payloads carrying `agent_id` are subagent events (system events, no turn); the subagent's own Bash and PowerShell verbs carry the parent's `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` and bind to the parent's write target; stop uses payload `stop_hook_active`. Arrival bridge (D5), the only minter on claude-code (known-good single source of truth: the host transcript is one ordered log written by one producer, so the bridge needs no key shared with the hook, which the host does not provide: Claude Code's `queue-operation` records carry no `promptId` although its user records do): started by SessionStart with the payload `transcript_path`, it applies `queue-operation` lines in file order. Every `enqueue` takes a queue position, so FIFO `dequeue` stays exact. An `enqueue` whose `content` the system-event classifier marks as a system event is added as `{ kind: system, enqueueOffset, enqueueTimestamp, promptSha256, state: queued }` with the session-level envelope `{ workspacePath, agent, mcpSessionId, hostSessionKey }` (it has no nonce or requestId, and none is ever invented for it) and is never minted. An `enqueue` whose `content` the classifier accepts as a user request: mint with nonce `q-` + SHA-256(`session_id` LF the record's starting byte offset in the transcript LF enqueue `timestamp` LF `content`) truncated to 62 hex characters (the offset is unique per record and identical on replay, so two distinct records with equal timestamp and content get two nonces), and add `{ kind: request, requestId, nonce, enqueueOffset, enqueueTimestamp, promptSha256, state: queued, stateOffset, mintedAtUtc }` with the root-id envelope to the queue in `sessions/<key>/turn-state.yaml`. `dequeue` (no content) removes the head of the host session's `queued` entries: a `kind: request` head becomes `delivered` and the write target; a `kind: system` head is removed and delivers nothing. `remove` and `popAll` records that match a `kind: system` entry drop it without a turn. `remove` with reason `absorbed_mid_turn`: the `queued` entry whose `promptSha256` equals the record's `content` hash (both written by the same producer) becomes `delivered` and the write target when exactly one queued entry has that hash. `remove` with any other or no reason: the matching `queued` entry becomes `withdrawn` when exactly one queued entry has that content hash. `popAll`: when, for every content line it carries, the number of lines with that content equals the number of queued entries with that content hash, those entries become `withdrawn`. Fail-closed rule (D6): a `remove` carries `commandUuid` and `deliveryId` (Claude Code 2.1.293), which also appear on the delivered message's `queued_command` attachment, but `enqueue` and `dequeue` carry no key shared with it, so a `remove` that matches two or more queued entries, or a `popAll` that carries fewer lines of a content than there are queued entries with that content, cannot be attributed (a `popAll` whose line count equals the matching entry count withdraws them all, because every candidate is withdrawn). The bridge never guesses: at the first such record it stops before it without advancing `appliedThroughOffset`, sets `turn-state.yaml` `unsupportedSince: { causeOffset, causeOperation, reason: queue_removal_ambiguous }`, and from then on every session-log verb in that host session reports `turn_logging_unsupported` and writes nothing to the server until the session ends, except that writes for a turn already open at that moment go to the plugin failsafe (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001). Turns that are open at that moment are left exactly as they are on the server, the stop gate blocks on each only until the model's close of it is captured in the plugin failsafe and then warns `queue_removal_ambiguous` listing them (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001), and the next SessionStart for the agent lists them with their original (MCP `sessionId`, `requestId`) pairs for closing through the trusted recovery flow (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC006). P0.2 records the `remove` identity fields; the bridge never uses them to pair with an `enqueue`. Only `queue-operation` lines are arrival records; every other record type is ignored by design. A `queue-operation` with an unknown operation or a missing required field is never skipped: the bridge stops before it without advancing `appliedThroughOffset`, records warning `arrival_bridge_format_unrecognized` and, after a delivered request, `unsupportedSince` with that reason and the record's `causeOffset` in the same atomic replace (unsupported-session rule), and the host session reports `turn_logging_unsupported` on every session-log verb (D6) until a verified adapter handles that shape, while writes for a turn already open go to the plugin failsafe (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001). Each record's state change and the advance of `appliedThroughOffset` past it are one atomic replace of `turn-state.yaml` (temp file, flush, rename, under `turn-open.lock`); records that start below `appliedThroughOffset` are skipped, so a restart never re-applies a record; the server mint for `enqueue` happens before that replace and is idempotent (same nonce, `merged`). Records are applied by whichever process holds `turn-open.lock`: the bridge, or a session-log verb, which first records the transcript length at the moment the invocation starts and, after taking the lock, applies every pending record that ends at or before that length, never a record appended later, before it binds (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-007-AC006), so a verb started after a `dequeue` line was written binds to that request, and a `dequeue` appended after the verb started never retargets it (D2). The prompt hook on claude-code and claude-cowork neither mints nor claims; it classifies system events and starts no turn. P0.2 verifies that every user prompt (in each prompt form of FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001-AC009) produces an `enqueue` record, that `dequeue` always takes the head, and that a request's `dequeue` (or its `remove` with `absorbed_mid_turn`) is in the transcript before that request's first tool call starts (section 3.1, Late `dequeue` on claude-code); if any fails, claude-code is `turn_logging_unsupported` (D6) until an adapter with a traceable key exists, recorded in the feature matrix.\n272:- C11 Paginated turn listing and detail reads return `requestClass`, `agentReview`, and `hostileReview`, sanitized as FR-MCP-SESSIONLOGSAN-001 requires, without weakening stable-snapshot pagination, ordered detail retrieval, or browser credential exclusion; this read contract is fixed now, and S4 and S5 implement it on current `develop`.\n283:- Stop gate: blocks `turn_in_progress` for a delivered-ledger turn still in_progress (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-003-AC004), blocks `turn_withdrawn_unclosed` through the separate scan of `withdrawn` entries still in_progress, and only warns for a minted request still `queued` although its server turn is in_progress (`StopGateQueuedInProgressServerTurnNoBlockBeforeDeliveryWarns`); in a host session with `unsupportedSince`, a turn whose close is captured as an `unsupported_session` failsafe record is warned with the cause instead, and before that its remedy names only the missing model evidence, the missing `requestClass` (from S8/S9), and the close call, the terminal reviews being judged at the drain, FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001; outside `unsupportedSince`, a turn whose model close is held in the ordinary failsafe by a transport outage is warned as degraded persistence instead, and its ledger entry stays until the server confirms the close), `session_turn_completion_gate` (a turn whose most recent close the server refused with that code, recorded locally from the server response; such a turn is still in progress; no marker condition, FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC003), failed build after edits (existing; read from the `lastBuildStatus` of this host session's `turns/<requestId>.yaml` files, where code-verify writes it, section 4.1), stale session (existing); blocks `turn_withdrawn_unclosed` (a withdrawn queued request still in_progress); warns on exactly the FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC005 list (process EvaluateStopGate, section 4.4), whose retained failsafe records are a mint refused for a final reason, queued or quarantined, and an `open-failed` queue entry, each naming the prompt, and every quarantined record of an open turn with its reason; re-evaluates on every Stop, including `stop_hook_active=true`, blocking again while turns are open and recording `stop_gate_overridden_by_host` if the host stops anyway; passes with `{}` and clears closed entries. Auto-close removed. Block text: `session_turn_stop_gate: <n> turn(s) are not closed correctly:` then one line per turn `<id> (<code>): <remedy>`, then `The stop gate never closes turns for you.`; each turn's remedy accumulates every unmet prerequisite of that turn, in this order: for a turn whose most recent close the server refused with `session_turn_completion_gate`, the server's missing items first (in place of the locally computed `requestClass` and review items); at least one model-authored action, designDecision, or commit (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC002); `requestClass` (from S8/S9, when the plugin's requestClass and recordReview verbs ship); the terminal reviews the completion gate names (from S8/S9, when the plugin's requestClass and recordReview verbs ship, gate order; before S10 the server accepts those writes, so following the remedy literally still passes); then the single close call valid for its status: `workflow.sessionlog.failTurn` with reason 'withdrawn before delivery' for a withdrawn request, otherwise `workflow.sessionlog.completeTurn` (or `failTurn` if the work failed), passing requestId <id>. Following a turn's remedy literally makes the next Stop pass for that turn, including a Code turn that lacks both model evidence and terminal reviews (`StopGateEachRemedyFollowedLiterallyNextStopPasses`, `StopGateCodeTurnMissingEvidenceAndReviewsAccumulatedRemedyNextStopPasses`).\n299:\"hostileReview\": { \"status\": \"Unstarted\" }\n301:- Unset review fields are emitted as `null` on every read surface (the `hostileReview` example above abbreviates them).\n303:- Errors (HTTP 400 with the listed code in the `ClassifiedError` `code` field, unless noted; the completion gate is the one exception, section 3 Errors): `review_invalid_status`, `review_unnecessary_not_client_writable`, `review_other_notes_not_allowed`, `review_other_notes_required`, `review_field_forbidden_for_status`, `review_field_required_for_status`, `review_score_out_of_range`, `review_receipt_paths_required`, `review_score_format`, `review_score_precision`, `review_section_reset_on_terminal_turn`, `review_field_too_long`, `review_timestamp_invalid`, `review_reason_blank`, `review_status_conflict` (422, includes thresholds in force and derived status), `request_class_invalid`, and the completion gate (`code` `validation_error`, message starting with the token `session_turn_completion_gate` and listing the missing items in order requestClass, agentReview, hostileReview; wherever this document names \"the completion-gate refusal\" or `session_turn_completion_gate` as a refusal, it means this body). A body-binding failure (a body that does not parse or bind) keeps today's validation problem, `ValidationProblemDetails` with `errors` keyed by the offending JSON path (`InvalidModelStateResponseFactory`, `src/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp/Program.cs:743-770` at `a8d3934b`), as FR-SUPPORT-012 and TR-SUPPORT-LOG-010 require; the domain refusals above are `ClassifiedError` problem+json bodies that, where they name a field, name it by its JSON path in `details.field` (for example `status` or `hvVerdict`), never by an action-parameter name, and carry no `errors` member (replaces, for these domain refusals only, the `errors` placement of the JSON path in FR-SUPPORT-012's third acceptance criterion and its twin FR-SUPPORT-010B with `details.field`).\n305:- Sections: `agentReview`, `hostileReview` (case-insensitive; aliases `agent-review`, `agent_review`, `hostile-review`, `hostile_review`). Section PUT replaces (latest wins); section DELETE resets to the default status, except on a completed or failed turn, where it returns 400 `review_section_reset_on_terminal_turn` and changes nothing; item DELETE returns 400.\n445:- `ScoreLexeme` and `ScoreLexemeJsonConverter` (namespace `QBrainAi.Client.Scoring`; the rule of section 3.4, Scores): validates a score lexeme (pattern, at most 28 fractional digits, value in [0, 1]) and parses it exactly; errors 400 `review_score_format`, `review_score_precision`, `review_score_out_of_range`. The converter validates at deserialization, so an invalid score is refused before any lookup (an invalid score on an unknown turn is 400, not 404). The REPL's lossless normalizer calls `ScoreLexeme` for the review keys (`agentReview`, `hostileReview`, `recordReview`) and for the agent-context grade keys (`correctness` and `completeness` of `RecordModelGradeAsync`), so `verdict_match` (section 6.3) compares lexemes that no hop has re-serialized. The PowerShell core's copy (`Protect-ReplLosslessScalars`) is a language boundary, kept in step by the golden contract test of the review contract.\n447:- `ReviewDefaults` (namespace `QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.Reviews`): the default review status for a kind and class: `agentReview` `Unstarted`; `hostileReview` `Unnecessary` when the class is `Chore`, otherwise `Unstarted`. Applied at turn insert, by a section reset, by the Chore flip of policy RequestClassification (only a record still at a default status, only on a nonterminal turn), and by the S3 backfill.\n916:Existing session-log routes this plan does not change (they keep today's behavior and are not units of this plan): `POST .../{requestId}/begin` (`BeginTurnAsync`; the plugins stop calling it, because ingress mints through MintSessionTurnCommand), `DELETE .../{requestId}` and `DELETE .../{sessionId}` (turn and session delete), `POST /qbrainai/sessionlog/repair-workspace-stamps`, and the section routes `PUT` and `DELETE .../sections/{section}` and `DELETE .../sections/{section}/items/{itemKey}` for every section other than the two review sections. For `agentReview` and `hostileReview` (and their aliases) the section `PUT` dispatches RecordSessionTurnReviewCommand, the section `DELETE` dispatches ResetSessionTurnReviewCommand, and the item `DELETE` is refused by the existing delete-item handler (Phase B, S4).\n1140:#### Process PersistTurnWriteWithReceipt: persist a turn write with a truthful receipt (dispatches SubmitSessionLogCommand with the one bound turn, AppendTurnDialogCommand, or SetTurnTitleCommand through the REPL) [FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-004, FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005]\n1144:- Close verbs first: `completeTurn` and `failTurn` call `Test-ModelEvidence` and are refused locally with `turn_missing_model_evidence` when the turn has no model-authored action, design decision, or commit (hook invocations excluded; FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC002). A close that carries evidence sends it first as an additive non-transition write, verified against the sanitized read projection (section 3.1), and sends the transition only after that write is confirmed, so a gate refusal loses nothing (FR-MCP-SESSIONCLASS-004-AC003, from S8); a refused evidence write stops the close.\n1214:#### Process DrainFailsafe: drain the failsafe [FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001, FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-004, FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005]\n1223:  - Not drained, no attempt counted: an `unsupported_session` record of a host session that is still live with `unsupportedSince` (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001, section 3.1). It drains only after that host session has ended or a SessionStart cleared its cause (a post-compact or other SessionStart of the live session keeps it), and only while its turn is still in progress.\n1291:#### Process EvaluateStopGate: evaluate the Stop gate [FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005]\n1295:- Blocks: `turn_in_progress`, `turn_withdrawn_unclosed`, `session_turn_completion_gate`, the reduced unsupported-session remedy, and the existing failed-build and stale-session blocks (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC007, section 3.4); the failed-build block reads `lastBuildStatus` from this host session's `turns/<requestId>.yaml` files, where code-verify writes it (section 4.1).\n1297:- Warnings: exactly the FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC005 list, each drawn in one of the two diagrams below. Per turn: model-canceled turns; a turn closed without model evidence by another surface; degraded persistence (a model close, or the plugin's `canceled` close of a package failure, held by an outage, naming that failure); a withdrawn close held by an outage; a captured unsupported-session close (naming the `unsupportedSince` reason and the captured records); and queued undelivered requests. Per host session: each `unsupportedSince` reason (`queue_removal_ambiguous`, `indistinguishable_arrival`, `duplicate_prompt_hook`, `arrival_bridge_format_unrecognized`, or any other) naming the open turns of its fail-closed host session and their captured records; retained failsafe records (a mint refused for a final reason, queued or quarantined, and an `open-failed` queue entry, each naming the prompt; every quarantined record of an open turn with its reason, process DrainFailsafe); `duplicate_delivery_suspected`; `nonce_source_missing` and `turn-open-contended` naming the prompt (section 3.1); `arrival_bridge_format_unrecognized`; an earlier `stop_gate_overridden_by_host`; a live bridge host session whose arrival bridge is not running; and a turn in progress in the legacy `current-turn.yaml`. Open turns of ended host sessions are not a Stop warning, because a Stop never blocks or warns on another host session's turns (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001); the next SessionStart lists them with their pairs (process StartHostSession, FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC006).\n1317:Each terminal below records one finding for the turn, and the loop continues with the next turn. A turn the server reports terminal leaves the open list and becomes eligible to clear; a `canceled` or `cancelled` turn is warned as model-canceled (a plugin `canceled` close names the package failure), and another terminal turn on which `Test-ModelEvidence` finds no model evidence is warned as closed without model evidence by another surface. A turn already open when this host session's `unsupportedSince` was recorded (a delivered open request or a withdrawn entry) is warned with the `unsupportedSince` reason when its close is captured in the failsafe (drained at the SessionStart after the host session ends or its cause is cleared, where the server gate judges it, FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001, AC003), and otherwise blocked with the reduced remedy of `Get-CloseRemedy` (missing model evidence and the close call, from S8 also `requestClass`, never the reviews).\n1380:- Output: the session open, the bridge, the drain, the Mode cache (from Phase C), and the open turns listed with their pairs: this host session's, the open turns of this agent's ended host sessions in the workspace (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC006), and a turn in progress in the legacy `current-turn.yaml` (addressable by explicit requestId). The listing is SessionStart `hookSpecificOutput`; a PostCompact run lists nothing and emits no `additionalContext` (FR-MCP-115, TR-MCP-PLUGIN-009, TEST-MCP-155 kept).\n1498:- The completion-gate refusal is 400 with `code` `validation_error` and a message that starts with the token `session_turn_completion_gate` and lists the missing items in the order requestClass, agentReview, hostileReview (section 3.4). The gate runs on CompletionGate's trigger (section 5.3): a write that moves the turn into `completed` or `failed`, or a Replace (PUT) that carries `completed` or `failed` over any stored status, an already terminal turn included, because a whole replacement is not enrichment; never on an additive write that only enriches a turn that is already terminal.\n2128:- Wire shape on `UnifiedRequestEntryDto` (both develop definitions, section 3.5): `requestClass` and the two review objects `agentReview`, `hostileReview` with `status`, `otherNotes`, `correctness`, `completeness`, `correctnessThreshold`, `completenessThreshold`, `reasons`, `reviewer`, `reviewedAt`, `requestJsonlPath`, `responseJsonlPath`; every read surface returns them through `TurnReadProjection` (section 3.5.3), so unset fields are emitted as `null`. The new review DTOs (`SessionLogTurnReviewKind`, `SessionLogTurnReviewInput`, `SessionLogTurnReviewDto`) are defined once in `src/QBrainAi.Client/Models/SessionLog/` (namespace `QBrainAi.Client.Models.SessionLog`).\n2161:- Handler: `RecordSessionTurnReviewCommand(string WorkspacePath, string Agent, string SessionId, string RequestId, SessionLogTurnReviewKind Kind, SessionLogTurnReviewInput Review) : ICommand<SessionLogTurnReviewDto>` in `src/QBrainAi.Services/Reviews/Commands/RecordSessionTurnReviewCommand.cs` and `RecordSessionTurnReviewCommandHandler` in `src/QBrainAi.Services/Reviews/Commands/RecordSessionTurnReviewCommandHandler.cs` (namespace `QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.Reviews.Commands`). It applies policy HvVerdictMapping when `hvVerdict` is supplied and policy ReviewValidation (origin Agent) to every write, then delegates the save to `ISessionLogService.ReplaceTurnSectionAsync` with section `agentReview` or `hostileReview` (`src/QBrainAi.Services/Services/ISessionLogService.cs:108`; `TurnSection` and `ParseSection` gain the two review members, `src/QBrainAi.Services/Services/SessionLogService.cs:1287-1317`), which runs `TurnLocator` and the save inside `SerializableWrite`; `TransactionGatedSessionLogService` wraps that member with its pre-mutation restore as it does today (`src/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp/Services/TransactionGatedSessionLogService.cs:169-183`). The review is not written through `UpsertTurnAsync`, whose mode `Merge` inserts an absent turn, while a review never creates a turn (404). This updates TR-SUPPORT-CORE-014 ac-5 (\"record review uses UpsertTurnAsync\") to the section member, and its case `RecordReviewWritesThroughUpsertTurnAsync` becomes `RecordReviewWritesThroughReplaceTurnSectionNeverCreatesTurn`.\n2162:- Surfaces: `PUT /qbrainai/sessionlog/{agent}/{sessionId}/{requestId}/sections/{section}` with section `agentReview` or `hostileReview` (aliases `agent-review`, `agent_review`, `hostile-review`, `hostile_review`, case-insensitive; a known non-review section stays on the existing section-replace handler, `SessionLogController.cs:576-601`); MCP `sessionlog_record_review(agent, sessionId, requestId, kind, reviewJson, workspacePath)` and MCP `sessionlog_replace_section` with a review section or alias, whose `sectionJson` is read as the same `SessionLogTurnReviewInput` body; client `RecordTurnReviewAsync`; REPL and plugin `workflow.sessionlog.recordReview` (plugin also accepts `hvReceiptPath`, mapped locally by process ConvertHvReceiptToReview; hostile kind accepts `hvVerdict`). Both MCP tools dispatch this command: today `sessionlog_replace_section` calls the service member directly (`src/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp/McpStdio/FwhMcpTools.SessionLog.cs:203-228`) and would skip the policies, the workspace behavior, and `hvVerdict`; every non-review section keeps that direct call (`McpSectionToolsReviewSectionDispatchReviewUnits`).\n2170:    S([\"Review write on a surface\"]) --> SEC{\"Section or kind agentReview, hostileReview, or an alias?\"}\n2201:    S([\"DELETE on a turn section\"]) --> SEC{\"Section agentReview, hostileReview, or an alias?\"}\n2230:    PR --> OK([\"200 turns with requestClass, agentReview, hostileReview\"])\n2276:- Applied by: RecordSessionTurnReviewCommand (origin Agent), and `TurnWritePipeline` for each review record a turn body carries: `agentReview` and `hostileReview` on SubmitSessionLogCommand, UpsertSessionTurnCommand, PatchSessionTurnCommand, ReplaceSessionTurnCommand, CompleteSessionTurnCommand, and FailSessionTurnCommand from the Agent origin (McpAgent requests carry them, TR-MCP-SESSIONCLASS-004 AC-001) and from the Import and Replication origins, which store a supplied record exactly as received (status and stamped thresholds, not re-derived) after the same per-status and score validation (FR-MCP-SESSIONCLASS-003-AC002). It runs before policies RequestClassification and CompletionGate, so one write carrying `requestClass`, terminal reviews, and `completed` succeeds (FR-MCP-SESSIONCLASS-002-AC001). A turn body that omits a review keeps the stored record in the `Merge` modes and resets it to `ReviewDefaults` in the `Replace` mode (FR-MCP-SESSIONREVIEW-001-AC004); thresholds on input are ignored; ServerInternal writers never write a review (no review is ever fabricated).\n2323:- Rule: `completed` needs `requestClass` (`Unclassified` counts as missing) and `agentReview.status` in Pass, Fail, or Other; unless the class is `Chore`, also `hostileReview.status` in Pass, Fail, or Other (a missing class is not `Chore`, so following the remedy literally passes whichever class the model then sets); `failed` needs `requestClass` only. Output: the missing items in the order requestClass, agentReview, hostileReview; none means the rule is met.\n2337:    CH -->|no, or missing| HR{\"hostileReview Pass, Fail, or Other?\"}\n2338:    HR -->|no| M3[\"Missing: hostileReview\"]\n2340:    M3 --> RES([\"Missing items in order: requestClass, agentReview, hostileReview\"])\n2349:  - QBAgent's `mcp_session_turn_complete` (`src/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp/Services/QuadBrainInternalToolExecutor.cs:314-328`, declared at `src/QBrainAi.McpAgent/Hosting/McpHostedAgentToolAdapter.cs:68` and `src/QBrainAi.McpAgent/QBAgentDefinition.cs:15`) stays gated and gains the optional `requestClass`, `agentReview`, and `hostileReview` arguments of CompleteSessionTurnCommand. It already reads its arguments as a `UnifiedRequestEntryDto` (`:323`), so the Phase B fields on that DTO carry them (inference). TR-MCP-SESSIONCLASS-004 is updated to cover this tool beside `src/QBrainAi.McpAgent/SessionLog/SessionLogWorkflow.cs`.\n2365:#### Process ConvertHvReceiptToReview: convert a hostile-validation receipt into a review write (plugin), then policy HvVerdictMapping [FR-MCP-SESSIONREVIEW-005]\n2370:- The HostileReview subsystem (FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW-001 to -006: its durable review queue, its server-owned worker pinned to `gpt-6-astra` at effort `xhigh` by `HostileReviewWorkerOptions`, `src/QBrainAi.Services/Options/HostileReviewWorkerOptions.cs:16-19,79-82`, and its AGREE, DISAGREE, or UNKNOWN verdicts) is separate from a turn's `hostileReview`: its results are not HV receipts for this record, never pass through this process, and are not judged by UC-7's `max` rule. FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW-003 keeps its `xhigh` worker unchanged, and FR-MCP-SESSIONREVIEW-005 is updated with this statement.\n2371:- The HV reviewer's brief and its turn: a user prompt that carries a hostile-review brief is a user message and opens its own turn, because the prompt hook classifies a background agent only by the host's subagent markers (process OpenTurnFromPromptHook, section 4.4), never by prompt text; the reviewer records the reviewed turn's `hostileReview` by that turn's original pair. This replaces FR-MCP-TRIAGEPLUGIN-001's text-detected background-brief reuse (`Test-PluginPromptIsBackgroundAgent` and `Get-PluginRootTurnIsolationDecision`, `plugins/core/lib-ps/plugin-hook.ps1:693,725`): the `reuse` and `turn-already-open` expectations of `plugins/core/test-fixtures/pester/TriagePluginIdentity.Tests.ps1:290,327,420` (TEST-MCP-TRIAGEPLUGIN-001, BUG-TRIAGE-121) become a new turn, and their no-cancel expectations still hold, because no prompt changes another turn (section 3.1, independent turn edits).\n2451:- Keys: `correctness` and `completeness` inside `agentReview`, `hostileReview`, or `recordReview`, and inside the request of `client.AgentContext.RecordModelGradeAsync` (the HV agent's model grades). `verdict_match` (section 6.3) compares score lexemes, and these lexemes reach the server exactly as the reviewer wrote them; the normalized number a read surface returns (trailing zeros stripped) is never the compared value.\n2455:    S([\"Number token in a REPL payload\"]) --> K{\"correctness or completeness inside agentReview, hostileReview, recordReview, or a RecordModelGradeAsync request?\"}\n2466:`A` is the model or the clear-session coordinator, `P` the plugin's `recordReview` verb, `R` `qbrain-ai-repl`, and `S` the surface: `SessionLogController` (PUT on the review section), or the MCP tool `sessionlog_record_review` or `sessionlog_replace_section` with a review section. The verb carries the kind and the review input or `hvReceiptPath`, with an optional `requestId` or original pair. Process BindVerbToTurn binds it: an unqualified `recordReview` binds to the write target even when that turn is closed (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-004-AC004), and a binding refusal is `request_unknown`, `request_ambiguous`, `host_session_unknown`, or `host_session_ambiguous`, with nothing sent. A host session reported `turn_logging_unsupported` refuses the review, which is recorded after the drain through the original pair. Before any write the plugin refuses an invalid receipt with its exact code (process ConvertHvReceiptToReview) and an invalid score lexeme with `review_score_format`, `review_score_precision`, or `review_score_out_of_range` (`Protect-ReplLosslessScalars`); the REPL normalizer calls `ScoreLexeme`, so the lexemes reach the server unchanged. A review is never captured in the failsafe (an older review must never overwrite a newer one, section 3.1, \"Retryable answers\"), so a transport outage or a retryable 503 (`backend_unavailable`, or a busy store's `persistence_error` with `retryable: true`) is reported with its code and `retryable: true`, not queued, exit 1, and the model records the review again after the drain (section 5.2, RecordSessionTurnReviewCommand). The server side, with each answer and the plugin's report of it, is the nested sequence ServeReviewWrite.\n2481:    else receipt invalid (ConvertHvReceiptToReview)\n2673:  - The FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW queue (requests with resolved artifact links, `src/QBrainAi.Services/Services/HostileReviewService.cs:290`, executions, findings, and its server-owned worker): separate. An HV request of this phase is a turn range an external HV agent grades, because this effort builds no grading worker (section 3.1); only RecordModelGradeCommand, after its eight checks, records a model grade, so a hostile review result is never one. The worker's identity (agent `Astra`, model `gpt-6-astra`, effort `xhigh`, enforced at startup, `src/QBrainAi.Services/Options/HostileReviewWorkerOptions.cs:13,16,19,77-82`) and `ModelGrader:*` (section 6.2) are separate option sets, so neither constrains the other; FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW-003 is unchanged by this phase.\n2691:- `ModelGrader:AgentName` (default `Codex`), `ModelGrader:RequiredModel` (default `gpt-6-astra`), `ModelGrader:RequiredEffort` (default `max`), `ModelGrader:ReceiptRoot` (workspace-relative, default `docs/receipts/hv`): a model grade from any other grader, or whose receipts are outside the receipt root or do not verify, is refused.\n2694:- Validation failures (startup): an unknown `Mode`, `TokenBudget`, `BytesPerToken`, `HvRequest:MaxTurns`, thresholds outside their ranges, an empty `RuleSources`, an empty grader identity, or an empty `ModelGrader:ReceiptRoot`. These ranges are enforced at server start by `Validate()` with `ValidateOnStart()` and proven by its G2 cases; they are not a G5 (`ValidateConfig`) check, an exclusion TR-MCP-NUKE-004 states (section 8.4).\n2803:- `ReviewerAgent` (64, required for `Model`: the `ModelGrader:AgentName` the `reviewer_turn` check located the turn with), `ReviewerSessionId`, `ReviewerRequestId` (256, required for `Model`): the reviewer's own session-log turn that holds its entire result, by its full turn key, so the reference stays exact after a configuration change; `VerdictJsonSha256` (64): the hash of the verdict block the grade was verified against, over its stored normal form.\n2804:- `RequestJsonlPath`, `ResponseJsonlPath` (2048, required for `Model`, workspace-relative under `ModelGrader:ReceiptRoot`), `RequestJsonlSha256`, `ResponseJsonlSha256` (64): the receipts and their hashes as verified when the grade was recorded.\n2805:- `RolloutJsonlPath` (2048, required for `Model`, under `ModelGrader:ReceiptRoot`), `RolloutJsonlSha256` (64), `ReviewerThreadId` (64, required for `Model`): the reviewer's own Codex rollout receipt (after the review execution completes, the HV runner freezes a copy of that thread's rollout file next to the request and response jsonl, redacted by the receipt rule, before any grade is submitted) and the thread id it records; check 8 (`runtime_identity`) verifies them against that frozen copy, so the later grade-submission continuation of the same thread (RecordModelGradeCommand, two-step protocol) never changes the receipt it is checked against.\n2836:1. `receipt_path`: both jsonl paths resolve inside the workspace under `ModelGrader:ReceiptRoot`.\n2837:2. `request_meta`: the request jsonl's first line is a JSON object with `type` `hv_request_meta` whose `reviewer.agent` contains `ModelGrader:AgentName`, `reviewer.model` equals `ModelGrader:RequiredModel`, and `reviewer.effort` equals `ModelGrader:RequiredEffort` (the HV runner writes exactly this object), and the request jsonl's body record names the `hvRequestId`.\n2841:6. `reviewer_turn`: TurnLocator (section 3.5.3) finds the turn with agent `ModelGrader:AgentName`, `reviewerSessionId`, and `reviewerRequestId` in the workspace, the turn was created at or after the HV request's `CreatedUtc`, and its stored result contains the same verdict block byte for byte, both compared in their stored normal form (the receipt's block passes through ContentHasher's normalization, since the store saved the turn in that form, `ModelGradeVerdictWithEmDashReviewerTurnMatchesInNormalForm`), where the stored result is the turn's response followed by its processingDialog parts in part order with each part's header line removed (the HV protocol stores the entire result on the reviewer's own turn and splits a long result into ordered parts; `ModelGradeVerdictSplitAcrossDialogPartsRejoinedAndAccepted`).\n2843:8. `runtime_identity`: the rollout receipt at `rolloutJsonlPath` resolves under `ModelGrader:ReceiptRoot`; its `session_meta` record's `id` equals `reviewerThreadId` and the `thread_id` of the response jsonl's `thread.started` event; it holds at least one `turn_context` record before the record of the completed review turn whose verdict check 3 read, and every `turn_context` record in it has `model` equal to `ModelGrader:RequiredModel` and `effort` equal to `ModelGrader:RequiredEffort`, so a rollout without live runtime context is refused (`ModelGradeRolloutWithoutTurnContextRefusedRuntimeIdentity`); and the verdict JSON names the same `reviewerThreadId`. The declared `request_meta` alone never satisfies this check (`ModelGradeLaunchMetaCorrectRolloutTurnContextOtherModelOrEffortRefusedRuntimeIdentity`, `ModelGradeRolloutThreadDiffersFromResponseStreamRefusedRuntimeIdentity`).\n3767:- REST `POST /qbrainai/agent-context/hv-requests`; MCP `agent_context_create_hv_request(string workspacePath, string sessionId, string agent, string fromRequestId, string toRequestId, string requestedBy, string reason, string idempotencyKey)`; client `CreateHvRequestAsync(CreateAgentContextHvRequestRequest)`; REPL `client.AgentContext.CreateHvRequestAsync`; PowerShell `New-McpAgentContextCreateHvRequestParams`; tool descriptor `agent_context_create_hv_request`.\n3768:- Response 201 `AgentContextHvRequestResult` (Location `/qbrainai/agent-context/hv-requests/{hvRequestId}`) with its items: every agent response whose `RequestId` lies in the range and whose status is not `Rejected` or `Superseded` and that is not already `Pending` in another `Open` HV request, in turn order, each `Pending` (`HvRequestResponsePendingInOpenRequestNotListedAgain`). A repeat with the same `idempotencyKey` and the same `sessionId`, `agent`, range, `requestedBy`, and `reason` returns 200 with the stored request and its current items and status, so a retry after a lost 201 never creates a second request (`HvRequestRetrySameKeySameInputsReturns200Stored`); the same key with any other input returns 409 `conflict` with `retryable: false` (`HvRequestSameKeyOtherInputs409Conflict`). The key lookup (IdempotentCreate, policy `Conflict`, fingerprint of the client inputs) is the first step inside the transaction, before the session, range, and status checks; the unit reads only the database, so it needs no read-only lookup before its SerializableWrite (section 3.5.2). A concurrent create that wins the key's unique index ends the loser's attempt, SerializableWrite runs the delegate again, and the lookup returns the winner, the same rule Build and Submit follow.\n3778:    S([\"Handler runs: POST hv-requests\"]) --> V{\"Every request field nonblank?\"}\n3818:- REST `GET /qbrainai/agent-context/hv-requests/{hvRequestId}`; MCP `agent_context_get_hv_request(string workspacePath, string hvRequestId)`; client `GetHvRequestAsync(hvRequestId)`; REPL `client.AgentContext.GetHvRequestAsync`; PowerShell `New-McpAgentContextGetHvRequestParams`; tool descriptor `agent_context_get_hv_request`.\n3823:    S([\"Handler runs: GET hv-requests/{hvRequestId}\"]) --> F{\"HV request found in the workspace?\"}\n3832:- REST `GET /qbrainai/agent-context/hv-requests?sessionId&status&limit&offset`; MCP `agent_context_query_hv_requests(string workspacePath, string? sessionId = null, string? status = null, int limit = 50, int offset = 0)`; client `QueryHvRequestsAsync(AgentContextHvRequestQuery)`; REPL `client.AgentContext.QueryHvRequestsAsync`; PowerShell `New-McpAgentContextQueryHvRequestsParams`; tool descriptor `agent_context_query_hv_requests`.\n3838:    S([\"Handler runs: GET hv-requests\"]) --> V{\"Status filter absent or known?\"}\n4384:    Agent->>Controller: POST /hv-requests (CreateAgentContextHvRequestCommand)\n4992:    Caller->>Controller: POST /hv-requests (CreateAgentContextHvRequestRequest)\n5033:    Caller->>Controller: GET /hv-requests/{hvRequestId}\n5054:    Caller->>Controller: GET /hv-requests?sessionId&status&limit&offset\n5160:### 7.4 Use case: Close every request turn before stopping (plan UC-3) [FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005, -006]\n5162:UC-3 Close every request turn before stopping. Actors: model, host Stop hook. Basic flow: model records actions/decisions (incl. via `designDecisions`) and closes each turn (a `completed` or `failed` close requires model evidence, Test-ModelEvidence); Stop hook (process EvaluateStopGate) finds all closed with evidence and passes, clearing the ledger. Alternate: a turn is open (including one whose close the plugin refused for missing model evidence) or its most recent close was refused by the server's completion gate, the Stop hook blocks with the reason and the accepted remedy (Get-CloseRemedy); a turn closed without evidence by another surface is only warned and never closes turns itself. Alternate: the host session failed closed under D6 (`unsupportedSince`) while a turn was open; the Stop hook blocks with the reduced remedy (only the missing model evidence, the missing `requestClass` from S8/S9, and the close call; the terminal reviews are judged at the drain), the model's evidence and close for that turn are captured in the plugin failsafe (reviews are refused and recorded after the drain through the original pair), the next Stop warns instead of blocking, and a later SessionStart drains the captured records with the turn's original pair through the trusted recovery flow (a post-compact SessionStart of the still-live host session drains none of them). Alternate: a model close held in the failsafe by a transport outage, or the package-failure `canceled` close of a stateless or pilot invocation (section 7.15) held there, is warned as degraded persistence (naming the package failure for the `canceled` close, which never needs model evidence) and never blocks; its ledger entry stays until the drain confirms the close. Alternate: a contended turn-open lock or a final mint refusal at prompt time left no turn, and the Stop hook warns naming the prompt. Alternate: the failsafe quarantine holds a record of an open turn, and the Stop hook warns and never blocks (section 3.1, \"Failsafe drain\"). A missing agent-context response submission (section 7.12) is not a Stop-gate item. Realizes FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005, -006; updated FR-MCP-SESSIONLIFE-005, FR-MCP-CLEARSESSION-001.\n5194:UC-5 Complete or fail a turn under the completion gate. Actors: model, server, server-internal writers, QBAgent and the REPL's transcript import. Basic flow: model completes; the server's turn write (TurnWritePipeline) runs the gate (policy CompletionGate), which checks class and review statuses; turn completes. Alternate: gate unmet, 400 with missing items (`code` `validation_error`, token `session_turn_completion_gate`), turn stays in progress; fail requires only class; exempt origins (the server-internal writers of section 3.1, \"Completion rules\") bypass; the plugin, REPL, and McpAgent pre-checks only warn with the exact gate message and never refuse (the server gate is the only authority), and a server refusal is reported with the server's message and missing items; QBAgent's `mcp_session_turn_complete` tool stays gated and passes the optional `requestClass`, `agentReview`, and `hostileReview` arguments of CompleteSessionTurnCommand (TR-MCP-SESSIONCLASS-004); REPL `importRecovery` stays gated, reports each recovered turn the gate refuses in its import result with the gate's missing items, never writes a fabricated review, and has a recovered status outside the agent write set refused with 400 `validation_error` (section 3.1, \"Completion gate writers\"; the REPL keeps its status normalization, `completed-local` and `partial` to `completed` and `interrupted` to `failed`, before it submits, so the one normalized status refused is `superseded`, `src/QBrainAi.Repl.Core/SessionLogWorkflow.cs:725-750` at `a8d3934b`); `canceled` is not gated; a write that only enriches an already terminal turn is not gated, while a Replace (PUT) that carries `completed` or `failed` is gated over any stored status, because a whole replacement is not enrichment (FR-MCP-SESSIONCLASS-002-AC004); an agent surface (REST, MCP, client, REPL, plugin, and QBAgent's internal session-log tools, which call `UpsertTurnAsync` directly, `src/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp/Services/QuadBrainInternalToolExecutor.cs:299-328` at `a8d3934b`) writes only `in_progress`, `completed`, `failed`, `canceled`, and the legacy `cancelled`, and a write naming `closed` or any other value is refused with 400 `validation_error` (server-internal writers and existing rows keep `closed`, TurnStatus); a close racing a review reset or class change on the same turn commits before or after it, never between its gate check and its save (SerializableWrite, section 3.4 write isolation). Realizes FR-MCP-SESSIONCLASS-002, -003, -004; updated FR-SUPPORT-013, FR-SUPPORT-014.\n5230:UC-7 Record a hostile validation review with receipts. Actors: hostile reviewer (Codex CLI, gpt-6-astra, max), coordinator agent. Basic flow: at each HV point of \"Execution organization\" once its gates are green (D10), the coordinator writes the HV brief and launches the Codex reviewer; the runner records the request and response jsonl; the reviewer writes its markdown and JSON receipts and stores its entire result on its own session-log turn; from the Phase 4 live-check deploy on, the coordinator records `hostileReview` from the receipt (accuracy/completeness mapped, receipt paths set) and backfills the Phase 4 context verdicts by original pair before the phase-wide Phase 4 HV; for every HV point before that deploy, it records the verdict on the work package's coordinating turn (dialog and designDecisions with the receipt paths) and with `RecordValidationResultAsync`. Alternate: DISAGREE with passing scores recorded as Other with notes; missing receipt paths rejected; a retryable answer to the review write is reported with its code and `retryable: true`, exit 1, never queued, and the coordinator records the review again after the drain (as in UC-6, section 5.2); `hvVerdict` sent together with an explicit `status` is refused with 400 `validation_error` naming `status`; a run whose live model or effort is not gpt-6-astra/max is not accepted as HV (an HV-protocol rule: the coordinator checks the reviewer's recorded model and effort in its receipts before recording any verdict, so process ConvertHvReceiptToReview and the plugin do not check it); from the Phase 4 live-check deploy on, Code or Docs turns of a work package that completed before its HV carry `Other` \"pending HV <gate>\" and receive the verdict through `recordReview`, each addressed by its original (`sessionId`, `requestId`) pair (FR-MCP-SESSIONREVIEW-002-AC002). The reviewer's brief is a user prompt and opens its own turn, because the prompt hook classifies a background agent only by the host's subagent markers, never by prompt text (section 5.4; this replaces FR-MCP-TRIAGEPLUGIN-001's text-detected background-brief reuse); the HostileReview subsystem (FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW-001 to -006, its `xhigh` worker, and its AGREE, DISAGREE, or UNKNOWN verdicts) is separate: its results are not HV receipts for this use case and are not judged by its `max` rule (section 5.4; FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW-003 is unchanged). Realizes FR-MCP-SESSIONREVIEW-002, -005 (-005 updated with that separation); updated FR-MCP-TRIAGEPLUGIN-001.\n5237:    U -. include .-> I2((\"Map the receipt to a review (process ConvertHvReceiptToReview)\"))\n5453:- ReplAndAgents: the REPL normalizer (which calls `ScoreLexeme`) and workflow, including `importRecovery`'s report of the turns the completion gate refuses (`src/QBrainAi.Repl.Core/SessionLogWorkflow.cs:326`), the McpAgent workflow files named by S6 and S7, and the optional `requestClass`, `agentReview`, and `hostileReview` arguments of QBAgent's `mcp_session_turn_complete` (`src/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp/Services/QuadBrainInternalToolExecutor.cs:314-328`; section 3.1, \"Completion gate writers\").\n5607:  - processes (5.4): ConvertHvReceiptToReview, ClearSession (nested activities ClearOneOpenTurn, and ReportCloseReceipt after it), AdvisoryCompletionPrecheck, PreserveReviewScoreLexemes;\nEXIT_CODE=0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
