rain through the original pair), so the Stop gate's remedy stays accepted; the captured records drain at the next SessionStart through the trusted recovery flow with the turn's original pair (C5); every mint and every write for any other turn stays refused (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001).\n99:- Clear-session closing rule (operator, 2026-10-10, choosing option A over leaving blocked and withdrawn turns open: \"A\"): clear-session completes each turn that meets the completion policy, fails each withdrawn turn (reason \"withdrawn before delivery\") and each turn the model marks blocked (with its blocker reason; `failed` requires `requestClass` only, FR-MCP-SESSIONCLASS-002-AC002), and leaves every other turn in progress, reported by requestId with its exact missing items, never fabricating reviews (TR-MCP-CLEARSESSION-001-AC001). Definition (catalog cross-reference, not a change to the decision): \"marked blocked\" means the turn's `blockers` section (the existing decision-list field, FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-006-AC001) holds at least one nonblank model-authored entry, read by the clear-session coordinator for that turn by its original (`sessionId`, `requestId`) pair, and the first entry is the fail reason; precedence per turn: withdrawn -> `failTurn` (\"withdrawn before delivery\"); else blocked -> `failTurn` with the blocker reason (blocked takes precedence over a ready completion); else meets the completion policy -> `completeTurn`; else stays in progress, reported with its exact missing items. Close prerequisites (catalog cross-reference, not a change to the decision): before clear-session fails a withdrawn or blocked turn, the model writes by that turn's original pair, within the same clear-session run and before its session-end step, every prerequisite the plugin and the server require for that close, in the Stop gate remedy order (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC009): a designDecision when the turn has no model evidence (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC002), then a `requestClass` when it is missing or `Unclassified`, judged from the turn's `queryText`; the coordinator then fails the turn with its reason (TR-MCP-CLEARSESSION-001-AC001).\n164:- Soft delete (TR-MCP-DB-003): IdempotentCreate and ReadableKeyAllocator read past the soft-delete filter; a soft-deleted key is revived and written anew, as develop revives a tombstoned session (`SessionLogService.cs:161-169`); a deleted suffix is never reallocated.\n208:- Test placement: a Phase 2 change that completes behavior rather than stubbing it (the `agent_context` federation registration, FR-MCP-103-AC006) records its case red before the change, in Phase 2, and Phase 3 keeps it green; a case that needs a later context's types runs at that context's gate (section 8.4; `ReadableKeyAllocatorSuffixPast99WidensLatestGradeOrderKept` at ResponsesAndTrust's); `--test-projects` naming `build/Build.Tests` is refused before any test runs; a BuildTooling case broken by another context's change is fixed by that context under the amendment rule of section 8.3 (`Catalog_HasExactlyEightEnabledScenarios` and PluginCorePowerShell's `GrokBot` scenario); the clear-session skill parity evidence lives in PluginIntegration (G3) and each plugin repo's Pester `Skills.Tests.ps1` (G6), never in Build.Tests.\n229:- Nonce: computed from the event itself, never from the hook process, so every delivery of one event yields the same nonce; a value outside `^[A-Za-z0-9._:-]{1,64}$` is replaced by the 64 lowercase hex characters of its SHA-256. Equal-field rule (copilot and classic cline, whose nonce hashes event fields with no per-message id): under `turn-open.lock`, a new prompt-hook invocation whose nonce already has a failsafe mint record or a `pending-<nonce>` turn in that host session, or whose mint returns `merged`, cannot be told apart from a separate message with equal session, timestamp, and prompt, so it mints and binds nothing and the host session fails closed under D6 exactly as the bridge does for an unattributable record (`unsupportedSince: { causeNonce, causeOperation: prompt_hook, reason: indistinguishable_arrival }` with the session-level envelope; every session-log verb then reports `turn_logging_unsupported` and writes nothing to the server until the session ends, except that writes for a turn already open at that moment go to the plugin failsafe under the unsupported-session failsafe rule, FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001; its open turns are listed at the next SessionStart). A network retry or failsafe replay of a stored mint record is a drain of that record, not a new invocation, and reuses its nonce without failing closed.\n230:- Unsupported-session rule (D6; operator option 1 of 2026-10-10): every check that makes a host session report `turn_logging_unsupported` after one of its requests was delivered records `unsupportedSince` with the session-level envelope in `turn-state.yaml` in the same atomic replace that makes the session unsupported, with `reason` set to that check's code: `queue_removal_ambiguous` (bridge), `indistinguishable_arrival` (equal-field rule), `arrival_bridge_format_unrecognized` (an unknown queue operation, a record missing a required field, or a transcript shorter than `appliedThroughOffset`), `duplicate_prompt_hook` (the copilot or classic-cline single-registration check starts failing; the first verb or hook in that host session that observes it records it), or, for any other D6 check, its own reason code; plus `causeOffset` or `causeNonce` where the cause has one, else `detectedAtUtc`. Whatever the reason, the unsupported-session failsafe of FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001 then applies: writes for a turn of that host session still in progress when `unsupportedSince` was recorded (a delivered open request, or a `withdrawn` queue entry still `in_progress`, addressed by its original pair; a minted request still `queued` is not covered and stays a warning) are captured, and every mint and every other write is refused. The state lasts until the host session ends, or until a SessionStart clears its cause where the cause's own rule allows (the bridge restarting on a valid transcript); the captured records drain at that SessionStart. A host that is unsupported from its first event mints nothing, has no open turn, and records no `unsupportedSince`.\n233:- Queued-message arrival on every host (D5, D6): P0.2 records, per host, whether a message sent while a turn is running reaches an arrival mechanism at enqueue. Where the prompt event itself fires at enqueue for a message the host takes into the running turn, it is the arrival mechanism; a prompt event that fires at enqueue for a message the host runs as its own later turn is not, because a prompt-hook mint is delivered at once (TR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001 AC-005) and would move the write target and the stop ledger to a request the model has not received (D2; FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001), so P0.2 records that result as only when processed. The only queue adapter this plan specifies is the claude-code/claude-cowork arrival bridge. For every other host whose P0.2 result is only when processed (under the rule above) (codex excepted: that result stops P0, because every HV reviewer persists its own turn through the Codex plugin), the host is unsupported for turn logging in this plan (`turn_logging_unsupported`, D6), whether or not it has a separate enqueue or message-queue event; when it has one, P0.2 records that event's observed payload, and P0.8 files a traced follow-up TODO proposing the host's adapter (payload, nonce, correlation, lifecycle, replay, tests) for operator approval; until that adapter is specified, approved, and delivered, the host stays `turn_logging_unsupported` in this plan and its `<Host>P02NotPassedTurnLoggingUnsupportedZeroMintsZeroWrites` case applies.\n235:- claude-code: nonce: the bridge's `q-` enqueue nonce (the bridge is the only minter, FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001-AC009); hook key payload `session_id`; verb key env `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` read at verb start in shell, PowerShell, and hook subprocesses (equals `session_id`; those subprocesses see the updated value after `/clear`); stdio MCP verbs use the live-session rule; payloads carrying `agent_id` are subagent events (system events, no turn); the subagent's own Bash and PowerShell verbs carry the parent's `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` and bind to the parent's write target; stop uses payload `stop_hook_active`. Arrival bridge (D5), the only minter on claude-code (known-good single source of truth: the host transcript is one ordered log written by one producer, so the bridge needs no key shared with the hook, which the host does not provide: Claude Code's `queue-operation` records carry no `promptId` although its user records do): started by SessionStart with the payload `transcript_path`, it applies `queue-operation` lines in file order. Every `enqueue` takes a queue position, so FIFO `dequeue` stays exact. An `enqueue` whose `content` the system-event classifier marks as a system event is added as `{ kind: system, enqueueOffset, enqueueTimestamp, promptSha256, state: queued }` with the session-level envelope `{ workspacePath, agent, mcpSessionId, hostSessionKey }` (it has no nonce or requestId, and none is ever invented for it) and is never minted. An `enqueue` whose `content` the classifier accepts as a user request: mint with nonce `q-` + SHA-256(`session_id` LF the record's starting byte offset in the transcript LF enqueue `timestamp` LF `content`) truncated to 62 hex characters (the offset is unique per record and identical on replay, so two distinct records with equal timestamp and content get two nonces), and add `{ kind: request, requestId, nonce, enqueueOffset, enqueueTimestamp, promptSha256, state: queued, stateOffset, mintedAtUtc }` with the root-id envelope to the queue in `sessions/<key>/turn-state.yaml`. `dequeue` (no content) removes the head of the host session's `queued` entries: a `kind: request` head becomes `delivered` and the write target; a `kind: system` head is removed and delivers nothing. `remove` and `popAll` records that match a `kind: system` entry drop it without a turn. `remove` with reason `absorbed_mid_turn`: the `queued` entry whose `promptSha256` equals the record's `content` hash (both written by the same producer) becomes `delivered` and the write target when exactly one queued entry has that hash. `remove` with any other or no reason: the matching `queued` entry becomes `withdrawn` when exactly one queued entry has that content hash. `popAll`: when, for every content line it carries, the number of lines with that content equals the number of queued entries with that content hash, those entries become `withdrawn`. Fail-closed rule (D6): a `remove` carries `commandUuid` and `deliveryId` (Claude Code 2.1.293), which also appear on the delivered message's `queued_command` attachment, but `enqueue` and `dequeue` carry no key shared with it, so a `remove` that matches two or more queued entries, or a `popAll` that carries fewer lines of a content than there are queued entries with that content, cannot be attributed (a `popAll` whose line count equals the matching entry count withdraws them all, because every candidate is withdrawn). The bridge never guesses: at the first such record it stops before it without advancing `appliedThroughOffset`, sets `turn-state.yaml` `unsupportedSince: { causeOffset, causeOperation, reason: queue_removal_ambiguous }`, and from then on every session-log verb in that host session reports `turn_logging_unsupported` and writes nothing to the server until the session ends, except that writes for a turn already open at that moment go to the plugin failsafe (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001). Turns that are open at that moment are left exactly as they are on the server, the stop gate blocks on each only until the model's close of it is captured in the plugin failsafe and then warns `queue_removal_ambiguous` listing them (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001), and the next SessionStart for the agent lists them with their original (MCP `sessionId`, `requestId`) pairs for closing through the trusted recovery flow (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC006). P0.2 records the `remove` identity fields; the bridge never uses them to pair with an `enqueue`. Only `queue-operation` lines are arrival records; every other record type is ignored by design. A `queue-operation` with an unknown operation or a missing required field is never skipped: the bridge stops before it without advancing `appliedThroughOffset`, records warning `arrival_bridge_format_unrecognized` and, after a delivered request, `unsupportedSince` with that reason and the record's `causeOffset` in the same atomic replace (unsupported-session rule), and the host session reports `turn_logging_unsupported` on every session-log verb (D6) until a verified adapter handles that shape, while writes for a turn already open go to the plugin failsafe (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001). Each record's state change and the advance of `appliedThroughOffset` past it are one atomic replace of `turn-state.yaml` (temp file, flush, rename, under `turn-open.lock`); records that start below `appliedThroughOffset` are skipped, so a restart never re-applies a record; the server mint for `enqueue` happens before that replace and is idempotent (same nonce, `merged`). Records are applied by whichever process holds `turn-open.lock`: the bridge, or a session-log verb, which first records the transcript length at the moment the invocation starts and, after taking the lock, applies every pending record that ends at or before that length, never a record appended later, before it binds (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-007-AC006), so a verb started after a `dequeue` line was written binds to that request, and a `dequeue` appended after the verb started never retargets it (D2). The prompt hook on claude-code and claude-cowork neither mints nor claims; it classifies system events and starts no turn. P0.2 verifies that every user prompt (in each prompt form of FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001-AC009) produces an `enqueue` record, that `dequeue` always takes the head, and that a request's `dequeue` (or its `remove` with `absorbed_mid_turn`) is in the transcript before that request's first tool call starts (section 3.1, Late `dequeue` on claude-code); if any fails, claude-code is `turn_logging_unsupported` (D6) until an adapter with a traceable key exists, recorded in the feature matrix.\n283:- Stop gate: blocks `turn_in_progress` for a delivered-ledger turn still in_progress (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-003-AC004), blocks `turn_withdrawn_unclosed` through the separate scan of `withdrawn` entries still in_progress, and only warns for a minted request still `queued` although its server turn is in_progress (`StopGateQueuedInProgressServerTurnNoBlockBeforeDeliveryWarns`); in a host session with `unsupportedSince`, a turn whose close is captured as an `unsupported_session` failsafe record is warned with the cause instead, and before that its remedy names only the missing model evidence, the missing `requestClass` (from S8/S9), and the close call, the terminal reviews being judged at the drain, FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001; outside `unsupportedSince`, a turn whose model close is held in the ordinary failsafe by a transport outage is warned as degraded persistence instead, and its ledger entry stays until the server confirms the close), `session_turn_completion_gate` (a turn whose most recent close the server refused with that code, recorded locally from the server response; such a turn is still in progress; no marker condition, FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC003), failed build after edits (existing; read from the `lastBuildStatus` of this host session's `turns/<requestId>.yaml` files, where code-verify writes it, section 4.1), stale session (existing); blocks `turn_withdrawn_unclosed` (a withdrawn queued request still in_progress); warns on exactly the FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC005 list (process EvaluateStopGate, section 4.4), whose retained failsafe records are a mint refused for a final reason, queued or quarantined, and an `open-failed` queue entry, each naming the prompt, and every quarantined record of an open turn with its reason; re-evaluates on every Stop, including `stop_hook_active=true`, blocking again while turns are open and recording `stop_gate_overridden_by_host` if the host stops anyway; passes with `{}` and clears closed entries. Auto-close removed. Block text: `session_turn_stop_gate: <n> turn(s) are not closed correctly:` then one line per turn `<id> (<code>): <remedy>`, then `The stop gate never closes turns for you.`; each turn's remedy accumulates every unmet prerequisite of that turn, in this order: for a turn whose most recent close the server refused with `session_turn_completion_gate`, the server's missing items first (in place of the locally computed `requestClass` and review items); at least one model-authored action, designDecision, or commit (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC002); `requestClass` (from S8/S9, when the plugin's requestClass and recordReview verbs ship); the terminal reviews the completion gate names (from S8/S9, when the plugin's requestClass and recordReview verbs ship, gate order; before S10 the server accepts those writes, so following the remedy literally still passes); then the single close call valid for its status: `workflow.sessionlog.failTurn` with reason 'withdrawn before delivery' for a withdrawn request, otherwise `workflow.sessionlog.completeTurn` (or `failTurn` if the work failed), passing requestId <id>. Following a turn's remedy literally makes the next Stop pass for that turn, including a Code turn that lacks both model evidence and terminal reviews (`StopGateEachRemedyFollowedLiterallyNextStopPasses`, `StopGateCodeTurnMissingEvidenceAndReviewsAccumulatedRemedyNextStopPasses`).\n344:- `SerializableWrite` (namespace `QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.Storage.Transactions`): runs a unit's checks and its save as one delegate in one Serializable transaction on SQLite, PostgreSQL, and SQL Server (the pattern of `SessionLogService.SubmitAsync`, `SessionLogService.cs:153-157`, and `RequirementsRecoveryService.cs:80-126`). Every unit that evaluates the completion gate, the terminal-state guard, a review rule, a response status, or an idempotency key and then saves runs through it, so a concurrent write to the same turn or response commits wholly before or after it. Per-response writes name their responses: the first statements increment each response's `StatusVersion` in ascending `ResponseId` order. Isolation scope (section 3.1, \"Turn-level isolation\"): a single-turn write reads its session row only to locate and validate the parent and writes only the one turn with its children; the session row's summary fields change only in the separate short write after the turn commits, whose failure never fails the turn write (`TurnWriteSummaryRefreshFailureNeverFailsTurnWrite`; develop refreshes them inside the turn's save, `SessionLogService.cs:762,794,879` at `a8d3934b`), so no two single-turn transactions write the same row, and writes to different turns of one session never answer a conflict (`TurnWriteConcurrentDifferentTurnsSameSessionBothCommitNoConflictAnswer`, dense-session fixture, three providers). Per provider: on SQL Server and PostgreSQL both transactions read the session row without updating it, so neither blocks the other on it, and a key-range or serialization conflict between two inserts near the same index position ends the loser's attempt, which SerializableWrite reruns within its bound; on SQLite the database write lock serializes the two writes, which wait within the busy timeout. A multi-turn submission keeps its session-level scope and refreshes the summary inside its own transaction. On the EF in-memory provider, which has no transactions, SerializableWrite runs the delegate once, as develop does (`SessionLogService.cs:155`). A caller-owned transaction is refused as develop refuses it today (`SessionLogService.cs:119-128`). A delegate reads and writes only the database: a unit reads the git commits, files, sources, and GraphRAG results it needs before SerializableWrite, and the delegate re-checks the database state those reads relied on (each locked response's `StatusVersion`, the turn's status); a changed state ends the attempt and the unit runs again from its reads, within the same bound. On SQLite a Serializable transaction holds the database write lock from its start, so no hook write waits on a retrieval or a git read (`BuildOnSqliteHookWriteDuringRetrievalNotBlocked`). A transient conflict, or a unique violation on a key the delegate declares (an idempotency key, a readable key), rolls the transaction back and runs the whole delegate again, at most three times (four attempts) unless the caller states another bound and exhaustion outcome (only the Phase A minter does, ReadableKeyAllocator below); then the unit returns 409 `conflict` with `retryable: true` and saves nothing. The attempts run within the unit's existing storage budget (FR-MCP-SESSIONLIFE-003). A refusal the delegate returns rolls back and is returned unchanged. A command dispatched from inside a delegate (for example DecideAgentResponseTrustCommand from a grade unit) joins the caller's transaction and retry; it never opens a second one. SharedComponents proves the component on a test entity it owns (`SerializableWriteForcedInterleavingTestEntityCommitsWhollyBeforeOrAfter`, `SerializableWriteRetriesExhausted409ConflictRetryableNothingSaved`); the turn cases belong to the gates of the contexts that own those units: `TurnWriteForcedInterleavingReviewResetVsCompleteNeverClosedWithoutReview`, `TurnWriteForcedInterleavingClassChangeVsCompleteGateSeesCommittedClass`, and `TurnWriteForcedInterleavingChoreFlipVsRunningWriteRunningKept`. Every case runs on SQLite, PostgreSQL, and SQL Server.\nEXIT_CODE=0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
