Integration, live checks, independent validation, operator approval\"]\n2459:     H6 --> VAL\n2460: ```\n2461: \n2462: - H0 (Phases 1 to 3 for the whole HV catalog): the requirement records and mappings of section 19 (created 2026-10-16 as `pending`) updated to the text the hostile validation and the operator's approval settle, and the use cases of section 18 created (store ids recorded beside the labels), all through the plugin's workflows; the import of FR, TR, and TEST-MCP-PATHNORM-001 into this workspace's store and the strengthened acceptance criteria, each with a named test, of the existing requirements through which P10 to P15 are fixed (section 20.4); the verification items of section 20.5; every interface, DTO, entity class, option, and stub of sections 10 to 16 compiling and unregistered, as the foundation's Phase 2 does (foundation section 8.2); every test of section 19 written from its acceptance criteria and proven against mocks with negative controls before any production code (the profile's BDPv4 procedure: tests and their mocks first, a green mock gate with every planned negative control rejected, then production code); the decision on the foundation amendments A-01 to A-12, which is a change to the approved PLAN-SESSIONTURNREVIEW-001 and needs its new hostile validation (section 1). The implementation worktree is cut from `develop` when Phase 2 starts (S-31), never from the foundation's worktree.\n2463: - HvStorageSchema: the entities of section 12, their configurations, migrations for SQLite, PostgreSQL, and SQL Server (Up, Down, Up with schema and data equal, as the foundation's StorageSchema), the two turn columns of A-11 with their database default and their migration rule for turns already reviewed (FR-MCP-HVPIPE-012-AC007), the `AgentResponseGrade` change of A-05, the dump policies, and the federation domain; after the foundation's StorageSchema. Before it creates any HV entity, it fixes the workspace-key problems P10, P12, and P13 (D-07, section 20.4), because every HV entity is keyed by `WorkspaceId` first (section 12).\n2464: - HvSharedComponents: sections 11.1 to 11.8, each with its one test class.\n2465: - H1 HvReviewerPool: options and seeding, AgentIdentity with its golden contract, the reviewer adapters with the shared launch builder (its one table of highest efforts, P9), the true live-line path (problem P8), the record extraction and the write barriers of section 16.3, the Copilot qualification (P6), the PowerShell plugin core fixes P11, P14, and P15, synced to every plugin that carries the core, before FR-MCP-HVPIPE-008-AC010 is bound to the real hosts (D-07, section 20.4), StartHvReviewerNonceCommand, ReadinessProbeVerdict, the reviewer queries and update.\n2466: - H2 HvAdmission: SubmitHvRequestCommand with its policies, TurnSnapshotBuilder use, the freeze guard wired into TurnWritePipeline and every unit of A-07, the request queries, CancelHvRequestCommand.\n2467: - H3 HvExecution: HvWorkerHost, RunReviewerProcess, the claim, heartbeat, append, close, fail, and expiry units, FileHvIncidentCommand, and HvRetryDecision.\n2468: - H7 HvFinalization: HvFinalVerification, FinalizeHvRequestCommand with CommitFinalResult, the amended RecordSessionTurnReviewCommand and RecordModelGradeCommand callers (A-05, A-06), and the retirement of ConvertHvReceiptToReview. It precedes the public surfaces, because no review result may be published before finalization is atomic (handoff, H7).\n2469: - H4 HvRestSurfaces: HostileValidationController with the error mapping.\n2470: - H5 HvClientParity: the client, REPL verbs and policy classification, MCP tools, Director commands, and the plugin verbs and watcher in the PowerShell core, synced to every plugin that carries the core (foundation section 3.2), with their golden contracts and documentation.\n2471: - H6 HvStreamsAndOperations: the two streams, the notification poll, the incident query, evidence retrieval and removal, the read audit rows, and the operations documentation (recovery, NONCE, removal).\n2472: \n2473: ### 20.2 Gates\n2474: \n2475: The foundation's gates apply unchanged (foundation section 8.4): G1 targeted tests, G2 the unit gate, G3 the integration suites with the three-provider cases, G4 the PowerShell plugin core, G5 configuration and traceability (strict), G6 the plugin suites, every one through Nuke, with zero failures and zero skips in the executed scope; the full unit suite of the current and earlier work passes before a context is left (AGENTS.md, \"Byrd Test Gate\"). The cases marked \"three providers\" in section 19 run in G3. The live check runs on a separate server instance started for it, never on a shared server (foundation section 8.6): it NONCEs every reviewer and records which pass and why (VI-01), submits a session-scope HV, watches the output stream receive lines while the reviewer runs (the end-to-end form of FR-MCP-HVPIPE-008-AC003), kills the worker mid-run and observes the lease recovery, cancels a second request, finalizes the first, reads its result and evidence, and confirms that writes to its turns are refused. Build, test, and deploy run through Nuke; releases through Octopus; GitHub Actions and Azure Pipelines are no part of any gate.\n2476: \n2477: ### 20.3 Implementation discipline\n2478: \n2479: - Each implementation slice stops at `Validating`; the coordinator decides completion after the independent validation agrees (handoff, \"Validation and completion gate\").\n2480: - Every hostile validation of this plan's work is run by the reviewer the operator names, never of the requesting agent's family (P-01), passes only at accuracy and completeness of at least 98 with no FAIL or UNKNOWN (P-05), has unbounded review time (P-02), and keeps its request and response jsonl and its entire result in the session log (the operator's HV receipts rules).\n2481: - The new pipeline does not validate its own plan's work until its live check has passed and the operator approves the switch (handoff: \"Do not prematurely use the new HV pipeline to validate itself\").\n2482: - No code is written for this plan until the operator approves it; the standing code freeze applies (section 1).\n2483: \n2484: ### 20.4 Problems pulled into the plan\n2485: \n2486: REMED-HVPIPELINE-001 (section 21) is pulled into the plan as follows: P5 and P7 in H0 (the foundation text and pins change with A-01 to A-03, and the requirement id cited by `EventStreamController` is corrected before H6 builds on that controller); P6, P8, and P9 in H1; P4 in HvSharedComponents (AuditLedgerGuard) and HvStorageSchema (`IsDeleted` on every HV entity); P1, P2, and P3 in H3 (D-02 settled: the HostileReview subsystem stays separate), where the worker FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW-003 already specifies is built on HvWorkerHost and the reviewer adapters, and P2 and P3 are fixed in that subsystem. P10 to P15 (the findings of the Codex review of pull request 81) sit in code that other plans own or have redesigned, and this plan fixes all six (D-07, settled), each under the owning plan's approved design and through its existing requirement, whose acceptance criteria H0 strengthens with a named test for the case the problem shows:\n2487: \n2488: - P10, P12, and P13 in HvStorageSchema, before any HV entity is created, because every HV entity is keyed by `WorkspaceId` first (section 12). P10 and P13 are fixed as the federation plan's P1.5 decided them: an explicit rekey maintenance command with its startup guard and the token-store key rewrite (its D5 and D7, `docs/plans/2026-10-07-federation-fixes.md:166-175`), never an automatic startup migration and never a dual-key lookup; P12 in the normalizer. Their requirements are FR, TR, and TEST-MCP-PATHNORM-001, which this workspace's store does not hold (requirement reads of 2026-10-17: `not_found`) and the federation handoff places in the `F:\\GitHub\\McpServer` store (`docs/handoffs/federation-fixes-handoff-20261016T121400.md:97`); H0 imports them into this workspace's store through the requirements workflow, with their stored text, before strengthening them.\n2489: - P11, P14, and P15 in H1, in the PowerShell plugin core and synced to every plugin that carries it, before FR-MCP-HVPIPE-008-AC010 (a reviewer closes its own turn and exits under the Stop gate) is bound to the real hosts: P11 and P15 break the Stop path that criterion runs through, and P14 is in the REPL wrapper that every hook call runs through (`plugins/core/lib-ps/plugin-hook.ps1:230`, `plugins/core/lib-ps/repl-invoke.ps1:771,959`). P11 and P15 follow the foundation's stop gate, which blocks while a delivered request is in progress and never closes a turn (its FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005, slice A7), never Codex's suggestion to complete the turn in the hook; their requirements are FR-MCP-SESSIONLIFE-004 and FR-MCP-SESSIONLIFE-005. P14's is FR-MCP-SESSIONLIFE-004-AC001.\n2490: - Where an owning plan has already delivered the same fix under its approved design when the phase starts, the phase proves it with the named test and does not change that code a second time (the DRY ruling).\nEXIT_CODE=0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_164","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -Command \"pwsh.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -File './docs/receipts/hv/hv-r1-inspect.ps1' -Task Read -Path './docs/architecture/SessionTurn-Review-AgentContext-Architecture-Amendment-1-Durable-HV.md' -Start 143 -Count 32\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_164","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -Command \"pwsh.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -File './docs/receipts/hv/hv-r1-inspect.ps1' -Task Read -Path './docs/architecture/SessionTurn-Review-AgentContext-Architecture-Amendment-1-Durable-HV.md' -Start 143 -Count 32\"","aggregated_output":"SOURCE ./docs/architecture/SessionTurn-Review-AgentContext-Architecture-Amendment-1-Durable-HV.md totalLines=2785\n143: - R-01 One bounded context. HostileValidation owns the pipeline and is not gated by `Mcp:AgentContext:Mode`: HV of turns works in every Mode, because the operator's HV requirements (S-01 to S-30) name turns, not agent-context responses. Rejected: extending the foundation's `AgentContextHvRequest` in ResponsesAndTrust (draft 103), because that record is mode-gated (foundation section 3.5.1) and lists responses, not turns, so a turn with no response would silently drop out of review (draft 213, \"turn coverage versus response coverage\").\n144: - R-02 The HV request replaces the foundation's agent-context HV request. `HvRequest` keeps the foundation's key format `hvr-<sessionId>-<yyyyMMddTHHmmssZ>` (with `-<nn>` from the second key of one second, ReadableKeyAllocator) and lists turns (`HvRequestTurn`); the agent responses of those turns are linked as `HvRequestResponse` items when Phase C has recorded any. CreateAgentContextHvRequestCommand, GetAgentContextHvRequestQuery, QueryAgentContextHvRequestsQuery, and their entities are superseded (amendment A-04). Rejected: keeping two HV request records (one per turn, one per response), which would need a cross-record consistency rule the foundation never needed.\n145: - R-03 Model grades come from finalization only. FinalizeHvRequestCommand dispatches the foundation's RecordModelGradeCommand for each linked response inside its own transaction; the public model-grade route, the two-step `codex exec resume` protocol, and `ModelGrader:*` are removed, and the eight receipt checks become the verification steps V-1 to V-8 of policy HvFinalVerification against evidence the server itself captured (amendment A-05). Rationale: the pipeline launches the reviewer and records its complete output, so the server no longer has to trust receipts written by a runner it did not control. Rejected: keeping the reviewer-submitted grade route beside the pipeline, which would let a grade bypass reviewer exclusion and atomic finalization (S-17, S-23).\n146: - R-04 Freeze at admission, finality at finalization (S-06, S-07). Admission sets each member turn's HV state to `Pending` in the same transaction that creates the request; finalization sets it to `Final`. Every write to a `Pending` or `Final` turn or its children, from every surface and origin, is refused with 409 `turn_immutable` and `details.reason` `hv_pending` or `hv_final` (policy HvTurnFreeze), except the one write of finalization itself (origin `HvFinalization`). A request that ends `Canceled` or `Failed` releases its turns back to `None`. Rationale: the reviewed content equals the finalized content without a content comparison race, and the operator's \"may not allow updates\" is one guard in the existing write path. Rejected: letting writes continue and refusing finalization on a changed snapshot hash, which would waste completed reviews and still leave a window between the last check and the commit.\n147: - R-05 Only terminal turns are admitted. A member turn that is not terminal (`TurnStatus.IsTerminal`, foundation section 3.5.3) refuses the whole request with 409 `hv_turns_not_terminal`, naming each turn. The submitting turn is never a member: a session scope excludes it and reports the exclusion in the result, and a turn or range scope that names it is refused with 400 `hv_scope_includes_submitting_turn` (the foundation's rule that \"the request's own turn is never a candidate, a dependency, or cited evidence\", foundation section 3.1). A Code or Docs turn that needs HV before it can complete is completed first with `hostileReview` `Other` (for example \"HV requested hvr-...\"), which the foundation already allows and never counts as an AGREE (foundation C8); finalization then replaces that `Other` with the HV result. Rationale: no turn changes status after it is frozen, so there is no close-inside-finalization path. Rejected: admitting in-progress turns with a declared close intent (draft 213, section 3.B), which adds a mechanism and still freezes a turn the model is working in.\n148: - R-06 Hostile reviews are written only by the pipeline. On agent surfaces a `hostileReview` write may carry only `status` `Other` (with its required `otherNotes`); a write naming `Pass`, `Fail`, or `Running`, or carrying `hvVerdict`, is refused with 403 `review_hostile_pipeline_only` (amendment A-06). Process ConvertHvReceiptToReview is retired. Rationale: a manual Pass or Fail would bypass reviewer exclusion, complete evidence, and atomic finalization, and no surface may bypass them (draft 213, H5, adopted). Rejected: keeping the manual route for emergencies; a reviewer can be enabled and checked instead (S-27).\n149: - R-07 Assignment at admission, durable round-robin. The submit transaction picks the next eligible reviewer after the workspace's round-robin cursor and advances the cursor in the same SerializableWrite (policy RoundRobinAssignment), so assignment survives restarts and two concurrent submissions never pick from the same cursor value. When the assigned reviewer is no longer eligible at claim time (offline, disabled), the claim reassigns through the same policy and records the reassignment. Rejected: assignment at claim only, which hides the assignee from the submitter and lets claim order, not round-robin order, pick reviewers.\n150: - R-08 One agent identity, server-attributed. `AgentIdentity` (namespace `QBrainAi.Client.Agents`, shared by the server, the REPL, and, through the golden contract, the PowerShell core) maps every known alias to one of the five reviewer ids: Claude (`claude`, `claudecode`, `claude-code`, `claudecowork`, `claude-cowork`, `cowork`), Codex (`codex`), Copilot (`copilot`), Grok (`grok`, `grokcode`, `grok-code`, `grokbot`, `grok-bot`), Cline (`cline`, `cline-v2`, `clinev2`), case-insensitively and ignoring separators. The submitter is the agent of the submitting turn, located by its full turn key (TurnLocator); a free-text name never overrides it. The author is the agent of the reviewed session (one request names turns of one MCP session, R-26), stored normalized as `AuthorReviewerId`. A reviewer whose id equals the submitter's or the author's is never eligible (S-17, S-28, P-01, P-07). Develop holds three alias maps with different canonical forms (`src/QBrainAi.Repl.Host/MarkerFileClientOptionsResolver.cs:64-81`, `src/QBrainAi.Repl.Core/TodoSelectionStore.cs:166-171`, the plugin's `Get-ReplCanonicalAgentName`); they are context under the DRY ruling and are not changed by this amendment. Exclusion is by agent family, not by model vendor (D-01, settled): S-17 names \"the agent submitting the review\", P-01 constrains the agent family and asks separately for the agent and the model, and the operator runs Cline on `gpt-6-astra` (provider `openai-codex`, VI-01 receipt) while keeping Cline and Codex as two reviewers (S-02); a model a reviewer shares with the submitter stays visible in the run's provenance, and the operator can disable that reviewer (UpdateHvReviewerCommand). Exclusion covers the author as well as the submitter (D-05, settled): a review by the agent that wrote the turns is a self-review, which S-28 rules out of HV and P-07 rules out of validation.\n151: - R-09 Operator submissions. A caller with no session-log turn (Director, an operator at the REPL) submits with `submitter.kind` `Operator` and a name; no reviewer is excluded for the submitter, and the author of the reviewed session is still excluded (R-08). Agent surfaces (plugin verbs, MCP tools, the REPL workflow verb) always send the submitting turn's key, which the plugin binds automatically (foundation process BindVerbToTurn). Limit (section 8): every caller holds the same workspace key, so the server cannot prove that an `Operator` submission did not come from an agent.\n152: - R-10 Readiness through NONCE (S-15, S-27). A reviewer is `Unverified`, `Online`, or `Offline`, and separately enabled or disabled. \"NONCE the agent\" is a probe run of the reviewer through its adapter with a fresh random nonce: it passes when the reviewer answers with the probe record that echoes the nonce and the adapter's runtime provenance shows the reviewer's resolved model and effort (P-04). Only a passing NONCE moves a reviewer to `Online`. An execution failure moves it to `Offline` until a later NONCE passes. The worker host NONCEs every enabled `Unverified` reviewer at startup and after a configuration change; an `Offline` reviewer is NONCEd only when a caller asks (S-15: \"until resolved\"). Rejected: a liveness ping or the server health nonce, which S-27 rules out.\n153: - R-11 No execution time limit (P-02). A run has no timeout and no deadline. Liveness is the worker's lease, renewed by heartbeats; a stalled run stays visible (`lastOutputUtc`, the last progress record) and anyone can cancel it (S-18). The only expiry is the lease of a dead worker (R-13). This replaces the 30-minute `ExecutionTimeout` of the separate HostileReview options (`src/QBrainAi.Services/Options/HostileReviewWorkerOptions.cs:45-46`) for HV runs only. The process exit code is never the outcome of a run: the reviewer's records decide it (V-8 for a review, policy ReadinessProbeVerdict for a probe), because a CLI can end a refused or empty run with exit code 0 (VI-03: a headless Grok run whose write call was refused ended `cancelled` with exit 0, and two Copilot permission variants exited 0 with no output).\n154: - R-12 True live output (S-22, P8). Each reviewer adapter delivers every output line as the process writes it, through a line callback, never after the process ends; the worker appends lines in batches (at most `OutputFlushInterval`, default 250 ms, or `OutputFlushBytes`, default 64 KiB, whichever comes first) through AppendHvRunOutputCommand, which stores each batch as one GZIP chunk and wakes the streams. The output stream replays stored chunks and then follows new ones (shared component OrderedEventReplay). Develop's one-shot strategy yields its output only after completion (`src/QBrainAi.Services/Services/OneShotCliAgentExecutionStrategy.cs:57-75`), so the adapters need a real line path (section 21, P8). Acceptance criterion FR-MCP-HVPIPE-008-AC003 makes the difference testable: a line is observable on the stream while the reviewer process is still running. The reviewer's records are read from each agent message's complete text, assembled from the CLI's native events (Grok's `streaming-json` sends message text one token per event, and Copilot sends deltas and then the whole message, as the probe runs of 2026-10-16 show), by one extraction function that the worker runs as lines arrive and finalization runs over the stored log (section 16.3), so a record split across events is read exactly once and both read the same records (FR-MCP-HVPIPE-010-AC006).\n155: - R-13 Leases and fences (handoff requirement 10). A run is owned through a lease (owner, expiry) and a fence token that increases on every claim and takeover, the pattern develop already uses for handoff ingestion (owner and expiry with a `StateVersion` takeover, `src/QBrainAi.Services/Services/HandoffIngestionService.cs:724-735`, and a fenced terminal write that matches owner and version, `:782-807`). Every worker write carries the fence; a write with a stale fence changes nothing and returns `stale_fence`, and the worker that receives it stops its process. Expired leases are swept (ExpireHvRunLeasesCommand): the attempt becomes `Abandoned` and the request is queued again until `MaxAttemptsPerRequest` (default 3) is spent, then `Failed`, or `Canceled` when a cancel was requested; policy HvRetryDecision writes that outcome, with its turn release and its notification, for the sweep exactly as for FailHvRunCommand. Rejected: the unused lease options of the HostileReview subsystem (`HostileReviewWorkerOptions.cs:30-46`), which no code reads (section 21, P1).\n156: - R-14 Evidence in the relational store, GZIP at rest (S-13, S-29, S-30). The accepted request is one GZIP blob written at admission; the response log is the run's ordered GZIP chunks; the result document is one GZIP blob written at finalization. No uncompressed copy of an evidence item is stored in the evidence tables, and each evidence item is stored there once (the structured rows of section 12, such as the request's `Context` and the findings, are records the units derive, not copies of the evidence blobs). The audit ledger keeps its own history of every evidence write, as it does for every audited entity: develop's ledger snapshots every property of an audited entity, binary columns included (`src/QBrainAi.Storage/McpDbContext.cs:1540-1589`), compressed with GZIP (`src/QBrainAi.Storage/Entities/DataAuditLogEntity.cs:119-147`), and a removal never changes it (R-20, I-13). Retrieval returns `application/gzip` (download name `<hvRequestId>-<kind>.jsonl.gz` or `.json.gz`) or, when asked, the decompressed bytes. The pattern is develop's audit ledger, which stores GZIP payloads in the database (`src/QBrainAi.Storage/Entities/DataAuditLogEntity.cs:66-147`). Rationale: S-23 (\"Single trx at end\") and S-29 (the database audit log) both hold only when the evidence is in the same transactional store as the result, and no crash window between a file system and the database remains. Rejected: `.jsonl.gz` files beside the database, which would need a two-store protocol, keep evidence outside the audit ledger, and split backups.\n157: - R-15 Atomic finalization (S-23). FinalizeHvRequestCommand verifies the evidence and the reviewer output before it opens a transaction, then commits in one SerializableWrite: the result document, the per-turn assessments, the holistic evaluation, the failures, each turn's `hostileReview` through policy HvVerdictMapping (origin `HvFinalization`), each turn's `Final` HV state, the linked responses' model grades and trust decisions (when Mode is not `Off`), the request's `Completed` state, its notification, and the audit rows. The transaction re-checks everything the earlier reads relied on (the run's fence, the chunk manifest, the request and turn states). Crash windows are in section 9.\n158: - R-16 Transactional notification outbox (S-19). Every state change that callers observe writes an `HvNotification` row in its own transaction, numbered by a per-workspace sequence that the same transaction advances, so the sequence has no gaps and no row is visible before its change commits. The notification stream replays rows after the caller's `Last-Event-ID` and then follows new ones (OrderedEventReplay). Delivery is at least once; consumers deduplicate by sequence. `IChangeEventBus` (develop's in-memory bus, `src/QBrainAi.Services/Notifications/IChangeEventBus.cs:4`) is used only to wake waiting streams and the worker, never as the record.\n159: - R-17 Incidents through the triage endpoint (S-15, S-26). An execution failure writes an `HvIncident` row in the failure's transaction; FileHvIncidentCommand then submits it through `ITriageService` with its `dedupeKey` (`hv-reviewer-<reviewerId>-<failureCode>` for a failure attributable to the reviewer, `hv-server-<failureCode>` otherwise, section 12), which triage groups as `dedupe:<key>` (`src/QBrainAi.Services/Services/TriageService.cs:1475-1476`), and records the returned report and group ids. A filing that fails stays `Pending` and is retried by the worker host's sweep. Rejected: new incident tables with their own deduplication (S-26: \"File through endpoint\").\n160: - R-18 Honest ETA (S-18, P-03). The ETA a caller sees is the reviewer's own latest progress record (`percentComplete`, `etaSeconds`), with `etaStatus` `Reported` and the record's time; with no progress record it is `etaStatus` `Unknown` and no number. The server never estimates.\n161: - R-19 Audit participation and hard stop (S-29). Every HV entity carries `IsDeleted`, so `McpDbContext` writes its ledger rows for every HV mutation in the same `SaveChanges` (develop audits only entities with that property, `src/QBrainAi.Storage/McpDbContext.cs:1620-1627`). The ledger is append-only for HV: no HV unit or process updates or deletes a ledger row (I-13). Because develop skips the ledger when the audit table is unavailable (`McpDbContext.cs:1507-1538`), every HV unit first calls the shared component AuditLedgerGuard, which refuses with 503 `audit_unavailable` and writes nothing when the ledger cannot be written. Evidence reads (GetHvEvidenceQuery, GetHvResultQuery, and the output stream) write an explicit `read` ledger row before returning bytes, and a read whose row cannot be written is refused (the \"protected-log access\" of draft 213 participates in the audit log, S-29).\n162: - R-20 Retention until removal (S-29). No unit deletes, expires, or compacts HV evidence except RemoveHvEvidenceCommand, an explicit call that removes the evidence bytes of one request, keeps its result rows, hashes, and sizes, records who removed it and why, and is audited. It clears the evidence tables only and never changes or deletes an audit ledger row: the ledger keeps the history of the evidence's writes and gains the record of the removal, because an audit log is never purged (I-13). There are no roles: any caller with the workspace key can call it, as S-29 requires.\n163: - R-21 Self-review independence (S-28). No HV unit, policy, or process reads `agentReview`. The turn snapshot sent to a reviewer omits both review records (shared component TurnSnapshotBuilder), so a reviewer never sees a self-review grade. S-12 (a plugin list of missing agent reviews) is met by the foundation's Stop-gate remedy, which already lists each open turn's missing `agentReview` (foundation section 3.4, Stop gate, and `Get-CloseRemedy`, section 3.5.6), and is not an HV input. Reconciliation with the foundation's completion gate: HV admits only terminal turns (R-05), and the foundation's gate needs an `agentReview` status of `Pass`, `Fail`, or `Other` before a turn can close `completed` (foundation section 5.3, CompletionPolicy); that is a rule of closing a turn, not of HV, it is met by `Other` with a note as well as by a grade, and a turn closed `failed` or `canceled` needs no agent review at all; HV admission, execution, and verdict read none of it, and FR-MCP-HVPIPE-013 proves that every terminal turn is admitted and judged identically whatever its self-review.\n164: - R-22 Verdict only (S-25). No HV unit writes TODO state. A result names the reviewed turns' `todoId` values for information only.\n165: - R-23 Requirement references (S-14). The submitted context is stored as written (Markdown, YAML, or text, declared by `contextFormat`) and parsed for requirement references: FR, TR, and TEST ids of the form `<KIND>-<AREA>-<NNN>` with optional further dash-separated area segments, acceptance criteria written as `<FR id>-AC<NNN>` or `<FR id> ac-<n>`, and use cases by their store id or exact title. Each reference is resolved against the workspace's effective requirements and use cases and handed to the reviewer as a delineated list with its resolution status; an unresolved reference is listed as unresolved, never refused (shared component RequirementReferenceExtractor).\n166: - R-24 The reviewer's own turn (P-06). The brief tells the reviewer to log its review as its own session-log turn, classified `Chore` so that its turn never needs an HV of its own (foundation completion policy: Chore needs no hostile review), and to store its entire result there. Finalization requires that turn to exist, to be persisted (not a failsafe or queued record), to have been created after the run started, and to contain the run's `hv_verdict` record byte for byte in its stored normal form (V-6). A reviewer whose host cannot log turns fails its NONCE with `reviewer_turn_logging_unsupported`. The reviewer's plugin runs as a host session of its own: a review or probe run never reads or changes the plugin state or the session-log session of a live session of the same agent in the workspace (FR-MCP-HVPIPE-008-AC008), which matters because a reviewer starts in the workspace (section 16.2) and Grok's headless hooks write their host state under the working directory's `.mcpServer/grok/` (VI-04).\n167: - R-25 The HostileReview subsystem stays separate. FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW-001 to -006 (its queue, artifact links, findings, and query surface) are not changed by this amendment, as the foundation already states (foundation section 5.4); the problems found in it (section 21, P1 to P3) are tracked in REMED-HVPIPELINE-001. It stays separate (D-02, settled): it reviews artifacts (TODO, FR/TR/TEST, plans, files, commits; FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW-002) while this pipeline reviews session turns (S-03), the approved foundation keeps it separate, and consolidating it is scope the operator has not named. Its missing worker (P1) is built on this amendment's execution components (process HvWorkerHost and the reviewer adapters), so reviewer execution exists once (the DRY ruling).\n168: - R-26 Scope limits. One request names turns of one MCP session. `MaxTurnsPerRequest` (default 200, the foundation's `HvRequest:MaxTurns` default) bounds every scope; a session scope above it is refused with 400 `hv_scope_too_large` naming the count, never truncated.\n169: - R-27 One open review per turn. A turn is `Pending` in at most one open request (a unique filtered index on the turn key where the item is `Pending`); admission over a pending turn is refused with 409 `hv_turns_pending` naming the turns and their requests, and over a final turn with 409 `hv_turns_already_reviewed` (S-06, S-20).\n170: - R-28 Cancellation by anyone (S-18). `Queued` becomes `Canceled` at once; `Running` becomes `CancelRequested`, and the worker stops the process at its next heartbeat and closes the run `Canceled`; `Finalizing`, `Completed`, `Failed`, and `Canceled` refuse with 409 `hv_request_not_cancelable`. A cancel and a finalization of the same request are serialized on the request row, so exactly one of them commits.\n171: - R-29 Worker units are internal. ClaimHvRunCommand, RecordHvRunHeartbeatCommand, AppendHvRunOutputCommand, CloseHvRunOutputCommand, FailHvRunCommand, ExpireHvRunLeasesCommand, FinalizeHvRequestCommand, and FileHvIncidentCommand have no REST route, MCP tool, or client method; only the worker host dispatches them, in-process, through `IDispatcher`. The public mutations are submit, cancel, reviewer update, NONCE, and evidence removal.\n172: - R-30 Plugins consume notifications through the REPL. Plugins never call REST (foundation section 3); the REPL gains a streaming verb that follows the notification stream, and the PowerShell core runs it in the background per host session (process WatchHvNotifications), keeping the last sequence in the host session's state and handing completions of requests that host session submitted to the next prompt as context.\n173: - R-31 The 98/98 bar for the overall verdict (P-05). The overall verdict is `AGREE` only when the holistic correctness and completeness both meet `max(configured hostile threshold, 0.98)` (ScoreVerdict, foundation section 3.5.4) and no failure is FAIL or UNKNOWN; per-turn hostile reviews keep the foundation's configured hostile thresholds through HvVerdictMapping.\n174: \nEXIT_CODE=0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
