{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_184","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -Command \"pwsh.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -File './docs/receipts/hv/hv-r1-inspect.ps1' -Task Native -CommandName rg -ArgumentText 'FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005|Close-PluginTurnIfNeeded|NOT APPROVED|APPROVED|D5|D7:::docs/receipts/hv/hv-r1-store-PLAN-SESSIONTURNREVIEW-001.txt:::-n:::-m:::8'\"","aggregated_output":"10:    note: \"APPROVED by the operator on 2026-10-10T16:19:44Z (\\\"Plan approved\\\"), after plan-readiness HV round 43 (AGREE, accuracy 99, completeness 98, every claim PASS; receipts committed in 465bf4e2). Amended 2026-10-10 for the operator's Build.Tests and Nuke rulings, the \\\"Separate Nuke-only TRs\\\" decision, and the npm lockfile ruling (operator-approved approach; HV-P0 checks the amendment). Execution follows the plan from P0; slice TODOs are created in P0.8; nothing is marked done without its hostile AGREE. Before that: NOT APPROVED (operator, 2026-10-09).\"\n13:    - \"Status: APPROVED (operator, 2026-10-10T16:19:44Z: \\\"Plan approved\\\"), after plan-readiness HV round 43 (AGREE, accuracy 99, completeness 98, every claim PASS; receipts committed in 465bf4e2). Before that: NOT APPROVED (operator, 2026-10-09). Execution proceeds step by step from P0 under the gates below. Amended 2026-10-10 for two operator rulings (the Build.Tests infrastructure step and TR-only accountability for Nuke code in TR-MCP-NUKE-003 and TR-MCP-NUKE-004 under the build-tooling FR FR-MCP-BUILD-001, both quoted in the BDPv4 contract) and the operator's npm lockfile ruling (quoted in TR-MCP-NUKE-003 AC-004), under the operator-approved approach; HV-P0 checks the amendment. Persisted as MCP TODO `PLAN-SESSIONTURNREVIEW-001` (priority high); handoff `docs/handoffs/sessionturn-review-plan-20261009T022141Z.md`.\"\n32:    - \"- Unsupported-session failsafe (operator, 2026-10-10: \\\"If QBrain is truly not available, use the failsafe already in the plugin.\\\" and, choosing between a failsafe capture and a warn-only Stop, \\\"1\\\"): when a host session fails closed under D6 (`unsupportedSince`), the model's session-log writes for a turn that was already open at that moment go to the plugin's existing failsafe instead of being refused (reviews excepted: they keep their no-failsafe rule and are recorded after the drain through the original pair), so the Stop gate's remedy stays accepted; the captured records drain at the next SessionStart through the trusted recovery flow with the turn's original pair (C5); every mint and every write for any other turn stays refused (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001).\"\n33:    - \"- Clear-session closing rule (operator, 2026-10-10, choosing option A over leaving blocked and withdrawn turns open: \\\"A\\\"): clear-session completes each turn that meets the completion policy, fails each withdrawn turn (reason \\\"withdrawn before delivery\\\") and each turn the model marks blocked (with its blocker reason; `failed` requires `requestClass` only, FR-MCP-SESSIONCLASS-002-AC002), and leaves every other turn in progress, reported by requestId with its exact missing items, never fabricating reviews (TR-MCP-CLEARSESSION-001-AC001). Definition (catalog cross-reference, not a change to the decision): \\\"marked blocked\\\" means the turn's `blockers` section (the existing decision-list field, FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-006-AC001) holds at least one nonblank model-authored entry, read by the clear-session coordinator for that turn by its original (`sessionId`, `requestId`) pair, and the first entry is the fail reason; precedence per turn: withdrawn -> `failTurn` (\\\"withdrawn before delivery\\\"); else blocked -> `failTurn` with the blocker reason (blocked takes precedence over a ready completion); else meets the completion policy -> `completeTurn`; else stays in progress, reported with its exact missing items. Close prerequisites (catalog cross-reference, not a change to the decision): before clear-session fails a withdrawn or blocked turn, the model writes by that turn's original pair, within the same clear-session run and before its session-end step, every prerequisite the plugin and the server require for that close, in the Stop gate remedy order (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC009): a designDecision when the turn has no model evidence (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC002), then a `requestClass` when it is missing or `Unclassified`, judged from the turn's `queryText`; the coordinator then fails the turn with its reason (TR-MCP-CLEARSESSION-001-AC001).\"\n35:    - \"- Queued-message arrival (operator D5, 2026-10-09: \\\"Arrival bridge\\\"): a message the operator sends while a turn is running is minted when it is enqueued, not when the model later processes it. On hosts that record the enqueue in a transcript (Claude Code), a per-host-session arrival bridge started by the SessionStart hook tails the transcript and follows each queued message through the lifecycle the transcript records. Observed in this workspace's Claude Code transcripts on 2026-10-09: 117 `enqueue`, 79 `dequeue`, 35 `remove` (27 with reason `absorbed_mid_turn`), 2 `popAll`; a normal prompt is `enqueue` then `dequeue` within milliseconds, a mid-turn message is `enqueue` then `remove` with `absorbed_mid_turn` when it is injected into the running turn (for example the operator's mid-turn message beginning \\\"write a global memory with the\\\" was removed with `absorbed_mid_turn` at 2026-10-09T14:35:47.730Z), and `popAll` pulls queued text back into the input box. The bridge mints at `enqueue` (state `queued`); the request becomes `delivered` at `dequeue` (applied by the bridge from the transcript itself; on hosts with a bridge the prompt hook neither mints nor claims) or at `remove` with `absorbed_mid_turn`; it becomes `withdrawn` at `popAll` or a `remove` with any other or no reason. Task notifications and other system events that the host also enqueues are classified and never minted; on bridge hosts they keep their queue position (`kind: system`), so their `dequeue` never delivers a user request.\"\n37:    - \"- Plugin equivalence (operator D7, 2026-10-09, confirmed in chat \\\"1 yes\\\" for the requirement and its qualification posted by Codex in `MEMORY-PROCESS-007` at 17:48:48Z and 17:52:06Z): all QBrain.AI plugins keep equivalent functionality; shared capabilities, contracts, fixes, and behavior changes go to every plugin; host-specific adaptations preserve equivalent supported behavior; equivalence is limited by what each host's plugin infrastructure can support, no extraordinary effort is spent forcing unworkable equivalence, and each such limit is documented as a feature-specific capability limitation instead of being claimed as equivalent. A D6 `turn_logging_unsupported` host is such a documented limitation. The limitations live in `docs/AGENT-PLUGIN-FEATURE-MATRIX.md` (one row per plugin and feature: supported, adapted, or limited, with the reason and the receipt), first written in P0.8 from the P0.2 receipts, updated in A9 and S11, and checked by a docs contract test.\"\n41:    - \"- Copilot duplicate deliveries (operator D11, 2026-10-09: \\\"Supported only if single hook\\\"): Copilot gives no stable per-prompt id and separately registered hooks may see different `timestamp` values, so Copilot turn logging is supported only while the plugin's duplicate check proves exactly one MCP prompt hook across all Copilot hook sources; otherwise the Copilot prompt hook reports `turn_logging_unsupported` before any mint, and the condition is a D7 documented limitation until fixed. One Copilot prompt therefore never becomes two turns.\"\n76:    - 'Host binding table (locks the D1 nonce source, the D5 arrival source, and the D2 host session key per host; every turn token is still minted by the server). Rules common to all hosts:'\nEXIT_CODE=0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
