), `SupersededByResponseId` (128).\n2782: - `ContentFormat` (16): `markdown`, `text`, or `json`. `Content` (text, sanitized), `ContentSha256` (64, over the stored normal form of `Content`, so a reviewer that hashes the content it reads from the server gets this value, `SubmitContentWithEmDashContentSha256MatchesStoredContent`).\n2783: - `CommitSha` (64): the workspace HEAD commit at submission (`SourceResolver.ReadCommit`; unreadable is 409 `agent_context_commit_unavailable`, `SubmitCommitUnreadable409CommitUnavailable`).\n2784: - `SubmittedUtc`, `StatusChangedUtc`, `TrustedUtc`.\n2785: - Indexes: (`WorkspaceId`, `Status`), (`WorkspaceId`, `RequestId`), (`WorkspaceId`, `PackageId`), unique (`WorkspaceId`, `IdempotencyKey`).\n2786: - Children: `Claims`, `Evidence`, each through the composite foreign key (`WorkspaceId`, `ResponseId`).\n2787: \n2788: `AgentResponseClaimEntity` (table `AgentResponseClaims`, `long Id`): `WorkspaceId`, `ResponseId` (FK (`WorkspaceId`, `ResponseId`)), `PackageId` (128), `Agent` (64), `SessionId` (256), `RequestId` (256) (root ids), `Ordinal`, `ClaimId` (64), `Text` (4096); unique (`WorkspaceId`, `ResponseId`, `ClaimId`) and unique (`WorkspaceId`, `ResponseId`, `Ordinal`).\n2789: \n2790: `AgentResponseEvidenceEntity` (table `AgentResponseEvidence`, `long Id`): `WorkspaceId`, `ResponseId` (FK (`WorkspaceId`, `ResponseId`)), `PackageId`, `Agent`, `SessionId`, `RequestId` (root ids), `Ordinal` (unique (`WorkspaceId`, `ResponseId`, `Ordinal`)), `ClaimId` (64), `Kind` (16: `File` (the kind `CodeFile`), `Requirement`, `Receipt`, `Commit`, `SessionLogTurn`), `Ref` (2048: a workspace-relative path for `File` and `Receipt`, a requirement id, a full 40-hex commit SHA for `Commit`, or the turn reference `<agent>/<sessionId>/<requestId>` of a `SessionLogTurn`, never the response's own turn), `CitedSha256` (64, nullable, as submitted), `Sha256` (64, nullable, computed by the server), `CommitSha` (64: for `File`, the commit the cited content is read at; null otherwise).\n2791: \n2792: `AgentResponseGradeEntity` (table `AgentResponseGrades`, key (`WorkspaceId`, `GradeId`), `GradeId` = `grd-<responseId>-<deterministic|model>-<nn>`, for example `grd-rsp-req-20261011T223749Z-prompt-cc85-01-model-01`)\n2793: \n2794: - `WorkspaceId`, `ResponseId` (FK (`WorkspaceId`, `ResponseId`)), `PackageId` (128), `Agent` (64), `SessionId` (256), `RequestId` (256) (root ids).\n2795: - `Kind` (16): `Deterministic` or `Model`.\n2796: - `Grader` (256): `agent-context-deterministic/<BuilderVersion>`, or `<AgentName>/<model>/<effort>`.\n2797: - `Correctness`, `Completeness`, `CorrectnessThreshold`, `CompletenessThreshold` (decimal; the scores as exact lexemes, ScoreLexeme, section 3.5.4).\n2798: - `PassCount`, `FailCount`, `UnknownCount` (int).\n2799: - `Verdict` (16): `Pass` or `Fail`, derived by ScoreVerdict (section 3.5.4) when the grade is recorded: `Pass` only when both scores meet `max(threshold, 0.98)`, inclusive, and `FailCount` and `UnknownCount` are zero; the thresholds then in force are stamped in `CorrectnessThreshold` and `CompletenessThreshold`. The trust decision never reads this stored verdict; it re-derives it from the stored scores and counts (section 6.2).\n2800: - \"Latest grade\" of a kind means the grade with the greatest server-stamped `RecordedUtc`, ties broken by the greater `GradeId` suffix compared as a number; a client-supplied time never orders grades (`ModelGradeClientGradedUtcInFutureIgnoredServerStampedNewerFailBecomesLatest`). Concurrent grade writes are all kept, and the trust decision (DecideAgentResponseTrustCommand) is recomputed after each one inside that grade's SerializableWrite (write isolation above), so the stored status always reflects the latest committed grade pair.\n2801: - `HvRequestId` (128, required for `Model`, null for `Deterministic`; a root id and the composite foreign key (`WorkspaceId`, `HvRequestId`) to the HV request): the HV request whose review produced the grade. A unique filtered index on (`WorkspaceId`, `ResponseId`, `HvRequestId`) where `Kind` is `Model` is the IdempotentCreate key that makes the grade-submission continuation retry-safe: it is looked up first, before the HV request and response status checks; a repeated submission whose `PayloadSha256` equals the stored one returns that grade with 200, and a different one is refused with 409 `conflict` with `retryable: false` (`ModelGradeRetrySameSubmission200Existing`, `ModelGradeRetryDifferentSubmission409Conflict`).\n2802: - `PayloadSha256` (64, required for `Model`): the PayloadFingerprint of the RecordModelGradeRequest's client inputs (every request field in record order), never of the receipt hashes the server reads.\n2803: - `ReviewerAgent` (64, required for `Model`: the `ModelGrader:AgentName` the `reviewer_turn` check located the turn with), `ReviewerSessionId`, `ReviewerRequestId` (256, required for `Model`): the reviewer's own session-log turn that holds its entire result, by its full turn key, so the reference stays exact after a configuration change; `VerdictJsonSha256` (64): the hash of the verdict block the grade was verified against, over its stored normal form.\n2804: - `RequestJsonlPath`, `ResponseJsonlPath` (2048, required for `Model`, workspace-relative under `ModelGrader:ReceiptRoot`), `RequestJsonlSha256`, `ResponseJsonlSha256` (64): the receipts and their hashes as verified when the grade was recorded.\n2805: - `RolloutJsonlPath` (2048, required for `Model`, under `ModelGrader:ReceiptRoot`), `RolloutJsonlSha256` (64), `ReviewerThreadId` (64, required for `Model`): the reviewer's own Codex rollout receipt (after the review execution completes, the HV runner freezes a copy of that thread's rollout file next to the request and response jsonl, redacted by the receipt rule, before any grade is submitted) and the thread id it records; check 8 (`runtime_identity`) verifies them against that frozen copy, so the later grade-submission continuation of the same thread (RecordModelGradeCommand, two-step protocol) never changes the receipt it is checked against.\n2806: - `GradedUtc`: for a model grade, the time the reviewer reports in the request, kept as provenance only; for a deterministic grade, the server time of the grade. `RecordedUtc` (required): stamped by the server when the grade is saved; it alone orders grades.\n2807: - Child `Findings`, through the composite foreign key (`WorkspaceId`, `GradeId`).\n2808: \n2809: `AgentResponseGradeFindingEntity` (table `AgentResponseGradeFindings`, `long Id`): `WorkspaceId`, `GradeId` (FK (`WorkspaceId`, `GradeId`)), `ResponseId` (128), `PackageId` (128), `Agent` (64), `SessionId` (256), `RequestId` (256), `HvRequestId` (128, null for a deterministic grade) (root ids, the grade's), `Ordinal` (unique (`WorkspaceId`, `GradeId`, `Ordinal`)), `ClaimId` (64), `Check` (256), `Outcome` (16: `PASS`, `FAIL`, `UNKNOWN`), `Evidence` (text, sanitized).\n2810: \n2811: `AgentContextRelationshipEntity` (table `AgentContextRelationships`, key (`WorkspaceId`, `RelationshipId`), `RelationshipId` = `rel-<type>-<yyyyMMddTHHmmssZ>-<nn>`, for example `rel-depends_on-20261011T234000Z-01`; a link is a root record whose nodes are references, so it carries no parent id)\n2812: \n2813: - `WorkspaceId`, `FromKind` (32), `FromRef` (512), `Type` (32), `ToKind` (32), `ToRef` (512).\n2814: - Node kinds (the `SourceKind` wire values of relationship nodes): `Package`, `Response` (the kind `PriorResponse`), `Requirement`, `SessionLogTurn`, `Todo`, `CodeFile`, `Receipt`, `Profile`, `Rule`.\n2815: - Types: `implements`, `verifies`, `depends_on`, `supports`, `contradicts`, `supersedes`, `blocks`, `derived_from`, `relevant_to`. A `supersedes` link is written only by SupersedeAgentResponseCommand through RelationshipWriter; a client request for type `supersedes` is 400 `validation_error`, `details.field` `type`, so one data source holds the supersede chain.\n2816: - `ToSha256` (64): the target's content hash when linked, through SourceResolver, read by the unit before its SerializableWrite (database-only delegate) and handed to RelationshipWriter (the staleness dependency hash of `depends_on`, `derived_from`, and `supports` links, IResponseStalenessRule, section 3.5.5).\n2817: - `EvidenceRef` (2048), `CreatedUtc`, and `CreatedBy` (128): the calling agent for a link made through CreateAgentContextRelationshipCommand, or the name of the command that made it (`BuildAgentContextPackageCommand` for a package's `derived_from` links to its included sources and its `relevant_to` link to its turn, `SubmitAgentResponseCommand` for a response's `derived_from` link to its package and its `supports` evidence links, `SupersedeAgentResponseCommand` for a `supersedes` link). Every link a command made is protected: its deletion is 409 `agent_context_relationship_protected`; a client-made link of any type can be deleted.\n2818: - Unique (`WorkspaceId`, `FromKind`, `FromRef`, `Type`, `ToKind`, `ToRef`): the IdempotentCreate key of RelationshipWriter with policy `ReturnStored`, so an identical link, including one created concurrently, returns the stored link with `created: false` (`CreateRelationshipConcurrentIdenticalOne201OneCreatedFalse`); indexes on (`WorkspaceId`, `FromKind`, `FromRef`) and (`WorkspaceId`, `ToKind`, `ToRef`).\n2819: - Why a separate link store: develop's `GraphRelationshipEntity` (`src/QBrainAi.Storage/Entities/GraphRelationshipEntity.cs:10`) joins two `GraphEntityEntity` nodes of the GraphRAG knowledge graph, which is a projection that can be disabled; `TodoRequirementLinkEntity` and `RequirementTraceabilityLinkEntity` (`src/QBrainAi.Storage/Entities/TodoRequirementLinkEntity.cs:10`, `src/QBrainAi.Storage/Entities/RequirementTraceabilityLinkEntity.cs:9`) each join one fixed pair of record kinds; `MemoryEdgeEntity` (TR-MCP-MEMORY-MODEL-002, `src/QBrainAi.Storage/Entities/MemoryEdgeEntity.cs:5-32`) joins two memories by memory id with a type and a weight; and `HostileReviewArtifactLinkEntity` (TR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW-002, `src/QBrainAi.Storage/Entities/HostileReviewArtifactLinkEntity.cs:6-7`) ties one hostile review request to one artifact. An agent-context link joins any two `SourceKind` references, keeps the target's hash at link time for staleness, records the command that made it for protection, and stays authoritative while GraphRAG is disabled; none of the five existing stores holds those fields, so the links are one store of their own. One rule differs on purpose: an identical agent-context link returns the stored link with `created: false`, while a duplicate explicit memory edge is 409 (`src/QBrainAi.Services/Memory/Models/MemoryCompetitiveModels.cs:282`), because a repeated or concurrent identical create, by a client or by a command, is a retry of one link, not a conflict between two.\n2820: \n2821: `AgentContextHvRequestEntity` (table `AgentContextHvRequests`, key (`WorkspaceId`, `HvRequestId`), `HvRequestId` = `hvr-<sessionId>-<yyyyMMddTHHmmssZ>`, with `-<nn>` appended only when two are created in the same second)\n2822: \n2823: - `WorkspaceId`, `SessionId` (256), `Agent` (64): the MCP session whose turns are reviewed, located by TurnLocator (section 3.5.3) with `agent` and `sessionId`.\n2824: - `FromRequestId`, `ToRequestId` (256): the first and last turns of the range, inclusive, in the session's turn order.\n2825: - `RequestedBy` (128), `Reason` (2048).\n2826: - `IdempotencyKey` (128, required): unique with `WorkspaceId`. `PayloadSha256` (64, required): the PayloadFingerprint of the request's client inputs (`sessionId`, `agent`, `fromRequestId`, `toRequestId`, `requestedBy`, and `reason`); a repeat with the same key and an equal fingerprint returns the stored request, and any other input with the same key is 409 `conflict` with `retryable: false`.\n2827: - `Status` (16): `Open` (accepting grades) or `Completed` (no item is `Pending`: every item is `Graded` by this request or `Closed` because its response became `Rejected` or `Superseded`).\n2828: - `CreatedUtc`, `CompletedUtc` (set when the request becomes `Completed`, by RecordModelGradeCommand or by ResponseStatusTransition).\n2829: - An `Open` request accepts grades from any later review run of the configured grader that passes the eight checks below, so an abandoned or failed review is recovered by running the review again for the same `hvRequestId`; an item already `Graded` keeps its grade, and a different grade for it is 409 `conflict` with `retryable: false` (`HvRequestReviewRerunAfterAbandonPendingItemsGradedRequestCompletes`).\n2830: - Child `Items`, through the composite foreign key (`WorkspaceId`, `HvRequestId`).\n2831: \n2832: `AgentContextHvRequestItemEntity` (table `AgentContextHvRequestItems`, `long Id`): `WorkspaceId`, `HvRequestId` (FK (`WorkspaceId`, `HvRequestId`)), `ResponseId` (128, FK (`WorkspaceId`, `ResponseId`); that key's index is the lookup ResponseStatusTransition uses to close a response's `Pending` items), `PackageId` (128), `Agent` (64), `SessionId` (256), `RequestId` (256) (root ids: the response's, whose turn key names the session the HV request reviews), `Ordinal`, `Disposition` (16): `Pending`, `Graded`, or `Closed`, and `ClosedReason` (32): `rejected` or `superseded`; unique (`WorkspaceId`, `HvRequestId`, `ResponseId`) and unique (`WorkspaceId`, `HvRequestId`, `Ordinal`), so a response can be an item of several HV requests over time but `Pending` in at most one `Open` request. Items are the agent responses whose `RequestId` lies in the range when the request is created and whose status is `Submitted`, `Grading`, `Trusted`, or `Stale` (a `Rejected` or `Superseded` response is never listed, and a response already `Pending` in another `Open` request is not listed again, `HvRequestResponsePendingInOpenRequestNotListedAgain`); when a listed response becomes `Rejected` or `Superseded` while the request is `Open`, ResponseStatusTransition (section 3.5.5, called by RejectAgentResponseCommand or SupersedeAgentResponseCommand) sets its item `Closed` with that reason in the same SerializableWrite and, when no item stays `Pending`, sets the request `Completed` with `CompletedUtc` (`HvRequestRangeWithRejectedResponseNotListed`, `HvRequestItemRejectedMidReviewClosedAndRequestCompletes`, `HvRequestItemSupersededMidReviewClosed`).\n2833: \n2834: Receipt and reviewer verification (RecordModelGradeCommand), before a model grade is saved; each failure refuses the grade with 422 `agent_context_receipt_unverified` and `details.check` naming the check:\n2835: \n2836: 1. `receipt_path`: both jsonl paths resolve inside the workspace under `ModelGrader:ReceiptRoot`.\n2837: 2. `request_meta`: the request jsonl's first line is a JSON object with `type` `hv_request_meta` whose `reviewer.agent` contains `ModelGrader:AgentName`, `reviewer.model` equals `ModelGrader:RequiredModel`, and `reviewer.effort` equals `ModelGrader:RequiredEffort` (the HV runner writes exactly this object), and the request jsonl's body record names the `hvRequestId`.\n2838: 3. `verdict_block`: the response jsonl records a completed turn (`turn.completed`) whose last agent message contains exactly one `=== VERDICT JSON ===` block that parses as one JSON object.\n2839: 4. `verdict_binding`: that verdict names this `hvRequestId` and has a `grades` entry for this `responseId` whose `contentSha256` equals the response's `ContentSha256` (both over the stored normal form of the content the reviewer read from the server).\n2840: 5. `verdict_match`: the submitted `correctness`, `completeness`, and `findings` equal that entry exactly (the same score lexemes, compared as ScoreLexeme values that no hop re-serializes, section 3.5.4; the same claimIds, checks, and outcomes in the same order), and the entry's findings name every claim of the response at least once (an empty or partial finding list is refused). Verdict semantics: each `grades` entry carries `verdict` `AGREE` or `DISAGREE` for its one response, and the verdict JSON's `overallVerdict` is the request's; an entry's `verdict` is `AGREE` exactly when ScoreVerdict derives `Pass` from its scores and counts at `max(Trust:Correctness, 0.98)` and `max(Trust:Completeness, 0.98)` (section 3.5.4) and its findings hold no FAIL or UNKNOWN, and `DISAGREE` otherwise, and `overallVerdict` is `AGREE` exactly when every entry is `AGREE`; any other combination is refused (`ModelGradeDisagreeVerdictPassingScoresRefusedVerdictMatch`, `ModelGradeAgreeVerdictFailingScoresOrFailFindingRefusedVerdictMatch`, `ModelGradeOverallAgreeWithDisagreeEntryRefusedVerdictMatch`). A consistent `DISAGREE` entry is a legitimate failing grade and is recorded, and in a mixed range each response's grade is recorded from its own entry under an overall `DISAGREE` (`ModelGradeConsistentDisagreeEntryRecordedFailingGrade`, `ModelGradeMixedRangeEachEntryRecordedOverallDisagree`).\n2841: 6. `reviewer_turn`: TurnLocator (section 3.5.3) finds the turn with agent `ModelGrader:AgentName`, `reviewerSessionId`, and `reviewerRequestId` in the workspace, the turn was created at or after the HV request's `CreatedUtc`, and its stored result contains the same verdict block byte for byte, both compared in their stored normal form (the receipt's block passes through ContentHasher's normalization, since the store saved the turn in that form, `ModelGradeVerdictWithEmDashReviewerTurnMatchesInNormalForm`), where the stored result is the turn's response followed by its processingDialog parts in part order with each part's header line removed (the HV protocol stores the entire result on the reviewer's own turn and splits a long result into ordered parts; `ModelGradeVerdictSplitAcrossDialogPartsRejoinedAndAccepted`).\n2842: 7. `reviewer_independent`: the reviewer's MCP session is neither the session the HV request reviews nor the session that submitted the response; independence is a separate review session and execution, not a different agent name, so an independently launched Codex reviewer grades a Codex-authored response (`ModelGradeCodexResponseIndependentCodexReviewerSessionAccepted`).\n2843: 8. `runtime_identity`: the rollout receipt at `rolloutJsonlPath` resolves under `ModelGrader:ReceiptRoot`; its `session_meta` record's `id` equals `reviewerThreadId` and the `thread_id` of the response jsonl's `thread.started` event; it holds at least one `turn_context` record before the record of the completed review turn whose verdict check 3 read, and every `turn_context` record in it has `model` equal to `ModelGrader:RequiredModel` and `effort` equal to `ModelGrader:RequiredEffort`, so a rollout without live runtime context is refused (`ModelGradeRolloutWithoutTurnContextRefusedRuntimeIdentity`); and the verdict JSON names the same `reviewerThreadId`. The declared `request_meta` alone never satisfies this check (`ModelGradeLaunchMetaCorrectRolloutTurnContextOtherModelOrEffortRefusedRuntimeIdentity`, `ModelGradeRolloutThreadDiffersFromResponseStreamRefusedRuntimeIdentity`).\n2844: \n2845: The hashes of the three jsonl receipts (request, response, rollout) and of the verdict block are stored on the grade. The remaining trust assumption is the workspace's session-log identity model (every agent of a workspace authenticates with the workspace key), which this plan does not change: a forged grade would need a forged reviewer session, a forged verdict, a forged rollout, and matching receipts, and each is checked against the others. Negative cases: `ModelGradeReceiptOutsideReceiptRootRefusedReceiptPath`, `ModelGradeRequestMetaOtherAgentModelOrEffortRefusedRequestMeta`, `ModelGradeDisagreeVerdictPassingScoresRefusedVerdictMatch`, `ModelGradeEmptyOrPartialFindingsRefusedVerdictMatch`, `ModelGradeVerdictForOtherResponseOrContentRefusedVerdictBinding`, `ModelGradeNoCompletedTurnOrTwoVerdictsRefusedVerdictBlock`, `ModelGradeReviewerTurnMissingRefusedReviewerTurn`, `ModelGradeReviewerTurnVerdictDiffersRefusedReviewerTurn`, `ModelGradeReviewerTurnBeforeHvRequestRefusedReviewerTurn`, `ModelGradeReviewerSameSessionAsProducingOrReviewedRefusedReviewerIndependent`, `ModelGradeLaunchMetaCorrectRolloutTurnContextOtherModelOrEffortRefusedRuntimeIdentity`, `ModelGradeRolloutThreadDiffersFromResponseStreamRefusedRuntimeIdentity`, `ModelGradeRolloutWithoutTurnContextRefusedRuntimeIdentity`, `ModelGradeAgreeVerdictFailingScoresOrFailFindingRefusedVerdictMatch`, `ModelGradeOverallAgreeWithDisagreeEntryRefusedVerdictMatch`; acceptance cases `ModelGradeCodexResponseIndependentCodexReviewerSessionAccepted`, `ModelGradeConsistentDisagreeEntryRecordedFailingGrade`, `ModelGradeMixedRangeEachEntryRecordedOverallDisagree`.\n2846: \nEXIT_CODE=0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_191","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -Command \"pwsh.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -File './docs/receipts/hv/hv-r1-inspect.ps1' -Task Native -CommandName rg -ArgumentText 'reviewer_independent|\"'^###? 6'\"\\\\.[123]|\"'^###? 5'\"\\\\.4|\"'^###? 8'\"\\\\.1|\"'^- `ReadableKeyAllocator`:::docs/architecture/SessionTurn-Review-AgentContext-Architecture.md:::-n'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"136:- Model grade (operator, round-1 finding F3 and the follow-ups, verbatim: \"Server grading worker (Recommended)\", then \"We are going to add an HV pipeline in the future, but not in this effort.\" and \"Agents will specify a range of session turns to be HV, the HV agent will mark the grade you have been working.\"): this effort builds no grading worker or HV pipeline. An agent creates an HV request for a range of session-log turns (CreateAgentContextHvRequestCommand); the HV agent (the plan's Codex gpt-6-astra max reviewer, launched as every HV is) reviews that range and then, in a grade-only continuation of its own reviewer thread (`codex exec resume`) after the runner freezes the review receipts, records a model grade for each agent response in it (RecordModelGradeCommand; retry-safe, because an equal resubmission returns the stored grade), which the server accepts only from the configured grader, only for a response inside an open HV request, and only after the eight receipt, reviewer, and runtime-identity checks of the architecture document section 6.3 pass (`receipt_path`, `request_meta`, `verdict_block`, `verdict_binding`, `verdict_match`, `reviewer_turn`, `reviewer_independent`, and `runtime_identity`, which reads the reviewer's own rollout receipt and requires its recorded runtime model and effort to equal the configured grader's; otherwise 422 `agent_context_receipt_unverified` naming the check). Until a response has such a grade it stays `Grading` and is never trusted.\n378:- `ReadableKeyAllocator` (namespace `QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.Storage.Keys`): allocates the human-readable keys of section 3 (Identifiers). Strategy `NextOrdinal` (`<prefix><parent>-<nn>`, highest stored suffix within the key's unique index scope plus one; the agent-context keys `pkg-`, `rsp-`, and `grd-` under their record parent, `rel-` under its type and timestamp, and `hvr-` under its session and timestamp with `-<nn>` appended only from the second key of the same second, each with the shortened parent id rule and the formats of section 6.3) or `RandomHex` (`req-<yyyyMMddTHHmmssZ>-<8 hex>`, the Phase A minter). `NextOrdinal` reads past the soft-delete filter, so a deleted record's suffix is never allocated again (`ReadableKeyAllocatorSoftDeletedSuffixNeverReused`). A collision on the key's unique index declares a retry to SerializableWrite with the next candidate. The Phase A minter states its own bound and exhaustion outcome to SerializableWrite for request-id collisions only: today's six attempts and 500 `mint_collision_exhausted` (InsertMintedTurnWithRetry, section 4.2); its transient conflicts keep the default bound and exit; agent-context keys use SerializableWrite's default bound (four attempts), then 409 `conflict` with `retryable: true`.\n2363:### 5.4 Processes (plugin, REPL, and McpAgent)\n2661:### 6.1 Scope and principles\n2681:### 6.2 Configuration: `AgentContextOptions`\n2696:### 6.3 Entities (namespace `QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.Storage.Entities.AgentContext`)\n2842:7. `reviewer_independent`: the reviewer's MCP session is neither the session the HV request reviews nor the session that submitted the response; independence is a separate review session and execution, not a different agent name, so an independently launched Codex reviewer grades a Codex-authored response (`ModelGradeCodexResponseIndependentCodexReviewerSessionAccepted`).\n3032:- 422 `agent_context_receipt_unverified` with `details.check` naming the failed check: `receipt_path`, `request_meta`, `verdict_block`, `verdict_binding`, `verdict_match`, `reviewer_turn`, `reviewer_independent`, or `runtime_identity` (each check's case is listed in section 6.3, receipt and reviewer verification).\n3410:No transaction is open. The gradable check runs in this order: the response is neither `Rejected` nor `Superseded`, the HV request is `Open`, and the response is an item of it. The eight checks are `receipt_path`, `request_meta`, `verdict_block`, `verdict_binding`, `verdict_match`, `reviewer_turn`, `reviewer_independent`, and `runtime_identity` (section 6.3); they read the frozen receipt files and the reviewer's own session-log turn and compare normal forms (ContentHasher, `ModelGradeVerdictWithEmDashReviewerTurnMatchesInNormalForm`). After every read-only check, the receipt, reviewer, and runtime-identity checks included (section 3.1, Database-only delegates), the dispatched EvaluateStalenessCommand persists `Stale` when a dependency changed; a `Stale` response stays gradable and DecideAgentResponseTrustCommand leaves it `Stale`. ScoreVerdict counts the PASS, FAIL, and UNKNOWN findings at `max(configured, 0.98)`. The diagram draws the response, claim, HV request, and item checks as one call of the nested activity CheckGradeTarget below.\n5379:### 8.1 Phase order\nEXIT_CODE=0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
