{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_194","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -Command \"pwsh.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -File './docs/receipts/hv/hv-r1-inspect.ps1' -Task Read -Path './docs/architecture/SessionTurn-Review-AgentContext-Architecture.md' -Start 2661 -Count 44\"","aggregated_output":"SOURCE ./docs/architecture/SessionTurn-Review-AgentContext-Architecture.md totalLines=6032\n2661: ### 6.1 Scope and principles\n2662: \n2663: - Each agent invocation can run stateless: it receives a bounded, reproducible context package instead of carrying conversation history forward. In `Stateless` the model's input is the rendered package, the current request, and the deterministic `REQUIRED MEMORIES` block the host already injects (FR-MCP-MEMORY-003, -007, which hold unchanged; section 3.1, \"Memories in Stateless mode\"); history stays excluded.\n2664: - A package contains the current request, the canonical operator profile and standing rules, the applicable requirements, related session-log evidence, trusted prior responses, and relevant code at the current commit, each with its provenance.\n2665: - The relational store is authoritative for packages, responses, grades, and relationships. Response artifacts are projected into GraphRAG for semantic retrieval, with a deterministic relational fallback that gives the same result whenever GraphRAG is disabled, uninitialized, or unavailable.\n2666: - A response becomes trusted only when a deterministic evidence grade and an independent model grade both reach correctness and completeness of at least 98 percent with zero FAIL and zero UNKNOWN findings. The model grade is recorded by the HV agent for the responses of an HV request that names a range of session-log turns; this effort has no grading worker or HV pipeline (operator, 2026-10-11). Rejected, stale, and superseded responses are kept for audit and never retrieved as trusted context.\n2667: - Mandatory context is never truncated: when it exceeds the token budget, package construction fails explicitly, and in `Pilot` or `Stateless` mode the invocation fails: no answer is produced (operator, 2026-10-11: \"Fail the invocation\").\n2668: - The mode is opt-in (`Mcp:AgentContext:Mode`); the current stateful behavior stays the default during the pilot. In `Off` the agent-context commands are refused and every stored record stays readable for audit (section 6.2).\n2669: - Requirement ids (reserved for Phase 1, the plan's P0 steps 1-9, and created there with acceptance criteria, mappings, and use cases): FR-MCP-AGENTCONTEXT-001 package construction and provenance; -002 token budget and overflow; -003 response submission; -004 grading, HV requests, and trust; -005 rejection, staleness, and supersession; -006 typed relationships; -007 GraphRAG projection and fallback; -008 REST, MCP, client, REPL, and plugin surfaces; -009 opt-in mode and pilot. The matching TR-MCP-AGENTCONTEXT-* and TEST-MCP-AGENTCONTEXT-* records are created in Phase 1.\n2670: - Existing records and stores this phase meets (the reconciliation, section 3.1), each with its relationship; none changes behavior unless the bullet names an update:\n2671:   - FR-SUPPORT-010 context pack (`ContextPack`, `src/QBrainAi.Services/Models/ContextPack.cs:3-16`, `POST /qbrainai/context/pack`, `src/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp/Controllers/ContextController.cs:164-165`): related and separate, no reuse and no supersede. It returns ranked context chunks for a query; a context package is a stored, hashed, replayable snapshot of one request's mandatory and ranked sources. The FR-MCP-AGENTCONTEXT-001 record states the distinction as a glossary entry.\n2672:   - FR-MCP-082 bounded execution context (`TodoExecutionService.GetExecutionContextAsync`, `src/QBrainAi.Services/Services/TodoExecutionService.cs:456`): related, not reused, cross-referenced from FR-MCP-AGENTCONTEXT-001. It summarizes one TODO's requirements and recent turns for resuming work; a package cites the TODO, requirement, and turn records themselves, each with the source hash staleness needs, which a summary does not carry.\n2673:   - The FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW queue (requests with resolved artifact links, `src/QBrainAi.Services/Services/HostileReviewService.cs:290`, executions, findings, and its server-owned worker): separate. An HV request of this phase is a turn range an external HV agent grades, because this effort builds no grading worker (section 3.1); only RecordModelGradeCommand, after its eight checks, records a model grade, so a hostile review result is never one. The worker's identity (agent `Astra`, model `gpt-6-astra`, effort `xhigh`, enforced at startup, `src/QBrainAi.Services/Options/HostileReviewWorkerOptions.cs:13,16,19,77-82`) and `ModelGrader:*` (section 6.2) are separate option sets, so neither constrains the other; FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW-003 is unchanged by this phase.\n2674:   - `MemoryEdgeEntity` (TR-MCP-MEMORY-MODEL-002, `src/QBrainAi.Storage/Entities/MemoryEdgeEntity.cs:5-32`): strengthen. The separate link store is justified against it beside the other link stores (section 6.3, `AgentContextRelationshipEntity`).\n2675:   - GraphRAG: reuse. Projection calls `IGraphRagService.IngestTextAsync` and `DeleteDocumentAsync` (`src/QBrainAi.GraphRag/Services/IGraphRagService.cs:35,46`) and retrieval reports develop's fallback reasons (`src/QBrainAi.GraphRag/Services/GraphRagService.cs:880-889`). The registered service refuses those mutations while required turn transactions are active (`TransactionGatedGraphRagService`, TR-MCP-TXN-001, `src/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp/Services/TransactionGatedGraphRagService.cs:68-74,89-93`); a projection save then records the row `Failed` with `LastError`, and a later pass retries it (section 6.3, projection row states).\n2676:   - Handoff idempotency: reuse. IdempotentCreate keeps the rule of TR-HANDOFF-TODO-001 (`src/QBrainAi.Services/Services/TodoPayloadFingerprint.cs:10`: the same key with an equal fingerprint returns the stored record, any other input is a conflict), and `HandoffDbExceptions.IsCommitAmbiguous` keeps its meaning (section 3.5.2).\n2677:   - FR-MCP-130 AC 3 (\"Only CuriosityEngine may request GraphRAG/context admission\") governs QuadBrain brain-slot output (its description), and `src/QBrainAi.GraphRag` holds no Curiosity check (git grep at `a8d3934b`); projecting a trusted agent response is no brain-slot admission (updates FR-MCP-130 AC 3's wording to brain-slot output).\n2678:   - FR-MCP-103 federation: strengthen. AC 1 enumerates every mutable state domain, so `agent_context` joins `FederationStateAdapterRegistry.RequiredDomains` (`src/QBrainAi.Services/Services/FederationStateAdapters.cs:128-142`) as a local-only domain through the existing `LocalOnlyFederationStateAdapter` registration (`src/QBrainAi.Services/Services/FederationAdapters/FederationStateAdapterServiceCollectionExtensions.cs:23-37`) and `InferDomain` maps `/qbrainai/agent-context` to it (`src/QBrainAi.Services/Services/FederationProxyService.cs:440-469`): it is never queued during a hub outage (`CanQueueDomain`, `:332-338`) and never applied from hub fanout (`src/QBrainAi.Services/Services/FederationOperationApplyService.cs:32-39`). A LocalProxy still forwards these routes live to the hub (AC 2, `FederationProxyService.ProxyAsync`, `:78`), so (inference) the hub's server holds the records and reads the commit from its own checkout of the workspace path, and answers 409 `agent_context_commit_unavailable` when it has none.\n2679:   - FR-MCP-WIKIEXPORT-003: strengthen. Each new `DbSet` gets an explicit dump policy in `WikiDumpTablePolicyRegistry.PolicyFor` (`src/QBrainAi.Services/Services/WikiDumpTablePolicyRegistry.cs:19-43`): `COPY-SANITIZE+HISTORY+INERT`, the policy of the HostileReview tables (`:35-36`), for every agent-context table except `AgentContextProjections`, which is `OMIT-LOCAL` (`:28`) because its rows name GraphRAG documents of the source workspace (inference: a copied row would point at a document the destination does not hold). The registry coverage test (`tests/QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.Tests/Services/WikiDumpG7OverlayTests.cs:56-71`) covers the new sets.\n2680: \n2681: ### 6.2 Configuration: `AgentContextOptions`\n2682: \n2683: Section `Mcp:AgentContext` (declared under `QBrainAi: AgentContext:` in `appsettings.yaml`, projected onto `Mcp:` by `CanonicalSectionProjectionProvider`), class `AgentContextOptions` in `src/QBrainAi.Services/AgentContext/AgentContextOptions.cs` (namespace `QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.AgentContext`) with `SectionName = \"Mcp:AgentContext\"`, a `Validate()` method, and `ValidateOnStart()`.\n2684: \n2685: - `Mode` (enum `AgentContextMode`: `Off`, `Pilot`, `Stateless`; default `Off`). `Off`: ModeGateBehavior (section 3.5.1) refuses every agent-context command except DecideAgentResponseTrustCommand (dispatched only by the grade units) with 409 `agent_context_disabled`; GetAgentContextStatusQuery and every read-only agent-context query stay available, so packages, responses, grades, relationships, HV requests, and the pilot report stay readable for audit; a requirement update or delete dispatches EvaluateStalenessCommand only while Mode is not `Off`. `Pilot`: packages are built and responses graded, but plugins keep the stateful conversation (no injection). `Stateless`: plugins inject the package beside the host's `REQUIRED MEMORIES` block and carry no history, on a host with an authoritative history-exclusion operation recorded in its P0.2 row; any other host treats `Stateless` as `Pilot` (section 6.6). Plugin reading of the mode (section 3.1, resolutions of round 5): the plugin reads `mode` through GetAgentContextStatusQuery at host session start (StartHostSession, or the first prompt on a host without a SessionStart event: cline, cline-v2, and opencode) and caches it for that host session as `agentContextMode` in its `turn-state.yaml` through `Update-TurnState` (section 3.4, plugin per-turn store); that read is the plugin's only agent-context read at host session start, and the query counts responses by effective status with one source read per distinct dependency and commit, so it answers within the SessionStart hook bound (section 6.4, GetAgentContextStatusQuery); a `mode` it cannot read, including a read that times out or misses the hook bound, or a value it does not know, is treated as `Off`; a 409 `agent_context_disabled` answer to a build or a submission caches `Off`, and the host session continues statefully (no refusal, no `canceled` close); a change away from `Off` takes effect at the next host session start. A host whose prompt hook cannot refuse a prompt, or that runs the prompt anyway after a hook timeout, crash, or `plugin_core_unavailable`, runs `Pilot` and `Stateless` as `Off` and records that as a feature-matrix limitation (P0.2 probe (j) records each host's refusal capability); a message absorbed into a running turn (`absorbed_mid_turn`) runs as `Pilot`.\n2686: - `TokenBudget` (int, default 32000, must be 1024..1000000).\n2687: - `BytesPerToken` (int, default 4, must be 1..16): the deterministic token estimate is `ceil(utf8ByteCount / BytesPerToken)` per item.\n2688: - `Trust:Correctness`, `Trust:Completeness` (decimal in [0.98, 1], default 0.98, inclusive): the attached plan's minimum of 98 percent is a floor that configuration cannot lower; a value below 0.98 or above 1 fails startup validation (`AgentContextOptionsTrustBelow098FailsStartup`, `AgentContextOptionsTrustAt098And1Accepted`). RunDeterministicGradeCommand and RecordModelGradeCommand derive and stamp each grade's verdict through ScoreVerdict (section 3.5.4) with `max(configured, 0.98)`; DecideAgentResponseTrustCommand never reads a stored verdict: it re-derives the verdict of each latest grade from that grade's stored scores and finding counts through ScoreVerdict against `max(configured now, 0.98)`, so no stored verdict or earlier threshold below the floor can trust a response (`TrustStoredThresholdBelowFloorFloorApplied`) and a raised threshold demotes a `Trusted` response at its next trust decision (`TrustThresholdRaisedAfterGradeAppliedAtNextDecision`). The Phase B review thresholds (FR-MCP-SESSIONREVIEW-007) stay configurable as approved.\n2689: - `MaxCandidatesPerKind` (int, default 50, 1..1000) and `MaxPriorResponses` (int, default 10, 0..100): after filtering and ranking, each optional kind keeps at most `MaxCandidatesPerKind` candidates in rank order (`PriorResponse` at most `MaxPriorResponses`), and the rest are omitted with reason `below_candidate_limit`; mandatory items are never cut by these limits (`BuildCandidatesBeyondPerKindLimitOmittedBelowCandidateLimit`, `BuildMaxPriorResponsesZeroNoPriorResponseItem`).\n2690: - `ProfileSources` (list of file globs resolved on the server host; default `~/.claude/profile/*.md` excluding `add-profile*.md`, the set the `/add-profile` skill reads) and `RuleSources` (list of workspace-relative paths; default `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, and `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml`, secret values redacted through SecretRedactor). Profile and rules are mandatory: when no profile file or no rule file resolves, the build fails with 409 `agent_context_profile_unavailable` instead of building a package without them. A rule file outside version control (the default `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml`, regenerated at every server start) is a package item with its hash for provenance and never a staleness dependency (no `derived_from` link), so a server restart does not make responses stale (`BuildUntrackedRuleSourceNoDerivedFromLinkHashKeptOnItem`, `StalenessServerRestartRegeneratesMarkerTrustedResponseStaysTrusted`).\n2691: - `ModelGrader:AgentName` (default `Codex`), `ModelGrader:RequiredModel` (default `gpt-6-astra`), `ModelGrader:RequiredEffort` (default `max`), `ModelGrader:ReceiptRoot` (workspace-relative, default `docs/receipts/hv`): a model grade from any other grader, or whose receipts are outside the receipt root or do not verify, is refused.\n2692: - `HvRequest:MaxTurns` (int, default 200, 1..2000): the largest turn range one HV request may name.\n2693: - `GraphRag:ProjectionEnabled` (bool, default true): false skips ingest only; removal of a projected document still runs while GraphRAG is enabled (section 6.3, projection row states). `GraphRag:UseForRetrieval` (bool, default true): BuildAgentContextPackageCommand retrieves its candidates with `preferGraphRag` equal to this value, recorded in `OptionsSnapshotJson`, and every candidate's ranking inputs, GraphRAG scores included, are part of `InputsSha256` through `RankingFeaturesJson`, so two builds that rank differently never share it (`BuildGraphRagScoreChangedSameSourcesDifferentInputsSha256`).\n2694: - Validation failures (startup): an unknown `Mode`, `TokenBudget`, `BytesPerToken`, `HvRequest:MaxTurns`, thresholds outside their ranges, an empty `RuleSources`, an empty grader identity, or an empty `ModelGrader:ReceiptRoot`. These ranges are enforced at server start by `Validate()` with `ValidateOnStart()` and proven by its G2 cases; they are not a G5 (`ValidateConfig`) check, an exclusion TR-MCP-NUKE-004 states (section 8.4).\n2695: \n2696: ### 6.3 Entities (namespace `QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.Storage.Entities.AgentContext`)\n2697: \n2698: Every entity below is a new type in `src/QBrainAi.Storage/Entities/AgentContext/`, so its namespace follows the folder rule of section 3.5; develop's existing entities keep `QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.Storage.Entities`. Table names keep the `AgentContext` or `AgentResponse` prefix because the database has no namespace.\n2699: \n2700: Common rules:\n2701: \n2702: - Readable keys: string keys `[StringLength(128)]`, human-readable and derived from the records they belong to (formats per entity below), allocated by ReadableKeyAllocator (section 3.5.2, strategy `NextOrdinal`). A key is `<prefix><parentId><tail>` when that is at most 128 characters; otherwise the embedded parent id is shortened to its first `128 - length(prefix) - length(tail) - 13` characters followed by `-` and the first 12 hex digits of the SHA-256 of the full parent id, so keys stay readable, deterministic, and distinct for distinct parents. The complete parent ids are always stored in their own root-id columns, which keep the live length of 256 (`SessionLogEntity.SessionId` and `SessionLogTurnEntity.RequestId` allow 256), so no root id is ever truncated (`ReadableKeyAllocatorMaxLengthRootIdsFitAndStayDistinct` on SQLite, PostgreSQL, and SQL Server; `ReadableKeyAllocatorShortRootIdsKeepFullReadableForm`). The sequence suffix `<nn>` has at least two digits, starts at 01, is one more than the highest stored suffix for that prefix in the workspace compared as a number, and widens past 99 (100, 101, and so on) (`ReadableKeyAllocatorSuffixPast99WidensLatestGradeOrderKept`). A unique-index violation from a concurrent insert declares a retry to SerializableWrite with the next suffix; agent-context keys use SerializableWrite's default bound (four attempts), then 409 `conflict` with `retryable: true` (only the Phase A minter states its own bound, for one cause, a collision of its random request id: six attempts and 500 `mint_collision_exhausted`; its transient conflicts keep the default bound and the 409 exit, section 3.1, \"Retry bound\", and section 4.2).\n2703: - Keys are per workspace: every entity with a readable key has the composite primary key (`WorkspaceId`, `<key>`), the pattern develop uses so one TODO id can exist in several workspaces (TR-MCP-TODO-008, `src/QBrainAi.Storage/McpDbContext.cs:583-585`), and every reference to it is the composite foreign key (`WorkspaceId`, `<key>`). Every readable-key, idempotency-key, and natural unique index therefore includes `WorkspaceId`, and ReadableKeyAllocator reads its highest suffix in the workspace (under the `Workspace` query filter and past the soft-delete filter), so two workspaces that hold the same legacy request id get the same key string on two rows, without a collision and without exhausting the retries (`ReadableKeyAllocatorSameParentIdTwoWorkspacesSameKeyNoCollisionNoExhaustion`; request ids are unique only within a session, `McpDbContext.cs:391,404`).\n2704: - Child rows use `long` identity keys; each references its parent through the composite foreign key (`WorkspaceId`, parent key), and every composite unique key of a child is (`WorkspaceId`, parent key, ...) (stated per entity). Because `WorkspaceId` is part of that foreign key, a child's `WorkspaceId` always equals its parent's; StorageSchema's `ResolveWorkspaceIdForAddedEntity` cases stamp it from the parent, as develop stamps session-log children (BUG-SESSIONLOG-WS-002, `McpDbContext.cs:1665-1669`).\nEXIT_CODE=0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
