{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_244","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -Command \"& './docs/receipts/hv/hv-r1-inspect.ps1' -Task Read -Path './docs/architecture/SessionTurn-Review-AgentContext-Architecture.md' -Start 2828 -Count 22\"","aggregated_output":"SOURCE ./docs/architecture/SessionTurn-Review-AgentContext-Architecture.md totalLines=6032\n2828: - `CreatedUtc`, `CompletedUtc` (set when the request becomes `Completed`, by RecordModelGradeCommand or by ResponseStatusTransition).\n2829: - An `Open` request accepts grades from any later review run of the configured grader that passes the eight checks below, so an abandoned or failed review is recovered by running the review again for the same `hvRequestId`; an item already `Graded` keeps its grade, and a different grade for it is 409 `conflict` with `retryable: false` (`HvRequestReviewRerunAfterAbandonPendingItemsGradedRequestCompletes`).\n2830: - Child `Items`, through the composite foreign key (`WorkspaceId`, `HvRequestId`).\n2831: \n2832: `AgentContextHvRequestItemEntity` (table `AgentContextHvRequestItems`, `long Id`): `WorkspaceId`, `HvRequestId` (FK (`WorkspaceId`, `HvRequestId`)), `ResponseId` (128, FK (`WorkspaceId`, `ResponseId`); that key's index is the lookup ResponseStatusTransition uses to close a response's `Pending` items), `PackageId` (128), `Agent` (64), `SessionId` (256), `RequestId` (256) (root ids: the response's, whose turn key names the session the HV request reviews), `Ordinal`, `Disposition` (16): `Pending`, `Graded`, or `Closed`, and `ClosedReason` (32): `rejected` or `superseded`; unique (`WorkspaceId`, `HvRequestId`, `ResponseId`) and unique (`WorkspaceId`, `HvRequestId`, `Ordinal`), so a response can be an item of several HV requests over time but `Pending` in at most one `Open` request. Items are the agent responses whose `RequestId` lies in the range when the request is created and whose status is `Submitted`, `Grading`, `Trusted`, or `Stale` (a `Rejected` or `Superseded` response is never listed, and a response already `Pending` in another `Open` request is not listed again, `HvRequestResponsePendingInOpenRequestNotListedAgain`); when a listed response becomes `Rejected` or `Superseded` while the request is `Open`, ResponseStatusTransition (section 3.5.5, called by RejectAgentResponseCommand or SupersedeAgentResponseCommand) sets its item `Closed` with that reason in the same SerializableWrite and, when no item stays `Pending`, sets the request `Completed` with `CompletedUtc` (`HvRequestRangeWithRejectedResponseNotListed`, `HvRequestItemRejectedMidReviewClosedAndRequestCompletes`, `HvRequestItemSupersededMidReviewClosed`).\n2833: \n2834: Receipt and reviewer verification (RecordModelGradeCommand), before a model grade is saved; each failure refuses the grade with 422 `agent_context_receipt_unverified` and `details.check` naming the check:\n2835: \n2836: 1. `receipt_path`: both jsonl paths resolve inside the workspace under `ModelGrader:ReceiptRoot`.\n2837: 2. `request_meta`: the request jsonl's first line is a JSON object with `type` `hv_request_meta` whose `reviewer.agent` contains `ModelGrader:AgentName`, `reviewer.model` equals `ModelGrader:RequiredModel`, and `reviewer.effort` equals `ModelGrader:RequiredEffort` (the HV runner writes exactly this object), and the request jsonl's body record names the `hvRequestId`.\n2838: 3. `verdict_block`: the response jsonl records a completed turn (`turn.completed`) whose last agent message contains exactly one `=== VERDICT JSON ===` block that parses as one JSON object.\n2839: 4. `verdict_binding`: that verdict names this `hvRequestId` and has a `grades` entry for this `responseId` whose `contentSha256` equals the response's `ContentSha256` (both over the stored normal form of the content the reviewer read from the server).\n2840: 5. `verdict_match`: the submitted `correctness`, `completeness`, and `findings` equal that entry exactly (the same score lexemes, compared as ScoreLexeme values that no hop re-serializes, section 3.5.4; the same claimIds, checks, and outcomes in the same order), and the entry's findings name every claim of the response at least once (an empty or partial finding list is refused). Verdict semantics: each `grades` entry carries `verdict` `AGREE` or `DISAGREE` for its one response, and the verdict JSON's `overallVerdict` is the request's; an entry's `verdict` is `AGREE` exactly when ScoreVerdict derives `Pass` from its scores and counts at `max(Trust:Correctness, 0.98)` and `max(Trust:Completeness, 0.98)` (section 3.5.4) and its findings hold no FAIL or UNKNOWN, and `DISAGREE` otherwise, and `overallVerdict` is `AGREE` exactly when every entry is `AGREE`; any other combination is refused (`ModelGradeDisagreeVerdictPassingScoresRefusedVerdictMatch`, `ModelGradeAgreeVerdictFailingScoresOrFailFindingRefusedVerdictMatch`, `ModelGradeOverallAgreeWithDisagreeEntryRefusedVerdictMatch`). A consistent `DISAGREE` entry is a legitimate failing grade and is recorded, and in a mixed range each response's grade is recorded from its own entry under an overall `DISAGREE` (`ModelGradeConsistentDisagreeEntryRecordedFailingGrade`, `ModelGradeMixedRangeEachEntryRecordedOverallDisagree`).\n2841: 6. `reviewer_turn`: TurnLocator (section 3.5.3) finds the turn with agent `ModelGrader:AgentName`, `reviewerSessionId`, and `reviewerRequestId` in the workspace, the turn was created at or after the HV request's `CreatedUtc`, and its stored result contains the same verdict block byte for byte, both compared in their stored normal form (the receipt's block passes through ContentHasher's normalization, since the store saved the turn in that form, `ModelGradeVerdictWithEmDashReviewerTurnMatchesInNormalForm`), where the stored result is the turn's response followed by its processingDialog parts in part order with each part's header line removed (the HV protocol stores the entire result on the reviewer's own turn and splits a long result into ordered parts; `ModelGradeVerdictSplitAcrossDialogPartsRejoinedAndAccepted`).\n2842: 7. `reviewer_independent`: the reviewer's MCP session is neither the session the HV request reviews nor the session that submitted the response; independence is a separate review session and execution, not a different agent name, so an independently launched Codex reviewer grades a Codex-authored response (`ModelGradeCodexResponseIndependentCodexReviewerSessionAccepted`).\n2843: 8. `runtime_identity`: the rollout receipt at `rolloutJsonlPath` resolves under `ModelGrader:ReceiptRoot`; its `session_meta` record's `id` equals `reviewerThreadId` and the `thread_id` of the response jsonl's `thread.started` event; it holds at least one `turn_context` record before the record of the completed review turn whose verdict check 3 read, and every `turn_context` record in it has `model` equal to `ModelGrader:RequiredModel` and `effort` equal to `ModelGrader:RequiredEffort`, so a rollout without live runtime context is refused (`ModelGradeRolloutWithoutTurnContextRefusedRuntimeIdentity`); and the verdict JSON names the same `reviewerThreadId`. The declared `request_meta` alone never satisfies this check (`ModelGradeLaunchMetaCorrectRolloutTurnContextOtherModelOrEffortRefusedRuntimeIdentity`, `ModelGradeRolloutThreadDiffersFromResponseStreamRefusedRuntimeIdentity`).\n2844: \n2845: The hashes of the three jsonl receipts (request, response, rollout) and of the verdict block are stored on the grade. The remaining trust assumption is the workspace's session-log identity model (every agent of a workspace authenticates with the workspace key), which this plan does not change: a forged grade would need a forged reviewer session, a forged verdict, a forged rollout, and matching receipts, and each is checked against the others. Negative cases: `ModelGradeReceiptOutsideReceiptRootRefusedReceiptPath`, `ModelGradeRequestMetaOtherAgentModelOrEffortRefusedRequestMeta`, `ModelGradeDisagreeVerdictPassingScoresRefusedVerdictMatch`, `ModelGradeEmptyOrPartialFindingsRefusedVerdictMatch`, `ModelGradeVerdictForOtherResponseOrContentRefusedVerdictBinding`, `ModelGradeNoCompletedTurnOrTwoVerdictsRefusedVerdictBlock`, `ModelGradeReviewerTurnMissingRefusedReviewerTurn`, `ModelGradeReviewerTurnVerdictDiffersRefusedReviewerTurn`, `ModelGradeReviewerTurnBeforeHvRequestRefusedReviewerTurn`, `ModelGradeReviewerSameSessionAsProducingOrReviewedRefusedReviewerIndependent`, `ModelGradeLaunchMetaCorrectRolloutTurnContextOtherModelOrEffortRefusedRuntimeIdentity`, `ModelGradeRolloutThreadDiffersFromResponseStreamRefusedRuntimeIdentity`, `ModelGradeRolloutWithoutTurnContextRefusedRuntimeIdentity`, `ModelGradeAgreeVerdictFailingScoresOrFailFindingRefusedVerdictMatch`, `ModelGradeOverallAgreeWithDisagreeEntryRefusedVerdictMatch`; acceptance cases `ModelGradeCodexResponseIndependentCodexReviewerSessionAccepted`, `ModelGradeConsistentDisagreeEntryRecordedFailingGrade`, `ModelGradeMixedRangeEachEntryRecordedOverallDisagree`.\n2846: \n2847: `AgentContextProjectionEntity` (table `AgentContextProjections`, key (`WorkspaceId`, `ResponseId`), one row per response through that composite foreign key)\n2848: \n2849: - `WorkspaceId`, `ResponseId`, and the response's root ids `PackageId` (128), `Agent` (64), `SessionId` (256), `RequestId` (256).\nEXIT_CODE=0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_242","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -Command \"& './docs/receipts/hv/hv-r1-inspect.ps1' -Task Read -Path './docs/architecture/SessionTurn-Review-AgentContext-Architecture-Amendment-1-Durable-HV.md' -Start 1370 -Count 123\"","aggregated_output":"SOURCE ./docs/architecture/SessionTurn-Review-AgentContext-Architecture-Amendment-1-Durable-HV.md totalLines=2785\n1370: ### 16.1 Process HvWorkerHost: the durable worker [FR-MCP-HVPIPE-007, -008, -009]\n1371: \n1372: A hosted `BackgroundService` per workspace application (`src/QBrainAi.Services/HostileValidation/Execution/HvWorkerHost.cs`), with a worker id of the host name, process id, and a random suffix. It is the only dispatcher of the internal units (R-29). At start, while `WorkerEnabled`, it requests a NONCE for every enabled `Unverified` reviewer (StartHvReviewerNonceCommand, R-10). Then it loops, woken by an `IChangeEventBus` wake-up or at `PollInterval`: a sweep (ExpireHvRunLeasesCommand, FinalizeHvRequestCommand for every `Finalizing` request it lists, FileHvIncidentCommand for every `Pending` incident), then claims until ClaimHvRunCommand returns null, starting one process RunReviewerProcess per claim. On a graceful shutdown it stops claiming and ends each of its running runs with FailHvRunCommand code `worker_shutdown`, which files no incident, marks no reviewer offline, and does not count as an attempt (policy HvRetryDecision); after a crash the leases expire instead (W-03). A 503 `audit_unavailable` from any unit stops the loop iteration and the next iteration starts from the sweep (W-12).\n1373: \n1374: ```mermaid\n1375: flowchart TD\n1376:     S([\"HvWorkerHost starts\"]) --> EN{\"WorkerEnabled?\"}\n1377:     EN -->|no| IDLE([\"idle: no claims\"])\n1378:     EN -->|yes| NV[[\"StartHvReviewerNonceCommand for each enabled Unverified reviewer\"]]\n1379:     NV --> W[\"Wait for a wake-up or PollInterval\"]\n1380:     W --> SD{\"Shutting down?\"}\n1381:     SD -->|yes| FS[[\"FailHvRunCommand worker_shutdown for each own running run\"]]\n1382:     FS --> END([\"stopped\"])\n1383:     SD -->|no| SW[[\"ExpireHvRunLeasesCommand\"]]\n1384:     SW -->|audit_unavailable| W\n1385:     SW -->|\"sweep result\"| FZ[[\"FinalizeHvRequestCommand for each Finalizing request\"]]\n1386:     FZ --> FI[[\"FileHvIncidentCommand for each Pending incident\"]]\n1387:     FI --> CL[[\"ClaimHvRunCommand\"]]\n1388:     CL -->|\"null, audit_unavailable, or conflict\"| W\n1389:     CL -->|claim| RP[[\"Process RunReviewerProcess, started for this claim\"]]\n1390:     RP --> CL\n1391: ```\n1392: \n1393: ### 16.2 Process RunReviewerProcess: one attempt, live [FR-MCP-HVPIPE-008, -016]\n1394: \n1395: Runs one claimed run. It builds the brief (section 13.3: the accepted request read from the `Request` evidence inside the run envelope, or the probe brief with its nonce), asks the reviewer's adapter for the launch (section 16.3), starts the process with the workspace as its working directory and its plugin as a host session of its own (R-24), and appends the `hv_brief` record as sequence 1. While the process runs it has three concurrent duties and no time limit (R-11, P-02): every line the adapter delivers goes into a buffer that is appended (AppendHvRunOutputCommand) whenever `OutputFlushInterval` passes or `OutputFlushBytes` accumulate (R-12); a heartbeat every `HeartbeatInterval` (RecordHvRunHeartbeatCommand) renews the lease and reads cancellation; and the process exit is awaited. `stale_fence` from any unit kills the process and ends the attempt without another write (W-03); `audit_unavailable` kills the process and ends the attempt (W-12); a retryable `conflict` repeats the same unit call with the same sequence. Every append carries the records the adapter's extraction completed with that batch. On exit the remaining buffer is appended, the adapter's provenance lines are appended (section 16.3), and the run is closed (exit code 0, CloseHvRunOutputCommand, then FinalizeHvRequestCommand for a review; exit code 0 is not evidence of a review, R-11) or failed (FailHvRunCommand with `reviewer_exited_nonzero`, or `reviewer_launch_failed` when the process never started). A cancellation read at a heartbeat kills the process and fails the run with `canceled`, which policy HvRetryDecision turns into the request's `Canceled` (R-28). Killing the process, here and in the diagrams, ends the reviewer's whole process tree, descendants included, also when the root process has already exited; and when the root exits while a descendant still holds its output pipes, the worker ends that descendant before it reads the output to its end. Without that, a descendant that outlives the reviewer would hold the run open forever, because no time limit ends a run (P-02); P14 is the same defect class in the plugin core (FR-MCP-HVPIPE-008-AC011).\n1396: \n1397: ```mermaid\n1398: flowchart TD\n1399:     S([\"RunReviewerProcess: claim\"]) --> B[\"Build the brief: accepted request in the run envelope, or the probe brief\"]\n1400:     B --> L[[\"Reviewer adapter BuildLaunch\"]]\n1401:     L --> ST{\"Process started?\"}\n1402:     ST -->|no| FL[[\"FailHvRunCommand reviewer_launch_failed\"]]\n1403:     FL --> END1([\"attempt ended\"])\n1404:     ST -->|yes| A1[[\"AppendHvRunOutputCommand: sequence 1, the hv_brief record\"]]\n1405:     A1 --> RUN[[\"StreamReviewerOutput: lines, flushes, heartbeats, until exit, stale fence, audit failure, or cancel\"]]\n1406:     RUN -->|\"stale_fence or audit_unavailable\"| K1[\"Kill the process\"]\n1407:     K1 --> END2([\"attempt ended, no further write\"])\n1408:     RUN -->|\"cancel requested\"| K2[\"Kill the process\"]\n1409:     K2 --> FC[[\"FailHvRunCommand canceled\"]]\n1410:     FC --> END3([\"attempt ended: request Canceled\"])\n1411:     RUN -->|\"exited\"| FLS[[\"AppendHvRunOutputCommand: remaining lines, then the adapter's hv_provenance lines\"]]\n1412:     FLS --> EX{\"Exit code 0?\"}\n1413:     EX -->|no| FN[[\"FailHvRunCommand reviewer_exited_nonzero\"]]\n1414:     FN --> END4([\"attempt ended\"])\n1415:     EX -->|yes| CO[[\"CloseHvRunOutputCommand\"]]\n1416:     CO -->|\"Review: Finalizing\"| FZ[[\"FinalizeHvRequestCommand\"]]\n1417:     FZ --> END5([\"attempt ended: request Completed, Queued, or Failed\"])\n1418:     CO -->|\"Probe: passed or failed\"| END6([\"attempt ended: reviewer Online or Offline\"])\n1419: ```\n1420: \n1421: ##### Nested activity: StreamReviewerOutput (the loop of RunReviewerProcess, collapsed above)\n1422: \n1423: ```mermaid\n1424: flowchart TD\n1425:     S([\"StreamReviewerOutput\"]) --> E{\"Next event?\"}\n1426:     E -->|\"line from the adapter\"| BUF[\"Add the JSONL line to the buffer\"]\n1427:     BUF --> FT{\"Flush interval passed or flush bytes reached?\"}\n1428:     FT -->|no| E\n1429:     FT -->|yes| AP[[\"AppendHvRunOutputCommand: the buffer as the next sequence\"]]\n1430:     AP -->|appended| E\n1431:     AP -->|\"retryable conflict\"| AP\n1432:     AP -->|\"stale_fence or audit_unavailable\"| X1([\"stop: stale fence or audit failure\"])\n1433:     E -->|\"heartbeat timer\"| HB[[\"RecordHvRunHeartbeatCommand\"]]\n1434:     HB -->|\"renewed, no cancel\"| E\n1435:     HB -->|\"cancelRequested\"| X2([\"stop: cancel requested\"])\n1436:     HB -->|\"stale_fence or audit_unavailable\"| X1\n1437:     E -->|\"process exited\"| X3([\"stop: exited, with the exit code\"])\n1438: ```\n1439: \n1440: ### 16.3 Reviewer adapter contract [FR-MCP-HVPIPE-001, -003, -008]\n1441: \n1442: `IHvReviewerAdapter` (namespace `QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.HostileValidation.Reviewers`), one implementation per reviewer id:\n1443: \n1444: - `BuildLaunch(brief, reviewer)`: a `ProcessStartInfo` built as an argument list, never a shell string, with the brief on standard input or in a temporary file the adapter deletes after the run; the launch arguments come from one shared launch builder that develop's one-shot strategy also uses, so the per-CLI arguments of `OneShotCliAgentExecutionStrategy` (`AddCodexArguments`, `AddClaudeArguments`, `AddGrokArguments`, `AddClineArguments`, `OneShotCliAgentExecutionStrategy.cs:238-262`) are written once (DRY); processes start through the existing `IProcessSpawner` and `IProcessEnvironmentService` (`OneShotCliAgentExecutionStrategy.cs:18-21`). The shared launch builder holds the one table of highest efforts of section 10 (P9). The reviewer runs behind a write barrier verified for its CLI (VI-03, probes of 2026-10-16): Codex `exec -s read-only` (a write attempt was refused by its sandbox), Grok `--permission-mode plan` (a write call was refused; a headless Grok run then ends `cancelled` at the first call that needs approval, so its launch allows the read tools a review uses), and Copilot `--available-tools` naming only read tools (write and shell tools are not offered at all). Claude's and Cline's plan modes are not accepted as barriers on that evidence (Claude's model declined before its barrier was exercised; Cline ran a shell command in plan mode), so Claude's launch restricts its tools structurally (`--tools`) and Cline stays unqualified until H1 proves a barrier. Whether each reviewer's plugin can still log its turn behind its barrier (the plugin writes host-session state and calls the server) is verified in H1 (VI-03, VI-04); a reviewer that cannot fails its NONCE (R-24).\n1445: - Live lines (R-12, P8): standard output and standard error are read line by line as the process writes them (asynchronous line reads on the redirected streams), and each line is handed to the run's callback at once; nothing waits for the process to end.\n1446: - `NormalizeLine(line, stream)`: a native JSON event line of a CLI run in a JSON streaming mode is kept as written; any other line becomes `{ \"type\": \"hv_raw\", \"stream\", \"line\" }`.\n1447: - `ExtractReviewerRecords(lines)`: one pure function over a run's ordered native lines. It assembles each agent message's complete text by the CLI's event model (Grok's per-token `text` events, Copilot's `assistant.message_delta` events followed by the whole `assistant.message`, Claude's whole assistant messages, Codex's `agent_message` items, Cline's accumulated content) and returns, in message order, every line of that text that is exactly one JSON object of a reviewer record type of section 13.3; the fence lines of a fenced block are ignored, and a line holding other text beside a record is free text. The worker runs it as lines arrive and passes each batch's completed records to AppendHvRunOutputCommand; finalization runs it over the stored log; both get the same records (R-12). Record types the worker writes itself (`hv_brief`, `hv_provenance`, `hv_raw`) are never accepted from message text, so a reviewer cannot forge them.\n1448: - `ReadProvenance(run)`: `hv_provenance` lines read from the CLI's own runtime receipt after the run (P-04), sources verified on 2026-10-16 (VI-01): for Codex, the thread's rollout `turn_context` (fields `model` and `effort`; observed `model` `gpt-6-astra`, `effort` `max`), the source the foundation's `runtime_identity` check reads (foundation section 6.3, check 8); for Claude, the session transcript under `~/.claude/projects` (the assistant message `model` and the request `effort`), written only when session persistence is on, so the launch never passes `--no-session-persistence`; for Grok, the session store `~/.grok/sessions/<cwd>/<sessionId>/` (`summary.json` `reasoning_effort`, and a model id that `chat_history.jsonl` names `grok-4.7-build` while the events name `grok-4.7`, so H1 pins the field V-7 compares); for Copilot, the run's own JSON stream (each call's `assistant.message.model` and `user.message.responsesReasoning.effort`; `--usage-output-file` holds the model only); for Cline, none, because its run records hold the model and only whether thinking was on, so V-7 and the NONCE fail closed (L-04, D-06).\n1449: - Highest effort when none is configured: the verified values of section 10 (VI-08), never develop's one-shot constants (P9).\n1450: \n1451: ### 16.4 Process SubmitHvFromPlugin: the plugin verb [FR-MCP-HVPIPE-005, -020]\n1452: \n1453: `workflow.hv.submit` in the PowerShell core (`plugins/core/lib-ps/repl-invoke.ps1`): process BindVerbToTurn (foundation section 4.4) binds the invocation to its turn; the verb sends SubmitHvRequestCommand through the REPL with `submitter` that turn's key and `idempotencyKey` `hv-<requestId>-<first 16 hex of the SHA-256 of the scope and context>`, so a repeat of the same call is the same request. A created or stored request is recorded in `sessions/<hostSessionKey>/hv-notifications.yaml` (`submitted`) for the watcher (section 16.5) and reported with its id, status, and reviewer. `hv_no_reviewer_available` is reported with the context S-16 asks for: each reviewer's enabled flag, readiness, and reason from `details.reviewers`, the open incident of each `Offline` reviewer (through `workflow.hv.incidents`), and the remedy text \"after the cause is fixed, NONCE the reviewer: workflow.hv.nonce <reviewerId>\". Every other refusal is reported with its code, final. A retryable answer (transport outage, 503, 409 with `retryable: true`) is reported with `retryable: true` and exit 1 and is not queued in the failsafe, because the same call with the same key can simply be repeated (the reviews' no-failsafe rule, foundation section 5.2).\n1454: \n1455: ```mermaid\n1456: flowchart TD\n1457:     S([\"workflow.hv.submit: scope, context\"]) --> B[[\"Process BindVerbToTurn\"]]\n1458:     B -->|refused| E1([\"refusal of BindVerbToTurn, drawn there\"])\n1459:     B -->|bound turn| K[\"Submitter the bound turn key; idempotencyKey from requestId, scope, and context\"]\n1460:     K --> SUB[\"REPL: SubmitHvRequestCommand\"]\n1461:     SUB -->|\"201 or 200\"| REC[\"Record the hvRequestId in hv-notifications.yaml submitted\"]\n1462:     REC --> R1([\"reported: id, status, reviewer\"])\n1463:     SUB -->|\"409 hv_no_reviewer_available\"| CTX[\"Add reviewer statuses, open incidents, and the NONCE remedy\"]\n1464:     CTX --> R2([\"reported: refusal with context, exit 1\"])\n1465:     SUB -->|\"retryable answer\"| R3([\"reported: code, retryable true, exit 1, not queued\"])\n1466:     SUB -->|\"other refusal\"| R4([\"reported: code, final, exit 1\"])\n1467: ```\n1468: \n1469: ### 16.5 Process WatchHvNotifications: completions into the plugin [FR-MCP-HVPIPE-017, -020]\n1470: \n1471: Started by process StartHostSession (foundation section 4.4) for each host session and stopped by EndHostSession; one instance per host session (`sessions/<hostSessionKey>/hv-watcher.pid`, the arrival bridge's pattern). It runs the REPL streaming verb `workflow.hv.watchNotifications` with `Last-Event-ID` the `lastSequence` of `sessions/<hostSessionKey>/hv-notifications.yaml`. For each event it records `lastSequence` and, when the event is `hv_completed`, `hv_failed`, or `hv_canceled` for a request in `submitted`, appends it to `sessions/<hostSessionKey>/hv-inbox.yaml`; each write is one atomic replace (temp file, flush, rename) under `hv-notifications.lock`. A disconnect reconnects with backoff from the recorded sequence, so nothing is missed (R-16). The prompt hook adds each undelivered inbox entry once to the next prompt's context (\"HV <hvRequestId> <status>, overall verdict <verdict>; result: workflow.hv.result <hvRequestId>\") and marks it delivered. Plugins never call REST; the REPL holds the stream (R-30).\n1472: \n1473: ```mermaid\n1474: flowchart TD\n1475:     S([\"WatchHvNotifications: host session\"]) --> P{\"Watcher already running for this host session?\"}\n1476:     P -->|yes| X([\"exit: one instance\"])\n1477:     P -->|no| C[\"REPL workflow.hv.watchNotifications from lastSequence\"]\n1478:     C --> E{\"Next event?\"}\n1479:     E -->|\"notification\"| M{\"Terminal kind for a submitted request?\"}\n1480:     M -->|yes| IN[\"Append to hv-inbox.yaml and record lastSequence, one atomic replace\"]\n1481:     M -->|no| LS[\"Record lastSequence, one atomic replace\"]\n1482:     IN --> E\n1483:     LS --> E\n1484:     E -->|disconnected| BK[\"Wait with backoff\"]\n1485:     BK --> C\n1486:     E -->|\"host session ended\"| END([\"stopped\"])\n1487: ```\n1488: \n1489: ### 16.6 Director HV commands [FR-MCP-HVPIPE-020]\n1490: \n1491: The Director commands of section 13.1 run through `IHostileValidationDirectorExecutor` in `src/QBrainAi.Cqrs.Mvvm/HostileValidationDirectorCommands.cs`, the pattern of the existing hostile-review Director commands (`src/QBrainAi.Cqrs.Mvvm/HostileReviewDirectorCommands.cs:22`); `hv-output` and `hv-notifications` consume the client streams and reconnect from their last event id; `hv-submit` submits as `Operator` with the operator's name (R-09). Each command is a thin call of one client method and carries no logic of its own.\n1492: \nEXIT_CODE=0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_243","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -Command \"& './docs/receipts/hv/hv-r1-inspect.ps1' -Task Read -Path './docs/architecture/SessionTurn-Review-AgentContext-Architecture-Amendment-1-Durable-HV.md' -Start 164 -Count 31\"","aggregated_output":"SOURCE ./docs/architecture/SessionTurn-Review-AgentContext-Architecture-Amendment-1-Durable-HV.md totalLines=2785\n164: - R-22 Verdict only (S-25). No HV unit writes TODO state. A result names the reviewed turns' `todoId` values for information only.\n165: - R-23 Requirement references (S-14). The submitted context is stored as written (Markdown, YAML, or text, declared by `contextFormat`) and parsed for requirement references: FR, TR, and TEST ids of the form `<KIND>-<AREA>-<NNN>` with optional further dash-separated area segments, acceptance criteria written as `<FR id>-AC<NNN>` or `<FR id> ac-<n>`, and use cases by their store id or exact title. Each reference is resolved against the workspace's effective requirements and use cases and handed to the reviewer as a delineated list with its resolution status; an unresolved reference is listed as unresolved, never refused (shared component RequirementReferenceExtractor).\n166: - R-24 The reviewer's own turn (P-06). The brief tells the reviewer to log its review as its own session-log turn, classified `Chore` so that its turn never needs an HV of its own (foundation completion policy: Chore needs no hostile review), and to store its entire result there. Finalization requires that turn to exist, to be persisted (not a failsafe or queued record), to have been created after the run started, and to contain the run's `hv_verdict` record byte for byte in its stored normal form (V-6). A reviewer whose host cannot log turns fails its NONCE with `reviewer_turn_logging_unsupported`. The reviewer's plugin runs as a host session of its own: a review or probe run never reads or changes the plugin state or the session-log session of a live session of the same agent in the workspace (FR-MCP-HVPIPE-008-AC008), which matters because a reviewer starts in the workspace (section 16.2) and Grok's headless hooks write their host state under the working directory's `.mcpServer/grok/` (VI-04).\n167: - R-25 The HostileReview subsystem stays separate. FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW-001 to -006 (its queue, artifact links, findings, and query surface) are not changed by this amendment, as the foundation already states (foundation section 5.4); the problems found in it (section 21, P1 to P3) are tracked in REMED-HVPIPELINE-001. It stays separate (D-02, settled): it reviews artifacts (TODO, FR/TR/TEST, plans, files, commits; FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW-002) while this pipeline reviews session turns (S-03), the approved foundation keeps it separate, and consolidating it is scope the operator has not named. Its missing worker (P1) is built on this amendment's execution components (process HvWorkerHost and the reviewer adapters), so reviewer execution exists once (the DRY ruling).\n168: - R-26 Scope limits. One request names turns of one MCP session. `MaxTurnsPerRequest` (default 200, the foundation's `HvRequest:MaxTurns` default) bounds every scope; a session scope above it is refused with 400 `hv_scope_too_large` naming the count, never truncated.\n169: - R-27 One open review per turn. A turn is `Pending` in at most one open request (a unique filtered index on the turn key where the item is `Pending`); admission over a pending turn is refused with 409 `hv_turns_pending` naming the turns and their requests, and over a final turn with 409 `hv_turns_already_reviewed` (S-06, S-20).\n170: - R-28 Cancellation by anyone (S-18). `Queued` becomes `Canceled` at once; `Running` becomes `CancelRequested`, and the worker stops the process at its next heartbeat and closes the run `Canceled`; `Finalizing`, `Completed`, `Failed`, and `Canceled` refuse with 409 `hv_request_not_cancelable`. A cancel and a finalization of the same request are serialized on the request row, so exactly one of them commits.\n171: - R-29 Worker units are internal. ClaimHvRunCommand, RecordHvRunHeartbeatCommand, AppendHvRunOutputCommand, CloseHvRunOutputCommand, FailHvRunCommand, ExpireHvRunLeasesCommand, FinalizeHvRequestCommand, and FileHvIncidentCommand have no REST route, MCP tool, or client method; only the worker host dispatches them, in-process, through `IDispatcher`. The public mutations are submit, cancel, reviewer update, NONCE, and evidence removal.\n172: - R-30 Plugins consume notifications through the REPL. Plugins never call REST (foundation section 3); the REPL gains a streaming verb that follows the notification stream, and the PowerShell core runs it in the background per host session (process WatchHvNotifications), keeping the last sequence in the host session's state and handing completions of requests that host session submitted to the next prompt as context.\n173: - R-31 The 98/98 bar for the overall verdict (P-05). The overall verdict is `AGREE` only when the holistic correctness and completeness both meet `max(configured hostile threshold, 0.98)` (ScoreVerdict, foundation section 3.5.4) and no failure is FAIL or UNKNOWN; per-turn hostile reviews keep the foundation's configured hostile thresholds through HvVerdictMapping.\n174: \n175: ## 6. Amended foundation clauses\n176: \n177: Each amendment names the foundation clause it replaces and states the replacement; nothing else in the named section changes.\n178: \n179: - A-01 Foundation section 3.1, \"Trust\" and \"Model grade\", and section 6.1, fourth bullet: \"model grades come only from the configured grader (default Codex, `gpt-6-astra`, `max`, the plan's HV reviewer)\" and \"this effort builds no grading worker or HV pipeline\" are replaced by: model grades come only from FinalizeHvRequestCommand, for the reviewer the pipeline assigned to the request (R-03); the HV pipeline is this amendment.\n180: - A-02 Foundation section 3 (\"Process\") and section 8.1: \"a Codex gpt-6-astra max hostile validation\" for every gate is replaced by: a hostile validation by the reviewer the operator names for the session, never of the requesting agent's family (P-01). The HV of a plan's own work does not use the pipeline that plan is building until that pipeline has passed its own end-to-end verification (section 20.3).\n181: - A-03 Foundation section 6.2: `ModelGrader:AgentName`, `ModelGrader:RequiredModel`, `ModelGrader:RequiredEffort`, `ModelGrader:ReceiptRoot`, and `HvRequest:MaxTurns` are removed; the reviewer pool and `MaxTurnsPerRequest` of `HostileValidationOptions` (section 10) replace them. The startup validation of an empty grader identity and receipt root is removed with them.\n182: - A-04 Foundation sections 6.3 (`AgentContextHvRequestEntity`, `AgentContextHvRequestItemEntity`), 6.4 (`CreateAgentContextHvRequestRequest`, `AgentContextHvRequestResult`, `AgentContextHvRequestQuery`), 6.5 (CreateAgentContextHvRequestCommand, GetAgentContextHvRequestQuery, QueryAgentContextHvRequestsQuery with their nested activities), 6.7 (their sequences), and Addendum A.9 are superseded by `HvRequest`, `HvRequestTurn`, `HvRequestResponse` (section 12), SubmitHvRequestCommand, GetHvRequestQuery, QueryHvRequestsQuery (section 14), and Addendum B. Every foundation reference to an HV request's `HvRequestId` (the grade's root id and foreign key, the finding's root id) now names `HvRequest`. ResponseStatusTransition's side effect (\"the response's `Pending` items in `Open` HV requests become `Closed` with that reason, and each request left with no `Pending` item becomes `Completed`\") becomes: the response's `Pending` `HvRequestResponse` items become `Closed` with that reason; the HV request's own state is decided by its turns and is never completed by a response transition.\n183: - A-05 Foundation section 6.5, RecordModelGradeCommand: its caller becomes FinalizeHvRequestCommand only (internal dispatch inside the finalization transaction, origin `HvFinalization`); the REST route `POST /qbrainai/agent-context/responses/{responseId}/grades/model`, the MCP tool `agent_context_record_model_grade`, the client and REPL methods, and `New-McpAgentContextRecordModelGradeParams` are removed; 403 `agent_context_grader_not_allowed` becomes \"grader is not the reviewer assigned to the HV request\"; the eight receipt and reviewer checks of foundation section 6.3 are replaced by the verification steps V-1 to V-8 of policy HvFinalVerification (section 15), which finalization runs before it dispatches the grade; the receipt path, rollout, and thread columns of `AgentResponseGradeEntity` become the run reference `RunId` and the evidence hashes (section 12).\n184: - A-06 Foundation section 3.1, \"Review record\" (\"One record per kind per turn; latest write wins\") and section 5.2, RecordSessionTurnReviewCommand: on agent surfaces a `hostileReview` write may carry only `status` `Other`; `Pass`, `Fail`, `Running`, and `hvVerdict` are refused with 403 `review_hostile_pipeline_only` (R-06); `Unnecessary` and `Unstarted` stay server-managed as before. Policy HvVerdictMapping is applied only by FinalizeHvRequestCommand. Section 5.4, process ConvertHvReceiptToReview, and its plugin function `ConvertFrom-McpHvReceipt` are retired, with the `hvReceiptPath` parameter of the plugin verbs.\n185: - A-07 Foundation section 3.1, \"Reviews remain writable on terminal turns\", section 3.4 (section PUT and DELETE), and section 3.5.3, TurnWritePipeline: every turn write, review write, review reset, section replace or clear, dialog append, title change, and session-level replace or delete that would change a turn whose HV state is `Pending` or `Final`, or any of that turn's children, is refused with 409 `turn_immutable` (`details.reason` `hv_pending` or `hv_final`, `details.hvRequestId`), from every origin except `HvFinalization` (policy HvTurnFreeze, R-04). TurnWritePipeline runs that policy after TurnLocator and before every other policy.\n186: - A-08 Foundation section 3.5.3, `WriteOrigin`: a fifth origin `HvFinalization` is added; it is exempt from the completion gate (it never moves a turn's status) and is the only origin that may write a turn whose HV state is `Pending`.\n187: - A-09 Foundation section 3.1, check 7 (`reviewer_independent`, \"not a different agent name, so an independently launched Codex reviewer grades a Codex-authored response\"): replaced by V-5, which requires both a separate session and a reviewer id different from the submitter's and the author's (R-08, S-17, P-01, P-07).\n188: - A-10 Foundation section 7.8 (UC-7, \"Record a hostile validation review with receipts\") is replaced by the use cases of section 18 (UC-HV-03 to UC-HV-05); foundation section 7.12 (UC \"Submit and grade an agent response\") keeps its flow, and its model grade now comes from UC-HV-05.\n189: - A-11 Foundation section 5.1, `SessionLogTurnEntity`: two columns are added, `HvState` (16: `None`, `Pending`, `Final`; database default `None`) and `HvRequestId` (128, null while `None`), written only by the migration that adds them, which sets `HvState` `Final` (with `HvRequestId` null) on every turn whose hostile review is already `Pass` or `Fail`, because S-07 forbids updates to a turn with an HV review, and then by SubmitHvRequestCommand, CancelHvRequestCommand, FailHvRunCommand and ExpireHvRunLeasesCommand (both through policy HvRetryDecision), and FinalizeHvRequestCommand, through policy HvTurnFreeze's state changes (Addendum B.8).\n190: - A-12 Foundation section 6.1, \"Existing records and stores\", the FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW bullet: unchanged in substance (R-25); its phrase \"because this effort builds no grading worker\" is replaced by \"because turn HV runs through the HostileValidation pipeline of Amendment 1\".\n191: \n192: ## 7. Lifecycles\n193: \n194: ### 7.1 HV request\nEXIT_CODE=0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
