{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_254","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -Command \"& './docs/receipts/hv/hv-r1-inspect.ps1' -Task Native -CommandName rg -ArgumentText 'AgentPool|pool-job|model-execution|HOSTILEREVIEW-003|artifact|P1, P2:::docs/architecture/SessionTurn-Review-AgentContext-Architecture-Amendment-1-Durable-HV.md:::-n'\"","aggregated_output":"167:- R-25 The HostileReview subsystem stays separate. FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW-001 to -006 (its queue, artifact links, findings, and query surface) are not changed by this amendment, as the foundation already states (foundation section 5.4); the problems found in it (section 21, P1 to P3) are tracked in REMED-HVPIPELINE-001. It stays separate (D-02, settled): it reviews artifacts (TODO, FR/TR/TEST, plans, files, commits; FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW-002) while this pipeline reviews session turns (S-03), the approved foundation keeps it separate, and consolidating it is scope the operator has not named. Its missing worker (P1) is built on this amendment's execution components (process HvWorkerHost and the reviewer adapters), so reviewer execution exists once (the DRY ruling).\n321:- Seeding: at startup each reviewer entry is inserted as an `HvReviewer` row when the workspace has none for its id (readiness `Unverified`); an existing row is never overwritten, because UpdateHvReviewerCommand is the real-time management surface (S-02), the pattern develop uses for agent definitions seeded per workspace (`SeedWorkspaceAgentsAsync`, `src/QBrainAi.Services/Services/IAgentPoolService.cs:83`).\n2486:REMED-HVPIPELINE-001 (section 21) is pulled into the plan as follows: P5 and P7 in H0 (the foundation text and pins change with A-01 to A-03, and the requirement id cited by `EventStreamController` is corrected before H6 builds on that controller); P6, P8, and P9 in H1; P4 in HvSharedComponents (AuditLedgerGuard) and HvStorageSchema (`IsDeleted` on every HV entity); P1, P2, and P3 in H3 (D-02 settled: the HostileReview subsystem stays separate), where the worker FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW-003 already specifies is built on HvWorkerHost and the reviewer adapters, and P2 and P3 are fixed in that subsystem. P10 to P15 (the findings of the Codex review of pull request 81) sit in code that other plans own or have redesigned, and this plan fixes all six (D-07, settled), each under the owning plan's approved design and through its existing requirement, whose acceptance criteria H0 strengthens with a named test for the case the problem shows:\n2509:- P1 Requirement drift: FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW-003 acceptance criterion 4 (a server-owned leased worker) is checked as satisfied, but no worker is registered and `AcceptReviewerOutputAsync` has no caller in `src` (`HostileReviewServiceCollectionExtensions.cs:11-42`; lease options unread, `HostileReviewWorkerOptions.cs:30-46`).\n2511:- P3 Requirement drift: FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW-002 lists many artifact types, but only `todo` resolves, and the stored hash is of the TODO id, not its content (`HostileReviewService.cs:294-314`).\n2530:- D-02 (settled) The HostileReview subsystem stays separate (R-25), and P1 to P3 are fixed in it (section 20.4). Sources: FR-MCP-HOSTILEREVIEW-002 (it reviews artifacts) against S-03 (this pipeline reviews session turns); the approved foundation's position (foundation section 5.4); the operator rule that new scope the operator has not named needs approval, which consolidation would be. Its missing worker reuses this amendment's execution components (the DRY ruling).\nEXIT_CODE=0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
