{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_265","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -Command \"& './docs/receipts/hv/hv-r1-inspect.ps1' -Task Native -CommandName rg -ArgumentText 'FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005|Close-PluginTurnIfNeeded|APPROVED|A7:::docs/receipts/hv/hv-r1-store-PLAN-SESSIONTURNREVIEW-001.txt:::-n'\"","aggregated_output":"10:    note: \"APPROVED by the operator on 2026-10-10T16:19:44Z (\\\"Plan approved\\\"), after plan-readiness HV round 43 (AGREE, accuracy 99, completeness 98, every claim PASS; receipts committed in 465bf4e2). Amended 2026-10-10 for the operator's Build.Tests and Nuke rulings, the \\\"Separate Nuke-only TRs\\\" decision, and the npm lockfile ruling (operator-approved approach; HV-P0 checks the amendment). Execution follows the plan from P0; slice TODOs are created in P0.8; nothing is marked done without its hostile AGREE. Before that: NOT APPROVED (operator, 2026-10-09).\"\n13:    - \"Status: APPROVED (operator, 2026-10-10T16:19:44Z: \\\"Plan approved\\\"), after plan-readiness HV round 43 (AGREE, accuracy 99, completeness 98, every claim PASS; receipts committed in 465bf4e2). Before that: NOT APPROVED (operator, 2026-10-09). Execution proceeds step by step from P0 under the gates below. Amended 2026-10-10 for two operator rulings (the Build.Tests infrastructure step and TR-only accountability for Nuke code in TR-MCP-NUKE-003 and TR-MCP-NUKE-004 under the build-tooling FR FR-MCP-BUILD-001, both quoted in the BDPv4 contract) and the operator's npm lockfile ruling (quoted in TR-MCP-NUKE-003 AC-004), under the operator-approved approach; HV-P0 checks the amendment. Persisted as MCP TODO `PLAN-SESSIONTURNREVIEW-001` (priority high); handoff `docs/handoffs/sessionturn-review-plan-20261009T022141Z.md`.\"\n32:    - \"- Unsupported-session failsafe (operator, 2026-10-10: \\\"If QBrain is truly not available, use the failsafe already in the plugin.\\\" and, choosing between a failsafe capture and a warn-only Stop, \\\"1\\\"): when a host session fails closed under D6 (`unsupportedSince`), the model's session-log writes for a turn that was already open at that moment go to the plugin's existing failsafe instead of being refused (reviews excepted: they keep their no-failsafe rule and are recorded after the drain through the original pair), so the Stop gate's remedy stays accepted; the captured records drain at the next SessionStart through the trusted recovery flow with the turn's original pair (C5); every mint and every write for any other turn stays refused (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001).\"\n33:    - \"- Clear-session closing rule (operator, 2026-10-10, choosing option A over leaving blocked and withdrawn turns open: \\\"A\\\"): clear-session completes each turn that meets the completion policy, fails each withdrawn turn (reason \\\"withdrawn before delivery\\\") and each turn the model marks blocked (with its blocker reason; `failed` requires `requestClass` only, FR-MCP-SESSIONCLASS-002-AC002), and leaves every other turn in progress, reported by requestId with its exact missing items, never fabricating reviews (TR-MCP-CLEARSESSION-001-AC001). Definition (catalog cross-reference, not a change to the decision): \\\"marked blocked\\\" means the turn's `blockers` section (the existing decision-list field, FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-006-AC001) holds at least one nonblank model-authored entry, read by the clear-session coordinator for that turn by its original (`sessionId`, `requestId`) pair, and the first entry is the fail reason; precedence per turn: withdrawn -> `failTurn` (\\\"withdrawn before delivery\\\"); else blocked -> `failTurn` with the blocker reason (blocked takes precedence over a ready completion); else meets the completion policy -> `completeTurn`; else stays in progress, reported with its exact missing items. Close prerequisites (catalog cross-reference, not a change to the decision): before clear-session fails a withdrawn or blocked turn, the model writes by that turn's original pair, within the same clear-session run and before its session-end step, every prerequisite the plugin and the server require for that close, in the Stop gate remedy order (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC009): a designDecision when the turn has no model evidence (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC002), then a `requestClass` when it is missing or `Unclassified`, judged from the turn's `queryText`; the coordinator then fails the turn with its reason (TR-MCP-CLEARSESSION-001-AC001).\"\n77:    - '- Nonce: computed from the event itself, never from the hook process, so every delivery of one event yields the same nonce; a value outside `^[A-Za-z0-9._:-]{1,64}$` is replaced by the 64 lowercase hex characters of its SHA-256. Equal-field rule (copilot and classic cline, whose nonce hashes event fields with no per-message id): under `turn-open.lock`, a new prompt-hook invocation whose nonce already has a failsafe mint record or a `pending-<nonce>` turn in that host session, or whose mint returns `merged`, cannot be told apart from a separate message with equal session, timestamp, and prompt, so it mints and binds nothing and the host session fails closed under D6 exactly as the bridge does for an unattributable record (`unsupportedSince: { causeNonce, causeOperation: prompt_hook, reason: indistinguishable_arrival }` with the session-level envelope; every session-log verb then reports `turn_logging_unsupported` and writes nothing to the server until the session ends, except that writes for a turn already open at that moment go to the plugin failsafe under the unsupported-session failsafe rule, FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001; its open turns are listed at the next SessionStart). A network retry or failsafe replay of a stored mint record is a drain of that record, not a new invocation, and reuses its nonce without failing closed.'\n78:    - \"- Unsupported-session rule (D6; operator option 1 of 2026-10-10): every check that makes a host session report `turn_logging_unsupported` after one of its requests was delivered records `unsupportedSince` with the session-level envelope in `turn-state.yaml` in the same atomic replace that makes the session unsupported, with `reason` set to that check's code: `queue_removal_ambiguous` (bridge), `indistinguishable_arrival` (equal-field rule), `arrival_bridge_format_unrecognized` (an unknown queue operation, a record missing a required field, or a transcript shorter than `appliedThroughOffset`), `duplicate_prompt_hook` (the copilot or classic-cline single-registration check starts failing; the first verb or hook in that host session that observes it records it), or, for any other D6 check, its own reason code; plus `causeOffset` or `causeNonce` where the cause has one, else `detectedAtUtc`. Whatever the reason, the unsupported-session failsafe of FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001 then applies: writes for a turn of that host session still in progress when `unsupportedSince` was recorded (a delivered open request, or a `withdrawn` queue entry still `in_progress`, addressed by its original pair; a minted request still `queued` is not covered and stays a warning) are captured, and every mint and every other write is refused. The state lasts until the host session ends, or until a SessionStart clears its cause where the cause's own rule allows (the bridge restarting on a valid transcript); the captured records drain at that SessionStart. A host that is unsupported from its first event mints nothing, has no open turn, and records no `unsupportedSince`.\"\n81:    - \"- Queued-message arrival on every host (D5, D6): P0.2 records, per host, whether a message sent while a turn is running reaches an arrival mechanism at enqueue. Where the prompt event itself fires at enqueue for a message the host takes into the running turn, it is the arrival mechanism; a prompt event that fires at enqueue for a message the host runs as its own later turn is not, because a prompt-hook mint is delivered at once (TR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001 AC-005) and would move the write target and the stop ledger to a request the model has not received (D2; FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001), so P0.2 records that result as only when processed. The only queue adapter this plan specifies is the claude-code/claude-cowork arrival bridge. For every other host whose P0.2 result is only when processed (under the rule above) (codex excepted: that result stops P0, because every HV reviewer persists its own turn through the Codex plugin), the host is unsupported for turn logging in this plan (`turn_logging_unsupported`, D6), whether or not it has a separate enqueue or message-queue event; when it has one, P0.2 records that event's observed payload, and P0.8 files a traced follow-up TODO proposing the host's adapter (payload, nonce, correlation, lifecycle, replay, tests) for operator approval; until that adapter is specified, approved, and delivered, the host stays `turn_logging_unsupported` in this plan and its `<Host>_P0_2NotPassed_TurnLoggingUnsupported_ZeroMintsZeroWrites` case applies.\"\n83:    - \"- claude-code: nonce: the bridge's `q-` enqueue nonce (the bridge is the only minter, FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001-AC009); hook key payload `session_id`; verb key env `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` read at verb start in shell, PowerShell, and hook subprocesses (equals `session_id`; those subprocesses see the updated value after `/clear`); stdio MCP verbs use the live-session rule; payloads carrying `agent_id` are subagent events (system events, no turn); the subagent's own Bash and PowerShell verbs carry the parent's `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` and bind to the parent's write target; stop uses payload `stop_hook_active`. Arrival bridge (D5), the only minter on claude-code (known-good single source of truth: the host transcript is one ordered log written by one producer, so the bridge needs no key shared with the hook, which the host does not provide: Claude Code's `queue-operation` records carry no `promptId` although its user records do): started by SessionStart with the payload `transcript_path`, it applies `queue-operation` lines in file order. Every `enqueue` takes a queue position, so FIFO `dequeue` stays exact. An `enqueue` whose `content` the system-event classifier marks as a system event is added as `{ kind: system, enqueueOffset, enqueueTimestamp, promptSha256, state: queued }` with the session-level envelope `{ workspacePath, agent, mcpSessionId, hostSessionKey }` (it has no nonce or requestId, and none is ever invented for it) and is never minted. An `enqueue` whose `content` the classifier accepts as a user request: mint with nonce `q-` + SHA-256(`session_id` LF the record's starting byte offset in the transcript LF enqueue `timestamp` LF `content`) truncated to 62 hex characters (the offset is unique per record and identical on replay, so two distinct records with equal timestamp and content get two nonces), and add `{ kind: request, requestId, nonce, enqueueOffset, enqueueTimestamp, promptSha256, state: queued, stateOffset, mintedAtUtc }` with the root-id envelope to the queue in `sessions/<key>/turn-state.yaml`. `dequeue` (no content) removes the head of the host session's `queued` entries: a `kind: request` head becomes `delivered` and the write target; a `kind: system` head is removed and delivers nothing. `remove` and `popAll` records that match a `kind: system` entry drop it without a turn. `remove` with reason `absorbed_mid_turn`: the `queued` entry whose `promptSha256` equals the record's `content` hash (both written by the same producer) becomes `delivered` and the write target when exactly one queued entry has that hash. `remove` with any other or no reason: the matching `queued` entry becomes `withdrawn` when exactly one queued entry has that content hash. `popAll`: when, for every content line it carries, the number of lines with that content equals the number of queued entries with that content hash, those entries become `withdrawn`. Fail-closed rule (D6): a `remove` carries `commandUuid` and `deliveryId` (Claude Code 2.1.293), which also appear on the delivered message's `queued_command` attachment, but `enqueue` and `dequeue` carry no key shared with it, so a `remove` that matches two or more queued entries, or a `popAll` that carries fewer lines of a content than there are queued entries with that content, cannot be attributed (a `popAll` whose line count equals the matching entry count withdraws them all, because every candidate is withdrawn). The bridge never guesses: at the first such record it stops before it without advancing `appliedThroughOffset`, sets `turn-state.yaml` `unsupportedSince: { causeOffset, causeOperation, reason: queue_removal_ambiguous }`, and from then on every session-log verb in that host session reports `turn_logging_unsupported` and writes nothing to the server until the session ends, except that writes for a turn already open at that moment go to the plugin failsafe (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001). Turns that are open at that moment are left exactly as they are on the server, the stop gate blocks on each only until the model's close of it is captured in the plugin failsafe and then warns `queue_removal_ambiguous` listing them (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001), and the next SessionStart for the agent lists them with their original (MCP `sessionId`, `requestId`) pairs for closing through the trusted recovery flow (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC006). P0.2 records the `remove` identity fields; the bridge never uses them to pair with an `enqueue`. Only `queue-operation` lines are arrival records; every other record type is ignored by design. A `queue-operation` with an unknown operation or a missing required field is never skipped: the bridge stops before it without advancing `appliedThroughOffset`, records warning `arrival_bridge_format_unrecognized` and, after a delivered request, `unsupportedSince` with that reason and the record's `causeOffset` in the same atomic replace (unsupported-session rule), and the host session reports `turn_logging_unsupported` on every session-log verb (D6) until a verified adapter handles that shape, while writes for a turn already open go to the plugin failsafe (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001). Each record's state change and the advance of `appliedThroughOffset` past it are one atomic replace of `turn-state.yaml` (temp file, flush, rename, under `turn-open.lock`); records that start below `appliedThroughOffset` are skipped, so a restart never re-applies a record; the server mint for `enqueue` happens before that replace and is idempotent (same nonce, `merged`). Records are applied by whichever process holds `turn-open.lock`: the bridge, or a session-log verb, which first records the transcript length at the moment the invocation starts and, after taking the lock, applies every pending record that ends at or before that length, never a record appended later, before it binds (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-007-AC006), so a verb started after a `dequeue` line was written binds to that request, and a `dequeue` appended after the verb started never retargets it (D2). The prompt hook on claude-code and claude-cowork neither mints nor claims; it classifies system events and starts no turn. P0.2 verifies that every user prompt (in each prompt form of FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001-AC009) produces an `enqueue` record and that `dequeue` always takes the head; if either fails, claude-code is `turn_logging_unsupported` (D6) until an adapter with a traceable key exists, recorded in the feature matrix.\"\n94:    - \"- PowerShell hosts (claude-code, claude-cowork, codex, copilot, grok, grok-bot): receive all plugin-core changes through `./build.ps1 SyncAgentPlugins --agent-plugin-parent /home/sharpninja/github --plugin-version <version>` (A9 with 1.119.0; S8 and S9 with 1.120.0) plus host wrappers from `hooks-templates/generate-wrappers.ps1`; host-specific glue exactly as the host binding table states (nonce source, hook and verb host-session keys, subagent and system-event markers, stop field); the A9 host-repo repairs named there (codex `hooks/hooks.json`, copilot hook path, cowork connector, and each host's manifest and hook registration); the core reads land in `plugins/core/lib-ps` with their first consumer and reach the hosts through the sync: the per-host nonce sources (codex `turn_id`, grok and grok-bot `promptId`, copilot `cp-`), the subagent markers (`agent_id`, `subagentType`, `MCP_SUBAGENT_ID`/`GROK_SUBAGENT_ID`), and the arrival-bridge launch from SessionStart in A3; the `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` verb key, the verb-key env reads P0.2 selected (`CODEX_THREAD_ID` for codex, `GROK_SESSION_ID` for grok and grok-bot, each only when its P0.2 row selected it, else that host's verbs use the live-session rule), the grok `sessionId` hook key, and the SessionEnd ended-marker writer in A4; the `stop_hook_active`/`stopHookActive` reads in A7; duplicate-registration detection where the host can double-register hooks (A2 equivalent; Grok's default run of `~/.claude/settings.json` hooks included); skill/README docs for recordReview, requestClass, automatic binding with optional requestId override.\"\n158:    - \"8. Create TODOs with `workflow.todo.create` (section SessionLog, priority high, FR/TR arrays, dependsOn): MCP-SESSIONTURN-001..009 (A1..A9), MCP-SESSIONREVIEW-001..011 (S1..S11). Chain: A1 and A2 roots; A3 depends on A1; A4 on A1, A3 (its RED consumes A3's arrival bridge, queue, and minted turns); A5 on A1, A4; A6 on A1, A3, A4 (its RED uses A3's `MCP_PLUGIN_HOOK_INVOCATION=1` and `SessionTurnIntegrity.Tests.ps1` and A4's per-turn store); A7 on A3, A4, A5, A6 (it consumes A5's outcome mapping and A6's model-evidence counters and hook-invocation exclusion); A8 on A1; A9 on A2..A8; S1 on A9; S2 on S1; S3 on S2; S4 on S3; S5 on S4; S6 on S5; S7 on S2, S5; S8 on S6, S7; S9 on S5, S6, S7, S8 (C12: the S6 REPL release carrying `recordReview` and lossless scores precedes the Node host pins; S9 re-syncs the same 1.120.0 version S8 writes); S10 on S6, S7, S8, S9; S11 on S10. Append PlanningDecision checkpoints for every locked decision and rejected alternative. Write each host's D6/D7 capability disposition from its P0.2 receipt into `docs/AGENT-PLUGIN-FEATURE-MATRIX.md` (one row per plugin and feature with the receipt; P0.2 itself stays read-only). For each queue or enqueue event that P0.2 recorded on a host without a specified adapter, also create one follow-up TODO (canonical id assigned at creation; it carries the P0.2 receipt, the observed payload, and the host's feature-matrix row, and proposes that adapter for operator approval); none of them is a dependency of A1-S11.\"\n179:    - \"- FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-001-AC010 Queue lifecycle: a `queued` request becomes `delivered` (and the host session's write target) at `dequeue` (the head of the queue) or at `remove` with reason `absorbed_mid_turn`, and becomes `withdrawn` at a `popAll` or a `remove` with any other or no reason; minting never moves the write target; a withdrawn request stays `in_progress` on the server until the model closes it (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC009). A record that cannot be attributed to exactly one queued entry (equal content with no shared key: a `remove` matching two or more entries, or a `popAll` carrying fewer lines of a content than there are matching entries) is never guessed: the host session fails closed under D6 (the bridge stops before the record, `unsupportedSince` is recorded, every session-log verb in that host session reports `turn_logging_unsupported` and writes nothing to the server until it ends, writes for its already open turns go to the plugin failsafe and drain at the next SessionStart (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001), and its open turns are listed with their pairs at the next SessionStart); a `popAll` whose line count equals the matching entry count withdraws them all. Every `enqueue` takes a queue position: system events as unminted `kind: system` entries, so a `dequeue` whose head is a system entry delivers nothing and the user request behind it stays `queued` until its own delivery (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-002-AC001). [A3 +A4]\"\n187:    - '- FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-003-AC003 Only the model closes turns; no hook calls complete or fail. [A7]'\n188:    - '- FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-003-AC004 The stop ledger lists every request delivered since the last passing Stop, plus every earlier entry whose close the server has not confirmed; a request that is minted but still `queued` is not in it until it is delivered (the single enrollment rule of FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001). Only a server-confirmed close leaves the ledger (A5 outcome mapping, `CloseVerb_OnlyServerConfirmedClose_LeavesOpenListAndLedger`), and the Stop gate evaluates that ledger plus one separate scan of `withdrawn` queue entries still `in_progress` (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC009, TR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-006 AC-005), so a request withdrawn before delivery is blocked as `turn_withdrawn_unclosed` without ever entering the ledger (A7, `StopGate_LedgerListsDeliveredSinceLastPass_PlusUnconfirmedCloses`, `StopGate_WithdrawnBeforeDelivery_EmptyLedger_BlocksWithdrawnUnclosed_FailByPair_NextStopPasses`). [A4 +A5 +A7]'\n195:    - FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005 The stop gate enforces and never closes turns.\n196:    - \"- FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001 Blocks with `turn_in_progress` when a delivered request in the stop ledger (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-003-AC004) is still in progress; a withdrawn request still in progress blocks under AC009, and a minted request still `queued` only warns. A request in state `queued` (minted, not yet delivered) never produces `turn_in_progress`; the gate warns, naming its requestId and enqueue time. A Stop in one host session never blocks or warns on another host session's turns (`StopGate_OtherHostSession_NeverBlocksOrWarns`). A request enters the stop ledger when it is delivered, so a request minted before a passing Stop and delivered after it is evaluated at the next Stop (`StopGate_EnqueueR2_PassForR1_DequeueR2_NextStopBlocksUntilClosed`, including a restart between the two Stops). Unsupported-session failsafe: in a host session with `unsupportedSince` (whatever its reason, host binding table unsupported-session rule), every session-log write the model makes for a turn of that host session still in progress when `unsupportedSince` was recorded (a delivered open request, or a `withdrawn` queue entry still `in_progress`, addressed by its original pair; a minted request still `queued` is not covered and stays a warning) (evidence actions, design decisions, `requestClass`, and the close call; a review is never captured, keeping the no-failsafe rule of TR-MCP-SESSIONREVIEW-005 AC-002 and S9, and is refused with `turn_logging_unsupported`) is captured as a plugin failsafe record that carries the turn's C4 binding and root-id envelope, is marked `unsupported_session`, and is never sent to the server during that host session; every mint and every write for any other turn stays refused with `turn_logging_unsupported` and captures nothing (`UnsupportedSession_MintOrWriteForOtherTurn_Refused_NothingCaptured`). The Stop gate evaluates such a turn with its captured records counted: it blocks until the model's close is captured, with a remedy that names only the missing model evidence, the missing `requestClass` (from S8/S9, when the plugin's requestClass verb ships; a class write is captured like any other write), and the close call (all accepted, AC002; the plugin pre-check lets that close through, FR-MCP-SESSIONCLASS-004-AC002), because only the completion gate's terminal reviews cannot be written in that session; it then only warns with the cause and the retained failsafe records. The completion gate is judged at the drain: the server's gate judges the drained close, and a gate rejection keeps the turn in progress and listed with its original pair at that SessionStart, where its reviews are recorded through the C5 pair (FR-MCP-SESSIONREVIEW-002-AC002) before it is closed (`UnsupportedSession_ReviewRefused_NotCaptured`, `UnsupportedSession_CodeTurn_DrainedCloseRejectedByGate_StaysListed_ReviewViaPair_ThenCloses`). The next SessionStart for the agent drains the captured records in original order through the trusted recovery flow with the turn's original (MCP `sessionId`, `requestId`) pair (C5); a record whose turn is no longer in progress on the server is retained and warned, never forced. Each reason is covered from an earlier open turn (`StopGate_UnknownBridgeRecord_EarlierOpenTurn_CloseCaptured_ThenWarnsOnly`, `StopGate_TruncatedTranscript_EarlierOpenTurn_CloseCaptured_ThenWarnsOnly`, `StopGate_SyntheticUnsupportedSince_AnyReason_EarlierOpenTurn_CloseCaptured_ThenWarnsOnly` in A7; the real duplicate-hook transition `DuplicateHookAfterDelivery_UnsupportedSince_OpenTurnCloseCaptured` for copilot in A9 and `nodeDuplicateHookAfterDelivery_unsupportedSince_openTurnCloseCaptured` for classic cline in A8). A withdrawn-before-delivery turn is covered too: its decision, its `requestClass` (from S8/S9), and `failTurn` by original pair are captured, the next Stop warns instead of blocking, and the drain closes it (after S10 the server gate accepts that fail, which needs only the class) (`StopGate_WithdrawnBeforeDelivery_ThenUnsupported_CloseCapturedByPair_NextStopWarns_DrainCloses`). A Stop in another live host session still blocks on that session's own turns (`StopGate_EqualFieldFailClosed_EarlierOpenTurn_CloseCapturedToFailsafe_ThenWarnsOnly_DrainsWithOriginalPair`, `StopGate_BridgeAmbiguity_EarlierOpenTurn_CloseCapturedToFailsafe_ThenWarnsOnly_DrainsWithOriginalPair`). Outage close: a model close held in the ordinary plugin failsafe because of a transport outage turns that turn's block into the existing degraded persistence warning (AC005); its ledger entry stays until the server confirms the close (`StopGate_OutageCloseQueued_WarnsDegraded_LedgerKeptUntilConfirmed`). [A7 +A8 +A9 +S8 +S9 +S10]\"\n197:    - \"- FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC002 The plugin close verbs refuse to close a turn that has no model-authored action, design decision, or commit (the evidence predicate TR-MCP-PLUGIN-011-AC003 keeps), with code `turn_missing_model_evidence`; the turn stays in progress, so the stop gate blocks it as `turn_in_progress` with the accumulated remedy of the wire contract (every unmet prerequisite, then the valid close call). A turn closed without model evidence by another surface accepts no plugin edit, so the gate reports it as a warning naming it and never blocks on it. Every stop-gate block names a remedy the plugin accepts on the blocked turn's current status (`StopGate_EveryBlockRemedy_AcceptedOnBlockedTurnStatus`, `CloseVerb_NoEvidence_Refused_TurnStaysInProgress`, `StopGate_ClosedWithoutEvidenceElsewhere_WarnsOnly`, `CloseVerb_CommitOnlyEvidence_Accepted`). [A7 +A8]\"\n198:    - \"- FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC003 Blocks with `session_turn_completion_gate` a turn whose most recent close the server refused with that code (recorded locally from the server response; such a turn is still in progress), naming the server's missing items first in its remedy (`StopGate_ServerGateRefusal_BlocksWithServerMissingItems`), except for an already open turn of a host session with `unsupportedSince`, whose completion gate is judged at the drain (AC001). There is no marker condition: the server gate (FR-MCP-SESSIONCLASS-002) is the only completion authority. Its remedy is the accumulated per-turn remedy of the wire contract, so it also names any missing model evidence. [S8 +S9 +S10]\"\n199:    - '- FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC004 Never calls `completeTurn` or `failTurn`. [A7 +A8]'\n200:    - '- FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC005 Warns without blocking for model-canceled turns, retained failsafe records, and degraded persistence. The warning list is exactly: model-canceled turns, `queue_removal_ambiguous`, `indistinguishable_arrival`, and `duplicate_prompt_hook` (each, and `arrival_bridge_format_unrecognized` when it set `unsupportedSince`, or any other `unsupportedSince` reason, naming the open turns of its fail-closed host session and their captured `unsupported_session` failsafe records), queued undelivered requests, retained failsafe records (including final mint refusals and unbound legacy records), degraded persistence, `duplicate_delivery_suspected`, `nonce_source_missing`, `arrival_bridge_format_unrecognized`, `stop_gate_overridden_by_host`, open turns of ended host sessions, a turn closed without model evidence by another surface, a live host session whose arrival bridge is not running, and a turn in progress in the legacy `current-turn.yaml` at upgrade (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-007-AC004, `StopGate_LegacyCurrentTurnInProgress_Warned`). [A7]'\n201:    - '- FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC006 Passes and clears the ledger only when every check passes. Every Stop invocation re-evaluates the ledger, including when the host reports that an earlier Stop already blocked (Claude Code `stop_hook_active`, Grok `stopHookActive`): while any turn remains open it blocks again with the same findings. If the host ends the session anyway (its own continuation limit), the gate records `stop_gate_overridden_by_host` with the open turn ids in the ledger, and the next SessionStart or prompt hook shows it as a warning until the model closes those turns. A ledger entry whose closing write the server has not confirmed terminal stays in the ledger across passing Stops until the server reports it terminal. Turns still in progress when their host session ends (any reason, including `/clear` and a host override) stay in progress and are listed at the next SessionStart for that agent in the workspace with their original (MCP `sessionId`, `requestId`) pairs; closing one through the trusted recovery flow with that pair succeeds, and a `requestId` alone is refused (C5). [A7 +A8]'\n202:    - '- FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC007 The existing failed-build block is retained. [A7]'\n203:    - \"- FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC008 Warns (never merges, never blocks) with `duplicate_delivery_suspected` when one host session minted two requestIds within 2 seconds for prompts whose SHA-256 of the normalized text is equal, naming both requestIds so the operator can check hook registration; the warning is diagnostic only (it can fire only where a host's event identity is weaker than expected) and is not evidence of D1 compliance. [A7]\"\n204:    - \"- FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC009 Blocks with `turn_withdrawn_unclosed` while a `withdrawn` request is still `in_progress`, naming it, its withdrawal record (`popAll` or `remove` reason), and the action: close it with `workflow.sessionlog.failTurn` (`requestId` override, reason \\\"withdrawn before delivery\\\"); hooks still never close it. The instruction text includes a designDecision and, from S8/S9 (when the plugin's requestClass and recordReview verbs ship), the `requestClass` the close needs, so following it exactly makes the next Stop pass (`StopGate_WithdrawnFailedPerInstruction_NextStopPasses`). [A7]\"\n214:    - \"- FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-007-AC004 Failsafe records and fingerprints are namespaced by requestId; replay never targets another turn. A failsafe record without a captured binding (written before the upgrade) is never replayed automatically: it is retained and listed with its path by the stop gate's retained-failsafe warning; a turn in progress in the legacy `current-turn.yaml` at upgrade is listed as a warning by the first SessionStart or Stop and stays addressable by explicit requestId (`Failsafe_LegacyUnbound_NeverAutoReplayed_Retained`, `LegacyCurrentTurnInProgress_ListedAtFirstSessionStart_AddressableByRequestId` in A4; the Stop warnings `StopGate_UnboundLegacyFailsafe_WarnedWithPath`, `StopGate_LegacyCurrentTurnInProgress_Warned` in A7). [A4 +A7 +A8]\"\n217:    - \"- FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-007-AC007 Interleaving: with R1 delivered and R2 enqueued mid-turn in the same host session, a verb started after R2's mint but before R2's delivery (`absorbed_mid_turn` or `dequeue`) writes to R1; a verb started after R2's delivery without `requestId` is refused with `request_ambiguous` while R1 and R2 are both open (D9); with explicit ids an R1, R2, R1 sequence of writes lands on R1, R2, R1 respectively; once R1 is closed an unqualified verb writes to R2; a withdrawn R2 never becomes a write target; R1 stays in progress throughout. When the verb cannot read its host session key, the live-session rule applies: with exactly one live host session of its agent it binds there with warning `host_session_inferred`; with none or several it is refused (`host_session_unknown`, `host_session_ambiguous`) unless it carries the trusted (MCP `sessionId`, `requestId`) pair of C5 that the server verifies for this workspace and agent; a `requestId` alone is refused the same way; with two concurrent host sessions in one workspace and no keys, zero writes land on the wrong turn. After a prompt's open fails (`turn-open-contended`, `turn-open-failed`, `nonce_source_missing`), unqualified verbs in that host session are refused with `request_unknown` until a later delivery succeeds, so that prompt's work never lands on the earlier turn (`Binding_AfterFailedOpen_UnqualifiedVerb_RequestUnknown`). A turn the server reports terminal, whichever surface closed it, stops counting as an open delivered request at the next verb or Stop, and a request leaves the open list only when the server confirms its close (a queued, refused, or failed close leaves it open; A5 outcome mapping `CloseVerb_OnlyServerConfirmedClose_LeavesOpenListAndLedger`, A7 Stop `StopGate_TurnClosedElsewhere_DroppedFromOpenList`). When the write target is closed while an earlier delivered request is still open, an unqualified verb is refused with `turn_immutable` naming the open request (`Binding_WriteTargetClosed_EarlierStillOpen_TurnImmutable`). [A4 +A5 +A7 +A8]\"\n221:    - '- FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-008-AC001 The Node core satisfies, for its tools and cache (jest), exactly the ACs in the A8 allocation, including the stop-equivalent rules FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001, -AC002, -AC004, and -AC006 through cline-v2 `afterRun` and opencode `session.idle` (in A8 driven through recorded host payload fixtures; the host repos run them in A9). Every Node session tool keeps the structured failure cause of a response (detail code and retryable flag, through the exported `classifyOperationFailure` / `CoreOperationError`) and never builds a string-only error, so C9 detail codes such as `mint_collision_exhausted` reach the caller (`sessionTool_preservesStructuredCause_C9`); a governed context uses only its bound bridge and never an ambient HTTP fallback such as `session-shim.ts` `querySessionHistoryHttpFallback`, and a missing bridge is a typed final failure (`governedContext_noAmbientHttpFallback`). A governed flush (`host-context.flushCacheBestEffort`, and the legacy cache use in `session-shim`) uses only the context-bound durable outbox path, keeps its pending, rejected, and quarantined completion blockers, and never replays a global or foreign-workspace failsafe directory through a governed bridge; suppressing the legacy flush is not audit clearance (`governedFlush_contextBoundOutboxOnly_noForeignReplay`). Both governed cases run in A8 against the port doubles: the outbox port double (of `AuditOutbox.enqueue` and `AuditAliasConsumer.enqueue`, as in the `nodeOutbox_*` cases) and a bound-bridge port double of `src/transport/*`. [A8]'\n224:    - '- FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-008-AC004 Every host either has an arrival mechanism listed in the host binding table (operator D6) or reports `turn_logging_unsupported` on every session-log verb and writes nothing; no host relies on the model to open turns. While the copilot or classic-cline duplicate check fails, every session-log verb of that plugin, not only its prompt hook, reports `turn_logging_unsupported` and writes nothing to the server; when the check starts failing in a host session after one of its requests was delivered, the first verb or hook that observes it records `unsupportedSince` with reason `duplicate_prompt_hook`, and writes for a turn already open go to the plugin failsafe (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001, `nodeDuplicateHookAfterDelivery_unsupportedSince_openTurnCloseCaptured` for classic cline in A8, `DuplicateHookAfterDelivery_UnsupportedSince_OpenTurnCloseCaptured` for copilot in A9). [A8 +A9]'\n248:    - \"- FR-MCP-SESSIONCLASS-004-AC002 The local pre-check is advisory: before the close is sent it computes the missing completion items with the exact gate message (the golden-contract rules) and reports them as a warning; it never refuses a close. A class or review recorded by another process, host session, or surface before the completion is honored and is not reported missing. The server gate (FR-MCP-SESSIONCLASS-002, S10) is the only completion authority, so nothing refuses a close that a pre-S10 or rolled-back server would accept, and no activation signal is needed: this plan adds no marker field, and the marker and its signature stay exactly as they are (FR-MCP-140, TR-MCP-SEC-005, TEST-MCP-189 unaffected). When the server refuses a close with 400 `session_turn_completion_gate`, the surface reports the server's message and missing items, the turn stays in progress, and AC003 applies (`Precheck_Advisory_WarnsMissingItems_NeverRefuses`, `nodePrecheck_advisory_warnsMissingItems_neverRefuses`). Unsupported session (operator option 1, FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001): for a turn of that host session still in progress when `unsupportedSince` was recorded (a delivered open request, or a `withdrawn` queue entry still `in_progress`, addressed by its original pair; a minted request still `queued` is not covered and stays a warning), the close goes to the `unsupported_session` failsafe with no server write per FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001 (the advisory warning still prints), and the server gate judges it at the drain; the model-evidence check (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC002) still applies. This is plugin-core state only; the McpAgent pre-check of TR-MCP-SESSIONCLASS-004 has no host session (`Precheck_UnsupportedSession_CodeTurnMissingReviews_CloseCaptured_NoServerWrite`, `nodePrecheck_unsupportedSession_codeTurnMissingReviews_closeCaptured`). [S8 +S9 +S10]\"\n301:    - \"- TR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-003 Per-turn store, pointer, target resolution, stop ledger (lib-ps). AC-001 `turns/<requestId>.yaml` plus `.lock`; AC-002 `Resolve-ReplTargetTurn` contract (precedence: explicit requestId, else invocation binding; an unqualified verb is refused with `request_ambiguous` while two or more delivered requests are open, D9; an unknown explicit id is refused with `request_unknown`); AC-003 `Invoke-ReplSupersedeCurrentTurnIfInProgress` removed; AC-004 stop-ledger contract (`ledger` inside `turn-state.yaml`, written in the same atomic replace as the delivery it records); AC-005 per-turn edits never overwrite another turn's title (absorbs the intent of retired TR-MCP-REPL-019); AC-006 `Get-PluginHostSessionKey` implements the host binding table (invocation-context key, else the live-session rule with `host_session_inferred`, `host_session_unknown`, `host_session_ambiguous`), and `New-ReplInvocationBinding` captures `{ hostSessionKey, keySource, ingressNonce, requestId, boundAtUtc }` (C4; `ingressNonce` is the bound turn's nonce) once per verb invocation into its persist and failsafe records; AC-007 `sessions/<hostSessionKey>/turn-state.yaml` (Node: `turn-state.json`) is written only by the arrival bridge, or a verb, the Stop hook, or SessionStart applying pending transcript records through `Sync-PluginArrivalLog` up to the transcript length at its own start (the Stop payload carries `transcript_path`; `StopGate_BridgeDead_AppliesPendingRecords_BlocksDeliveredUnclosed`) (mint, delivery, withdrawal, `appliedThroughOffset`), the prompt hook on hosts without a bridge (mint and delivery), the close verbs (removing a request from the open delivered list only when the server confirms its close), the failsafe drain (replacing `pending-<nonce>` with the minted id in the write target and open list), the Stop gate (clearing ledger entries of confirmed-closed turns on a passing Stop, and recording `stop_gate_overridden_by_host` with the open turn ids when the host ends the session anyway), the first verb or hook in a host session that observes a failing copilot or classic-cline single-registration check (recording `unsupportedSince` with reason `duplicate_prompt_hook`), and SessionStart (recording the host session's own `mcpSessionId`, and clearing `unsupportedSince` where its cause's own rule allows), each as one atomic replace under `turn-open.lock`; and `sessions/<hostSessionKey>/ended.yaml` (`{ reason: session_end | idle, endedAtUtc }` with the session-level envelope) only by the session-end hook, the 12-hour idle rule, and the revival of an idle-ended session (removal, atomic under `turn-open.lock`). The arrival bridge, the mint, and every verb read the MCP session id only from their own host session's turn-state; the workspace-level `session-state.yaml` is a legacy file never used for targeting, and the rotated-sessionId rebind (`repl-invoke.ps1:1954-1965`, TR-MCP-PLUGIN-012) is never applied to per-turn files (`TwoHostSessions_OwnMcpSessionIds_SecondSessionStartNeverRetargetsFirst`). A5 extends AC-007 with the close verbs' server-confirmed removal, and A7 with the Stop gate's writes. [A4 +A5 +A7]\"\n304:    - \"- TR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-006 Stop-gate functions. AC-001 `Get-PluginStopGateTurns` (IO) and `Get-PluginStopGateFindings` (pure); AC-002 block/warn/pass output contract and exact block text; AC-003 auto-close code removed; AC-004 `duplicate_delivery_suspected` computed from the queue entries' `mintedAtUtc` and `promptSha256`; AC-005 `turn_withdrawn_unclosed` computed from `turn-state.yaml` `withdrawn` entries whose turn is still `in_progress`; AC-006 in a host session with `unsupportedSince`, whatever its reason, the findings count a turn's captured `unsupported_session` failsafe records, so a captured close turns its block into a warning naming the cause and the retained records; outside `unsupportedSince`, a model close held in the ordinary failsafe because of a transport outage turns that turn's block into the degraded persistence warning, and its ledger entry stays until the server confirms the close (`StopGate_OutageCloseQueued_WarnsDegraded_LedgerKeptUntilConfirmed`). [A7]\"\n306:    - \"- TR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-008 Node core turn integrity (`plugins/core/lib-node`). AC-001 `classifySystemEvent`, `ingressNonce(host, payload)` (pure, per the host binding table; the equal-field rule is applied under the host session lock before the mint), `mintRequest` (server mint with failsafe and `pending-<nonce>` rebinding); AC-002 per-turn cache in `cache-manager.ts` with per-host-session write target, queue, and ended markers; AC-003 `resolveTargetTurn` and `bindInvocation` (D2 precedence, immutable capture, live-session rule); AC-004 `mapTurnWriteOutcome`; AC-005 session tools accept requestId and decision lists; AC-006 every Node persistence path, including an audit outbox if QB-AGENT-001's has landed, applies the C3 outcome mapping, carries the C4 binding on each event, and rebinds `pending-<nonce>` events in order (C2); the A8 admission check runs before either audit-outbox entry point (`AuditOutbox.enqueue`, `AuditAliasConsumer.enqueue`, neither of which knows `unsupportedSince`), so a write captured as `unsupported_session` goes to the failsafe only, and neither entry point nor any other persistence path is called for it before its drain (enqueue-then-quarantine does not satisfy this; reviews are refused at that admission; the drain uses C5 original attribution) (FR-MCP-SESSIONTURN-005-AC001, `nodeUnsupportedSession_capturedWrite_neverEntersOutboxBeforeDrain`, a spy on the A8 outbox port double asserting neither entry point is called; the same assertion against the real QB outbox runs in QB-AGENT-001's integration after A8, `qbOutbox_admissionBef